(window.LH_SEARCH = window.LH_SEARCH || {})["selenium"] = {"s":"selenium","t":[["what-is-selenium","What Selenium is and where it came from","1.1"],["architecture","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","1.2"],["setup-first-script","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","1.3"],["sessions-options","Sessions, options and timeouts","2.1"],["navigate-screenshots","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","2.2"],["locators","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","3.1"],["finding-elements","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","3.2"],["interacting","Interacting with elements and reading them","3.3"],["waits","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","4.1"],["windows-frames-alerts","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","4.2"],["actions","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","4.3"],["javascript-cookies","JavaScript execution and cookies","4.4"],["test-frameworks","Test frameworks: JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha","5.1"],["page-objects","Page objects and page components","5.2"],["practices","Good practices and things to avoid","5.3"],["troubleshooting","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","5.4"],["grid","Selenium Grid 4","6.1"],["bidi-cdp","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","6.2"],["revision","Revision: predict, debug and design","7.1"],["cheatsheet","Pick your cheatsheet","📄"],["cheatsheet-java","Java cheatsheet","📄"],["cheatsheet-py","Python cheatsheet","📄"],["cheatsheet-js","JavaScript cheatsheet","📄"],["interview-questions","Interview Questions","🎤"]],"c":[[0,"","What Selenium is and where it came from","What Selenium does, its three parts, the key words, its history to the W3C standard and a first real program. · selenium · what is selenium · webdriver · selenium ide · selenium grid · history · selenium 4 · w3c Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 1: Meet Selenium›What Selenium is Step 1 · Topic 1 of 3 · Beginner Meet the tools that let a program use a real browser the way you do, see the three parts of the project, and follow twenty years of history to the W3C standard. About 30 min4 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1What Selenium is 2WebDriver, IDE and Grid 3How Selenium grew 4A first look at real code Lesson 1 of 4"],[0,"what","What Selenium is","Think about the last time you bought a train ticket online. You opened a website, typed two station names, picked a date, clicked Search, chose a seat and paid. Every one of those steps is something a person does with a browser. Selenium is a set of free, open source tools that lets a program do the same steps in a real browser, as fast and as often as you like. The official documentation puts it this way: Selenium is an umbrella project for a range of tools and libraries that enable and support the automation of web browsers. At its core it is a browser user agent library: a library your code calls to control a browser the way a user would. It can type into fields, choose from drop downs, tick boxes, click links, move the mouse and run JavaScript. What people use Selenium for An everyday example Testing web applications Before a bank releases a new version of its login page, a test opens the page in Chrome, Firefox and Edge, logs in with a test account and checks the balance appears. Repetitive tasks A script logs into a supplier portal every morning at 6 and downloads the order report for the day. Collecting data from pages A researcher reads prices from a site that has no API. The docs ask you to check the terms of service of the site first, because some sites forbid it and some block Selenium. Most people use it for the first one: automated testing. That is why most of this subject is about testing, but every command you learn works for any browser job. Real world scenario: a shopping site on sale day A shop changes its checkout page on Friday. On Saturday thousands of people buy during a sale. If the Pay button stopped working in Firefox, the shop loses money every minute. A Selenium test that runs on every change opens the checkout in each browser, fills the form and presses Pay, so the broken button is caught on Friday afternoon instead of by customers on Saturday."],[0,"what","One interface for every browser","Chrome, Firefox, Edge and Safari are built very differently inside, even when the pages look the same. A guiding principle of the project is one common interface for all major browsers. Selenium hides the differences, so the same few lines of your code run on each browser. You learn the commands once. Common mistake Selenium only controls the browser. It does not decide whether a test passed, it does not write reports and it does not give you Given, When, Then syntax. Those come from a test framework such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha. Chapter 5.1 joins the two. Lesson 2 of 4"],[0,"family","WebDriver, IDE and Grid","Selenium is not one tool. The documentation names three parts, and each one answers a different need. Part What it is Use it when WebDriver A library for your programming language plus a driver for each browser. Your code sends commands, the driver makes the browser carry them out. You write automated tests or scripts in Java, Python, JavaScript, C# or Ruby. This is what most of this subject teaches. IDE A Chrome and Firefox extension that records what you do in the browser and plays it back as Selenium commands. You want a quick recording, or you are learning which command matches which action. Grid A server that spreads WebDriver sessions over many machines, browsers and operating systems. One machine is too slow, or you must test on browser and operating system pairs you do not have."],[0,"family","Four words you will hear every day","Word Meaning in the Selenium documentation API The set of commands you use to control WebDriver, such as get, findElement and click. Library The code module that holds those commands for one language: a .jar file for Java, a pip package for Python, an npm package for JavaScript. Driver The program that controls one browser, such as chromedriver for Chrome and geckodriver for Firefox. Browser vendors write most of them. It usually runs on the same machine as the browser. Framework An extra library that supports a test suite: test runners such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha, or tools such as Cucumber. Your codeCalls the WebDriver API in your language. Language bindingThe Selenium library turns each call into a command. Driverchromedriver receives the command. BrowserChrome does the work and the answer travels back. Chapter 1.2 opens up that chain and shows the real messages that travel along it. One idea, four depths Selenium is a remote control for a web browser. Your program presses the buttons, the browser does what a person would do. WebDriver is a library plus a browser specific driver. The library sends commands, the driver executes them in the browser, and results come back. IDE records and replays, and Grid runs many sessions on many machines. WebDriver is a client and server design: the language binding is the local end and the driver is the remote end of the W3C WebDriver protocol. Because the browser is driven through vendor automation APIs and not through code compiled into the app, the build you test is the build you ship. The W3C protocol separates intent from mechanism. Ongoing work in WebDriver BiDi adds a two way event channel over WebSocket, so a client can listen to console logs, network traffic and DOM changes as they happen, which the request and response design of classic WebDriver cannot do. Lesson 3 of 4"],[0,"history","How Selenium grew","Selenium is more than twenty years old, and its history explains its design. The early versions ran JavaScript inside the page. That broke on many browser rules, so the project moved to driving the browser from outside, and in the end the browser makers agreed on a standard. Version Released What changed Selenium 2.0 8 July 2011 The WebDriver APIs became the heart of Selenium. Selenium 1 code still ran. Selenium 3.0 13 October 2016 The original Selenium Core implementation was removed. Every major browser vendor now ships its own driver. W3C WebDriver 5 June 2018 WebDriver became a W3C Recommendation, a standard every browser can implement the same way. Selenium 4.0 13 October 2021 Support for the old JSON Wire Protocol was removed. Only the W3C protocol is used. Relative locators and a rebuilt Grid arrived. In the exam Interviewers like to ask what changed in Selenium 4. The short answer: W3C protocol only, so capabilities must follow the W3C names (browserVersion, platformName) or carry a vendor prefix such as goog:chromeOptions. Options classes replaced DesiredCapabilities, Java lost the findElementById style helpers, and relative locators and Grid 4 arrived. Selenium Manager came later, in 4.6. Going deeper: Why driving from outside the page matters A script running inside the page follows the same rules as the page. The browser same origin policy stops a page from one site touching another, so Selenium Core needed a proxy server to pretend every page came from the same place. Events made by JavaScript are also not the same as real ones: the Selenium 2 release notes describe how native events, sent at the operating system level, avoid browser quirks of JavaScript made events. WebDriver lets the browser vendor build the automation into the browser itself, which is why the vendors write the drivers today. Lesson 4 of 4"],[0,"look","A first look at real code","Here is the smallest useful Selenium program, written three times. Pick your language with the buttons, then press Explain line by line to read what each line does. The output under the code is real: this page was built by running each program in Chrome. A first look: open a page, read its title, close the browser FirstLook.java import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassFirstLook {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");System.out.println(\"Title: \" + driver.getTitle());System.out.println(\"URL: \" + driver.getCurrentUrl()); driver.quit(); }} Bring in the WebDriver interface. Every browser driver in Java speaks through it. ChromeDriver is the class that starts and talks to Chrome. A plain Java class. Java runs the main method below. Start Chrome. Selenium Manager finds a matching chromedriver for you, then a new browser session begins. Ask the browser to open the page and wait until it has loaded. Ask the browser for the page title and print it. Ask which address the browser is showing now. End the session. This closes every window and stops the driver process. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Title: Web form URL: https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html main.py from selenium import webdriverdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")print(\"Title:\", driver.title)print(\"URL:\", driver.current_url)driver.quit() Import the webdriver package, which holds one class per browser. Start Chrome. Selenium Manager finds a matching chromedriver, then a new session begins. Open the page and wait until it has loaded. driver.title asks the browser for the page title. current_url asks which address the browser is showing. End the session, close every window and stop chromedriver. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Title: Web form URL: https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction firstLook() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html'); console.log('Title: ' + await driver.getTitle()); console.log('URL: ' + await driver.getCurrentUrl());await driver.quit();})(); Load the two pieces we need from the selenium-webdriver package. Every Selenium call in JavaScript returns a promise, so we work inside an async function. Build a driver for Chrome. Selenium Manager finds chromedriver and a session starts. Open the page and wait for it to load. Ask for the title. await waits for the browser to answer. Ask which address the browser shows. End the session and close the browser. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Title: Web form URL: https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html All three programs do the same four things: start a session, send the browser somewhere, ask it a question, and end the session. Every Selenium program you will ever write follows that shape. Chapter 1.3 grows it into the eight standard steps. Remember Always end with quit. If a program stops without it, the driver process and the browser keep running in the background and hold memory and ports. On a busy test machine those leftovers pile up quickly. What happened when the Python version ran webdriver.Chrome() asked Selenium Manager for a chromedriver that matches the installed Chrome, started it, and sent it a New Session request. chromedriver launched Chrome with a fresh, empty profile and replied with a session id. get sent the address. The driver waited until the page reported it had finished loading, then answered. title and current_url were two more small questions, each with an answer. quit sent Delete Session. Chrome and chromedriver both closed."],[1,"","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","How a command travels from your code to the browser over the W3C WebDriver protocol, shown with real HTTP traffic. · architecture · w3c webdriver protocol · json wire · http · endpoint · session · driver · chromedriver · bidi · error codes Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 1: Meet Selenium›Architecture Step 1 · Topic 2 of 3 · Beginner Follow one click from your code through the language binding and the driver to the browser and back, then drive Chrome with plain HTTP to see that the protocol is simple. About 45 min4 lessons1 animation3 formulas6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1The pieces between your code and the browser 2The W3C WebDriver protocol 3See the wire for yourself 4Local, remote and Grid, plus BiDi Lesson 1 of 4"],[1,"pieces","The pieces between your code and the browser","When you call click(), your code does not reach into Chrome and press the button. A message travels through three separate programs. Knowing them makes every error message later in this subject easy to read. Your testJava, Python or JavaScript code that calls the Selenium API. Language bindingThe Selenium library. The W3C spec calls it the local end. Driverchromedriver, geckodriver, msedgedriver or safaridriver. The remote end. BrowserDoes the real work and reports back. The Selenium documentation describes the driver as the component responsible for delegating down to the browser, and handling communication to and from Selenium and the browser. That split is deliberate: browser vendors take responsibility for driving their own browser, and Selenium ties all the drivers together behind one interface. One command, from your code to the browser and backPick a command and a language, then step through the journey. Tick \"make it fail\" to see an error answer. Step 0 of 5 Command Language Make it failOnly find, click and read can fail here. Your code for Find an element. Try this Play \"Find an element\". The answer carries a long element reference under a fixed key. That key is the same in every browser. Tick \"make it fail\" for Find. The driver answers 404 with \"no such element\", and the binding turns it into an exception in your language. Switch the language between Java, Python and JavaScript. The request on the wire does not change, only your line of code does. Spec word In practice Example Local end The client side, usually a language library on top of the protocol selenium for Python, selenium-java, selenium-webdriver for npm Remote end The server side that carries out the commands chromedriver, or Selenium Grid Intermediary node A remote end that passes commands on to another remote end Selenium Grid, which forwards to a node Endpoint node The last remote end in the chain, built into or next to the browser chromedriver driving Chrome Real world scenario: ordering in a restaurant You tell the waiter \"one masala dosa\". The waiter writes it on a slip in a fixed format the kitchen understands, the kitchen cooks, and the waiter brings the plate back. You never enter the kitchen. Your code is the customer, the binding writes the slip, the driver is the kitchen pass that hands orders to the cook, and the browser is the cook. Whatever language you speak to the waiter, the slip looks the same. Lesson 2 of 4"],[1,"protocol","The W3C WebDriver protocol","The messages between the binding and the driver follow the W3C WebDriver specification. It is a plain HTTP protocol: each command is one HTTP request, and each answer is one HTTP response with a JSON body. Command Method Path What you write in Python New Session POST /session webdriver.Chrome() Delete Session DELETE /session/{session id} driver.quit() Navigate To POST /session/{id}/url driver.get(url) Get Title GET /session/{id}/title driver.title Find Element POST /session/{id}/element driver.find_element(...) Find Elements POST /session/{id}/elements driver.find_elements(...) Element Click POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/click element.click() Element Send Keys POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/value element.send_keys(...) Get Element Text GET /session/{id}/element/{element id}/text element.text Set Timeouts POST /session/{id}/timeouts driver.implicitly_wait(2) Execute Script POST /session/{id}/execute/sync driver.execute_script(...) Take Screenshot GET /session/{id}/screenshot driver.get_screenshot_as_png()"],[1,"protocol","The shape of every answer","SuccessExam favourite HTTP 200 and {\"value\": result} The result is null for commands that only act, a string for text and titles, a list for Find Elements. ErrorExam favourite HTTP 4xx or 5xx and {\"value\": {\"error\", \"message\", \"stacktrace\"}} error is a fixed code such as no such element. The binding turns it into an exception. Element reference {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.C6…e.6\"} A web element travels as an object with this one fixed key. The value is an opaque id made by the driver. Error code HTTP status Python exception you see no such element 404 NoSuchElementException stale element reference 404 StaleElementReferenceException invalid session id 404 InvalidSessionIdException element not interactable 400 ElementNotInteractableException element click intercepted 400 ElementClickInterceptedException invalid selector 400 InvalidSelectorException timeout 500 TimeoutException unexpected alert open 500 UnexpectedAlertPresentException session not created 500 SessionNotCreatedException Remember The binding changes some locators before they leave your machine. The Python binding sends By.ID \"x\" as the CSS selector [id=\"x\"], By.NAME as [name=\"x\"] and By.CLASS_NAME as .x. Look for it in the log in the next lesson. That is why the W3C spec only needs five strategies: css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath. Read one real request from the log The binding sent POST http://localhost:PORT/session/a443…/element. Its body was {\"using\": \"css selector\", \"value\": \"[name=\"my-text\"]\"}, made from By.NAME, \"my-text\". chromedriver answered 200 with {\"value\": {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.C6C4…e.6\"}}. The binding wrapped that id in a WebElement object, so later calls such as send_keys know which element to name in their path. Lesson 3 of 4"],[1,"wire","See the wire for yourself","The best way to believe the protocol is simple is to drive Chrome without Selenium. The programs below start chromedriver, then send it the same requests a binding would, with nothing but the HTTP client of each language. They fill the web form and read the message on the next page. The output is what chromedriver really answered. Drive Chrome with plain HTTP and no Selenium library at all RawWebDriver.java import java.net.URI;import java.net.http.HttpClient;import java.net.http.HttpRequest;import java.net.http.HttpResponse;publicclassRawWebDriver {staticHttpClient http = HttpClient.newHttpClient();staticString call(String method, String path, String body) throwsException {HttpRequest.Builder req = HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(\"http://localhost:9515\" + path)) .header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\"); req.method(method, body == null ? HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.noBody() : HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(body));String reply = http.send(req.build(), HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()).body();System.out.println(method + \" \" + path + \" -> \" + (reply.length() > 70 ? reply.substring(0, 70) + \"...\" : reply));return reply; }staticString value(String json, String key) {return json.replaceAll(\"(?s).*\\\"\" + key + \"\\\":\\\"([^\\\"]+)\\\".*\", \"$1\"); }publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {Process driver = newProcessBuilder(\"chromedriver\", \"--port=9515\").start();Thread.sleep(1000);String created = call(\"POST\", \"/session\", \"{\\\"capabilities\\\":{\\\"alwaysMatch\\\":{\\\"browserName\\\":\\\"chrome\\\"}}}\");String s = \"/session/\" + value(created, \"sessionId\"); call(\"POST\", s + \"/timeouts\", \"{\\\"implicit\\\":2000}\"); call(\"POST\", s + \"/url\", \"{\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\\\"}\"); call(\"GET\", s + \"/title\", null);String box = value(call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", \"{\\\"using\\\":\\\"css selector\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"[name='my-text']\\\"}\"), \"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\"); call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + box + \"/value\", \"{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Selenium\\\"}\");String button = value(call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", \"{\\\"using\\\":\\\"css selector\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"button\\\"}\"), \"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\"); call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + button + \"/click\", \"{}\");String message = value(call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", \"{\\\"using\\\":\\\"css selector\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"#message\\\"}\"), \"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\"); call(\"GET\", s + \"/element/\" + message + \"/text\", null); call(\"DELETE\", s, null); driver.destroy(); }} URI turns a text address into an address object the HTTP client accepts. The HTTP client built into Java 11 and later. One HTTP client, reused for every command. Send one W3C command and return the JSON answer as text. chromedriver listens on port 9515 unless told otherwise. The protocol speaks JSON in both directions. GET and DELETE carry no body. POST carries a JSON object. Send it and wait for the answer. Print the request and the start of the answer. A tiny helper that pulls one string out of the JSON answer. Start the driver program yourself, the job Selenium usually does for you. Give chromedriver a moment to open its port. New Session. Chrome opens. Every later command names this session in its path. Set Timeouts: let Find Element keep looking for up to 2 seconds, so the next page has time to load. Navigate To. Get Title. Find Element returns a reference under this fixed key. Element Send Keys types into that element. Element Click submits the form. Get Element Text on the next page. Delete Session closes Chrome. Stop chromedriver. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) POST /session -> {\"value\":{\"capabilities\":{\"acceptInsecureCerts\":false,\"browserName\":\"c... POST /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/timeouts -> {\"value\":null} POST /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/url -> {\"value\":null} GET /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/title -> {\"value\":\"Web form\"} POST /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/element -> {\"value\":{\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\":\"f.CEA8EA828217AE4BDE1... POST"],[1,"wire","See the wire for yourself","/session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/element/f.CEA8EA828217AE4BDE1BBE3050CC97CF.d.298B371AFB4A8DB17596DA16CFED7B72.e.6/value -> {\"value\":null} POST /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/element -> {\"value\":{\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\":\"f.CEA8EA828217AE4BDE1... POST /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/element/f.CEA8EA828217AE4BDE1BBE3050CC97CF.d.298B371AFB4A8DB17596DA16CFED7B72.e.21/click -> {\"value\":null} POST /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/element -> {\"value\":{\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\":\"f.CEA8EA828217AE4BDE1... GET /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28/element/f.CEA8EA828217AE4BDE1BBE3050CC97CF.d.C670560C5CAD8B17CAC20EB9BA743FEC.e.27/text -> {\"value\":\"Received!\"} DELETE /session/1572ea8ca4df6e7f14c3a8d4adc84e28 -> {\"value\":null} main.py import jsonimport subprocessimport timeimport urllib.requestdriver = subprocess.Popen([\"chromedriver\", \"--port=9515\"], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)time.sleep(1)def call(method, path, body=None): data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body isnotNoneelseNone req = urllib.request.Request(\"http://localhost:9515\" + path, data=data, method=method, headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"}) reply = json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(req)) text = json.dumps(reply)print(method, path, \" -> \", text[:70] + (\"...\"iflen(text) > 70else\"\"))return reply[\"value\"]KEY = \"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\"s = \"/session/\" + call(\"POST\", \"/session\", {\"capabilities\": {\"alwaysMatch\": {\"browserName\": \"chrome\"}}})[\"sessionId\"]call(\"POST\", s + \"/timeouts\", {\"implicit\": 2000})call(\"POST\", s + \"/url\", {\"url\": \"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\"})call(\"GET\", s + \"/title\")box = call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", {\"using\": \"css selector\", \"value\": \"[name='my-text']\"})[KEY]call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + box + \"/value\", {\"text\": \"Selenium\"})button = call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", {\"using\": \"css selector\", \"value\": \"button\"})[KEY]call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + button + \"/click\", {})message = call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", {\"using\": \"css selector\", \"value\": \"#message\"})[KEY]call(\"GET\", s + \"/element/\" + message + \"/text\")call(\"DELETE\", s)driver.terminate() For turning Python dictionaries into JSON text and back. For starting the chromedriver program. Python's built in HTTP client. No Selenium library is imported anywhere. Start the driver yourself, the job Selenium usually does for you. Its own messages are hidden. Give it a moment to open port 9515. Send one W3C command and return the part of the answer under \"value\". POST bodies are JSON objects. GET and DELETE have none. Send it and read the JSON answer. Print the request and the start of the answer. Every success answer keeps its result under \"value\". The fixed key the W3C spec uses for element references. New Session. Chrome opens and we keep the session path. Set Timeouts: Find Element may keep looking for 2 seconds, so the next page has time to load. Navigate To. Get Title. Find Element. Element Send Keys. Element Click submits the form. Get Element Text on the next page. Delete Session closes Chrome. Stop chromedriver. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) POST /session -> {\"value\": {\"capabilities\": {\"acceptInsecureCerts\": false, \"browserName... POST /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/timeouts -> {\"value\": null} POST /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/url -> {\"value\": null} GET /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/title -> {\"value\": \"Web form\"} POST /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/element -> {\"value\": {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.BC5FF324B64207214... POST /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/element/f.BC5FF324B642072145DC3CDEE73ADF5C.d.D9093E4BCA0DE174FCE7E68286174CFD.e.6/value -> {\"value\": null} POST /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/element -> {\"value\": {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.BC5FF324B64207214... POST"],[1,"wire","See the wire for yourself","/session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/element/f.BC5FF324B642072145DC3CDEE73ADF5C.d.D9093E4BCA0DE174FCE7E68286174CFD.e.21/click -> {\"value\": null} POST /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/element -> {\"value\": {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.BC5FF324B64207214... GET /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc/element/f.BC5FF324B642072145DC3CDEE73ADF5C.d.EA8082E1511DC98290D1B66DBB605CA8.e.27/text -> {\"value\": \"Received!\"} DELETE /session/8676a175324f14ce9347b93a7adbc2bc -> {\"value\": null} main.js const { spawn } = require('child_process');asyncfunction call(method, path, body) {const res = await fetch('http://localhost:9515' + path, { method, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: body === undefined ? undefined : JSON.stringify(body), });const reply = await res.json();const text = JSON.stringify(reply); console.log(method, path, ' -> ', text.length > 70 ? text.slice(0, 70) + '...' : text);return reply.value;}(async () => {const driver = spawn('chromedriver', ['--port=9515'], { stdio: 'ignore' });awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000));constKEY = 'element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf';const s = '/session/' + (await call('POST', '/session', { capabilities: { alwaysMatch: { browserName: 'chrome' } } })).sessionId;await call('POST', s + '/timeouts', { implicit: 2000 });await call('POST', s + '/url', { url: 'https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html' });await call('GET', s + '/title');const box = (await call('POST', s + '/element', { using: 'css selector', value: \"[name='my-text']\" }))[KEY];await call('POST', s + '/element/' + box + '/value', { text: 'Selenium' });const button = (await call('POST', s + '/element', { using: 'css selector', value: 'button' }))[KEY];await call('POST', s + '/element/' + button + '/click', {});const message = (await call('POST', s + '/element', { using: 'css selector', value: '#message' }))[KEY];await call('GET', s + '/element/' + message + '/text');await call('DELETE', s); driver.kill();})(); For starting the chromedriver program. No Selenium package is loaded. Send one W3C command and return the part of the answer under \"value\". fetch is built into Node.js 18 and later. POST bodies are JSON objects. GET and DELETE have none. Print the request and the start of the answer. Start the driver yourself and hide its own messages. Give it a moment to open its port. The fixed key for element references. New Session. Set Timeouts: Find Element may keep looking for 2 seconds while the next page loads. Navigate To. Get Title. Find Element. Element Send Keys. Element Click. Get Element Text. Delete Session. Stop chromedriver. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) POST /session -> {\"value\":{\"capabilities\":{\"acceptInsecureCerts\":false,\"browserName\":\"c... POST /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/timeouts -> {\"value\":null} POST /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/url -> {\"value\":null} GET /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/title -> {\"value\":\"Web form\"} POST /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/element -> {\"value\":{\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\":\"f.925130A9C3CE63D590C... POST /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/element/f.925130A9C3CE63D590C8022B6F7B34FC.d.FFD99782F6866C71F9472FF7B365A671.e.6/value -> {\"value\":null} POST /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/element -> {\"value\":{\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\":\"f.925130A9C3CE63D590C... POST /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/element/f.925130A9C3CE63D590C8022B6F7B34FC.d.FFD99782F6866C71F9472FF7B365A671.e.21/click -> {\"value\":null} POST /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/element -> {\"value\":{\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\":\"f.925130A9C3CE63D590C... GET /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7/element/f.925130A9C3CE63D590C8022B6F7B34FC.d.7A3C754C59BC38B27A8501799597EA4E.e.27/text -> {\"value\":\"Received!\"} DELETE /session/fe3fd9b77d510e3774c3e96a1d79dfa7 -> {\"value\":null} Every request gets an answer that starts with {\"value\":. Without the Set"],[1,"wire","See the wire for yourself","Timeouts line, Find Element can run before the next page has loaded and answer no such element, which is the first taste of the waiting problem in chapter 4.1. The session id and element ids are random, so your run will show different ones."],[1,"wire","The same traffic from a normal script","A real Selenium script sends exactly these requests for you. Turning on debug logging shows them. Note how By.NAME leaves your machine as a CSS selector. Watch the real requests a normal Selenium script sends Python is used here because its logging prints each request on one line. Java (java.util.logging at FINE for org.openqa.selenium.remote) and JavaScript (the webdriver.http logger) can show the same traffic. main.py import loggingimport sysfrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBylogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG, format=\"%(message)s\", stream=sys.stdout)for name in (\"urllib3\", \"selenium.webdriver.common\"): logging.getLogger(name).setLevel(logging.WARNING)driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")box = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")box.send_keys(\"Hi\")print(\"title is\", driver.title)driver.quit() Python's standard logging module. Print DEBUG messages, which include every HTTP request the binding sends. Hide two noisy loggers so only the protocol messages remain. POST /session POST /session/{id}/url POST /session/{id}/element. Watch how By.NAME becomes a CSS selector. POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/value GET /session/{id}/title DELETE /session/{id} Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) POST http://localhost:47995/session {'capabilities': {'firstMatch': [{}], 'alwaysMatch': {'browserName': 'chrome', 'pageLoadStrategy': <PageLoadStrategy.normal: 'normal'>, 'goog:chromeOptions': {'extensions': [], 'args': []}}}} Remote response: status=200 | data={\"value\":{\"capabilities\":{\"acceptInsecureCerts\":false,\"browserName\":\"chrome\",\"browserVersion\":\"153.0.8010.12\",\"chrome\":{\"chromedriverVersion\":\"153.0.8010.12 (971a7443b0c9b0a9b2860529b33331b76077ec62-refs/branch-heads/8010@{#343})\",\"userDataDir\":\"/tmp/org.chromium.Chromium.scoped_dir.xIOqAL\"},\"fedcm:accounts\":true,\"goog:chromeOptions\":{\"debuggerAddress\":\"localhost:36071\"},\"goog:processID\":5976,\"networkConnectionEnabled\":false,\"pageLoadStrategy\":\"normal\",\"platformName\":\"linux\",\"proxy\":{},\"setWindowRect\":true,\"strictFileInteractability\":false,\"timeouts\":{\"implicit\":0,\"pageLoad\":300000,\"script\":30000},\"unhandledPromptBehavior\":\"dismiss and notify\",\"webauthn:extension:cmtgKey\":true,\"webauthn:extension:credBlob\":true,\"webauthn:extension:largeBlob\":true,\"webauthn:extension:minPinLength\":true,\"webauthn:extension:prf\":true,\"webauthn:virtualAuthenticators\":true},\"sessionId\":\"d22f625b78c052911ff513d44ee74914\"}} | headers=HTTPHeaderDict({'Content-Length': '913', 'Content-Type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8', 'cache-control': 'no-cache'}) Finished Request POST http://localhost:47995/session/d22f625b78c052911ff513d44ee74914/url {'url': 'https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html'} Remote response: status=200 | data={\"value\":null} | headers=HTTPHeaderDict({'Content-Length': '14', 'Content-Type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8', 'cache-control': 'no-cache'}) Finished Request POST http://localhost:47995/session/d22f625b78c052911ff513d44ee74914/element {'using': 'css selector', 'value': '[name=\"my-text\"]'} Remote response: status=200 | data={\"value\":{\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\":\"f.0C92C16D926BE0471F7373028EABCFEF.d.73441B1E15E59DD3679B0F54BF11DFB0.e.6\"}} | headers=HTTPHeaderDict({'Content-Length': '125', 'Content-Type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8', 'cache-control': 'no-cache'}) Finished Request POST http://localhost:47995/session/d22f625b78c052911ff513d44ee74914/element/f.0C92C16D926BE0471F7373028EABCFEF.d.73441B1E15E59DD3679B0F54BF11DFB0.e.6/value {'text': 'Hi', 'value': ['H', 'i']} Remote response: status=200 | data={\"value\":null} | headers=HTTPHeaderDict({'Content-Length': '14', 'Content-Type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8', 'cache-control': 'no-cache'}) Finished Request GET http://localhost:47995/session/d22f625b78c052911ff513d44ee74914/title {} Remote response: status=200 | data={\"value\":\"Web form\"} |"],[1,"wire","The same traffic from a normal script","headers=HTTPHeaderDict({'Content-Length': '20', 'Content-Type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8', 'cache-control': 'no-cache'}) Finished Request title is Web form DELETE http://localhost:47995/session/d22f625b78c052911ff513d44ee74914 {} Remote response: status=200 | data={\"value\":null} | headers=HTTPHeaderDict({'Content-Length': '14', 'Content-Type': 'application/json; charset=utf-8', 'cache-control': 'no-cache'}) Finished Request Remember You will never write raw HTTP in real tests. The point is to see that nothing magic happens: Selenium is a polite HTTP client with good manners about waiting, errors and types. When a test fails with a strange message, picture the request that was sent and the error code that came back. Going deeper: How chromedriver controls Chrome The W3C spec only defines the HTTP side. How a driver controls its browser is up to the vendor. In the logged answer to New Session you can see goog:chromeOptions with a debuggerAddress: chromedriver connects to Chrome through its DevTools port. geckodriver uses the remote protocol built into Firefox, and safaridriver is built into macOS. This is why a driver version must match its browser: the inner protocol changes with each browser release. Lesson 4 of 4"],[1,"remote","Local, remote and Grid, plus BiDi","The documentation shows three ways the pieces can be arranged. The protocol is the same in all of them, which is why moving a test from your laptop to a Grid changes one line. Layout Where things run Code difference Direct Your code, the driver and the browser on one machine. new ChromeDriver(), webdriver.Chrome() Remote Your code on one machine, driver and browser on another, reached through Selenium Server or RemoteWebDriver. new RemoteWebDriver(url, options), webdriver.Remote(url, options=...) Grid Selenium Grid receives the commands and relays them to the driver on a node machine. Same as remote, the URL points at the Grid"],[1,"remote","Why a second protocol, WebDriver BiDi?","Classic WebDriver is request and response: your code asks, the browser answers. The browser cannot start a conversation. That is fine for clicking and reading, but not for \"tell me every time the page logs an error\" or \"tell me about every network request\". WebDriver BiDi, a W3C standard made by the Selenium project and the browser vendors, adds a WebSocket connection so the browser can send events as they happen. Before BiDi, Selenium offered these features through the Chrome DevTools Protocol, which the docs call temporary until BiDi is implemented. Chapter 6.2 uses BiDi. One idea, four depths Your program sends small written orders to a helper program, and the helper makes the browser do them. The binding sends HTTP requests with JSON bodies to a driver. Each command has a fixed method and path, and every answer has a value or an error code. The W3C protocol is stateless HTTP with a session id in the path. Intermediary nodes such as Grid can route by that id without understanding the command. Capabilities in New Session negotiate the browser, version, platform and vendor extensions such as goog:chromeOptions. BiDi moves automation to an event driven model over WebSocket, with modules such as browsingContext, script, network and log. It lets one client subscribe to browser events across tabs, which classic WebDriver could only approximate by polling."],[2,"","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","Install Selenium, let Selenium Manager match the driver, and write and tidy up your first real script. · setup · install · selenium manager · first script · eight components · quit · close · chromedriver Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 1: Meet Selenium›Setup and first script Step 1 · Topic 3 of 3 · Beginner Install Selenium in Java, Python or JavaScript, watch Selenium Manager find the right driver, and write the eight steps every Selenium script is made of. About 45 min4 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Install Selenium for your language 2Selenium Manager finds the driver 3The eight steps of a first script 4Always end the session Lesson 1 of 4"],[2,"install","Install Selenium for your language","Before any code runs you need two things on your machine: the Selenium library for your language and a browser. The third piece, the driver, is handled for you, as the next lesson shows. Language Minimum version for Selenium 4.51 How you add Selenium Java Java 11 (the jars are built for Java 11) A build tool. Add org.seleniumhq.selenium:selenium-java to Maven or Gradle. Python Python 3.10 pip install selenium, or a line selenium==4.51.0 in requirements.txt JavaScript Node.js 22 npm install selenium-webdriver The Selenium documentation points to the package pages for the current minimum, so check them when you upgrade. The versions above are what the 4.51.0 packages declare."],[2,"install","Java with Maven","The documentation shows the dependency for a Maven pom.xml. Maven downloads selenium-java and everything it needs when you build. pom.xml <dependency> <groupId>org.seleniumhq.selenium</groupId> <artifactId>selenium-java</artifactId> <version>4.51.0</version></dependency> With Gradle the same coordinates go in build.gradle as testImplementation 'org.seleniumhq.selenium:selenium-java:4.51.0'."],[2,"install","Python and JavaScript","Install in a fresh project python3 -m venv .venvsource .venv/bin/activatepip install seleniumnpm init -ynpm install selenium-webdriver Remember Use a virtual environment in Python and a package.json in JavaScript. Each project then pins its own Selenium version, so upgrading one project never breaks another. Real world scenario: a new tester joins the team Priya clones the test project on her first day. She runs mvn test, Maven reads the version from pom.xml and fetches selenium-java 4.51.0. She already has Chrome. She does not download chromedriver at all: the first test run asks Selenium Manager for it. Her setup takes minutes, and her machine runs the same Selenium version as everyone else. Lesson 2 of 4"],[2,"manager","Selenium Manager finds the driver","Every browser needs its driver: chromedriver for Chrome, geckodriver for Firefox, msedgedriver for Edge. For years people downloaded the driver by hand and put it on the PATH. The documentation describes why that broke. Chrome is evergreen: it updates itself quietly. Your chromedriver 113 worked on Monday, Chrome updated to 115 on Tuesday, and every test failed with session not created: This version of ChromeDriver only supports Chrome version 113. Since Selenium 4.6, every release ships Selenium Manager, the official driver manager. It is a small command line program written in Rust, built for Windows, Linux and macOS, and stored inside the library you installed. Your binding calls it only as a fallback, when you have not given a driver yourself. Automated driver management, as the documentation lists it Browser version discovery. It runs a shell command such as google-chrome --version to learn which browser version is installed. Driver version discovery. It asks the browser vendor which driver version fits that browser. Driver download. It downloads the driver archive, unpacks it and stores it. Driver cache. Drivers live in ~/.cache/selenium. Next time the same driver is taken from there. Since Selenium 4.11 it also manages browsers. If Chrome is missing, or you ask for a version you do not have through the browserVersion option, it downloads Chrome for Testing into the cache. Firefox followed in 4.12 and Edge in 4.14. Labels such as stable, beta, dev and canary work as versions too."],[2,"manager","Watch it work","You can run Selenium Manager yourself. This log was recorded on the Linux machine that built this page, with the network switched off and a chromedriver already on PATH. Read it from the top: driver found on PATH, browser found, browser version read, then the two paths the binding will use. Ask Selenium Manager what it would use selenium-manager --browser chrome --offline --debug Recorded with selenium-manager 0.4.51 on Linux (timestamps removed, folder names shortened) [DEBUG] Offline flag set, but also asked not to avoid browser downloads. Honouring offline flag [DEBUG] Using Selenium Manager in offline mode [DEBUG] Found chromedriver 153.0.8010.12 in PATH: /usr/local/bin/chromedriver [DEBUG] chrome detected at /opt/google/chrome/chrome [DEBUG] Running command: /opt/google/chrome/chrome --version [DEBUG] Output: \"Google Chrome for Testing 153.0.8010.12 \" [DEBUG] Detected browser: chrome 153.0.8010.12 [DEBUG] Exception managing chrome: Unable to discover proper chromedriver version in offline mode [INFO ] Driver path: /usr/local/bin/chromedriver [INFO ] Browser path: /opt/google/chrome/chrome The same machine with an old chromedriver 147 on PATH gave a warning that is worth knowing by heart, because it explains many broken builds: An old driver on PATH selenium-manager --browser chrome --offline --debug Recorded with selenium-manager 0.4.51 on Linux (timestamps removed, folder names and one line shortened) [DEBUG] Found chromedriver 147.0.7727.24 in PATH: /opt/drivers/chromedriver [DEBUG] Detected browser: chrome 153.0.8010.12 [WARN ] The chromedriver version (147.0.7727.24) detected in PATH at /opt/drivers/chromedriver might not be compatible with the detected chrome version (153.0.8010.12) ... so it is advised to delete the driver in PATH and retry [INFO ] Driver path: /opt/drivers/chromedriver What an old driver on a new browser looks like Python only, because the error comes from chromedriver and reads the same in every language. The driver path is given by hand, so Selenium Manager never gets the chance to fix it. main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importSessionNotCreatedExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.chrome.service importServiceservice = Service(executable_path=\"/opt/drivers/chromedriver-147\")try: driver = webdriver.Chrome(service=service) driver.quit()exceptSessionNotCreatedExceptionas e:print(e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0]) Service describes how to start the driver program. Point Selenium at an old chromedriver 147 on purpose. Selenium Manager is now skipped. Chrome on this machine is version 153, so New Session fails. The driver answered with the W3C error code session not created. Print the first line of the driver's message. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) session not created: This version of ChromeDriver only supports Chrome version 147 Common mistake If you pass a driver path yourself, Selenium trusts you and never calls Selenium Manager. The documentation says that with Selenium 4.6 or newer you should not need to set a driver location at all. Delete old drivers from PATH and let Selenium Manager do the job."],[2,"manager","Configuring it","Most people never configure Selenium Manager. When you must, for example behind a company proxy, there are three places, and the first one found wins: Command line argumentsWhat the binding passes, built from your browser options, such as --browser-version. se-config.tomlA file in the cache folder, for example proxy = \"myproxy:8080\". Environment variablesThe same key in capitals with SE_ in front, for example SE_PROXY or SE_OFFLINE. Setting Default What it does cache-path ~/.cache/selenium Where drivers, browsers and metadata are stored ttl 3600 seconds How long resolved versions are trusted before asking the vendor again offline false No network requests or downloads at all avoid-stats false Selenium Manager sends anonymous usage numbers to Plausible once a day. Set SE_AVOID_STATS=true to stop it. proxy none An HTTP proxy for the downloads Going deeper: Why Rust and why a separate program The same driver logic is needed by five bindings. Writing it once as a separate program, compiled for each operating system, means Java, Python, JavaScript, C# and Ruby all behave the same way, and a fix lands in every language at once. Selenium Manager versions mirror Selenium: Selenium 4.51.0 ships Selenium Manager 0.4.51. Lesson 3 of 4"],[2,"eight","The eight steps of a first script","The documentation says that almost everything Selenium does is one of two things: send the browser a command, or ask it for information. A first script uses eight basic components, and you will recognise them in every test you ever read. Step What it means In this example 1. Start the session Create a driver. A browser opens. Chrome 2. Take action on the browser Navigate, go back, resize and so on. Open the web form 3. Request browser information Title, address, window handles, cookies. Read the title 4. Establish a waiting strategy Make sure an element is present before you look for it. Implicit wait of 500 ms 5. Find an element Most commands are about elements. The text box and the button 6. Take action on an element Type, click, clear. Type Selenium, click Submit 7. Request element information Text, attributes, size, state. Read the message 8. End the session Quit the driver and close the browser. quit The eight basic components of a Selenium script FirstScript.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassFirstScript {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");String title = driver.getTitle();System.out.println(\"Title: \" + title); driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofMillis(500));WebElement textBox = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\"));WebElement submitButton = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")); textBox.sendKeys(\"Selenium\"); submitButton.click();WebElement message = driver.findElement(By.id(\"message\"));String value = message.getText();System.out.println(\"Message: \" + value); driver.quit(); }} Duration describes a length of time, used for the wait below. By holds the ways to locate an element: by name, by CSS selector and more. A WebElement is your handle on one element of the page. 1. Start the session. Chrome opens with a fresh profile. 2. Take action on the browser: go to the practice form. 3. Request browser information: the page title. 4. Establish a waiting strategy. Find calls will now retry for up to half a second. 5. Find an element: the text box whose name attribute is my-text. 5. Find another: the first button on the page. 6. Take action on an element: type into the box. 6. Click Submit. The browser loads the next page. Find the message on the new page. The implicit wait gives it time to appear. 7. Request element information: its visible text. 8. End the session. The browser and the driver both close. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Title: Web form Message: Received! main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")title = driver.titleprint(\"Title:\", title)driver.implicitly_wait(0.5)text_box = driver.find_element(by=By.NAME, value=\"my-text\")submit_button = driver.find_element(by=By.CSS_SELECTOR, value=\"button\")text_box.send_keys(\"Selenium\")submit_button.click()message = driver.find_element(by=By.ID, value=\"message\")value = message.textprint(\"Message:\", value)driver.quit() By holds the locator strategies: By.NAME, By.CSS_SELECTOR and more. 1. Start the session. Chrome opens with a fresh profile. 2. Take action on the browser: go to the practice form. 3. Request browser information: the page title. 4. Establish a waiting strategy. Python takes seconds, so 0.5 means half a second. 5. Find an element by its name attribute. 5. Find the first button with a CSS selector. 6. Take action on an element: type into the box. 6. Click Submit. The browser loads the next page. Find the message on the new page. 7. Request element information: its visible text. 8. End the session. The browser and the driver both close. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Title: Web form Message:"],[2,"eight","The eight steps of a first script","Received! main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction firstScript() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');const title = await driver.getTitle(); console.log('Title: ' + title);await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit: 500 });const textBox = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));const submitButton = await driver.findElement(By.css('button'));await textBox.sendKeys('Selenium');await submitButton.click();const message = await driver.findElement(By.id('message'));const value = await message.getText(); console.log('Message: ' + value);await driver.quit();})(); Builder makes drivers, Browser names browsers, By builds locators. 1. Start the session. Chrome opens with a fresh profile. 2. Take action on the browser: go to the practice form. 3. Request browser information: the page title. 4. Establish a waiting strategy. JavaScript takes milliseconds. 5. Find an element by its name attribute. 5. Find the first button with a CSS selector. 6. Take action on an element: type into the box. 6. Click Submit. The browser loads the next page. Find the message on the new page. 7. Request element information: its visible text. 8. End the session. The browser and the driver both close. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Title: Web form Message: Received! Press Explain line by line and step through it. Notice that the code for steps 5 and 6 is the same idea in all three languages, only the spelling changes: findElement in Java and JavaScript, find_element in Python. Python measures waits in seconds, Java uses a Duration, and JavaScript uses milliseconds. Common mistake The documentation itself warns that an implicit wait is rarely the best solution. It is used here because it is the easiest to show. Chapter 4.1 replaces it with explicit waits."],[2,"eight","Try it yourself","The box below is a practice browser that runs inside this page. It does not use real Selenium, but it understands the same lines in all three languages and shows the W3C request that each line would send. Run it one line at a time, then change \"Selenium\" to your own name and run it again. Try it: the eight steps of a first scriptRun it one line at a time. Watch the request each line sends, then change the text that is typed. no session Lesson 4 of 4"],[2,"tidy","Always end the session","Step 8 is the one beginners forget. The documentation explains that quit ends the driver process, which by default closes the browser too, and that no more commands can be sent to that driver afterwards. If a test fails before it reaches quit, the browser and the driver keep running. Run a thousand failing tests and you have a thousand Chrome windows eating the memory of the build machine. The fix is to put quit where it always runs: in a finally block, or in Python inside a with block, which quits for you. Quit even when something fails AlwaysQuit.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.NoSuchElementException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassAlwaysQuit {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();try { driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\"); driver.findElement(By.id(\"no-such-id\"));System.out.println(\"never printed\"); } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {System.out.println(\"Failed: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]); } finally { driver.quit();System.out.println(\"Browser closed\"); } }} Start the session outside the try, so driver is known in finally. Everything that might fail goes inside try. This element does not exist, so this line throws. Java jumps straight to catch and never reaches this line. Catch the error so we can report it. Print only the first line of the message. finally runs whether the try worked or failed. So the browser always closes. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Failed: no such element: Unable to locate element: {\"method\":\"css selector\",\"selector\":\"#no\\-such\\-id\"} Browser closed main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importNoSuchElementExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBywith webdriver.Chrome() as driver: driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")try: driver.find_element(By.ID, \"no-such-id\")print(\"never printed\")exceptNoSuchElementExceptionas e:print(\"Failed:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])print(\"Browser closed\") A with block calls driver.quit() for you when the block ends, even after an error. This element does not exist, so this line raises. Catch the error so we can report it. e.msg is the message from the driver. Keep its first line. We are outside the with block, so quit has already run. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Failed: no such element: Unable to locate element: {\"method\":\"css selector\",\"selector\":\"[id=\"no-such-id\"]\"} Browser closed main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction alwaysQuit() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();try {await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');await driver.findElement(By.id('no-such-id')); console.log('never printed'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.NoSuchElementError)) throw e; console.log('Failed: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]); } finally {await driver.quit(); console.log('Browser closed'); }})(); error holds the exception classes. Everything that might fail goes inside try. This element does not exist, so the promise rejects. Only swallow the error we expected. Anything else is a real bug. finally runs whether the try worked or failed. So the browser always closes. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Failed: no such element: Unable to locate element: {\"method\":\"css selector\",\"selector\":\"*[id=\"no-such-id\"]\"} Browser closed quit close Closes Every window and tab of the session Only the current window or tab Driver process Stopped Keeps running while other windows are open After it The driver object cannot be used again Switch to another window to carry on Remember In a test framework the setup and teardown hooks do this job: start the driver before each test and quit it after each test, pass or fail. Chapter 5.1 shows the hooks in JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha. One idea, four"],[2,"tidy","Always end the session","depths Open the browser, do your steps, close the browser. Close it even if something goes wrong. Wrap your steps in try and finally, or use Python with. quit sends Delete Session, the driver closes the browser, and the binding stops the driver process. Driver processes and browser profiles are operating system resources. A suite that leaks them slows down and then fails with port or memory errors far from the real cause. One driver per test with guaranteed teardown keeps every test independent. The binding owns the driver process through a Service object. quit both ends the W3C session and stops the service. A remote session on a Grid has no local service, so there quit only ends the session and the Grid frees the slot. Chapter 6.1 covers session timeouts that clean up after clients that never quit."],[3,"","Sessions, options and timeouts","Options and capabilities, page load strategy, the three session timeouts and the driver service, shown with real runs. · session · options · capabilities · page load strategy · timeouts · service · headless · chromeoptions Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 2: Control the browser›Sessions and options Step 2 · Topic 1 of 2 · Beginner Describe the session you want with Options, see what the browser agrees to, choose when navigation returns, and know the three timeouts every session has. About 45 min4 lessons1 animation3 formulas6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Options and capabilities 2Page load strategy 3The three session timeouts 4The driver service Lesson 1 of 4"],[3,"options","Options and capabilities","When your code creates a driver, the binding sends New Session with a set of capabilities: the features you want the session to have. In Selenium 3 people wrote them into a DesiredCapabilities map. Since Selenium 4 you must use the browser Options classes: ChromeOptions, FirefoxOptions, EdgeOptions and SafariOptions. An Options object already knows its browserName, and it gives you named methods instead of string keys you could misspell. Real world scenario: one test suite, two needs On a laptop, a tester wants to watch Chrome click through the checkout. On the build server there is no screen, the window should be a fixed size so screenshots line up, and slow product images should not delay the tests. The test code is the same in both places. Only the Options object changes."],[3,"options","The standard W3C capabilities","Capability What it controls Default browserName Which browser. Set for you by each Options class. set by the class browserVersion Which version. Selenium Manager can download it if missing. whatever is installed platformName The operating system at the remote end. Matters most on Grid and in the cloud. the local OS pageLoadStrategy When navigation commands return: normal, eager or none. normal acceptInsecureCerts Trust expired or self signed TLS certificates. false timeouts Script, page load and implicit wait. 30 s, 300 s, 0 unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to an alert your code did not handle. dismiss and notify proxy Send browser traffic through a proxy server. none setWindowRect Whether the browser supports resizing and moving windows. reported by the driver strictFileInteractability Strict checks for hidden file inputs. off Anything browser specific carries a vendor prefix. Chrome options travel as goog:chromeOptions, Firefox as moz:firefoxOptions. That is how command line switches such as --headless=new reach the browser. Ask for a session with options, then read what the browser agreed to SessionCaps.java import org.openqa.selenium.Capabilities;import org.openqa.selenium.PageLoadStrategy;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;publicclassSessionCaps {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions(); options.addArguments(\"--headless=new\"); options.addArguments(\"--window-size=1280,800\"); options.setPageLoadStrategy(PageLoadStrategy.EAGER);ChromeDriver driver = newChromeDriver(options);Capabilities caps = driver.getCapabilities();System.out.println(\"browserName: \" + caps.getBrowserName());System.out.println(\"browserVersion: \" + caps.getBrowserVersion());System.out.println(\"platformName: \" + caps.getPlatformName());System.out.println(\"pageLoadStrategy: \" + caps.getCapability(\"pageLoadStrategy\"));System.out.println(\"unhandledPromptBehavior: \" + caps.getCapability(\"unhandledPromptBehavior\")); driver.quit(); }} An Options object describes the session you want. browserName is already set to chrome. A Chrome command line switch: run without a visible window. Another switch: the size of the browser window. A W3C capability: get() returns at DOMContentLoaded. New Session is sent with these capabilities. The capabilities the driver actually granted. The exact version of the browser that started. The operating system at the remote end. Our request was honoured. A default we did not set. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browserName: chrome browserVersion: 153.0.8010.12 platformName: linux pageLoadStrategy: eager unhandledPromptBehavior: dismiss and notify main.py from selenium import webdriveroptions = webdriver.ChromeOptions()options.add_argument(\"--headless=new\")options.add_argument(\"--window-size=1280,800\")options.page_load_strategy = \"eager\"driver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options)caps = driver.capabilitiesfor key in (\"browserName\", \"browserVersion\", \"platformName\", \"pageLoadStrategy\", \"unhandledPromptBehavior\"):print(key + \":\", caps[key])driver.quit() An Options object describes the session you want. browserName is already chrome. A Chrome command line switch: run without a visible window. Another switch: the size of the browser window. A W3C capability: get() returns at DOMContentLoaded. New Session is sent with these capabilities. A plain dict of what the driver actually granted. Print a few of the standard keys. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browserName: chrome browserVersion: 153.0.8010.12 platformName: linux pageLoadStrategy: eager unhandledPromptBehavior: dismiss and notify main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');(asyncfunction sessionCaps() {const options = new chrome.Options(); options.addArguments('--headless=new', '--window-size=1280,800'); options.setPageLoadStrategy('eager');const driver ="],[3,"options","The standard W3C capabilities","awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(options).build();const caps = await driver.getCapabilities();for (const key of ['browserName', 'browserVersion', 'platformName', 'pageLoadStrategy', 'unhandledPromptBehavior']) { console.log(key + ': ' + caps.get(key)); }await driver.quit();})(); The Chrome module holds chrome.Options. An Options object describes the session you want. Chrome command line switches: no window, and a fixed size. A W3C capability: get() returns at DOMContentLoaded. New Session is sent with these capabilities. The capabilities the driver actually granted. caps.get reads one capability by name. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browserName: chrome browserVersion: 153.0.8010.12 platformName: linux pageLoadStrategy: eager unhandledPromptBehavior: dismiss and notify The output shows the two sides of the agreement. You asked for eager, and the driver confirmed it. You did not mention prompts, and the driver filled in the W3C default dismiss and notify. Reading the granted capabilities is the quickest way to learn which browser version a test really ran on. Chrome switch What it does --headless=new No visible window. Common on build servers. --start-maximized Open the window maximised. --window-size=1280,800 A fixed window size, so pages lay out the same on every machine. --user-data-dir=... Use a chosen profile folder instead of a fresh temporary one. Remember Each Options class can also take the binary path of a Chromium based browser, extensions, and excludeSwitches to turn off a default switch of chromedriver. The documentation lists them on the Chrome page. Use them only when you need them, because every extra setting is one more difference from what real users run. Lesson 2 of 4"],[3,"loading","Page load strategy","When you call get, WebDriver does not return the moment the address is sent. It waits for the page to reach a document.readyState. Which state it waits for is the page load strategy. Strategy Waits until readyState is Browser event Use it when normal (default) complete load: every image, font and style sheet is in You want the safest behaviour eager interactive DOMContentLoaded: the HTML is parsed, images may still load Large images or ads slow the page and the test does not need them none any only the first download of the page You control every wait yourself The documentation adds two warnings. First, complete does not mean the page is finished. A single page application can keep loading content with JavaScript long after the load event. Second, the strategy applies only to navigation by address. A click that leads to a new page is not covered, so your waits must handle that. The strategy applies to the whole session, so pick it once, in the Options. Common mistake Switching to eager or none to make tests faster is a trade. Every element that arrives later now needs an explicit wait, or the test becomes flaky. Chapter 4.1 shows how to write those waits. Lesson 3 of 4"],[3,"timeouts","The three session timeouts","Every session has three timeouts. They are W3C capabilities, so every browser has the same ones with the same defaults. Implicit wait 0 How long Find Element keeps looking before it gives up with no such element. Zero means it checks once. Page loadExam favourite 300,000 ms Five minutes. How long a navigation may take before a timeout error. Script 30,000 ms Thirty seconds. How long an injected script may run before the script timeout error. Read the three session timeouts, then trip one on purpose SessionTimeouts.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.ScriptTimeoutException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassSessionTimeouts {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();WebDriver.Timeouts t = driver.manage().timeouts();System.out.println(\"implicit: \" + t.getImplicitWaitTimeout());System.out.println(\"pageLoad: \" + t.getPageLoadTimeout());System.out.println(\"script: \" + t.getScriptTimeout()); t.scriptTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1)); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\");try { ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeAsyncScript(\"/* never calls its callback */\"); } catch (ScriptTimeoutException e) {System.out.println(\"Caught \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName()); } finally { driver.quit(); } }} The timeouts of this session. Java prints a Duration. PT0S means zero seconds. PT5M is five minutes, which is 300,000 ms. PT30S is thirty seconds. Allow scripts only one second from now on. An async script must call its callback. This one never does. After one second the driver gives up with the W3C error script timeout. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) implicit: PT0S pageLoad: PT5M script: PT30S Caught ScriptTimeoutException main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importTimeoutExceptiondriver = webdriver.Chrome()t = driver.timeoutsprint(\"implicit:\", t.implicit_wait)print(\"pageLoad:\", t.page_load)print(\"script:\", t.script)driver.set_script_timeout(1)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")try: driver.execute_async_script(\"/* never calls its callback */\")exceptTimeoutExceptionas e:print(\"Caught\", type(e).__name__)finally: driver.quit() The timeouts of this session. Python reports them in seconds. 0: find_element does not wait at all unless you ask. 300 seconds, which is 300,000 ms. 30 seconds. Allow scripts only one second from now on. An async script must call its callback. This one never does. Python raises TimeoutException for the W3C error script timeout. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) implicit: 0.0 pageLoad: 300.0 script: 30.0 Caught TimeoutException main.js const { Builder, Browser, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction sessionTimeouts() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();try { console.log(await driver.manage().getTimeouts());await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ script: 1000 });await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');await driver.executeAsyncScript('/* never calls its callback */'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.ScriptTimeoutError)) throw e; console.log('Caught ' + e.name); } finally {await driver.quit(); }})(); One object with all three timeouts, in milliseconds. Allow scripts only one second from now on. An async script must call its callback. This one never does. The W3C error script timeout becomes ScriptTimeoutError. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) { implicit: 0, pageLoad: 300000, script: 30000 } Caught ScriptTimeoutError Look at how each language shows the same three numbers. Java prints ISO durations: PT0S, PT5M and PT30S. Python gives seconds. JavaScript gives milliseconds. The browser underneath stored exactly the same values. Language Set the page load timeout to 30 s Java driver.manage().timeouts().pageLoadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30))"],[3,"timeouts","The three session timeouts","Python driver.set_page_load_timeout(30) JavaScript await driver.manage().setTimeouts({{ pageLoad: 30000 }}) In the exam These timeouts belong to the browser session. Explicit waits such as WebDriverWait are different: they run in your code and poll the browser. Mixing an implicit wait with explicit waits can give surprising wait times, which chapter 4.1 explains. Lesson 4 of 4"],[3,"service","The driver service","A local session has two programs: the browser and the driver. The Service classes manage the driver program: where its file is, which port it listens on, which arguments it gets and where its logs go. The documentation notes that a Service is only for local drivers. A remote session on a Grid has no local driver to manage. Start the driver on a port you choose and ask if it is ready DriverService.java import java.net.URI;import java.net.http.HttpClient;import java.net.http.HttpRequest;import java.net.http.HttpResponse;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriverService;publicclassDriverService {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {ChromeDriverService service = newChromeDriverService.Builder().usingPort(9600).build();ChromeDriver driver = newChromeDriver(service);System.out.println(\"Driver URL: \" + service.getUrl());HttpRequest status = HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(\"http://localhost:9600/status\")).build();String body = HttpClient.newHttpClient().send(status, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()).body();System.out.println(\"ready: \" + body.contains(\"\\\"ready\\\":true\")); driver.quit(); }} A Service describes how to start chromedriver: here, on port 9600. Selenium starts chromedriver on that port, then opens a session. The address the binding sends its HTTP commands to. GET /status is a W3C command that needs no session. The driver says whether it can start new sessions. Ends the session and stops the service. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Driver URL: http://localhost:9600 ready: true main.py import jsonimport urllib.requestfrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.chrome.service importServiceservice = Service(port=9600)driver = webdriver.Chrome(service=service)print(\"Driver URL:\", service.service_url)with urllib.request.urlopen(\"http://localhost:9600/status\") as reply: status = json.load(reply)[\"value\"]print(\"ready:\", status[\"ready\"])driver.quit() A Service describes how to start chromedriver: here, on port 9600. Selenium starts chromedriver on that port, then opens a session. The address the binding sends its HTTP commands to. GET /status is a W3C command that needs no session. The driver says whether it can start new sessions. Ends the session and stops the service. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Driver URL: http://localhost:9600 ready: True main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');(asyncfunction driverService() {const service = new chrome.ServiceBuilder().setPort(9600);const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeService(service).build(); console.log('Driver URL: http://localhost:9600');const reply = await fetch('http://localhost:9600/status');const status = (await reply.json()).value; console.log('ready: ' + status.ready);await driver.quit();})(); A ServiceBuilder describes how to start chromedriver: here, on port 9600. Selenium starts chromedriver on that port, then opens a session. GET /status is a W3C command that needs no session. fetch is built into Node. The driver says whether it can start new sessions. Ends the session and stops the service. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) Driver URL: http://localhost:9600 ready: true The example also uses GET /status, a W3C command that needs no session. It tells you whether the driver can start a new session. Grid uses the same idea to check its nodes, as chapter 6.1 shows. Service setting Why you would change it Driver location Only with Selenium older than 4.6 or a special setup. Otherwise let Selenium Manager find it. Port A firewall allows only certain ports, or you want to talk to the driver yourself. Log output and level Driver logs are thrown away unless you send them somewhere. Levels are ALL, DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, SEVERE and OFF. Arguments Pass chromedriver switches, such as appending to a log file."],[3,"service","The driver service","OptionsWhat session you want: browser, version, strategy, timeouts, switches. ServiceHow to start the local driver: file, port, logs. Driver classnew ChromeDriver(service, options) puts both together and sends New Session. One idea, four depths Options say what kind of browser you want. Service says how to start the little driver program. The driver class uses both. Options become the capabilities of New Session. Service starts chromedriver on a port and the binding sends HTTP there. For a remote session you pass a URL instead of a Service. Capability matching happens in two passes in W3C: alwaysMatch must hold, and one of the firstMatch entries is chosen. The Python binding, for example, sends your Options as alwaysMatch next to an empty firstMatch, as the real log in chapter 1.2 shows. The granted capabilities are the truth about the session, which is why logging them helps when a test passes locally and fails in CI. The intermediary design means a Grid Router accepts New Session, picks a node whose stereotype matches the capabilities, and forwards the request. Chapter 6.1 follows that path, including the se: prefixed capabilities Grid adds."],[4,"","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","Navigation and history, title and URL, window size and position, screenshots and printing pages to PDF. · navigation · back · forward · refresh · title · current url · window size · screenshot · print · pdf Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 2: Control the browser›Navigate and capture Step 2 · Topic 2 of 2 · Beginner Move around like a person with the toolbar buttons, read where you are, size the window for each layout, and capture what the browser shows as PNG or PDF. About 40 min4 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Navigate and ask where you are 2Window size and position 3Screenshots 4Print a page to PDF Lesson 1 of 4"],[4,"navigate","Navigate and ask where you are","Four commands move a tab around, and they match the buttons a person uses: the address bar, Back, Forward and Reload. Two more questions tell you where you ended up: the title and the current URL. What you want Java Python JavaScript Open an address driver.get(url) or navigate().to(url) driver.get(url) driver.get(url) or navigate().to(url) Back navigate().back() driver.back() navigate().back() Forward navigate().forward() driver.forward() navigate().forward() Reload navigate().refresh() driver.refresh() navigate().refresh() Page title getTitle() driver.title getTitle() Current address getCurrentUrl() driver.current_url getCurrentUrl() Go somewhere, go back, go forward, reload Navigate.java import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassNavigate {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");System.out.println(\"1 \" + driver.getTitle()); driver.navigate().to(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html\");System.out.println(\"2 \" + driver.getTitle() + \" at \" + driver.getCurrentUrl()); driver.navigate().back();System.out.println(\"3 \" + driver.getTitle()); driver.navigate().forward();System.out.println(\"4 \" + driver.getTitle()); driver.navigate().refresh();System.out.println(\"5 \" + driver.getTitle()); driver.quit(); }} Navigate To. The short form of driver.navigate().to(...). The long form does the same thing. Title and address of the page now showing. Press the Back button. Press the Forward button. Reload the current page. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) 1 Web form 2 XHTML Test Page at https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html 3 Web form 4 XHTML Test Page 5 XHTML Test Page main.py from selenium import webdriverdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")print(\"1\", driver.title)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html\")print(\"2\", driver.title, \"at\", driver.current_url)driver.back()print(\"3\", driver.title)driver.forward()print(\"4\", driver.title)driver.refresh()print(\"5\", driver.title)driver.quit() Navigate To. Waits for the page load strategy. A second page. History now holds two entries. Title and address of the page now showing. Press the Back button. Press the Forward button. Reload the current page. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) 1 Web form 2 XHTML Test Page at https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html 3 Web form 4 XHTML Test Page 5 XHTML Test Page main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction navigate() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html'); console.log('1 ' + await driver.getTitle());await driver.navigate().to('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html'); console.log('2 ' + await driver.getTitle() + ' at ' + await driver.getCurrentUrl());await driver.navigate().back(); console.log('3 ' + await driver.getTitle());await driver.navigate().forward(); console.log('4 ' + await driver.getTitle());await driver.navigate().refresh(); console.log('5 ' + await driver.getTitle());await driver.quit();})(); Navigate To. Waits for the page load strategy. The long form of get. Press the Back button. Press the Forward button. Reload the current page. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) 1 Web form 2 XHTML Test Page at https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html 3 Web form 4 XHTML Test Page 5 XHTML Test Page Back, forward and the history of a tabPick a plan and step through it. The highlighted card is the page the browser shows. Step 0 of 7 Plan Show the code in Showing home, with 0 pages behind and 0 ahead. Try this In the shopping trip, watch what happens to product when you go back and then get basket. A new get cuts off the pages ahead. Pressing too far: back on"],[4,"navigate","Navigate and ask where you are","the first page does nothing and raises no error. Check the title if your test depends on it. History works the way it does for a person. A new get after going back throws away the pages that were ahead. Back on the first page does nothing at all, and raises no error, so a test that needs to know where it is should check the title or the URL. Real world scenario: a login redirect A test opens the account page of a shop. The shop is not logged in, so it redirects to the login page. The test checks current_url and sees /login?next=/account. After logging in it expects to land back on /account. Reading the address after each step is how the test proves the redirect works. Common mistake After refresh or any navigation, the elements you found before belong to the old page. Using them raises stale element reference. Find them again on the new page. Chapter 5.4 covers this error in depth. Lesson 2 of 4"],[4,"windowsize","Window size and position","A page can look different at different sizes. A menu turns into a hamburger button on a narrow screen, and a table hides columns. The documentation reminds us that screen resolution can change how your application renders, so WebDriver lets you set the size and position of the window. Set the window size and position, then read them back WindowRect.java import org.openqa.selenium.Dimension;import org.openqa.selenium.Point;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassWindowRect {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();WebDriver.Window window = driver.manage().window(); window.setSize(newDimension(1024, 768)); window.setPosition(newPoint(40, 30));Dimension size = window.getSize();Point where = window.getPosition();System.out.println(\"size \" + size.getWidth() + \" x \" + size.getHeight());System.out.println(\"position \" + where.getX() + \", \" + where.getY()); driver.quit(); }} Every window command lives under manage().window(). Restore the window and make it 1024 by 768 pixels. Move its top left corner to x 40, y 30 on the screen. Ask the browser for the size it really has. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) size 1024 x 768 position 40, 30 main.py from selenium import webdriverdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.set_window_size(1024, 768)driver.set_window_position(40, 30)size = driver.get_window_size()where = driver.get_window_position()print(\"size\", size[\"width\"], \"x\", size[\"height\"])print(\"position\", str(where[\"x\"]) + \",\", where[\"y\"])print(\"rect\", driver.get_window_rect())driver.quit() Restore the window and make it 1024 by 768 pixels. Move its top left corner to x 40, y 30 on the screen. A dict with width and height. A dict with x and y. One W3C command, Get Window Rect, returns all four numbers at once. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) size 1024 x 768 position 40, 30 rect {'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'x': 40, 'y': 30} main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction windowRect() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();const window = driver.manage().window();await window.setRect({ width: 1024, height: 768, x: 40, y: 30 });const { width, height, x, y } = await window.getRect(); console.log('size ' + width + ' x ' + height); console.log('position ' + x + ', ' + y);await driver.quit();})(); Window commands live under manage().window(). JavaScript sets size and position in one call to Set Window Rect. Get Window Rect returns all four numbers. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) size 1024 x 768 position 40, 30 Command What it does Set window size Restores the window if it was maximised, then sets width and height in pixels Set window position Moves the top left corner to x and y on the screen Get window rect Width, height, x and y in one answer Maximize Makes the window fill the screen, without covering the system menus Minimize Hides the window, usually to the taskbar. The exact result depends on the window manager. Selenium 4 and later. Fullscreen Fills the whole screen, like pressing F11 Common mistake Maximize and minimize need a desktop window manager. On a bare Linux build server they can fail with an unknown error, which is what happened when this page was built. Setting an exact size with Set Window Rect or the --window-size switch works everywhere and gives the same layout on every machine. Real world scenario: testing the mobile menu A news site shows a full menu above 768 pixels and a hamburger button below. One test sets the window to 1280 by 800 and checks the full menu. Another sets it to 375 by 800 and checks that the hamburger opens the menu. Same page, two layouts, two sizes. Lesson 3 of 4"],[4,"screenshots","Screenshots","A screenshot is the most useful thing a failed test can leave behind. WebDriver has two commands: Take Screenshot for the visible part of the page and Take Element Screenshot for one element. Both return a PNG encoded as base64 text, and each binding gives you helpers to save it. Save a screenshot of the page and of one element Screenshots.java import java.io.File;import java.nio.file.Files;import java.nio.file.Path;import java.awt.image.BufferedImage;import javax.imageio.ImageIO;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.Dimension;import org.openqa.selenium.OutputType;import org.openqa.selenium.TakesScreenshot;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassScreenshots {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.manage().window().setSize(newDimension(1024, 768)); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");File page = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);Files.copy(page.toPath(), Path.of(\"page.png\"));WebElement button = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\"));File only = button.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);Files.copy(only.toPath(), Path.of(\"button.png\"));for (String name : newString[] {\"page.png\", \"button.png\"}) {BufferedImage img = ImageIO.read(newFile(name));System.out.println(name + \": \" + img.getWidth() + \" x \" + img.getHeight()); } driver.quit(); }} Take Screenshot of the viewport. Selenium writes a temporary PNG file. Copy it somewhere you will find it. Take Element Screenshot: just the area of the button. Open the PNG to read its size in pixels. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page.png: 1024 x 625 button.png: 79 x 38 main.py import structfrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydef png_size(path):with open(path, \"rb\") as f:return struct.unpack(\">II\", f.read(24)[16:24])driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.set_window_size(1024, 768)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")driver.save_screenshot(\"page.png\")button = driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\")button.screenshot(\"button.png\")for name in (\"page.png\", \"button.png\"): w, h = png_size(name)print(f\"{name}: {w} x {h}\")driver.quit() A PNG keeps its width and height at bytes 16 to 24 of the file. Take Screenshot of the viewport and save it as a PNG. Take Element Screenshot: just the area of the button. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page.png: 1024 x 625 button.png: 79 x 38 main.js const fs = require('fs');const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');const pngSize = file => { const b = fs.readFileSync(file); return [b.readUInt32BE(16), b.readUInt32BE(20)]; };(asyncfunction screenshots() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.manage().window().setRect({ width: 1024, height: 768 });await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');const page = await driver.takeScreenshot(); fs.writeFileSync('page.png', page, 'base64');const button = await driver.findElement(By.css('button')); fs.writeFileSync('button.png', await button.takeScreenshot(), 'base64');for (const name of ['page.png', 'button.png']) {const [w, h] = pngSize(name); console.log(`${name}: ${w} x ${h}`); }await driver.quit();})(); A PNG keeps width and height at bytes 16 and 20. Take Screenshot answers with the PNG as base64 text. Decode the base64 and write the bytes to a file. Take Element Screenshot: just the area of the button. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page.png: 1024 x 625 button.png: 79 x 38 The page screenshot is the size of the viewport, the part of the window that shows the page. Here it is 1024 by 625, smaller than the 1024 by 768 window, because the window also holds the tab strip and toolbar. The button screenshot is cut to the button. Language Page screenshot to file"],[4,"screenshots","Screenshots","Element screenshot to file Java ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE) element.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE) Python driver.save_screenshot(\"page.png\") element.screenshot(\"el.png\") JavaScript driver.takeScreenshot() gives base64 element.takeScreenshot() gives base64 Remember Take a screenshot in the teardown of a failing test, before quit. Name the file after the test, so the report can link to it. Chapter 5.1 wires this into JUnit, pytest and Mocha. Lesson 4 of 4"],[4,"print","Print a page to PDF","The W3C Print Page command turns the current page into a PDF. You describe the paper with PrintOptions and get the PDF back as base64. The documentation notes that Chromium browsers need headless mode for this. Print the page to a PDF file PrintPdf.java import java.nio.file.Files;import java.nio.file.Path;import java.util.Base64;import org.openqa.selenium.Pdf;import org.openqa.selenium.PrintsPage;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;import org.openqa.selenium.print.PrintOptions;publicclassPrintPdf {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions().addArguments(\"--headless=new\");ChromeDriver driver = newChromeDriver(options); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");PrintOptions print = newPrintOptions(); print.setOrientation(PrintOptions.Orientation.LANDSCAPE);Pdf pdf = ((PrintsPage) driver).print(print);byte[] bytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(pdf.getContent());Files.write(Path.of(\"page.pdf\"), bytes);System.out.println(\"page.pdf starts with \" + newString(bytes, 0, 5)); driver.quit(); }} Chromium prints to PDF only in headless mode. Paper settings: orientation, page ranges, size, margins, scale, background. The W3C Print Page command. The PDF comes back as base64 text. Decode it to the real bytes. Every PDF file starts with %PDF-. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page.pdf starts with %PDF- main.py import base64from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.print_page_options importPrintOptionsoptions = webdriver.ChromeOptions()options.add_argument(\"--headless=new\")driver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")print_options = PrintOptions()print_options.orientation = \"landscape\"pdf = base64.b64decode(driver.print_page(print_options))with open(\"page.pdf\", \"wb\") as f: f.write(pdf)print(\"page.pdf starts with\", pdf[:5].decode())driver.quit() Chromium prints to PDF only in headless mode. Paper settings: orientation, page ranges, size, margins, scale, background. The W3C Print Page command answers with base64, so decode it. Every PDF file starts with %PDF-. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page.pdf starts with %PDF- main.js const fs = require('fs');const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');(asyncfunction printPdf() {const options = new chrome.Options().addArguments('--headless=new');const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(options).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');const base64 = await driver.printPage({ orientation: 'landscape' });const pdf = Buffer.from(base64, 'base64'); fs.writeFileSync('page.pdf', pdf); console.log('page.pdf starts with ' + pdf.subarray(0, 5).toString());await driver.quit();})(); Chromium prints to PDF only in headless mode. The W3C Print Page command answers with base64 text. Decode it to the real bytes. Every PDF file starts with %PDF-. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page.pdf starts with %PDF- PrintOptions setting Example Orientation portrait or landscape Page ranges \"2-4\" Page size A4, Legal and others Margins top, bottom, left, right Scale 1.0 is 100 %, 0.25 is 25 % Background whether background colours and images print Shrink to fit whether wide content is shrunk to the page Real world scenario: archiving invoices An accounts team must keep a copy of every invoice page the system shows on the last day of each month. A scheduled Selenium job logs in, opens each invoice and prints it to PDF in landscape with backgrounds on. No person clicks Print a hundred times. One idea, four depths You can tell the browser where to go, ask where it is, change its size, take a picture and print the page, all from code. Each of these is one W3C command: Navigate To, Back, Forward, Refresh, Get"],[4,"print","Print a page to PDF","Title, Get Current URL, Set Window Rect, Take Screenshot and Print Page. Images and PDFs travel as base64 text inside the JSON answer. Navigation commands wait according to the page load strategy, but history and refresh can change the page under any element references you hold. Screenshots show the viewport only, so full page captures need a browser specific feature, such as Firefox full page screenshots. WebDriver BiDi adds a browsingContext module with navigate, print and captureScreenshot commands that can also report navigation events as they happen. Chapter 6.2 introduces BiDi."],[5,"","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","All eight locator strategies, CSS and XPath, relative locators and how to choose a locator that lasts. · locators · by · css selector · xpath · id · name · class name · relative locators · link text · tag name Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 3: Find and use elements›Locators Step 3 · Topic 1 of 3 · Beginner Learn all eight locator strategies on one page, read and write CSS selectors and XPath, place elements with relative locators, and pick locators that survive the next redesign. About 50 min4 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1The eight locator strategies 2CSS selectors and XPath 3Relative locators 4Choosing a good locator Lesson 1 of 4"],[5,"strategies","The eight locator strategies","Before Selenium can click or type, it must find the element. A locator is the description you give it: \"the input whose id is lname\", \"the first link that says Official\". The documentation lists eight traditional strategies, all shown on its Contact Selenium example page, which this chapter uses too. locators.html (shortened) <h2>ContactSelenium</h2><form> <input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"m\" />Male <input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"f\" />Female <label for=\"fname\">First name:</label> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"fname\" name=\"fname\" value=\"Jane\"> <label for=\"lname\">Last name:</label> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"lname\" name=\"lname\" value=\"Doe\"> <label for=\"newsletter\">Newsletter:</label> <input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"newsletter\" value=\"1\" /> <input type=\"submit\" value=\"Submit\"></form><p>... <a href=\"https://www.selenium.dev/\">SeleniumOfficialPage</a></p> Strategy Finds Example id The element whose id attribute matches lname name Elements whose name attribute matches newsletter class name Elements whose class list contains the value. Compound names such as \"a b\" are not allowed. information css selector Elements matching a CSS selector #fname xpath Elements matching an XPath expression //input[@value='f'] tag name Elements with that tag a link text Links whose visible text is exactly the value Selenium Official Page partial link text Links whose visible text contains the value Official All eight locator strategies on the documentation example page EightLocators.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassEightLocators {staticvoid show(String how, WebElement e) {System.out.println(how + \" -> <\" + e.getTagName() + \"> \" + e.getAttribute(\"value\") + \" \" + e.getText()); }publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\"); show(\"className\", driver.findElement(By.className(\"information\"))); show(\"cssSelector\", driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"#fname\"))); show(\"id\", driver.findElement(By.id(\"lname\"))); show(\"name\", driver.findElement(By.name(\"newsletter\"))); show(\"linkText\", driver.findElement(By.linkText(\"Selenium Official Page\"))); show(\"partialLinkText\", driver.findElement(By.partialLinkText(\"Official\"))); show(\"tagName\", driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"a\"))); show(\"xpath\", driver.findElement(By.xpath(\"//input[@value='f']\"))); driver.quit(); }} A helper that prints which element a locator found. The first element whose class list contains information. CSS: # means id, so the input with id fname. The element whose id attribute is lname. The element whose name attribute is newsletter. A link whose whole visible text matches. A link whose visible text contains Official. The first a element on the page. XPath: any input whose value attribute is f. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) className -> <input> Jane cssSelector -> <input> Jane id -> <input> Doe name -> <input> 1 linkText -> <a> null Selenium Official Page partialLinkText -> <a> null Selenium Official Page tagName -> <a> null Selenium Official Page xpath -> <input> f main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydef show(how, e):print(how, \"-> <\" + e.tag_name + \">\", e.get_attribute(\"value\"), e.text)driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\")show(\"CLASS_NAME\", driver.find_element(By.CLASS_NAME, \"information\"))show(\"CSS_SELECTOR\", driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"#fname\"))show(\"ID\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"lname\"))show(\"NAME\", driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"newsletter\"))show(\"LINK_TEXT\", driver.find_element(By.LINK_TEXT, \"Selenium Official Page\"))show(\"PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT\", driver.find_element(By.PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT, \"Official\"))show(\"TAG_NAME\", driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME,"],[5,"strategies","The eight locator strategies","\"a\"))show(\"XPATH\", driver.find_element(By.XPATH, \"//input[@value='f']\"))driver.quit() A helper that prints which element a locator found. The first element whose class list contains information. CSS: # means id, so the input with id fname. The element whose id attribute is lname. The element whose name attribute is newsletter. A link whose whole visible text matches. A link whose visible text contains Official. The first a element on the page. XPath: any input whose value attribute is f. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) CLASS_NAME -> <input> Jane CSS_SELECTOR -> <input> Jane ID -> <input> Doe NAME -> <input> 1 LINK_TEXT -> <a> None Selenium Official Page PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT -> <a> None Selenium Official Page TAG_NAME -> <a> None Selenium Official Page XPATH -> <input> f main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');asyncfunction show(how, e) { console.log(how + ' -> <' + await e.getTagName() + '> ' + await e.getAttribute('value') + ' ' + await e.getText());}(asyncfunction eightLocators() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html');await show('className', await driver.findElement(By.className('information')));await show('css', await driver.findElement(By.css('#fname')));await show('id', await driver.findElement(By.id('lname')));await show('name', await driver.findElement(By.name('newsletter')));await show('linkText', await driver.findElement(By.linkText('Selenium Official Page')));await show('partialLinkText', await driver.findElement(By.partialLinkText('Official')));await show('tagName', await driver.findElement(By.tagName('a')));await show('xpath', await driver.findElement(By.xpath(\"//input[@value='f']\")));await driver.quit();})(); A helper that prints which element a locator found. The first element whose class list contains information. CSS: # means id, so the input with id fname. The element whose id attribute is lname. The element whose name attribute is newsletter. A link whose whole visible text matches. A link whose visible text contains Official. The first a element on the page. XPath: any input whose value attribute is f. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) className -> <input> Jane css -> <input> Jane id -> <input> Doe name -> <input> 1 linkText -> <a> null Selenium Official Page partialLinkText -> <a> null Selenium Official Page tagName -> <a> null Selenium Official Page xpath -> <input> f Each line asked for one element and got the first match. class name information matches two inputs, Jane and Doe, and returned Jane because it comes first in the page. Try every locator strategy on one pagePick a strategy and type a value. Every element that matches lights up, and the first one is what find element returns. Strategy Value 1 element match. Try this class name \"information\" matches two inputs. find element returns the first, First name, and find elements returns both. Try tag name \"input\". Seven matches: the documentation calls tag name dangerous for a single element for this reason. Try class name \"information extra\". Compound class names are not allowed. Use the css selector .information.extra instead. An XPath with text(): //label[text()=\"Last name:\"]. XPath can match on text, CSS cannot. id \"email\" matches nothing. find element raises no such element, find elements returns an empty list."],[5,"strategies","Only five reach the browser","The W3C protocol knows five strategies: css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath. So where do id, name and class name go? The binding turns them into CSS before sending. You saw it in chapter 1.2: Python sent [name=\"my-text\"] for By.NAME. Python turns id into [id=\"lname\"] and class name into .information. Remember find element (singular) returns the first match or raises no such element. find elements (plural) returns every match in page order, or an empty list. Chapter 3.2 covers the difference in detail. Lesson 2 of 4"],[5,"cssxpath","CSS selectors and XPath","id and name only work when the page has them. For everything else you write a CSS selector or an XPath. Both describe a path through the page, in two different languages. To find CSS selector XPath By id #fname //*[@id=\"fname\"] By class .information //*[contains(@class,\"information\")] Tag with attribute input[name=\"fname\"] //input[@name=\"fname\"] Attribute starts with a[href^=\"https\"] //a[starts-with(@href,\"https\")] Attribute contains a[href*=\"selenium\"] //a[contains(@href,\"selenium\")] Direct child form > input //form/input Any descendant form input //form//input Second matching input input:nth-of-type(2) (//input)[2] By visible text not possible //label[text()=\"Last name:\"] The parent not possible //input[@id=\"lname\"]/.. Next sibling label + input //label[@for=\"lname\"]/following-sibling::input[1] Read a CSS selector from left to right form: start at a form element. >: go to its direct children only. input: keep the children that are inputs. [type=\"text\"]: keep the ones whose type attribute is text. So form > input[type=\"text\"] finds the First name and Last name boxes. XPath has two forms. An absolute path starts at the root, such as /html/body/form/input[1]. It breaks as soon as anyone adds a div. A relative path starts with // and searches anywhere, such as //input[@name=\"fname\"]. Use relative paths. Open the playground, change any locator and run it. The element found is outlined on the page, and the log shows exactly what was sent to the driver. Try it: find elements with every locatorChange a locator and run it again. The element found is outlined in orange on the page. no session Going deeper: How to test a selector before you use it Open the page in Chrome, press F12 and go to the Console. document.querySelectorAll(\"form > input\") lists what a CSS selector matches. $x(\"//label[text()='Last name:']\") does the same for XPath. In the Elements panel, Ctrl+F also accepts both. If the browser finds it, Selenium will too, because the driver asks the same browser. Lesson 3 of 4"],[5,"relative","Relative locators","Selenium 4 added relative locators, first called friendly locators. They help when an element is hard to describe but easy to place: \"the input below the Last name label\". The documentation explains that Selenium uses the JavaScript function getBoundingClientRect() to learn the size and position of elements, then picks neighbours. Method Finds an element that is Note above higher on the page than the anchor below lower on the page than the anchor toLeftOf / to_left_of left of the anchor toRightOf / to_right_of right of the anchor near at most 50 pixels from the anchor Useful for an input next to its label Relative locators: find an element by where it sits Relative.java importstatic org.openqa.selenium.support.locators.RelativeLocator.with;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassRelative {staticvoid show(String how, WebElement e) {System.out.println(how + \" -> \" + e.getAttribute(\"outerHTML\")); }publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\"); show(\"below last name label\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).below(By.cssSelector(\"label[for='lname']\")))); show(\"above lname\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).above(By.id(\"lname\")))); show(\"right of Male\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).toRightOf(By.cssSelector(\"input[value='m']\")))); show(\"below fname and above the checkbox\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).below(By.id(\"fname\")).above(By.name(\"newsletter\")))); show(\"near Newsletter label\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).near(By.cssSelector(\"label[for='newsletter']\")))); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html\"); show(\"left of password\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).toLeftOf(By.id(\"password-field\")))); driver.quit(); }} with() starts a relative locator. outerHTML shows the whole tag that was found. An input below the Last name label. The closest one wins. An input above the lname box. An input to the right of the Male radio. Chained: both conditions must hold. near means at most 50 pixels away. Look at what it really returned. On the login page the three inputs sit in one row. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) below last name label -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"lname\" name=\"lname\" value=\"Doe\"> above lname -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"fname\" name=\"fname\" value=\"Jane\"> right of Male -> <input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"f\"> below fname and above the checkbox -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"lname\" name=\"lname\" value=\"Doe\"> near Newsletter label -> <input type=\"submit\" value=\"Submit\"> left of password -> <input type=\"text\" name=\"username\" id=\"username-field\" class=\"login-form-field\" placeholder=\"Username\"> main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support.relative_locator import locate_withdef show(how, e):print(how, \"->\", e.get_attribute(\"outerHTML\"))driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\")show(\"below last name label\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").below({By.CSS_SELECTOR: \"label[for='lname']\"})))show(\"above lname\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").above({By.ID: \"lname\"})))show(\"right of Male\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").to_right_of({By.CSS_SELECTOR: \"input[value='m']\"})))show(\"below fname and above the checkbox\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").below({By.ID: \"fname\"}).above({By.NAME: \"newsletter\"})))show(\"near Newsletter label\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").near({By.CSS_SELECTOR: \"label[for='newsletter']\"})))driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html\")show(\"left of"],[5,"relative","Relative locators","password\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").to_left_of({By.ID: \"password-field\"})))driver.quit() locate_with() starts a relative locator. outerHTML shows the whole tag that was found. Python writes the anchor locator as a small dict. An input above the lname box. An input to the right of the Male radio. Chained: both conditions must hold. near means at most 50 pixels away. Look at what it really returned. On the login page the three inputs sit in one row. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) below last name label -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"lname\" name=\"lname\" value=\"Doe\"> above lname -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"fname\" name=\"fname\" value=\"Jane\"> right of Male -> <input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"f\"> below fname and above the checkbox -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"lname\" name=\"lname\" value=\"Doe\"> near Newsletter label -> <input type=\"submit\" value=\"Submit\"> left of password -> <input type=\"text\" name=\"username\" id=\"username-field\" class=\"login-form-field\" placeholder=\"Username\"> main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, locateWith } = require('selenium-webdriver');asyncfunction show(how, e) { console.log(how + ' -> ' + await e.getAttribute('outerHTML'));}(asyncfunction relative() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html');await show('below last name label', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).below(By.css(\"label[for='lname']\"))));await show('above lname', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).above(By.id('lname'))));await show('right of Male', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).toRightOf(By.css(\"input[value='m']\"))));await show('below fname and above the checkbox', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).below(By.id('fname')).above(By.name('newsletter'))));await show('near Newsletter label', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).near(By.css(\"label[for='newsletter']\"))));await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html');await show('left of password', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).toLeftOf(By.id('password-field'))));await driver.quit();})(); locateWith() starts a relative locator. outerHTML shows the whole tag that was found. An input below the Last name label. The closest one wins. An input above the lname box. An input to the right of the Male radio. Chained: both conditions must hold. near means at most 50 pixels away. Look at what it really returned. On the login page the three inputs sit in one row. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) below last name label -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"lname\" name=\"lname\" value=\"Doe\"> above lname -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"fname\" name=\"fname\" value=\"Jane\"> right of Male -> <input type=\"radio\" name=\"gender\" value=\"f\"> below fname and above the checkbox -> <input class=\"information\" type=\"text\" id=\"lname\" name=\"lname\" value=\"Doe\"> near Newsletter label -> <input type=\"submit\" value=\"Submit\"> left of password -> <input type=\"text\" name=\"username\" id=\"username-field\" class=\"login-form-field\" placeholder=\"Username\"> Look closely at the near line. You might expect the Newsletter checkbox, which sits right next to its label. The real run returned the Submit button. The relative locator code in the bindings explains why. near keeps every candidate whose box overlaps the anchor box grown by 50 pixels on each side, and both the checkbox and the button qualify. The candidates are then sorted by the distance between their centre and the centre of the anchor. The label text is wide, so its centre is closer to the Submit button underneath than to the checkbox at its right end. That is the honest lesson about relative locators: they depend on layout, so a different window size or a CSS change can"],[5,"relative","Relative locators","change the answer. Use them when nothing better exists, and check what they return. Common mistake Each relative locator sends the page a script to run, so it is slower than a plain CSS selector. Prefer an id or a short CSS selector, and keep relative locators for elements with no good attributes. Lesson 4 of 4"],[5,"choose","Choosing a good locator","A test is only as stable as its locators. The encouraged practices page of the documentation gives an order of preference. From best to last resort A unique, stable id. Fast, short and clear. Preferred whenever the id is unique and predictable. A well written CSS selector. The preferred choice when there is no good id. XPath. Works as well as CSS and can match text, but the syntax is harder to debug and browsers do not tune it for speed. Link text and partial link text. Only work on links. Tag name. Dangerous for a single element, because pages have many of each tag. Useful with find elements. Fragile locator Why it breaks Better /html/body/div[2]/form/input[3] Any new div or input shifts the path #lname .btn.btn-primary.mt-3 Styling classes change when the design changes [data-test=\"submit\"] //button[text()=\"Submit\"] Breaks when the text is translated or reworded An id or a data attribute #ember1234 Generated ids change on every page load A stable attribute near it Real world scenario: a test id agreement A team keeps breaking tests whenever designers restyle buttons. They agree with the developers that every element a test touches gets a data-test attribute, such as data-test=\"checkout-pay\". Tests use [data-test=\"checkout-pay\"]. Designers can now change classes and text freely, and the tests keep passing. In the exam Keep locators in one place, apart from the steps that use them. The documentation recommends declaring them separately, which is exactly what a page object does. Chapter 5.2 shows the pattern. One idea, four depths A locator is directions to an element. Short, unique directions that do not depend on looks are the best. id first, then CSS, then XPath. Avoid long absolute paths and styling classes. Relative locators help when an element has no good attribute. id, name and class name compile to CSS in the bindings, so all of them run on the browser selector engine. Narrowing the scope, for example finding inside one form, cuts the work the browser does and the chance of a wrong match. Locator stability is a contract between test and product code. Dedicated test attributes make that contract explicit and survive redesigns, translation and framework changes that rename generated ids."],[6,"","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","Singular and plural finds, scoped searches and the XPath trap, shadow DOM and the active element. · find element · find elements · search context · shadow dom · shadow root · active element · xpath scope · nosuchelement Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 3: Find and use elements›Finding elements Step 3 · Topic 2 of 3 · Beginner Know exactly what find element and find elements return, search inside one part of a page without falling into the XPath trap, reach into shadow DOM components and find the focused element. About 40 min4 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Find one or find many 2Search inside an element 3The shadow DOM 4The active element Lesson 1 of 4"],[6,"oneormany","Find one or find many","Every Selenium binding has two find methods, and their only difference is how many results they return and what they do when nothing matches. find element (singular) find elements (plural) Returns One element: the first match in page order A list of every match, in page order No match Raises no such element Returns an empty list W3C command Find Element Find Elements Use it for An element that must be there Counting, looping, or checking that something is absent One element, all elements, and what happens when nothing matches FindAll.java import java.util.List;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.NoSuchElementException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassFindAll {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\");List<WebElement> inputs = driver.findElements(By.tagName(\"input\"));System.out.println(\"inputs: \" + inputs.size());for (WebElement e : inputs) {System.out.println(\" \" + e.getAttribute(\"type\") + \" \" + e.getAttribute(\"name\")); }List<WebElement> none = driver.findElements(By.id(\"email\"));System.out.println(\"email boxes: \" + none.size());try { driver.findElement(By.id(\"email\")); } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {System.out.println(\"findElement: NoSuchElementException\"); } driver.quit(); }} Plural: a List of every input, in page order. Walk the list like any Java list. No match: an empty list, never an error. Singular with no match: an exception. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) inputs: 6 radio gender radio gender text fname text lname checkbox newsletter submit email boxes: 0 findElement: NoSuchElementException main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importNoSuchElementExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\")inputs = driver.find_elements(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\")print(\"inputs:\", len(inputs))for e in inputs:print(\" \", e.get_attribute(\"type\"), e.get_attribute(\"name\"))none = driver.find_elements(By.ID, \"email\")print(\"email boxes:\", len(none))try: driver.find_element(By.ID, \"email\")exceptNoSuchElementException:print(\"find_element: NoSuchElementException\")driver.quit() Plural: a list of every input, in page order. Walk the list like any Python list. No match: an empty list, never an error. Singular with no match: an exception. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) inputs: 6 radio gender radio gender text fname text lname checkbox newsletter submit email boxes: 0 find_element: NoSuchElementException main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction findAll() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html');const inputs = await driver.findElements(By.tagName('input')); console.log('inputs: ' + inputs.length);for (const e of inputs) { console.log(' ' + await e.getAttribute('type') + ' ' + await e.getAttribute('name')); }const none = await driver.findElements(By.id('email')); console.log('email boxes: ' + none.length);try {await driver.findElement(By.id('email')); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.NoSuchElementError)) throw e; console.log('findElement: NoSuchElementError'); }await driver.quit();})(); Plural: an array of every input, in page order. for...of with await inside, so the questions are asked one by one. No match: an empty array, never an error. Singular with no match: the promise rejects. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) inputs: 6 radio gender radio gender text fname text lname checkbox newsletter submit email boxes: 0 findElement: NoSuchElementError Real world scenario: checking the search results A test types \"laptop\" into a shop"],[6,"oneormany","Find one or find many","search and must check that exactly 20 results appear and that none says \"Out of stock\". It uses find elements on the result cards, checks the list has 20 entries, then loops over them reading each status. To check that an error banner is gone, it asks find elements for the banner and expects an empty list, which never raises. Common mistake find elements never waits for the first element to appear unless an implicit wait is set, and then it returns as soon as at least one matches. An empty list can mean the page is not ready yet. Chapter 4.1 shows how to wait properly. Lesson 2 of 4"],[6,"scope","Search inside an element","Instead of searching the whole page, you can first find a container and then search inside it. The documentation calls this evaluating a subset of the DOM. In Java and C# the driver, an element and a shadow root all implement the same SearchContext interface, which is why they share the find methods. Search inside one element, and the XPath trap Scope.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassScope {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\");WebElement form = driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"form\"));System.out.println(\"inputs in form: \" + form.findElements(By.cssSelector(\"input\")).size());System.out.println(\"links via //a: \" + form.findElements(By.xpath(\"//a\")).size());System.out.println(\"links via .//a: \" + form.findElements(By.xpath(\".//a\")).size());WebElement last = form.findElement(By.id(\"lname\"));WebElement same = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"form #lname\"));System.out.println(\"same element: \" + last.equals(same)); driver.quit(); }} First find the parent element. Called on the form, CSS only looks inside it. The trap: // starts at the top of the document, so the link outside the form is found. The dot means start here. Now nothing outside the form counts. Two commands: find the form, then find inside it. The optimised version: one CSS selector, one command. Both references point at the same element. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) inputs in form: 6 links via //a: 1 links via .//a: 0 same element: true main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html\")form = driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"form\")print(\"inputs in form:\", len(form.find_elements(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"input\")))print(\"links via //a:\", len(form.find_elements(By.XPATH, \"//a\")))print(\"links via .//a:\", len(form.find_elements(By.XPATH, \".//a\")))last = form.find_element(By.ID, \"lname\")same = driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"form #lname\")print(\"same element:\", last == same)driver.quit() First find the parent element. Called on the form, CSS only looks inside it. The trap: // starts at the top of the document, so the link outside the form is found. The dot means start here. Now nothing outside the form counts. Two commands: find the form, then find inside it. The optimised version: one CSS selector, one command. Both references point at the same element. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) inputs in form: 6 links via //a: 1 links via .//a: 0 same element: True main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, WebElement } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction scope() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/locators_tests/locators.html');const form = await driver.findElement(By.css('form')); console.log('inputs in form: ' + (await form.findElements(By.css('input'))).length); console.log('links via //a: ' + (await form.findElements(By.xpath('//a'))).length); console.log('links via .//a: ' + (await form.findElements(By.xpath('.//a'))).length);const last = await form.findElement(By.id('lname'));const same = await driver.findElement(By.css('form #lname')); console.log('same element: ' + awaitWebElement.equals(last, same));await driver.quit();})(); First find the parent element. Called on the form, CSS only looks inside it. The trap: // starts at the top of the document, so the link outside the form is found. The dot means start here. Now nothing outside the form counts. Two commands: find the form, then find inside it. The optimised version: one CSS selector, one command. Both references point at the same element. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with"],[6,"scope","Search inside an element","Chrome 153) inputs in form: 6 links via //a: 1 links via .//a: 0 same element: true The second line of output is the most common XPath mistake in Selenium. An XPath that begins with // always starts at the top of the document, even when you call it on an element. Start with .// to search below the element. CSS selectors called on an element never have this problem. The documentation also points out that a nested search costs two commands. When a single CSS selector such as form #lname can say the same thing, it is one command and slightly faster. Remember Searching inside a container is still very useful when the page repeats a pattern: the Delete button inside the third row of a table, or the price inside one product card. Find the row or card first, then the button inside it. Lesson 3 of 4"],[6,"shadow","The shadow DOM","Modern components often hide their insides in a shadow DOM: a separate tree attached to an element, called the shadow host. The page can see the host but a normal search does not go inside it. That keeps the component styles and ids apart from the rest of the page, and it also hides them from Selenium. Find the hostA normal locator finds the element the shadow tree hangs from. Get its shadow rootGet Element Shadow Root. Selenium 4 and Chromium 96 or later. Search insideFind Element From Shadow Root with a CSS selector. Reach into a shadow root Shadow.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.InvalidArgumentException;import org.openqa.selenium.SearchContext;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassShadow {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/shadowRootPage.html\");String box = \"input[type='checkbox']\";System.out.println(\"from the page: \" + driver.findElements(By.cssSelector(box)).size());WebElement host = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"custom-checkbox-element\"));SearchContext shadowRoot = host.getShadowRoot();WebElement checkbox = shadowRoot.findElement(By.cssSelector(box));System.out.println(\"from the shadow root: \" + checkbox.getAttribute(\"type\"));try { shadowRoot.findElement(By.xpath(\".//input\")); } catch (InvalidArgumentException e) {System.out.println(\"xpath in shadow root: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]); } driver.quit(); }} The checkbox lives in a shadow tree, so a normal search finds nothing. The shadow host: the element the hidden tree is attached to. Get Element Shadow Root. A SearchContext, like the driver and an element. Search inside the shadow tree. XPath inside a shadow root. Chrome refuses it. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) from the page: 0 from the shadow root: checkbox xpath in shadow root: invalid argument: invalid locator main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importInvalidArgumentExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/shadowRootPage.html\")box = \"input[type='checkbox']\"print(\"from the page:\", len(driver.find_elements(By.CSS_SELECTOR, box)))host = driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"custom-checkbox-element\")shadow_root = host.shadow_rootcheckbox = shadow_root.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, box)print(\"from the shadow root:\", checkbox.get_attribute(\"type\"))try: shadow_root.find_element(By.XPATH, \".//input\")exceptInvalidArgumentExceptionas e:print(\"xpath in shadow root:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])driver.quit() The checkbox lives in a shadow tree, so a normal search finds nothing. The shadow host: the element the hidden tree is attached to. Get Element Shadow Root. A ShadowRoot object with find_element and find_elements. Search inside the shadow tree. XPath inside a shadow root. Chrome refuses it. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) from the page: 0 from the shadow root: checkbox xpath in shadow root: invalid argument: invalid locator main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction shadow() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/shadowRootPage.html');const box = \"input[type='checkbox']\"; console.log('from the page: ' + (await driver.findElements(By.css(box))).length);const host = await driver.findElement(By.css('custom-checkbox-element'));const shadowRoot = await host.getShadowRoot();const checkbox = await shadowRoot.findElement(By.css(box)); console.log('from the shadow root: ' + await checkbox.getAttribute('type'));try {await shadowRoot.findElement(By.xpath('.//input')); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.InvalidArgumentError)) throw e; console.log('xpath in shadow root: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]); }await"],[6,"shadow","The shadow DOM","driver.quit();})(); The checkbox lives in a shadow tree, so a normal search finds nothing. The shadow host: the element the hidden tree is attached to. Get Element Shadow Root. Search inside the shadow tree. XPath inside a shadow root. Chrome refuses it. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) from the page: 0 from the shadow root: checkbox xpath in shadow root: invalid argument: invalid locator Three facts show in this run. A search from the page cannot see the checkbox. A search from the shadow root can. And Chrome refused an XPath inside the shadow root with invalid argument. When this page was built, tag name was refused too, while id, name and class name worked because the binding sends them as CSS. Use CSS selectors inside shadow roots. Remember A shadow root can contain another shadow host. Then you repeat the two steps: get that host, get its shadow root, search again. Each level is one more pair of commands. Lesson 4 of 4"],[6,"activeel","The active element","The active element is the element that has the keyboard focus right now: what would receive your typing. The W3C command Get Active Element returns it, and the bindings put it under switchTo(). Which element has the focus? Active.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.Keys;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassActive {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");System.out.println(\"at start: \" + driver.switchTo().activeElement().getTagName()); driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\")).click();System.out.println(\"after click: \" + driver.switchTo().activeElement().getAttribute(\"name\")); driver.switchTo().activeElement().sendKeys(Keys.TAB);System.out.println(\"after Tab: \" + driver.switchTo().activeElement().getAttribute(\"name\")); driver.quit(); }} Nothing is focused yet, so the active element is the body. Clicking a text box gives it the focus. Press Tab on whatever has the focus, as a user would. The focus moved to the next field. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) at start: body after click: my-text after Tab: my-password main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.common.keys importKeysdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")print(\"at start:\", driver.switch_to.active_element.tag_name)driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\").click()print(\"after click:\", driver.switch_to.active_element.get_attribute(\"name\"))driver.switch_to.active_element.send_keys(Keys.TAB)print(\"after Tab:\", driver.switch_to.active_element.get_attribute(\"name\"))driver.quit() Nothing is focused yet, so the active element is the body. Clicking a text box gives it the focus. Press Tab on whatever has the focus, as a user would. The focus moved to the next field. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) at start: body after click: my-text after Tab: my-password main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, Key } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction active() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html'); console.log('at start: ' + await (await driver.switchTo().activeElement()).getTagName());await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text')).click(); console.log('after click: ' + await (await driver.switchTo().activeElement()).getAttribute('name'));await (await driver.switchTo().activeElement()).sendKeys(Key.TAB); console.log('after Tab: ' + await (await driver.switchTo().activeElement()).getAttribute('name'));await driver.quit();})(); Nothing is focused yet, so the active element is the body. Clicking a text box gives it the focus. Press Tab on whatever has the focus, as a user would. The focus moved to the next field. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) at start: body after click: my-text after Tab: my-password This is how you test keyboard behaviour: that Tab moves through a form in a sensible order, that a dialog puts the focus on its first button, or that pressing Enter in a search box submits it. Keyboard users and screen reader users depend on that order. One idea, four depths Ask for one element or for all of them. Look inside a part of the page, inside a hidden component, or at the element that has the focus. find element raises when nothing matches, find elements returns an empty list. Searches from an element stay inside it, except XPath that starts with //. Shadow roots need host, shadow root, then CSS. Each search is a W3C command with its own endpoint: Find Element, Find Elements, Find Element From Element, Find Elements From Element, Get Element Shadow Root, Find Element From Shadow Root and Get Active Element. Fewer, more precise commands make suites faster. The shadow root reference is its own web object with the"],[6,"activeel","The active element","key shadow-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf, like the element key in chapter 1.2. The W3C spec allows any strategy inside a shadow root, but browsers do not have to support XPath there, which is why portable tests use CSS."],[7,"","Interacting with elements and reading them","Click, type, clear, the checks before every action and their errors, element information, select lists and uploads. · click · send keys · clear · select · upload · is displayed · attribute · property · interactable · intercepted Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 3: Find and use elements›Interact and inspect Step 3 · Topic 3 of 3 · Beginner Fill in forms with click, type and clear, understand the checks the driver makes before each action and the errors they give, read what an element knows about itself, and handle select lists and file uploads. About 55 min4 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Click, type and clear 2Read information about an element 3Select lists 4File upload Lesson 1 of 4"],[7,"commands","Click, type and clear","The documentation says there are only five basic commands for an element: click, send keys, clear, submit and select. Almost every form you will ever test needs nothing more. Command Works on Watch out for click Any element It clicks the centre of the element. If something else is on top there, you get element click intercepted. send keys Text fields and other keyboard interactable elements It adds to the existing text. Clear first if you want to replace it. clear Editable, resettable elements: text inputs and content editable Readonly and disabled inputs give invalid element state. submit Form elements In Selenium 4 it runs a script, so the documentation recommends clicking the submit button instead. select select and option elements Use the Select class, covered below. Fill in a form: clear, type, tick and click FillForm.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassFillForm {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(2)); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");WebElement text = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\")); text.sendKeys(\"first try\"); text.clear(); text.sendKeys(\"Selenium\");WebElement check2 = driver.findElement(By.id(\"my-check-2\"));System.out.println(\"check 2 before: \" + check2.isSelected()); check2.click();System.out.println(\"check 2 after: \" + check2.isSelected()); driver.findElement(By.id(\"my-radio-2\")).click();System.out.println(\"radio 1 now: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"my-radio-1\")).isSelected()); driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")).click();System.out.println(driver.findElement(By.id(\"message\")).getText()); driver.quit(); }} As in chapter 1.3: let finds retry for up to 2 seconds, so the page after Submit has time to load. Element Send Keys: types into the box, adding to what is already there. Element Clear: empties the box. Type the real value. isSelected reads whether a checkbox is ticked. Element Click: ticks it, as a mouse click would. Clicking the second radio button unselects the first one. Click Submit. The documentation prefers this to the submit() method. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) check 2 before: false check 2 after: true radio 1 now: false Received! main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.implicitly_wait(2)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")text = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")text.send_keys(\"first try\")text.clear()text.send_keys(\"Selenium\")check2 = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"my-check-2\")print(\"check 2 before:\", check2.is_selected())check2.click()print(\"check 2 after:\", check2.is_selected())driver.find_element(By.ID, \"my-radio-2\").click()print(\"radio 1 now:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"my-radio-1\").is_selected())driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\").click()print(driver.find_element(By.ID, \"message\").text)driver.quit() As in chapter 1.3: let finds retry for up to 2 seconds, so the page after Submit has time to load. Element Send Keys: types into the box, adding to what is already there. Element Clear: empties the box. Type the real value. is_selected reads whether a checkbox is ticked. Element Click: ticks it, as a mouse click would. Clicking the second radio button unselects the first one. Click Submit. The documentation prefers this to the submit() method. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) check 2 before: False check 2 after: True radio 1 now: False Received! main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction fillForm() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit: 2000 });await"],[7,"commands","Click, type and clear","driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');const text = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));await text.sendKeys('first try');await text.clear();await text.sendKeys('Selenium');const check2 = await driver.findElement(By.id('my-check-2')); console.log('check 2 before: ' + await check2.isSelected());await check2.click(); console.log('check 2 after: ' + await check2.isSelected());await driver.findElement(By.id('my-radio-2')).click(); console.log('radio 1 now: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('my-radio-1')).isSelected());await driver.findElement(By.css('button')).click(); console.log(await driver.findElement(By.id('message')).getText());await driver.quit();})(); As in chapter 1.3: let finds retry for up to 2 seconds, so the page after Submit has time to load. Element Send Keys: types into the box, adding to what is already there. Element Clear: empties the box. Type the real value. isSelected reads whether a checkbox is ticked. Element Click: ticks it, as a mouse click would. Clicking the second radio button unselects the first one. Click Submit. The documentation prefers this to the submit() method. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) check 2 before: false check 2 after: true radio 1 now: false Received!"],[7,"commands","Two checks before every action","These commands copy what a user can do, so the driver does two things first. It scrolls the element into view if it is outside the viewport, lining up its bottom with the bottom of the viewport. Then it checks that the element is interactable: displayed, with a size, and able to receive the pointer or the keyboard. If not, you get element not interactable. What happens inside one clickChange where the button is and what is wrong with it, then step through the checks the driver makes. Step 0 of 6 Button position Button state A cookie banner covers it Click requested Try this The happy path: the driver scrolls, checks, aims at the centre and clicks. Add the cookie banner. The click is intercepted. Close the banner in your test first, or wait for it to go. Hide the button. The driver stops early with element not interactable. Disable the button. Chrome reports the click as intercepted by the parent, a confusing message unless you check isEnabled() first. The three errors interactions give, made on purpose InteractErrors.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriverException;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassInteractErrors {staticvoid attempt(String what, Runnable step) {try { step.run();System.out.println(what + \": ok\"); } catch (WebDriverException e) {System.out.println(what + \": \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName() + \", \" + e.getMessage().split(\"[:\\n]\")[0]); } }publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\"); attempt(\"type into disabled\", () -> driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-disabled\")).sendKeys(\"x\")); attempt(\"clear readonly\", () -> driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-readonly\")).clear()); ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(\"const b = document.createElement('div'); b.textContent = 'We use cookies';\" + \"b.style.cssText = 'position:fixed;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;height:400px;background:orange';\" + \"document.body.appendChild(b);\"); attempt(\"click under banner\", () -> driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")).click()); driver.quit(); }} Run one step and report the error it raises, if any. A disabled input cannot take keyboard input. A readonly input cannot be reset. Add a cookie banner that covers the bottom of the page, as many real sites do. The banner sits over the centre of the button. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) type into disabled: ElementNotInteractableException, element not interactable clear readonly: InvalidElementStateException, invalid element state click under banner: ElementClickInterceptedException, element click intercepted main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importWebDriverExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydef attempt(what, step):try: step()print(what + \": ok\")exceptWebDriverExceptionas e:print(what + \":\", type(e).__name__ + \",\", e.msg.split(\":\")[0].split(\"\\n\")[0])driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")attempt(\"type into disabled\", lambda: driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-disabled\").send_keys(\"x\"))attempt(\"clear readonly\", lambda: driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-readonly\").clear())driver.execute_script(\"const b = document.createElement('div'); b.textContent = 'We use cookies';\"\"b.style.cssText = 'position:fixed;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;height:400px;background:orange';\"\"document.body.appendChild(b);\")attempt(\"click under banner\", lambda: driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\").click())driver.quit() Run one step and report the error it raises, if any. Every Selenium error is a WebDriverException. A disabled input cannot take keyboard input. A readonly input cannot be reset. Add a cookie banner that covers the bottom of the page, as many real sites do. The banner sits over the centre of the button. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome"],[7,"commands","Two checks before every action","153) type into disabled: ElementNotInteractableException, element not interactable clear readonly: InvalidElementStateException, invalid element state click under banner: ElementClickInterceptedException, element click intercepted main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');asyncfunction attempt(what, step) {try {await step(); console.log(what + ': ok'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.WebDriverError)) throw e; console.log(what + ': ' + e.name + ', ' + e.message.split(/[:\\n]/)[0]); }}(asyncfunction interactErrors() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');await attempt('type into disabled', async () => (await driver.findElement(By.name('my-disabled'))).sendKeys('x'));await attempt('clear readonly', async () => (await driver.findElement(By.name('my-readonly'))).clear());await driver.executeScript(\"const b = document.createElement('div'); b.textContent = 'We use cookies';\" +\"b.style.cssText = 'position:fixed;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;height:400px;background:orange';\" +'document.body.appendChild(b);');await attempt('click under banner', async () => (await driver.findElement(By.css('button'))).click());await driver.quit();})(); Run one step and report the error it raises, if any. Every Selenium error extends WebDriverError. A disabled input cannot take keyboard input. A readonly input cannot be reset. Add a cookie banner that covers the bottom of the page, as many real sites do. The banner sits over the centre of the button. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) type into disabled: ElementNotInteractableError, element not interactable clear readonly: InvalidElementStateError, invalid element state click under banner: ElementClickInterceptedError, element click intercepted Each error tells you something different. element not interactable: the element cannot take this action at all, here because it is disabled. invalid element state: the element exists and is visible, but its state forbids the action, here readonly. element click intercepted: the element is fine, but something else lies on top of it. Real world scenario: the cookie banner that broke every test A shop adds a cookie consent banner on Monday. On Tuesday half the checkout tests fail with element click intercepted. Nothing was wrong with the checkout: the banner covered the bottom of every page. The fix was one step at the start of each test that clicks Accept on the banner, or sets the consent cookie before the first page loads. Reading the \"Other element would receive the click\" part of the message points straight at the cause. Lesson 2 of 4"],[7,"info","Read information about an element","Tests do two things: act, then check. Checking means asking the element questions. Question Java Python JavaScript Is it displayed? isDisplayed() is_displayed() isDisplayed() Is it enabled? isEnabled() is_enabled() isEnabled() Is it selected? isSelected() is_selected() isSelected() Tag name getTagName() tag_name getTagName() Position and size getRect() rect getRect() Computed CSS getCssValue(name) value_of_css_property(name) getCssValue(name) Visible text getText() text getText() Ask an element about itself ElementInfo.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassElementInfo {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");WebElement text = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\")); text.sendKeys(\"hello\");System.out.println(\"displayed: \" + text.isDisplayed() + \", enabled: \" + text.isEnabled());System.out.println(\"disabled box enabled: \" + driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-disabled\")).isEnabled());System.out.println(\"tag: \" + text.getTagName() + \", rect: \" + text.getRect().getWidth() + \" x \" + text.getRect().getHeight());System.out.println(\"getAttribute(value): \" + text.getAttribute(\"value\"));System.out.println(\"getDomAttribute(value): \" + text.getDomAttribute(\"value\"));System.out.println(\"getDomProperty(value): \" + text.getDomProperty(\"value\"));System.out.println(\"getDomAttribute(myprop): \" + text.getDomAttribute(\"myprop\"));System.out.println(\"button colour: \" + driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")).getCssValue(\"color\"));System.out.println(\"heading text: \" + driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"h1\")).getText()); driver.quit(); }} Type, so the live value differs from the HTML. The disabled input reports false. Get Element Rect: position and size in pixels. The classic method: property first, then attribute. Only what the HTML says. The HTML has no value attribute. The live JavaScript property: what is typed now. A made up attribute in the HTML. The computed CSS value. The rendered, visible text. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) displayed: true, enabled: true disabled box enabled: false tag: input, rect: 296 x 38 getAttribute(value): hello getDomAttribute(value): null getDomProperty(value): hello getDomAttribute(myprop): myvalue button colour: rgba(13, 110, 253, 1) heading text: Web form main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")text = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")text.send_keys(\"hello\")print(\"displayed:\", text.is_displayed(), \", enabled:\", text.is_enabled())print(\"disabled box enabled:\", driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-disabled\").is_enabled())print(\"tag:\", text.tag_name, \", rect:\", text.rect[\"width\"], \"x\", text.rect[\"height\"])print(\"get_attribute(value):\", text.get_attribute(\"value\"))print(\"get_dom_attribute(value):\", text.get_dom_attribute(\"value\"))print(\"get_property(value):\", text.get_property(\"value\"))print(\"get_dom_attribute(myprop):\", text.get_dom_attribute(\"myprop\"))print(\"button colour:\", driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\").value_of_css_property(\"color\"))print(\"heading text:\", driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"h1\").text)driver.quit() Type, so the live value differs from the HTML. The disabled input reports False. Get Element Rect: position and size in pixels. The classic method: property first, then attribute. Only what the HTML says. The HTML has no value attribute. The live JavaScript property: what is typed now. A made up attribute in the HTML. The computed CSS value. The rendered, visible text. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) displayed: True , enabled: True disabled box enabled: False tag: input , rect: 296 x 38 get_attribute(value): hello get_dom_attribute(value): None get_property(value): hello"],[7,"info","Read information about an element","get_dom_attribute(myprop): myvalue button colour: rgba(13, 110, 253, 1) heading text: Web form main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction elementInfo() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');const text = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));await text.sendKeys('hello'); console.log('displayed: ' + await text.isDisplayed() + ', enabled: ' + await text.isEnabled()); console.log('disabled box enabled: ' + await driver.findElement(By.name('my-disabled')).isEnabled());const rect = await text.getRect(); console.log('tag: ' + await text.getTagName() + ', rect: ' + rect.width + ' x ' + rect.height); console.log('getAttribute(value): ' + await text.getAttribute('value')); console.log('getDomAttribute(value): ' + await text.getDomAttribute('value')); console.log('getProperty(value): ' + await text.getProperty('value')); console.log('getDomAttribute(myprop): ' + await text.getDomAttribute('myprop')); console.log('button colour: ' + await driver.findElement(By.css('button')).getCssValue('color')); console.log('heading text: ' + await driver.findElement(By.css('h1')).getText());await driver.quit();})(); Type, so the live value differs from the HTML. The disabled input reports false. Get Element Rect: position and size in pixels. The classic method: property first, then attribute. Only what the HTML says. The HTML has no value attribute. The live JavaScript property: what is typed now. A made up attribute in the HTML. The computed CSS value. The rendered, visible text. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) displayed: true, enabled: true disabled box enabled: false tag: input, rect: 296 x 38 getAttribute(value): hello getDomAttribute(value): null getProperty(value): hello getDomAttribute(myprop): myvalue button colour: rgba(13, 110, 253, 1) heading text: Web form"],[7,"info","Attribute or property","The three value lines show the difference that confuses many testers. An attribute is what the HTML says. A property is the live JavaScript value of the element right now. The text box has no value attribute in its HTML, so the DOM attribute is empty, but after typing, the value property is hello. Method Reads Use it for get DOM attribute Only the attribute in the HTML Checking what the server sent, such as a data-test value get DOM property Only the live JavaScript property What is in a field now, whether a box is checked now get attribute (classic) The property if there is one, otherwise the attribute. Selenium does this with a JavaScript atom Old code. Prefer the two precise methods. In the exam isDisplayed is not defined by the W3C spec, because deciding what a person can see covers too many cases. The documentation explains that Selenium runs a large JavaScript function that makes approximations. Treat it as a good guide, not a perfect one. Lesson 3 of 4"],[7,"selectlists","Select lists","A <select> drop down needs a helper: the Select class in the support package. Wrap the element, then choose options by visible text, by value or by index. formPage.html (the two lists used here) <select name=\"selectomatic\"> <option selected=\"selected\" id=\"non_multi_option\" value=\"one\">One</option> <option value=\"two\">Two</option> <option value=\"four\">Four</option> <option value=\"still learning how to count, apparently\">Still learning how to count, apparently</option></select><select name=\"multi\" id=\"multi\" multiple=\"multiple\"> <option selected=\"selected\" value=\"eggs\">Eggs</option> <option value=\"ham\">Ham</option> <option selected=\"selected\" value=\"sausages\">Sausages</option> <option value=\"onion gravy\">Onion gravy</option></select> Work with single and multiple select lists Selects.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.Select;publicclassSelects {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/formPage.html\");Select single = newSelect(driver.findElement(By.name(\"selectomatic\")));System.out.println(\"options: \" + single.getOptions().size()); single.selectByVisibleText(\"Four\");System.out.println(\"now: \" + single.getFirstSelectedOption().getText()); single.selectByValue(\"two\");System.out.println(\"now: \" + single.getFirstSelectedOption().getText()); single.selectByIndex(0);System.out.println(\"now: \" + single.getFirstSelectedOption().getText());Select multi = newSelect(driver.findElement(By.id(\"multi\")));System.out.println(\"multi starts with: \" + multi.getAllSelectedOptions().size() + \" selected\"); multi.deselectByValue(\"eggs\"); multi.selectByVisibleText(\"Ham\");for (WebElement o : multi.getAllSelectedOptions()) System.out.println(\" selected: \" + o.getText()); driver.quit(); }} Select lives in the support package. Wrap a select element in a Select object. getOptions lists every option element. Pick by the text the user sees. Pick by the value attribute. Pick by position, counting from 0. A select with the multiple attribute. Eggs and Sausages are selected in the HTML. Only multiple lists can deselect. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) options: 4 now: Four now: Two now: One multi starts with: 2 selected selected: Ham selected: Sausages main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support.select importSelectdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/formPage.html\")single = Select(driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"selectomatic\"))print(\"options:\", len(single.options))single.select_by_visible_text(\"Four\")print(\"now:\", single.first_selected_option.text)single.select_by_value(\"two\")print(\"now:\", single.first_selected_option.text)single.select_by_index(0)print(\"now:\", single.first_selected_option.text)multi = Select(driver.find_element(By.ID, \"multi\"))print(\"multi starts with:\", len(multi.all_selected_options), \"selected\")multi.deselect_by_value(\"eggs\")multi.select_by_visible_text(\"Ham\")for o in multi.all_selected_options:print(\" selected:\", o.text)driver.quit() Select lives in the support package. Wrap a select element in a Select object. options lists every option element. Pick by the text the user sees. Pick by the value attribute. Pick by position, counting from 0. A select with the multiple attribute. Eggs and Sausages are selected in the HTML. Only multiple lists can deselect. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) options: 4 now: Four now: Two now: One multi starts with: 2 selected selected: Ham selected: Sausages main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, Select } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction selects() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/formPage.html');const single ="],[7,"selectlists","Select lists","newSelect(await driver.findElement(By.name('selectomatic'))); console.log('options: ' + (await single.getOptions()).length);await single.selectByVisibleText('Four'); console.log('now: ' + await (await single.getFirstSelectedOption()).getText());await single.selectByValue('two'); console.log('now: ' + await (await single.getFirstSelectedOption()).getText());await single.selectByIndex(0); console.log('now: ' + await (await single.getFirstSelectedOption()).getText());const multi = newSelect(await driver.findElement(By.id('multi'))); console.log('multi starts with: ' + (await multi.getAllSelectedOptions()).length + ' selected');await multi.deselectByValue('eggs');await multi.selectByVisibleText('Ham');for (const o ofawait multi.getAllSelectedOptions()) console.log(' selected: ' + await o.getText());await driver.quit();})(); Wrap a select element in a Select object. getOptions lists every option element. Pick by the text the user sees. Pick by the value attribute. Pick by position, counting from 0. A select with the multiple attribute. Eggs and Sausages are selected in the HTML. Only multiple lists can deselect. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) options: 4 now: Four now: Two now: One multi starts with: 2 selected selected: Ham selected: Sausages Rule from the documentation What it means for you Select only works on select and option elements Drop downs built from div or li with JavaScript need normal clicks instead Since Selenium 4.5 a Select cannot be created on a disabled select Check isEnabled first if the list may be disabled Disabled options cannot be selected Trying raises an error Only multiple lists can deselect deselect on a single list raises an error Lesson 4 of 4"],[7,"upload","File upload","Selenium cannot touch the operating system file dialog. The documentation gives the way round it: send the full path of the file to the input type=\"file\" element with send keys. No dialog appears, and the browser attaches the file as if the user had picked it. Upload a file without the file dialog Upload.java import java.nio.file.Files;import java.nio.file.Path;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassUpload {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {Path file = Files.writeString(Path.of(\"report.txt\"), \"quarterly numbers\");WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");WebElement input = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-file\")); input.sendKeys(file.toAbsolutePath().toString());System.out.println(\"browser shows: \" + input.getDomProperty(\"value\")); driver.quit(); }} Make a small file to upload. The input with type=\"file\". Send the full path. No dialog opens, the file is simply attached. Browsers hide the real folder and show a made up folder called fakepath. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browser shows: C:\\fakepath\\report.txt main.py from pathlib importPathfrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfile = Path(\"report.txt\")file.write_text(\"quarterly numbers\")driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")upload = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-file\")upload.send_keys(str(file.resolve()))print(\"browser shows:\", upload.get_property(\"value\"))driver.quit() Make a small file to upload. The input with type=\"file\". Send the full path. No dialog opens, the file is simply attached. Browsers hide the real folder and show a made up folder called fakepath. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browser shows: C:\\fakepath\\report.txt main.js const fs = require('fs');const path = require('path');const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction upload() { fs.writeFileSync('report.txt', 'quarterly numbers');const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');const input = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-file'));await input.sendKeys(path.resolve('report.txt')); console.log('browser shows: ' + await input.getProperty('value'));await driver.quit();})(); Make a small file to upload. The input with type=\"file\". Send the full path. No dialog opens, the file is simply attached. Browsers hide the real folder and show a made up folder called fakepath. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browser shows: C:\\fakepath\\report.txt The value the page sees starts with C:\\fakepath\\ on every operating system. Browsers hide the real folder from web pages for privacy, so this is expected, not a bug. Remember When the test runs on a Grid, the file must reach the remote machine. The Java, Python and JavaScript bindings handle this with a file detector that uploads the file to the node first. Chapter 6.1 shows it. One idea, four depths Click, type, clear and pick from a list. Then ask the element whether it is shown, enabled, ticked and what it says. Before acting, the driver scrolls the element into view and checks it can be used. Errors say what was wrong: not interactable, invalid state or click intercepted. Attributes are the HTML, properties are the live value. Element Click aims at the in view centre point and checks the topmost element there. Element Send Keys focuses the element and dispatches key events, so page JavaScript sees real typing. File inputs are a special case where send keys sets the file list. Because Element Click needs the centre to be unobscured, sticky headers, overlays and animations cause most click failures. When you truly need pointer moves at exact offsets, the Actions API in chapter 4.3"],[7,"upload","File upload","skips these user like checks."],[8,"","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","Why tests race the page, implicit and explicit waits, expected conditions, custom waits and why not to mix them. · waits · implicit wait · explicit wait · webdriverwait · expected conditions · fluentwait · flaky tests · synchronization Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 4: Synchronise and control›Waits Step 4 · Topic 1 of 4 · Intermediate Beat the race between your code and the page. See why sleeps fail, how implicit and explicit waits work, which conditions to wait for, and prove with a real run why the two must never be mixed. About 55 min4 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1The race between your code and the page 2Implicit waits 3Explicit waits and expected conditions 4Customising waits, and never mixing them Lesson 1 of 4"],[8,"race","The race between your code and the page","The documentation calls synchronisation perhaps the most common challenge in browser automation. Your code and the page run at the same time. Sometimes the page is ready first and the test passes. Sometimes Selenium is first and the test fails. A test that passes and fails on the same code is a flaky test, and this race is one of their main causes. Navigation commands already wait for the page load strategy. But that only covers the HTML and its assets. Content that JavaScript adds or shows later, such as search results, a spinner that goes away or a dialog that slides in, is not covered. The documentation example is the dynamic.html page: Add a box! creates a red box and Reveal a new input shows a hidden input, each about a second after the click. The race: look for the box straight after the click Race.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.NoSuchElementException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassRace {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\"); driver.findElement(By.id(\"adder\")).click();try { driver.findElement(By.id(\"box0\"));System.out.println(\"found the box\"); } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {System.out.println(\"too early: no such element\"); } driver.quit(); }} The page adds a red box, but only 1 second after the click. Our code asks at once. No wait is set, so the driver checks only once. Selenium was faster than the page. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) too early: no such element main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importNoSuchElementExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\")driver.find_element(By.ID, \"adder\").click()try: driver.find_element(By.ID, \"box0\")print(\"found the box\")exceptNoSuchElementException:print(\"too early: no such element\")driver.quit() The page adds a red box, but only 1 second after the click. Our code asks at once. No wait is set, so the driver checks only once. Selenium was faster than the page. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) too early: no such element main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction race() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html');await driver.findElement(By.id('adder')).click();try {await driver.findElement(By.id('box0')); console.log('found the box'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.NoSuchElementError)) throw e; console.log('too early: no such element'); }await driver.quit();})(); The page adds a red box, but only 1 second after the click. Our code asks at once. No wait is set, so the driver checks only once. Selenium was faster than the page. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) too early: no such element The first idea most people have is to sleep for a few seconds. The documentation explains why that is poor: too short and it still fails on a slow day, too long and every test pays the full sleep every time. Selenium gives two better tools: implicit waits and explicit waits. Four ways to wait, on one clockThe element appears after a delay. Pick a strategy and change the numbers, then step through what your code and the driver do. Step 0 of 3 Strategy Element appears after (s)1.0 The element never appears Sleep length (s)3.0 Implicit wait (s)10.0 Explicit timeout (s)15 Polling interval (s)0.5 Result: found after 1.0 s. Try this No wait: the box from dynamic.html appears 1 s after the click, so the find fails straight away. This is the race the documentation describes. A 3 s sleep works, but wastes 2 s every time. Make the box take 4 s and the sleep fails. Explicit wait with polls every 0.5 s: it returns at the first check after the box"],[8,"race","The race between your code and the page","appears. The documentation warning: implicit 10 s mixed with explicit 15 s, and the element never comes. The wait ends after about 20.5 s, not 15. Lesson 2 of 4"],[8,"implicit","Implicit waits","An implicit wait is a session setting. Once set, every find element call keeps looking for up to that long before it raises no such element. It is set with the timeouts capability or a driver method, and its default is 0. Implicit wait: every find keeps looking Implicit.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassImplicit {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(2)); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\"); driver.findElement(By.id(\"adder\")).click();long start = System.nanoTime();WebElement box = driver.findElement(By.id(\"box0\"));double seconds = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1e9;System.out.println(\"found \" + box.getAttribute(\"class\") + \" after about \" + Math.round(seconds) + \" s\"); driver.quit(); }} For the whole session: a find may keep looking for up to 2 seconds. Not there yet, so the driver keeps checking until the box appears. Returns as soon as it is found, not after the full 2 seconds. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) found redbox after about 1 s main.py import timefrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.implicitly_wait(2)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\")driver.find_element(By.ID, \"adder\").click()start = time.monotonic()box = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"box0\")seconds = time.monotonic() - startprint(\"found\", box.get_attribute(\"class\"), \"after about\", round(seconds), \"s\")driver.quit() For the whole session: a find may keep looking for up to 2 seconds. Not there yet, so the driver keeps checking until the box appears. Returns as soon as it is found, not after the full 2 seconds. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) found redbox after about 1 s main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction implicit() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit: 2000 });await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html');await driver.findElement(By.id('adder')).click();const start = Date.now();const box = await driver.findElement(By.id('box0'));const seconds = (Date.now() - start) / 1000; console.log('found ' + await box.getAttribute('class') + ' after about ' + Math.round(seconds) + ' s');await driver.quit();})(); For the whole session: a find may keep looking for up to 2 seconds. Not there yet, so the driver keeps checking until the box appears. Returns as soon as it is found, not after the full 2 seconds. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) found redbox after about 1 s The box appeared after about one second and find returned straight away. A larger implicit wait does not slow down finds that succeed. It only changes how long a failing find takes to fail. Strength Weakness One line covers every find in the session It only waits for presence. A present element can still be hidden, covered or disabled Finds return as soon as the element appears Every check that something is absent now takes the full wait Easy to show in a first script It cannot wait for text, a title, a count or anything that is not a find Try it: a box that arrives one second lateRun it as it is and it fails. Then uncomment the implicit wait line, or swap the find for the explicit wait, and run again. no session Lesson 3 of 4"],[8,"explicit","Explicit waits and expected conditions","An explicit wait is a loop in your code. It checks a condition, sleeps for a polling interval, and checks again, until the condition is true or the timeout passes. Because you choose the condition, you can wait for exactly what the next step needs. The documentation adds that the wait class handles no such element for you while it loops. One explicit wait, step by step Create the wait with a timeout, for example 2 seconds. Call until with a condition: a lambda, a function, or a ready made expected condition. The wait calls the condition. If the result is true, or an element, it returns it to you. If the result is false, or an ignored error was raised, it looks at the clock. Past the timeout, it raises a timeout error with your message. Otherwise it sleeps for the polling interval and tries again. Explicit wait: wait for exactly the condition you need Explicit.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;publicclassExplicit {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\");WebElement revealed = driver.findElement(By.id(\"revealed\")); driver.findElement(By.id(\"reveal\")).click();WebDriverWait wait = newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)); wait.until(d -> revealed.isDisplayed()); revealed.sendKeys(\"Displayed\");System.out.println(\"typed: \" + revealed.getDomProperty(\"value\")); driver.quit(); }} The input is in the page already, just hidden. The page shows it 1 second later. Wait at most 2 seconds. Java checks every 500 ms. A lambda: keep calling it until it returns true. Safe now: the input is displayed, so it can take keys. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: Displayed main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\")revealed = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"revealed\")driver.find_element(By.ID, \"reveal\").click()wait = WebDriverWait(driver, timeout=2)wait.until(lambda d: revealed.is_displayed())revealed.send_keys(\"Displayed\")print(\"typed:\", revealed.get_property(\"value\"))driver.quit() The input is in the page already, just hidden. The page shows it 1 second later. Wait at most 2 seconds. Python checks every 0.5 s. A lambda: keep calling it until it returns something true. Safe now: the input is displayed, so it can take keys. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: Displayed main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction explicit() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html');const revealed = await driver.findElement(By.id('revealed'));await driver.findElement(By.id('reveal')).click();await driver.wait(() => revealed.isDisplayed(), 2000);await revealed.sendKeys('Displayed'); console.log('typed: ' + await revealed.getProperty('value'));await driver.quit();})(); The input is in the page already, just hidden. The page shows it 1 second later. Keep calling the function until it is true, for at most 2000 ms. JavaScript checks every 200 ms. Safe now: the input is displayed, so it can take keys. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: Displayed Default Java WebDriverWait Python WebDriverWait JavaScript driver.wait Polling interval 500 ms 0.5 s 200 ms Errors ignored while looping NotFoundException (such as no such element) NoSuchElementException none, catch them yourself Timeout you must give one you must give one 0, which means wait forever. Always pass one Error at the end TimeoutException TimeoutException TimeoutError"],[8,"explicit","Expected conditions","Expected conditions are ready made conditions for the common cases: an element exists, is visible, is clickable or has gone stale, some text is present, or the title contains a value. Java has ExpectedConditions, Python has expected_conditions (usually imported as EC) and JavaScript has until. Ready made conditions, and what a timeout looks like Conditions.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.TimeoutException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;publicclassConditions {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\");WebDriverWait wait = newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)); driver.findElement(By.id(\"adder\")).click();WebElement box = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.presenceOfElementLocated(By.id(\"box0\")));System.out.println(\"present: \" + box.getAttribute(\"id\")); driver.findElement(By.id(\"reveal\")).click();WebElement input = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id(\"revealed\")));System.out.println(\"visible: \" + input.isDisplayed());try {newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(1)).withMessage(\"the second box never came\") .until(ExpectedConditions.presenceOfElementLocated(By.id(\"box1\"))); } catch (TimeoutException e) {System.out.println(\"TimeoutException: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]); } driver.quit(); }} A ready made condition. It returns the element it waited for. Present is not enough for typing. Wait until it is visible. Our own message makes the failure readable. Nobody clicked again, so box1 never appears. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) present: box0 visible: true TimeoutException: Expected condition failed: the second box never came main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importTimeoutExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions asECfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\")wait = WebDriverWait(driver, 2)driver.find_element(By.ID, \"adder\").click()box = wait.until(EC.presence_of_element_located((By.ID, \"box0\")))print(\"present:\", box.get_attribute(\"id\"))driver.find_element(By.ID, \"reveal\").click()field = wait.until(EC.visibility_of_element_located((By.ID, \"revealed\")))print(\"visible:\", field.is_displayed())try:WebDriverWait(driver, 1).until(EC.presence_of_element_located((By.ID, \"box1\")), \"the second box never came\")exceptTimeoutExceptionas e:print(\"TimeoutException:\", e.msg)driver.quit() The usual short name for the expected conditions module. A ready made condition. Note the locator is one tuple. It returns the element. Present is not enough for typing. Wait until it is visible. Nobody clicked again, so box1 never appears. The second argument is our message. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) present: box0 visible: True TimeoutException: the second box never came main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, until, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction conditions() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html');await driver.findElement(By.id('adder')).click();const box = await driver.wait(until.elementLocated(By.id('box0')), 2000); console.log('present: ' + await box.getAttribute('id'));await driver.findElement(By.id('reveal')).click();const field = await driver.findElement(By.id('revealed'));await driver.wait(until.elementIsVisible(field), 2000); console.log('visible: ' + await field.isDisplayed());try {await driver.wait(until.elementLocated(By.id('box1')), 1000, 'the second box never came'); } catch (e)"],[8,"explicit","Expected conditions","{if (!(e instanceof error.TimeoutError)) throw e; console.log('TimeoutError: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]); }await driver.quit();})(); until holds the ready made conditions in JavaScript. A ready made condition. It resolves to the element. Present is not enough for typing. Wait until it is visible. Nobody clicked again, so box1 never appears. The third argument is our message. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) present: box0 visible: true TimeoutError: the second box never came Wait for Java Python JavaScript Element exists presenceOfElementLocated presence_of_element_located until.elementLocated Element visible visibilityOfElementLocated visibility_of_element_located until.elementIsVisible Element clickable elementToBeClickable element_to_be_clickable until.elementIsEnabled plus visible Text present textToBePresentInElement text_to_be_present_in_element until.elementTextContains Title contains titleContains title_contains until.titleContains Element gone stale stalenessOf staleness_of until.stalenessOf Try it: present is not the same as visibleThe input is in the page from the start but hidden. Typing into it too early fails. Add a wait for visibility. no session Lesson 4 of 4"],[8,"custom","Customising waits, and never mixing them","The wait class takes extra settings: how often to poll, which errors mean \"not yet\", the total timeout and the message on failure. The documentation example retries an action that raises element not interactable until it works. A customised wait: poll faster and retry on an error Fluent.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.ElementNotInteractableException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.FluentWait;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.Wait;publicclassFluent {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\");WebElement revealed = driver.findElement(By.id(\"revealed\")); driver.findElement(By.id(\"reveal\")).click();Wait<WebDriver> wait = newFluentWait<WebDriver>(driver) .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2)) .pollingEvery(Duration.ofMillis(300)) .ignoring(ElementNotInteractableException.class); wait.until(d -> { revealed.sendKeys(\"Displayed\");returntrue; });System.out.println(\"typed: \" + revealed.getDomProperty(\"value\")); driver.quit(); }} FluentWait lets you set every part of the loop. Give up after 2 seconds. Check every 300 ms instead of 500. Treat this error as not yet and try again. The condition does the action itself and returns true when it worked. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: Displayed main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importElementNotInteractableExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\")revealed = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"revealed\")driver.find_element(By.ID, \"reveal\").click()errors = [ElementNotInteractableException]wait = WebDriverWait(driver, timeout=2, poll_frequency=0.3, ignored_exceptions=errors)wait.until(lambda d: revealed.send_keys(\"Displayed\") orTrue)print(\"typed:\", revealed.get_property(\"value\"))driver.quit() The errors that mean not yet, try again. Give up after 2 s, check every 0.3 s. Do the action inside the condition. send_keys returns None, so \"or True\" reports success. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: Displayed main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction fluent() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html');const revealed = await driver.findElement(By.id('revealed'));await driver.findElement(By.id('reveal')).click();await driver.wait(async () => {try {await revealed.sendKeys('Displayed');returntrue; } catch (e) {if (e instanceof error.ElementNotInteractableError) returnfalse;throw e; } }, 2000, 'the input never became usable', 300); console.log('typed: ' + await revealed.getProperty('value'));await driver.quit();})(); JavaScript has no ignore list, so the condition catches the error itself. Worked: the wait ends. Not yet: the wait tries again. Any other error is a real problem. Timeout 2000 ms, our message, and poll every 300 ms. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: Displayed"],[8,"custom","Do not mix implicit and explicit waits","The documentation is direct: do not mix them, because the wait times become unpredictable. Its example is an implicit wait of 10 seconds with an explicit wait of 15 seconds, which can time out after 20 seconds. The reason is that each check of the explicit wait calls find element, and find element now blocks for the implicit wait every time the element is missing. Proof: mixing implicit and explicit waits stretches the timeout Python only, because the timing rule is the same in every binding. The documentation warns that 10 s implicit plus 15 s explicit can time out after 20 s. Here smaller numbers make the run short. main.py import timefrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importTimeoutExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions asECfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/dynamic.html\")driver.implicitly_wait(2)start = time.monotonic()try:WebDriverWait(driver, 3).until(EC.presence_of_element_located((By.ID, \"box9\")))exceptTimeoutException:print(f\"gave up after {time.monotonic() - start:.1f} s, not 3 s\")driver.quit() Implicit wait: each find may block for 2 seconds. Explicit wait of 3 seconds for an element that never comes. Each check inside the explicit wait blocked 2 seconds on the implicit wait. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) gave up after 4.6 s, not 3 s Three seconds were asked for, and the run gave up after more than four. With the documentation numbers the effect grows to many seconds per failing test. Pick one strategy for a project. Most teams keep the implicit wait at 0 and use explicit waits where they are needed. Real world scenario: the suite that took an hour longer A team set a 10 second implicit wait \"to be safe\", and their page objects also used 15 second explicit waits. Most tests passed, so nobody noticed. On a day when the login service was down, every one of 300 tests waited about 20 seconds per failed check instead of 15. The run that normally took 20 minutes ran past the CI limit. Removing the implicit wait made failures fast again. One idea, four depths Do not guess with sleep. Tell Selenium what you are waiting for and let it check again and again until it happens. An implicit wait makes every find patient. An explicit wait loops over a condition you choose, with a timeout and a polling interval. Use one or the other, not both. Explicit waits run in the client and send a command per poll. Implicit waits run in the driver inside one Find Element command. Combining them nests one loop inside the other, so the worst case is roughly the explicit timeout plus one whole implicit wait. Polling trades speed of reaction against commands sent. WebDriver BiDi lets a client subscribe to DOM mutations and network events instead, so future waits can react to the page instead of asking it repeatedly."],[9,"","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","Switching windows and tabs, working inside iframes, and handling alerts, confirms and prompts. · windows · tabs · window handle · iframe · frames · alerts · confirm · prompt · switch to · unhandledpromptbehavior Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 4: Synchronise and control›Windows, frames, alerts Step 4 · Topic 2 of 4 · Intermediate Point WebDriver at the right place: switch between windows and tabs, step into iframes and out again, and handle alerts, confirms and prompts, including the ones that appear late or are forgotten. About 50 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Windows and tabs 2Frames and iframes 3Alerts, confirms and prompts Lesson 1 of 3"],[9,"windows","Windows and tabs","WebDriver does not tell windows and tabs apart. Each one has a window handle, a unique id that stays the same for the whole session. Commands always go to one window, the current one. When a link opens a new window, the browser shows it on screen, but the documentation points out that WebDriver does not follow. You must switch. Where do my commands go?WebDriver talks to one window and one document at a time. Step through a plan and watch the pointer move. Step 0 of 5 Plan Commands now go to: the top document. Try this In the window plan, notice step 2: the browser shows the new window, but WebDriver has not moved. In the iframe plan, the first find fails for a reason that has nothing to do with waiting. parentFrame goes up one level, defaultContent goes to the top. Follow a link into a new window, come back, open a new tab Windows.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WindowType;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;publicclassWindows {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html\");String original = driver.getWindowHandle();System.out.println(\"windows at start: \" + driver.getWindowHandles().size()); driver.findElement(By.name(\"windowTwo\")).click();newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(ExpectedConditions.numberOfWindowsToBe(2));for (String handle : driver.getWindowHandles()) {if (!handle.equals(original)) driver.switchTo().window(handle); }System.out.println(\"new window: \" + driver.getTitle()); driver.close(); driver.switchTo().window(original);System.out.println(\"back to: \" + driver.getTitle()); driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/resultPage.html\");System.out.println(\"new tab: \" + driver.getTitle() + \", windows: \" + driver.getWindowHandles().size()); driver.quit(); }} The id of the current window. It stays the same for the whole session. This link has target=\"_blank\", so it opens a new window. Wait until the second window exists. Every open window, in the order they were opened. Switch to the one that is not the original. Close only this window. Switch back, or the next command fails with no such window. Selenium 4: open a new tab and switch to it in one step. Closes every window and tab at once. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) windows at start: 1 new window: This page has iframes back to: XHTML Test Page new tab: We Arrive Here, windows: 2 main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions asECfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html\")original = driver.current_window_handleprint(\"windows at start:\", len(driver.window_handles))driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"windowTwo\").click()WebDriverWait(driver, 2).until(EC.number_of_windows_to_be(2))for handle in driver.window_handles:if handle != original: driver.switch_to.window(handle)print(\"new window:\", driver.title)driver.close()driver.switch_to.window(original)print(\"back to:\", driver.title)driver.switch_to.new_window(\"tab\")driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/resultPage.html\")print(\"new tab:\", driver.title + \", windows:\", len(driver.window_handles))driver.quit() The id of the current window. It stays the same for the whole session. This link has target=\"_blank\", so it opens a new window. Wait until the second window exists. Every open window, in the order they were opened. Switch to the one that is not the original. Close only this window. Switch back, or the next command fails with no such window. Selenium 4: open a new tab and switch to it in one step. Closes every window and tab"],[9,"windows","Windows and tabs","at once. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) windows at start: 1 new window: This page has iframes back to: XHTML Test Page new tab: We Arrive Here, windows: 2 main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction windows() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html');const original = await driver.getWindowHandle(); console.log('windows at start: ' + (await driver.getAllWindowHandles()).length);await driver.findElement(By.name('windowTwo')).click();await driver.wait(async () => (await driver.getAllWindowHandles()).length === 2, 2000);for (const handle ofawait driver.getAllWindowHandles()) {if (handle !== original) await driver.switchTo().window(handle); } console.log('new window: ' + await driver.getTitle());await driver.close();await driver.switchTo().window(original); console.log('back to: ' + await driver.getTitle());await driver.switchTo().newWindow('tab');await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/resultPage.html'); console.log('new tab: ' + await driver.getTitle() + ', windows: ' + (await driver.getAllWindowHandles()).length);await driver.quit();})(); The id of the current window. It stays the same for the whole session. This link has target=\"_blank\", so it opens a new window. Wait until the second window exists. Every open window, in the order they were opened. Switch to the one that is not the original. Close only this window. Switch back, or the next command fails with no such window. Selenium 4: open a new tab and switch to it in one step. Closes every window and tab at once. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) windows at start: 1 new window: This page has iframes back to: XHTML Test Page new tab: We Arrive Here, windows: 2 What you want Java Python JavaScript Current handle getWindowHandle() current_window_handle getWindowHandle() All handles getWindowHandles() window_handles getAllWindowHandles() Switch switchTo().window(h) switch_to.window(h) switchTo().window(h) New tab or window switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB) switch_to.new_window(\"tab\") switchTo().newWindow(\"tab\") Close current close() close() close() Common mistake After close() you are still pointing at the closed window. The documentation warns that the next command raises no such window. Always switch back to a handle you saved before. Real world scenario: the payment pop up A shop opens the card payment page of its bank in a new window. The test saves the shop window handle, clicks Pay, waits for two windows, switches to the bank window, fills in the test card and confirms. The bank window closes itself, so the test switches back to the saved shop handle and checks the order confirmation. Lesson 2 of 3"],[9,"frames","Frames and iframes","An iframe puts a whole second document inside a page, often from another site: a payment form, a video player, a chat box or an editor. Selenium only sees the elements of the document it is pointing at, so the documentation explains that an element inside an iframe gives no such element until you switch into the frame. Switch into an iframe three ways and back out Frames.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassFrames {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/iframes.html\");System.out.println(\"email boxes seen from the top: \" + driver.findElements(By.id(\"email\")).size());WebElement frame = driver.findElement(By.id(\"iframe1\")); driver.switchTo().frame(frame);System.out.println(\"by element: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"email\")).getAttribute(\"type\")); driver.switchTo().defaultContent(); driver.switchTo().frame(\"iframe1-name\");System.out.println(\"by name: \" + driver.findElements(By.id(\"email\")).size() + \" email box\"); driver.switchTo().defaultContent(); driver.switchTo().frame(0);System.out.println(\"title inside the frame: \" + driver.getTitle()); driver.switchTo().defaultContent();System.out.println(\"heading: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"iframe_page_heading\")).getText()); driver.quit(); }} The form lives inside the iframe, so the top document sees none. 1. Switch with a WebElement: the most flexible way. Leave the frame: back to the top document. 2. Switch with the frame name or id. 3. Switch with an index: the first frame of the page. Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. Outside elements are reachable again. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) email boxes seen from the top: 0 by element: email by name: 1 email box title inside the frame: This page has iframes heading: This is the heading main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/iframes.html\")print(\"email boxes seen from the top:\", len(driver.find_elements(By.ID, \"email\")))frame = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"iframe1\")driver.switch_to.frame(frame)print(\"by element:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"email\").get_attribute(\"type\"))driver.switch_to.default_content()driver.switch_to.frame(\"iframe1-name\")print(\"by name:\", len(driver.find_elements(By.ID, \"email\")), \"email box\")driver.switch_to.default_content()driver.switch_to.frame(0)print(\"title inside the frame:\", driver.title)driver.switch_to.default_content()print(\"heading:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"iframe_page_heading\").text)driver.quit() The form lives inside the iframe, so the top document sees none. 1. Switch with a WebElement: the most flexible way. Leave the frame: back to the top document. 2. Switch with the frame name or id. 3. Switch with an index: the first frame of the page. Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. Outside elements are reachable again. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) email boxes seen from the top: 0 by element: email by name: 1 email box title inside the frame: This page has iframes heading: This is the heading main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction frames() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/iframes.html'); console.log('email boxes seen from the top: ' + (await driver.findElements(By.id('email'))).length);const frame = await driver.findElement(By.id('iframe1'));await driver.switchTo().frame(frame); console.log('by element: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('email')).getAttribute('type'));await driver.switchTo().defaultContent();await driver.switchTo().frame(await driver.findElement(By.name('iframe1-name'))); console.log('by name: '"],[9,"frames","Frames and iframes","+ (await driver.findElements(By.id('email'))).length + ' email box');await driver.switchTo().defaultContent();await driver.switchTo().frame(0); console.log('title inside the frame: ' + await driver.getTitle());await driver.switchTo().defaultContent(); console.log('heading: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('iframe_page_heading')).getText());await driver.quit();})(); The form lives inside the iframe, so the top document sees none. 1. Switch with a WebElement: the most flexible way. Leave the frame: back to the top document. 2. JavaScript has no switch by name string, so find the frame by its name first. 3. Switch with an index: the first frame of the page. Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. Outside elements are reachable again. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) email boxes seen from the top: 0 by element: email by name: 1 email box title inside the frame: This page has iframes heading: This is the heading Switch by Example When to use WebElement switchTo().frame(element) Most flexible. Find the frame with any locator. Name or id switchTo().frame(\"iframe1-name\") The frame has a stable name or id. If several match, the first one is used. Java and Python accept the string. JavaScript needs the element. Index switchTo().frame(0) Position in window.frames. Breaks if frames are added. Leave all frames switchTo().defaultContent() Back to the top document. Up one level switchTo().parentFrame() For frames inside frames. One detail in the output surprises people: inside the frame, Get Title still returned the title of the top page. Title belongs to the top level browsing context. Element commands, on the other hand, go to the frame you switched to. Try it: elements inside an iframeRun it once as it is. Then delete the switch line and see the NoSuchElement error, because the driver only looks in the current document. no session Lesson 3 of 3"],[9,"alerts","Alerts, confirms and prompts","JavaScript has three native pop ups, drawn by the browser and not by the page, so they cannot be found with locators. WebDriver has its own commands for them. Pop up Buttons What you can do alert OK Read the text, accept confirm OK and Cancel Read the text, accept or dismiss prompt OK and Cancel, plus a text box Read the text, send keys, accept or dismiss. Sending keys replaces the placeholder text Alert, confirm, prompt, a slow alert and one left open Alerts.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.Alert;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.UnhandledAlertException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;publicclassAlerts {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();WebDriverWait wait = newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/alerts.html\"); driver.findElement(By.id(\"alert\")).click();Alert alert = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent());System.out.println(\"alert: \" + alert.getText()); alert.accept(); driver.findElement(By.id(\"confirm\")).click();Alert confirm = driver.switchTo().alert();System.out.println(\"confirm: \" + confirm.getText()); confirm.dismiss(); driver.findElement(By.id(\"prompt\")).click();Alert prompt = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()); prompt.sendKeys(\"Selenium\"); prompt.accept();System.out.println(\"page shows: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"text\")).getText()); driver.findElement(By.id(\"slow-alert\")).click();System.out.println(\"slow: \" + wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()).getText()); driver.switchTo().alert().accept(); driver.findElement(By.id(\"alert\")).click();try { driver.getTitle(); } catch (UnhandledAlertException e) {System.out.println(\"left open: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]); } driver.quit(); }} The page calls alert('cheese'). Wait for the alert, then get a handle on it. Press OK. A confirm box: OK or Cancel. Switch to it without waiting, because it opens at once. Press Cancel. A prompt asks for text. Type the answer into the prompt. The page wrote our answer into a paragraph. This one opens 200 ms later. switchTo().alert() now would fail, so wait. Open one more alert and forget about it. Any command while an alert is open. The default behaviour dismisses it and reports an error. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) alert: cheese confirm: Are you sure? page shows: Selenium slow: Slow left open: unexpected alert open: {Alert text : cheese} main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importUnexpectedAlertPresentExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions asECfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitdriver = webdriver.Chrome()wait = WebDriverWait(driver, 2)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/alerts.html\")driver.find_element(By.ID, \"alert\").click()alert = wait.until(EC.alert_is_present())print(\"alert:\", alert.text)alert.accept()driver.find_element(By.ID, \"confirm\").click()confirm = driver.switch_to.alertprint(\"confirm:\", confirm.text)confirm.dismiss()driver.find_element(By.ID, \"prompt\").click()prompt = wait.until(EC.alert_is_present())prompt.send_keys(\"Selenium\")prompt.accept()print(\"page shows:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"text\").text)driver.find_element(By.ID, \"slow-alert\").click()print(\"slow:\", wait.until(EC.alert_is_present()).text)driver.switch_to.alert.accept()driver.find_element(By.ID, \"alert\").click()try: driver.titleexceptUnexpectedAlertPresentExceptionas e:print(\"left open:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])driver.quit() The page calls alert('cheese'). Wait for the alert, then get a handle on it. Press OK. A confirm box: OK or Cancel. Switch to it without waiting, because it opens at once. Press Cancel. A prompt asks for text. Type the answer into the"],[9,"alerts","Alerts, confirms and prompts","prompt. The page wrote our answer into a paragraph. This one opens 200 ms later. switch_to.alert now would fail, so wait. Open one more alert and forget about it. Any command while an alert is open. The default behaviour dismisses it and reports an error. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) alert: cheese confirm: Are you sure? page shows: Selenium slow: Slow left open: unexpected alert open: {Alert text : cheese} main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, until, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction alerts() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/alerts.html');await driver.findElement(By.id('alert')).click();const alert = await driver.wait(until.alertIsPresent(), 2000); console.log('alert: ' + await alert.getText());await alert.accept();await driver.findElement(By.id('confirm')).click();const confirm = await driver.switchTo().alert(); console.log('confirm: ' + await confirm.getText());await confirm.dismiss();await driver.findElement(By.id('prompt')).click();const prompt = await driver.wait(until.alertIsPresent(), 2000);await prompt.sendKeys('Selenium');await prompt.accept(); console.log('page shows: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('text')).getText());await driver.findElement(By.id('slow-alert')).click();const slow = await driver.wait(until.alertIsPresent(), 2000); console.log('slow: ' + await slow.getText());await slow.accept();await driver.findElement(By.id('alert')).click();try {await driver.getTitle(); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.UnexpectedAlertOpenError)) throw e; console.log('left open: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]); }await driver.quit();})(); The page calls alert('cheese'). Wait for the alert, then get a handle on it. Press OK. A confirm box: OK or Cancel. Switch to it without waiting, because it opens at once. Press Cancel. A prompt asks for text. Type the answer into the prompt. The page wrote our answer into a paragraph. This one opens 200 ms later. switchTo().alert() now would fail, so wait. Open one more alert and forget about it. Any command while an alert is open. The default behaviour dismisses it and reports an error. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) alert: cheese confirm: Are you sure? page shows: Selenium slow: Slow left open: unexpected alert open: {Alert text : cheese} Two lessons come out of this run. A slow alert needs a wait, exactly like a slow element: alertIsPresent in Java, alert_is_present in Python, until.alertIsPresent in JavaScript. And an alert you forget breaks the next command. The unhandledPromptBehavior capability decides what happens. Its default, dismiss and notify, closes the pop up and raises unexpected alert open, so the test fails at the line after the real cause. unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to a forgotten pop up dismiss and notify (default) Dismissed, and the command raises unexpected alert open accept and notify Accepted, and the command raises unexpected alert open dismiss Dismissed silently accept Accepted silently ignore Left open, and the command raises unexpected alert open Try it: alerts, confirms and promptsThe slow alert opens two seconds after the click. Remove the wait and see what switching too early gives you. no session Remember Recent drivers dismiss beforeunload prompts, the \"Leave site?\" box, by default. Chapter 2.1 showed where unhandledPromptBehavior is set. One idea, four depths Selenium works in one window and one document at a time. To use a new tab, a frame or a pop up, you tell it to switch there first. Windows have handles, frames are switched into by element, name or index, and alerts have their own accept, dismiss, text and send keys commands. Save the original handle and always switch back. The W3C spec keeps a current top level browsing context and a current browsing context. Window commands change the first, frame commands the second. Title and window rect read the top level context,"],[9,"alerts","Alerts, confirms and prompts","element commands use the current one. BiDi models every window, tab and frame as a browsingContext with events for creation, navigation and user prompts. A test can then react to a pop up or a new tab the moment it opens, instead of polling for it."],[10,"","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","Mouse, keyboard and wheel actions, and how a chain becomes ticks. · actions · action chains · mouse · keyboard · drag and drop · hover · double click · right click · scroll · wheel · ticks Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 4: Synchronise and control›Actions API Step 4 · Topic 3 of 4 · Intermediate Do what a person does with a mouse, a keyboard and a scroll wheel: double click, right click, hover, drag and drop, hold Shift, and scroll inside a page or a frame. About 55 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1How actions work, and the mouse 2Keyboard actions 3Scroll wheel actions Lesson 1 of 3"],[10,"mouse","How actions work, and the mouse","Most tests only need click() and sendKeys() on an element. Some things a real user does cannot be said with those: a double click, a right click, hovering to open a menu, dragging a card to another column, holding Shift while typing, or turning the mouse wheel. The Actions API is the low level way to describe exactly what the input devices do. You build a chain of steps and then call perform(). Only then is a request sent. The binding turns the chain into one POST /session/{id}/actions request. This is the body Python really sent for drag_and_drop in the run below, with the element ids shortened: POST /session/{id}/actions {\"actions\": [ {\"type\": \"pointer\", \"id\": \"mouse\", \"parameters\": {\"pointerType\": \"mouse\"},\"actions\": [ {\"type\": \"pointerMove\", \"duration\": 250, \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0, \"origin\": {\"element-6066-...\": \"...e.3\"}}, {\"type\": \"pointerDown\", \"duration\": 0, \"button\": 0}, {\"type\": \"pointerMove\", \"duration\": 250, \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0, \"origin\": {\"element-6066-...\": \"...e.4\"}}, {\"type\": \"pointerUp\", \"duration\": 0, \"button\": 0}]}, {\"type\": \"key\", \"id\": \"key\",\"actions\": [ {\"type\": \"pause\", \"duration\": 0}, {\"type\": \"pause\", \"duration\": 0}, {\"type\": \"pause\", \"duration\": 0}, {\"type\": \"pause\", \"duration\": 0}]}]} There is one list per input source: a pointer (mouse, pen or touch), a key source and a wheel. The W3C spec runs the lists together, one column at a time. Each column is called a tick. A tick lasts as long as the slowest action in it. Python filled the key list with pauses so that both lists have four ticks. How a chain of actions becomes ticksEvery input source gets one list of actions. The driver runs them column by column. Each column, a tick, lasts as long as its slowest action. Step 0 of 4 Chain Pointer move duration (ms)250 Time used so far: 0 ms of 500 ms. Try this In the drag plan, look at the key row: Python filled it with pauses so both rows have the same number of ticks. Set the move duration to 0. The drag now takes 0 ms of pointer movement, which some drag libraries do not notice. In the Shift plan, SHIFT stays down from the first tick to the last, so a and b arrive as capitals. Double click, right click, hover, drag and drop, and move to a point Mouse.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.interactions.Actions;publicclassMouse {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/mouse_interaction.html\");WebElement box = driver.findElement(By.id(\"clickable\"));newActions(driver).doubleClick(box).perform();System.out.println(\"double: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"click-status\")).getText());newActions(driver).contextClick(box).perform();System.out.println(\"right: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"click-status\")).getText());newActions(driver).moveToElement(driver.findElement(By.id(\"hover\"))).perform();System.out.println(\"hover: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"move-status\")).getText());WebElement from = driver.findElement(By.id(\"draggable\"));WebElement to = driver.findElement(By.id(\"droppable\"));newActions(driver).dragAndDrop(from, to).perform();System.out.println(\"drag: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"drop-status\")).getText());newActions(driver).moveToLocation(8, 12).perform();System.out.println(\"at: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"absolute-location\")).getText());newActions(driver).moveByOffset(13, 15).perform();System.out.println(\"moved by: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"absolute-location\")).getText()); driver.quit(); }} Build a chain, then perform() sends it to the browser in one request. A right click. Move to the centre of the element. Nothing is clicked. Move to from, press, move to to, release. A point measured from the top left of the viewport. From wherever the pointer is now. The driver remembered it between chains. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) double: double-clicked right:"],[10,"mouse","How actions work, and the mouse","context-clicked hover: hovered drag: dropped at: 8, 12 moved by: 21, 27 main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.action_chains importActionChainsfrom selenium.webdriver.common.actions.action_builder importActionBuilderfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/mouse_interaction.html\")box = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"clickable\")ActionChains(driver).double_click(box).perform()print(\"double:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"click-status\").text)ActionChains(driver).context_click(box).perform()print(\"right:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"click-status\").text)ActionChains(driver).move_to_element(driver.find_element(By.ID, \"hover\")).perform()print(\"hover:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"move-status\").text)source = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"draggable\")target = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"droppable\")ActionChains(driver).drag_and_drop(source, target).perform()print(\"drag:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"drop-status\").text)builder = ActionBuilder(driver)builder.pointer_action.move_to_location(8, 12)builder.perform()print(\"at:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"absolute-location\").text)ActionChains(driver).move_by_offset(13, 15).perform()print(\"moved by:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"absolute-location\").text)driver.quit() Build a chain, then perform() sends it to the browser in one request. A right click. Move to the centre of the element. Nothing is clicked. Move to source, press, move to target, release. The lower level builder. Python has no move_to_location on ActionChains. A point measured from the top left of the viewport. From wherever the pointer is now. The driver remembered it between chains. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) double: double-clicked right: context-clicked hover: hovered drag: dropped at: 8, 12 moved by: 21, 27 main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, Origin } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction mouse() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/mouse_interaction.html');const box = await driver.findElement(By.id('clickable'));await driver.actions().doubleClick(box).perform(); console.log('double: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('click-status')).getText());await driver.actions().contextClick(box).perform(); console.log('right: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('click-status')).getText());await driver.actions().move({ origin: await driver.findElement(By.id('hover')) }).perform(); console.log('hover: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('move-status')).getText());const from = await driver.findElement(By.id('draggable'));const to = await driver.findElement(By.id('droppable'));await driver.actions().dragAndDrop(from, to).perform(); console.log('drag: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('drop-status')).getText());await driver.actions().move({ x: 8, y: 12 }).perform(); console.log('at: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('absolute-location')).getText());await driver.actions().move({ x: 13, y: 15, origin: Origin.POINTER }).perform(); console.log('moved by: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('absolute-location')).getText());await driver.quit();})(); Build a chain, then perform() sends it to the browser in one request. A right click. Move to the centre of the element. Nothing is clicked. Move to from, press, move to to, release. With no origin, x and y are measured from the top left of the viewport. From wherever the pointer is now. The driver remembered it between chains. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) double: double-clicked right: context-clicked hover: hovered drag: dropped at: 8, 12 moved by: 21, 27 The pointer position is kept by the driver between chains. That is why move by offset (13, 15) after move to location (8, 12) printed 21, 27. The driver keeps pressed keys and buttons too, until the chain releases them or the test calls release actions. Mouse action Java Python JavaScript"],[10,"mouse","How actions work, and the mouse","Double click doubleClick(el) double_click(el) doubleClick(el) Right click contextClick(el) context_click(el) contextClick(el) Hover moveToElement(el) move_to_element(el) move({{origin: el}}) Press and hold clickAndHold(el) click_and_hold(el) press() Drag and drop dragAndDrop(a, b) drag_and_drop(a, b) dragAndDrop(a, b) Point in the viewport moveToLocation(x, y) ActionBuilder then move_to_location(x, y) move({{x, y}}) From the current point moveByOffset(x, y) move_by_offset(x, y) move({{x, y, origin: Origin.POINTER}}) Release everything ((RemoteWebDriver) driver).resetInputState() ActionBuilder(driver).clear_actions() actions().clear() Remember A pointer move has a duration. In the bindings used here, Python uses 250 ms by default and JavaScript 100 ms. Some drag libraries ignore a move that takes 0 ms, so if a drag does nothing, try a chain with a longer move. Real world scenario: a Kanban board A task board lets a user drag a card from To do to Done. element.click() cannot express that. The test finds the card and the Done column, calls drag and drop, then checks that the card is now inside the Done column. When the board is built with a library that needs several mouse moves, the test builds the chain by hand: click and hold the card, move by an offset, move to the column, release. Lesson 2 of 3"],[10,"keyboard","Keyboard actions","A key source sends keyDown and keyUp. sendKeys(\"abc\") inside a chain becomes a key down and key up for every character. Special keys such as Shift, Control, Enter and Backspace are characters from a private Unicode range. Shift is U+E008, Control is U+E009 and Enter is U+E007, which you can see in the payload of the Shift plan in the animation above. Hold Shift, select all with the right modifier, and type into an element KeyboardActions.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.Keys;import org.openqa.selenium.Platform;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.interactions.Actions;publicclassKeyboardActions {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/single_text_input.html\");WebElement input = driver.findElement(By.id(\"textInput\"));newActions(driver) .keyDown(Keys.SHIFT) .sendKeys(\"abc\") .keyUp(Keys.SHIFT) .sendKeys(\"def\") .perform();System.out.println(\"typed: \" + input.getAttribute(\"value\"));Keys cmdCtrl = Platform.getCurrent().is(Platform.MAC) ? Keys.COMMAND : Keys.CONTROL;newActions(driver).keyDown(cmdCtrl).sendKeys(\"a\").keyUp(cmdCtrl).sendKeys(Keys.BACK_SPACE).perform();System.out.println(\"after select all and delete: '\" + input.getAttribute(\"value\") + \"'\");newActions(driver).sendKeys(input, \"Selenium!\").perform();System.out.println(\"typed into element: \" + input.getAttribute(\"value\")); driver.quit(); }} Press Shift and keep holding it. Goes to the element that has focus. The page focuses this box on load. Let go of Shift. Select all is Command A on a Mac and Control A elsewhere. Clicks the element first, then types. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: ABCdef after select all and delete: '' typed into element: Selenium! main.py import sysfrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.action_chains importActionChainsfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.common.keys importKeysdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/single_text_input.html\")box = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"textInput\")ActionChains(driver) \\ .key_down(Keys.SHIFT) \\ .send_keys(\"abc\") \\ .key_up(Keys.SHIFT) \\ .send_keys(\"def\") \\ .perform()print(\"typed:\", box.get_attribute(\"value\"))cmd_ctrl = Keys.COMMANDif sys.platform == \"darwin\"elseKeys.CONTROLActionChains(driver).key_down(cmd_ctrl).send_keys(\"a\").key_up(cmd_ctrl).send_keys(Keys.BACKSPACE).perform()print(\"after select all and delete: '\" + box.get_attribute(\"value\") + \"'\")ActionChains(driver).send_keys_to_element(box, \"Selenium!\").perform()print(\"typed into element:\", box.get_attribute(\"value\"))driver.quit() Shift is held for abc only. send_keys goes to the box that has focus. Select all is Command A on a Mac and Control A elsewhere. Clicks the element first, then types. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: ABCdef after select all and delete: '' typed into element: Selenium! main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, Key } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction keyboard() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/single_text_input.html');const input = await driver.findElement(By.id('textInput'));await driver.actions() .keyDown(Key.SHIFT) .sendKeys('abc') .keyUp(Key.SHIFT) .sendKeys('def') .perform(); console.log('typed: ' + await input.getAttribute('value'));const cmdCtrl = process.platform === 'darwin' ? Key.COMMAND : Key.CONTROL;await driver.actions().keyDown(cmdCtrl).sendKeys('a').keyUp(cmdCtrl).sendKeys(Key.BACK_SPACE).perform(); console.log(\"after select all and delete: '\" + await input.getAttribute('value') + \"'\");await driver.actions().click(input).sendKeys('Selenium!').perform(); console.log('typed into element: ' + await input.getAttribute('value'));await driver.quit();})();"],[10,"keyboard","Keyboard actions","Press Shift and keep holding it. Goes to the element that has focus. The page focuses this box on load. Let go of Shift. Select all is Command A on a Mac and Control A elsewhere. JavaScript has no send keys to element in actions, so click first, then type. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) typed: ABCdef after select all and delete: '' typed into element: Selenium! Look at the first line of output. Shift was held for abc and released before def, so the box shows capitals only for the first three letters. The second line shows an empty value: Control A selected everything and Backspace deleted it. On a Mac that shortcut uses Command, which is why the code picks the key from the platform. Key method What it does keyDown(key) Press a key and keep it down. Meant for modifier keys. keyUp(key) Release it. Forget this and the key stays down for the next chain. sendKeys(text) Type into whatever has focus, one key down and key up per character. sendKeys(element, text) Click the element first, then type. Java and Python only. Common mistake Actions type into the focused element. If nothing has focus, the keys go to the page body and the test seems to type into nothing. Element sendKeys focuses the element itself, which is why it is the better choice for plain typing. Lesson 3 of 3"],[10,"wheel","Scroll wheel actions","The wheel source turns the mouse wheel. The documentation offers three cases: scroll an element into view, scroll by an amount, and scroll by an amount starting from a point or an element. The last one matters when only part of the page scrolls, such as a frame or a list with its own scroll bar. Scroll to an element, by an amount, and inside a frame Wheel.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.interactions.Actions;import org.openqa.selenium.interactions.WheelInput;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;publicclassWheel {staticfinalStringIN_VIEW = \"const r = arguments[0].getBoundingClientRect(); return r.top >= 0 && r.bottom <= window.innerHeight;\";publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver; driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html\");WebElement frame = driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"iframe\"));System.out.println(\"frame in view before: \" + js.executeScript(IN_VIEW, frame));newActions(driver).scrollToElement(frame).perform();System.out.println(\"frame in view after: \" + js.executeScript(IN_VIEW, frame)); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html\");newActions(driver).scrollByAmount(0, 300).perform();System.out.println(\"scrollY right after perform: \" + js.executeScript(\"return window.scrollY\"));newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(d -> ((Number) js.executeScript(\"return window.scrollY\")).intValue() >= 300);System.out.println(\"scrollY after waiting: \" + js.executeScript(\"return window.scrollY\")); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame.html\");WebElement inner = driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"iframe\"));newActions(driver).scrollFromOrigin(WheelInput.ScrollOrigin.fromElement(inner), 0, 200).perform(); driver.switchTo().frame(inner);newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(d -> ((Number) js.executeScript(\"return window.scrollY\")).intValue() > 0);System.out.println(\"frame scrollY: \" + js.executeScript(\"return window.scrollY\") + \" of at most \" + js.executeScript(\"return document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight\")); driver.quit(); }} The page starts with a 5000 px tall block, so the iframe is far below. Scroll until the bottom of the element is in view. Load it again. A refresh would keep the scroll position. Like turning the wheel: 300 px down from the top left of the viewport. The scroll can land after perform returns, so wait for it. Turn the wheel with the pointer over the frame, so the frame scrolls. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) frame in view before: false frame in view after: true scrollY right after perform: 300 scrollY after waiting: 300 frame scrollY: 118 of at most 118 main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.action_chains importActionChainsfrom selenium.webdriver.common.actions.wheel_input importScrollOriginfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitIN_VIEW = \"const r = arguments[0].getBoundingClientRect(); return r.top >= 0 && r.bottom <= window.innerHeight;\"driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html\")frame = driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"iframe\")print(\"frame in view before:\", driver.execute_script(IN_VIEW, frame))ActionChains(driver).scroll_to_element(frame).perform()print(\"frame in view after:\", driver.execute_script(IN_VIEW,"],[10,"wheel","Scroll wheel actions","frame))driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html\")ActionChains(driver).scroll_by_amount(0, 300).perform()print(\"scrollY right after perform:\", driver.execute_script(\"return window.scrollY\"))WebDriverWait(driver, 2).until(lambda d: d.execute_script(\"return window.scrollY\") >= 300)print(\"scrollY after waiting:\", driver.execute_script(\"return window.scrollY\"))driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame.html\")inner = driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"iframe\")ActionChains(driver).scroll_from_origin(ScrollOrigin.from_element(inner), 0, 200).perform()driver.switch_to.frame(inner)WebDriverWait(driver, 2).until(lambda d: d.execute_script(\"return window.scrollY\") > 0)print(\"frame scrollY:\", driver.execute_script(\"return window.scrollY\"), \"of at most\", driver.execute_script(\"return document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight\"))driver.quit() The page starts with a 5000 px tall block, so the iframe is far below. Scroll until the bottom of the element is in view. Load it again. A refresh would keep the scroll position. Like turning the wheel: 300 px down from the top left of the viewport. The scroll can land after perform returns, so wait for it. Turn the wheel with the pointer over the frame, so the frame scrolls. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) frame in view before: False frame in view after: True scrollY right after perform: 0 scrollY after waiting: 300 frame scrollY: 118 of at most 118 main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');constIN_VIEW = 'const r = arguments[0].getBoundingClientRect(); return r.top >= 0 && r.bottom <= window.innerHeight;';(asyncfunction wheel() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html');const frame = await driver.findElement(By.tagName('iframe')); console.log('frame in view before: ' + await driver.executeScript(IN_VIEW, frame));await driver.actions().scroll(0, 0, 0, 0, frame).perform(); console.log('frame in view after: ' + await driver.executeScript(IN_VIEW, frame));await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html');await driver.actions().scroll(0, 0, 0, 300).perform(); console.log('scrollY right after perform: ' + await driver.executeScript('return window.scrollY'));await driver.wait(async () => (await driver.executeScript('return window.scrollY')) >= 300, 2000); console.log('scrollY after waiting: ' + await driver.executeScript('return window.scrollY'));await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame.html');const inner = await driver.findElement(By.tagName('iframe'));await driver.actions().scroll(0, 0, 0, 200, inner).perform();await driver.switchTo().frame(inner);await driver.wait(async () => (await driver.executeScript('return window.scrollY')) > 0, 2000); console.log('frame scrollY: ' + await driver.executeScript('return window.scrollY') + ' of at most ' + await driver.executeScript('return document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight'));await driver.quit();})(); The page starts with a 5000 px tall block, so the iframe is far below. scroll(x, y, deltaX, deltaY, origin). With an element as origin and no delta, it scrolls the element into view. Load it again. A refresh would keep the scroll position. Like turning the wheel: 300 px down from the top left of the viewport. The scroll can land after perform returns, so wait for it. Turn the wheel with the pointer over the frame, so the frame scrolls. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) frame in view before: false frame in view after: true scrollY right after perform: 0 scrollY after waiting: 300 frame scrollY: 118 of at most 118 The output has two"],[10,"wheel","Scroll wheel actions","lessons. First, the frame was out of view and became visible after scroll to element. Second, scroll by amount is a real wheel event. In the Python and JavaScript runs the page had not moved yet when perform() returned and read 0. In the Java run it had already moved. The timing varies from run to run, and every language read 300 after the wait. Treat a scroll like any other change on the page and wait for its result. The last line shows that asking the frame to scroll 200 px moved it only as far as its content allows. Wheel action Java Python JavaScript Into view scrollToElement(el) scroll_to_element(el) scroll(0, 0, 0, 0, el) By an amount scrollByAmount(0, 300) scroll_by_amount(0, 300) scroll(0, 0, 0, 300) From an element scrollFromOrigin(ScrollOrigin.fromElement(el), 0, 200) scroll_from_origin(ScrollOrigin.from_element(el), 0, 200) scroll(0, 0, 0, 200, el) Real world scenario: an endless product feed A shop loads 20 more products each time the user reaches the bottom of the list. The test counts the products, scrolls the last one into view with the wheel, waits until the count grows, and repeats until it finds the product it needs. Scrolling with JavaScript would move the page without firing the wheel events some feeds listen for, so the wheel action is closer to a real user. One idea, four depths Actions let a test do what a person does with a mouse and keyboard: double click, right click, hover, drag, hold Shift and scroll. Build a chain, then call perform(). Each device is an input source. The browser runs the steps tick by tick, and waits for the slowest step in each tick. Perform Actions takes a list of input sources, each with a list of actions. Pointer moves have durations and origins: the viewport, the current pointer or an element centre. The driver keeps the input state between calls until Release Actions resets it. Because a whole chain is one request, timing inside it is exact and does not suffer from network delay between steps. Moves with a duration are broken into several pointer events, which is what some drag and drop and drawing libraries need."],[11,"","JavaScript execution and cookies","Running JavaScript in the page, async scripts, and working with cookies. · execute script · javascript executor · async script · promise · script timeout · cookies · httponly · samesite · invalid cookie domain Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 4: Synchronise and control›JavaScript and cookies Step 4 · Topic 4 of 4 · Intermediate Run your own JavaScript in the page and read the result in Java, Python or JavaScript, wait for asynchronous scripts, and add, read and delete cookies. About 45 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Run JavaScript in the page 2Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout 3Cookies Lesson 1 of 3"],[11,"javascript","Run JavaScript in the page","Sometimes a test needs something that no WebDriver command offers: read a value from the page state, scroll a box by a few pixels, or check how many items a list has without finding them all. executeScript sends a piece of JavaScript to the browser and returns the result to your code. The browser runs your text as the body of a function. That is why every script that gives back a value starts with return, and why 1 + 1 on its own gives back nothing. The values you pass after the script arrive as arguments[0], arguments[1] and so on. A WebElement you pass in becomes the real DOM node inside the script. What happens to an executeScript callPick a value to return and a language, then follow the script to the browser and the answer back. Step 0 of 5 The script returns Your language Command Step through the call. Try this Return a whole number and switch the language. The JSON in step 4 stays the same, only the last step changes. Pick Nothing (no return). The browser runs 1 + 1 but the function gives back undefined, which travels as null. Switch to executeAsyncScript and watch step 3: the browser waits for the callback, not for return. Run JavaScript, pass an element in, and see what type comes back RunScript.java import java.util.List;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassRunScript {staticString type(Object o) { return o == null ? \"null\" : o.getClass().getSimpleName(); }publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver; driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/javascriptPage.html\");for (String script : List.of(\"return 42\", \"return 4.5\", \"return 'hi'\", \"return [1, 'a']\", \"return {a: 1}\", \"return undefined\", \"return document.getElementById('dynamo')\", \"1 + 1\")) {System.out.println(script + \" -> \" + type(js.executeScript(script))); }WebElement dinner = driver.findElement(By.id(\"dynamo\"));System.out.println(\"arguments: \" + js.executeScript(\"return arguments[0].innerText + ' / ' + arguments[1]\", dinner, 7)); js.executeScript(\"arguments[0].click();\", driver.findElement(By.id(\"updatediv\")));System.out.println(\"after js click: \" + dinner.getText()); driver.quit(); }} The Java class of whatever came back. Every driver class implements JavascriptExecutor, so this cast is safe. Run the script as the body of a function and convert the result. Extra arguments arrive in the script as arguments[0], arguments[1] and so on. A click done by JavaScript. It skips the checks a real click makes. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) return 42 -> Long return 4.5 -> Double return 'hi' -> String return [1, 'a'] -> ArrayList return {a: 1} -> LinkedHashMap return undefined -> null return document.getElementById('dynamo') -> RemoteWebElement 1 + 1 -> null arguments: What's for dinner? / 7 after js click: Fish and chips! main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/javascriptPage.html\")for script in [\"return 42\", \"return 4.5\", \"return 'hi'\", \"return [1, 'a']\", \"return {a: 1}\", \"return undefined\", \"return document.getElementById('dynamo')\", \"1 + 1\"]:print(script, \"->\", type(driver.execute_script(script)).__name__)dinner = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"dynamo\")print(\"arguments:\", driver.execute_script(\"return arguments[0].innerText + ' / ' + arguments[1]\", dinner, 7))driver.execute_script(\"arguments[0].click();\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"updatediv\"))print(\"after js click:\", dinner.text)driver.quit() Run the script as the body of a function and convert the result. Extra arguments arrive in the script as arguments[0], arguments[1] and so on. A click done by JavaScript. It skips the checks a real click makes. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) return 42"],[11,"javascript","Run JavaScript in the page","-> int return 4.5 -> float return 'hi' -> str return [1, 'a'] -> list return {a: 1} -> dict return undefined -> NoneType return document.getElementById('dynamo') -> WebElement 1 + 1 -> NoneType arguments: What's for dinner? / 7 after js click: Fish and chips! main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');const type = v => v === null ? 'null' : Array.isArray(v) ? 'Array' : typeof v === 'object' ? v.constructor.name : typeof v;(asyncfunction runScript() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/javascriptPage.html');for (const script of [\"return 42\", \"return 4.5\", \"return 'hi'\", \"return [1, 'a']\", \"return {a: 1}\", \"return undefined\", \"return document.getElementById('dynamo')\", \"1 + 1\"]) { console.log(script + ' -> ' + type(await driver.executeScript(script))); }const dinner = await driver.findElement(By.id('dynamo')); console.log('arguments: ' + await driver.executeScript(\"return arguments[0].innerText + ' / ' + arguments[1]\", dinner, 7));await driver.executeScript('arguments[0].click();', await driver.findElement(By.id('updatediv'))); console.log('after js click: ' + await dinner.getText());await driver.quit();})(); A readable name for whatever came back. Run the script as the body of a function and convert the result. Extra arguments arrive in the script as arguments[0], arguments[1] and so on. A click done by JavaScript. It skips the checks a real click makes. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) return 42 -> number return 4.5 -> number return 'hi' -> string return [1, 'a'] -> Array return {a: 1} -> Object return undefined -> null return document.getElementById('dynamo') -> WebElement 1 + 1 -> null arguments: What's for dinner? / 7 after js click: Fish and chips! The same JavaScript value lands as a different type in each language. Look at the three outputs side by side: a whole number is a Long in Java, an int in Python and a number in JavaScript. A DOM node comes back as a WebElement you can click. undefined and a missing return both become null or None. Common mistake A click done with JavaScript always works, even on a covered or disabled element, because it skips every check a user faces. A test that passes with a JavaScript click may be hiding a real bug. Use it only when you know why the real click cannot work. Real world scenario: reading the cart total A shop keeps the cart in a JavaScript object called window.cart. The test adds three items through the user interface, then reads return window.cart.items.length and return window.cart.total with one script each, and compares them with what the page shows. If the numbers match the state but not the screen, the bug is in the rendering. Lesson 2 of 3"],[11,"async","Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout","Some answers only exist later: a timer, a network call, an animation that ends. executeAsyncScript adds a callback function as the last argument of your script. The command waits until your script calls it, and returns the value you pass to it. A script that returns a promise through plain executeScript is also waited for, because the W3C spec awaits a returned promise. Asynchronous scripts, promises, the script timeout and a JavaScript error AsyncScript.java import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptException;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.ScriptTimeoutException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassAsyncScript {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver; driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\");Object waited = js.executeAsyncScript(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('waited'), 300);\");System.out.println(\"async: \" + waited);System.out.println(\"promise: \" + js.executeScript(\"return new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('from promise'), 200));\")); driver.manage().timeouts().scriptTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(200));try { js.executeAsyncScript(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('late'), 500);\"); } catch (ScriptTimeoutException e) {System.out.println(\"too slow: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]); }try { js.executeScript(\"return nosuch.thing;\"); } catch (JavascriptException e) {System.out.println(\"broken script: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]); } driver.quit(); }} Selenium adds a callback as the last argument. The command returns when the script calls it. A returned promise is awaited by the browser before it answers. How long a script may run. The default is 30 seconds. The callback came after 500 ms, the limit was 200 ms. A JavaScript error in the page becomes javascript error. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) async: waited promise: from promise too slow: script timeout broken script: javascript error: nosuch is not defined main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importJavascriptException, TimeoutExceptiondriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")waited = driver.execute_async_script(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('waited'), 300);\")print(\"async:\", waited)print(\"promise:\", driver.execute_script(\"return new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('from promise'), 200));\"))driver.set_script_timeout(0.2)try: driver.execute_async_script(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('late'), 500);\")exceptTimeoutExceptionas e:print(\"too slow:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])try: driver.execute_script(\"return nosuch.thing;\")exceptJavascriptExceptionas e:print(\"broken script:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])driver.quit() Selenium adds a callback as the last argument. The command returns when the script calls it. A returned promise is awaited by the browser before it answers. How long a script may run, in seconds. The default is 30. The callback came after 500 ms, the limit was 200 ms. A JavaScript error in the page becomes javascript error. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) async: waited promise: from promise too slow: script timeout broken script: javascript error: nosuch is not defined main.js const { Builder, Browser, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction asyncScript() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');const waited = await driver.executeAsyncScript(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('waited'), 300);\"); console.log('async: ' + waited); console.log('promise: ' + await driver.executeScript(\"return new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('from"],[11,"async","Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout","promise'), 200));\"));await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ script: 200 });try {await driver.executeAsyncScript(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('late'), 500);\"); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.ScriptTimeoutError)) throw e; console.log('too slow: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]); }try {await driver.executeScript('return nosuch.thing;'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.JavascriptError)) throw e; console.log('broken script: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]); }await driver.quit();})(); Selenium adds a callback as the last argument. The command returns when the script calls it. A returned promise is awaited by the browser before it answers. How long a script may run, in ms. The default is 30000. The callback came after 500 ms, the limit was 200 ms. A JavaScript error in the page becomes javascript error. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) async: waited promise: from promise too slow: script timeout broken script: javascript error: nosuch is not defined Error Java Python JavaScript The script took longer than the script timeout ScriptTimeoutException TimeoutException ScriptTimeoutError The script threw an error JavascriptException JavascriptException JavascriptError Remember The script timeout is 30 seconds by default, as chapter 2.1 showed. It covers both sync and async scripts. A short timeout makes a broken async script fail fast. Lesson 3 of 3"],[11,"cookies","Cookies","A cookie is a small named value that a site stores in the browser and gets back with every request. Logins, language choices, consent banners and shopping carts usually live in cookies. Selenium can add, read and delete them, which lets a test skip a slow login screen or start with the banner already accepted. Add, read, protect and delete cookies Cookies.java import org.openqa.selenium.Cookie;import org.openqa.selenium.InvalidCookieDomainException;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassCookies {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();try { driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie(\"key\", \"value\")); } catch (InvalidCookieDomainException e) {System.out.println(\"before visiting: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]); } driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\"); driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie(\"key\", \"value\"));Cookie c = driver.manage().getCookieNamed(\"key\");System.out.println(\"key: \" + c.getValue() + \", domain \" + c.getDomain() + \", sameSite \" + c.getSameSite() + \", secure \" + c.isSecure() + \", httpOnly \" + c.isHttpOnly()); driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie.Builder(\"foo\", \"bar\").sameSite(\"Strict\").build()); driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie.Builder(\"secret\", \"s\").isHttpOnly(true).build());System.out.println(\"cookies: \" + driver.manage().getCookies().size());System.out.println(\"page script sees: \" + ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(\"return document.cookie\")); driver.manage().deleteCookieNamed(\"foo\");System.out.println(\"missing cookie: \" + driver.manage().getCookieNamed(\"foo\")); driver.manage().deleteAllCookies();System.out.println(\"after delete all: \" + driver.manage().getCookies().size()); driver.quit(); }} The browser is still on an empty start page with no domain. Visit the domain first. A small page is enough. Read one cookie by name. The builder sets the other fields. httpOnly hides the cookie from page JavaScript. Asking for a cookie that is not there gives null, not an error. A clean start, for example before logging in as another user. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) before visiting: invalid cookie domain key: value, domain www.selenium.dev, sameSite Lax, secure true, httpOnly false cookies: 3 page script sees: key=value; foo=bar missing cookie: null after delete all: 0 main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importInvalidCookieDomainExceptiondriver = webdriver.Chrome()try: driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"key\", \"value\": \"value\"})exceptInvalidCookieDomainExceptionas e:print(\"before visiting:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"key\", \"value\": \"value\"})c = driver.get_cookie(\"key\")print(f\"key: {c['value']}, domain {c['domain']}, sameSite {c['sameSite']}, secure {c['secure']}, httpOnly {c['httpOnly']}\")driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"foo\", \"value\": \"bar\", \"sameSite\": \"Strict\"})driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"secret\", \"value\": \"s\", \"httpOnly\": True})print(\"cookies:\", len(driver.get_cookies()))print(\"page script sees:\", driver.execute_script(\"return document.cookie\"))driver.delete_cookie(\"foo\")print(\"missing cookie:\", driver.get_cookie(\"foo\"))driver.delete_all_cookies()print(\"after delete all:\", len(driver.get_cookies()))driver.quit() The browser is still on an empty start page with no domain. Visit the domain first. A small page is enough. Read one cookie by name. It comes back as a dict. Any other field goes in the same dict. httpOnly hides the cookie from page JavaScript. Asking for a cookie that is not there gives None, not an error. A clean start, for example before logging in as another user. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) before visiting: invalid cookie domain key: value, domain www.selenium.dev, sameSite Lax, secure True, httpOnly False cookies: 3 page script sees: key=value; foo=bar missing cookie: None"],[11,"cookies","Cookies","after delete all: 0 main.js const { Builder, Browser, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction cookies() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();try {await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'key', value: 'value' }); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.InvalidCookieDomainError)) throw e; console.log('before visiting: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]); }await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'key', value: 'value' });const c = await driver.manage().getCookie('key'); console.log(`key: ${c.value}, domain ${c.domain}, sameSite ${c.sameSite}, secure ${c.secure}, httpOnly ${c.httpOnly}`);await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'foo', value: 'bar', sameSite: 'Strict' });await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'secret', value: 's', httpOnly: true }); console.log('cookies: ' + (await driver.manage().getCookies()).length); console.log('page script sees: ' + await driver.executeScript('return document.cookie'));await driver.manage().deleteCookie('foo');try { console.log('missing cookie: ' + await driver.manage().getCookie('foo')); } catch (e) { console.log('missing cookie: ' + e.name); }await driver.manage().deleteAllCookies(); console.log('after delete all: ' + (await driver.manage().getCookies()).length);await driver.quit();})(); The browser is still on an empty start page with no domain. Visit the domain first. A small page is enough. Read one cookie by name. It comes back as an object. Any other field goes in the same object. httpOnly hides the cookie from page JavaScript. JavaScript raises NoSuchCookieError here, where Java and Python return null or None. A clean start, for example before logging in as another user. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) before visiting: invalid cookie domain key: value, domain www.selenium.dev, sameSite Lax, secure true, httpOnly false cookies: 3 page script sees: key=value; foo=bar missing cookie: NoSuchCookieError after delete all: 0 Four things in this run are worth remembering. A cookie can only be added for the domain the browser is on, so the first call failed with invalid cookie domain on the empty start page. A new cookie on this https page came back with secure true and sameSite Lax, which Chrome chose because the test did not say. The httpOnly cookie secret was stored but not shown to document.cookie. And a missing cookie is not an error in Java and Python, which return null and None, while JavaScript raises NoSuchCookieError. Field Meaning name, value Required. The data. domain, path Where the cookie is sent. They must match the current page. secure Only sent over https. httpOnly Hidden from page JavaScript. sameSite Strict, Lax or None. Whether the cookie goes with requests started by other sites. expiry Seconds since 1970. With no expiry the cookie lasts for the session. Real world scenario: skip the login screen Every test in a suite of 300 needs a logged in user. Logging in through the form takes 4 seconds. The suite logs in once through the API, gets the session cookie, and each test opens a small page on the domain, adds that cookie and goes straight to the page it tests. The form login is still tested once, in its own test. One idea, four depths Selenium can run JavaScript in the page and give you the answer, and it can read and set the cookies a site stores. executeScript runs your text as a function body, passes arguments in and converts the return value. executeAsyncScript waits for a callback. Cookies need the browser to be on their domain first. Execute Script and Execute Async Script are W3C commands that serialise arguments and results as JSON, with element references for DOM nodes. A returned promise is awaited. Cookie commands work on the cookies of the current browsing context domain. BiDi adds script.evaluate and script.callFunction with realms and preload scripts, and storage.getCookies and storage.setCookie with partitions, which can set"],[11,"cookies","Cookies","cookies without visiting the site first."],[12,"","Test frameworks: JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha","Running Selenium inside JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha, with data-driven tests and screenshots on failure. · junit · pytest · mocha · test framework · fixture · before each · after each · parameterized · data driven · screenshot on failure Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 5: Build a test suite›Test frameworks Step 5 · Topic 1 of 4 · Intermediate Turn Selenium scripts into real tests: setup and teardown with a fresh browser for each test, assertions, data-driven tests, and a screenshot whenever a test fails. About 55 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Selenium inside a test framework 2Data-driven tests 3A screenshot when a test fails Lesson 1 of 3"],[12,"runners","Selenium inside a test framework","Selenium drives the browser. It does not decide whether a test passed, it does not group tests, and it does not write a report. A test framework does that: JUnit 5 for Java, pytest for Python and Mocha for JavaScript are the ones the Selenium documentation uses in its own examples. The framework finds your tests, runs setup before each one and teardown after it, compares values with assertions and reports what passed and failed. What a test framework runs, and in which orderChoose the framework, the number of tests and which one fails. Step through the hooks and watch the browsers. Step 0 of 10 Framework Tests3 Failing test (0 for none)2 Browser 10 steps. Press Play. Try this With a failing test, watch the screenshot step: it comes before quit, while the page is still there. Switch to one browser for all tests. Fewer starts, but every later test inherits what the earlier ones left behind. Switch the framework. The order stays the same, only the names of the hooks change. A first test class: a fresh browser for every test WebFormTest.java importstatic org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;import org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach;import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassWebFormTest {WebDriver driver;@BeforeEachvoid startBrowser() { driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\"); }@Testvoid pageHasTheRightTitle() { assertEquals(\"Web form\", driver.getTitle()); }@Testvoid submittingShowsReceived() { driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\")).sendKeys(\"Selenium\"); driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")).click(); assertEquals(\"Received!\", driver.findElement(By.id(\"message\")).getText()); }@AfterEachvoid quitBrowser() { driver.quit(); }} A field, so every method of this test can reach the driver. Runs before every test method. One test: one behaviour, one reason to fail. Expected value first in JUnit, then the actual one. Runs after every test, also when the test failed. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with JUnit 5 ╷ ├─ JUnit Platform Suite ✔ ├─ JUnit Jupiter ✔ │ └─ WebFormTest ✔ │ ├─ submittingShowsReceived() ✔ │ └─ pageHasTheRightTitle() ✔ └─ JUnit Vintage ✔ Test run finished after 2285 ms [ 4 containers found ] [ 0 containers skipped ] [ 4 containers started ] [ 0 containers aborted ] [ 4 containers successful ] [ 0 containers failed ] [ 2 tests found ] [ 0 tests skipped ] [ 2 tests started ] [ 0 tests aborted ] [ 2 tests successful ] [ 0 tests failed ] test_example.py import pytestfrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBy@pytest.fixturedef driver(): driver = webdriver.Chrome() driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")yield driver driver.quit()def test_page_has_the_right_title(driver):assert driver.title == \"Web form\"def test_submitting_shows_received(driver): driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\").send_keys(\"Selenium\") driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\").click()assert driver.find_element(By.ID, \"message\").text == \"Received!\" A fixture is pytest's setup and teardown in one function. Everything before yield is setup. The test runs here. Everything after yield is teardown, and runs also when the test failed. Asking for a parameter called driver makes pytest run the fixture. A plain assert. pytest shows both sides when it fails. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with pytest .. [100%] 2 passed in 1.82s example.spec.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');const assert = require('assert');describe('Web form', function () {let driver; beforeEach(asyncfunction () { driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html'); }); it('has the right title', asyncfunction () { assert.strictEqual(await"],[12,"runners","Selenium inside a test framework","driver.getTitle(), 'Web form'); }); it('shows Received! after submit', asyncfunction () {await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text')).sendKeys('Selenium');await driver.findElement(By.css('button')).click(); assert.strictEqual(await driver.findElement(By.id('message')).getText(), 'Received!'); }); afterEach(asyncfunction () {await driver.quit(); });}); describe groups tests. Mocha gives every test a name. Runs before every test. One test. Node's own assert module. Actual first, then expected. Runs after every test, also when the test failed. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with Mocha Web form ✔ has the right title ✔ shows Received! after submit (229ms) 2 passing (2s) Job JUnit 5 pytest Mocha Before every test @BeforeEach fixture code before yield beforeEach After every test @AfterEach fixture code after yield afterEach Once for the class or file @BeforeAll, @AfterAll scope=\"module\" or \"session\" before, after Check a value assertEquals(expected, actual) assert actual == expected assert.strictEqual(actual, expected) Run it build tool or console launcher pytest mocha or npx mocha Remember The Selenium documentation recommends a fresh browser for every test. Then no cookie, login or open dialog can leak from one test into the next, and the tests can run in any order or in parallel. It costs a browser start per test, a price that is almost always worth paying. Real world scenario: the flaky Friday test A team shares one browser across a whole test class to save time. One test logs out at the end, and on Friday the runner orders the tests differently: the logout test runs before the cart test, and the cart test fails with a login page. Nothing in the cart test is wrong. Moving the driver into a before each and after each pair makes the failure disappear for good. Lesson 2 of 3"],[12,"datadriven","Data-driven tests","The login page has one form and many cases: right user and password, wrong password, unknown user. Copying the test three times means three places to fix when the form changes. A parameterized test writes the steps once and feeds them a table of data. The report shows one result per row. One test, three rows of data LoginTest.java importstatic org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;import java.time.Duration;import org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach;import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;import org.openqa.selenium.Alert;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;publicclassLoginTest {WebDriver driver;@BeforeEachvoid open() { driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html\"); }@ParameterizedTest(name = \"{0} / {1}\")@CsvSource({\"username, password, You have successfully logged in.\",\"username, wrong, Please enter valid credentials\",\"admin, password, Please enter valid credentials\" })void login(String user, String password, String expected) { driver.findElement(By.id(\"username-field\")).sendKeys(user); driver.findElement(By.id(\"password-field\")).sendKeys(password); driver.findElement(By.id(\"login-form-submit\")).click();Alert alert = newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()); assertEquals(expected, alert.getText()); alert.accept(); }@AfterEachvoid close() { driver.quit(); }} The name shows the data of each run in the report. Each line is one run of the test: username, password, expected message. JUnit passes the columns in as parameters. The page answers with an alert. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with JUnit 5 ╷ ├─ JUnit Platform Suite ✔ ├─ JUnit Jupiter ✔ │ └─ LoginTest ✔ │ └─ login(String, String, String) ✔ │ ├─ username / password ✔ │ ├─ username / wrong ✔ │ └─ admin / password ✔ └─ JUnit Vintage ✔ Test run finished after 3574 ms [ 5 containers found ] [ 0 containers skipped ] [ 5 containers started ] [ 0 containers aborted ] [ 5 containers successful ] [ 0 containers failed ] [ 3 tests found ] [ 0 tests skipped ] [ 3 tests started ] [ 0 tests aborted ] [ 3 tests successful ] [ 0 tests failed ] test_example.py import pytestfrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions asECfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWait@pytest.fixturedef driver(): driver = webdriver.Chrome() driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html\")yield driver driver.quit()@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"user, password, expected\", [ (\"username\", \"password\", \"You have successfully logged in.\"), (\"username\", \"wrong\", \"Please enter valid credentials\"), (\"admin\", \"password\", \"Please enter valid credentials\"),])def test_login(driver, user, password, expected): driver.find_element(By.ID, \"username-field\").send_keys(user) driver.find_element(By.ID, \"password-field\").send_keys(password) driver.find_element(By.ID, \"login-form-submit\").click() alert = WebDriverWait(driver, 2).until(EC.alert_is_present())assert alert.text == expected alert.accept() Each tuple is one run of the test. pytest passes the columns in as parameters, next to the fixture. The page answers with an alert. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with pytest ... [100%] 3 passed in 3.00s example.spec.js const { Builder, Browser, By, until } = require('selenium-webdriver');const assert = require('assert');const rows = [ ['username', 'password', 'You have successfully logged in.'], ['username', 'wrong', 'Please enter valid credentials'], ['admin', 'password', 'Please enter valid credentials'],];describe('Login', function () {let driver; beforeEach(asyncfunction () { driver ="],[12,"datadriven","Data-driven tests","awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html'); });for (const [user, password, expected] of rows) { it(`${user} / ${password}`, asyncfunction () {await driver.findElement(By.id('username-field')).sendKeys(user);await driver.findElement(By.id('password-field')).sendKeys(password);await driver.findElement(By.id('login-form-submit')).click();const alert = await driver.wait(until.alertIsPresent(), 2000); assert.strictEqual(await alert.getText(), expected);await alert.accept(); }); } afterEach(asyncfunction () {await driver.quit(); });}); Each row is one run of the test. Mocha has no data annotation. A loop that calls it() once per row does the same job. The page answers with an alert. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with Mocha Login ✔ username / password (183ms) ✔ username / wrong (231ms) ✔ admin / password (187ms) 3 passing (2s) Look at each report. JUnit used the name pattern for every row and Mocha used the title built by the loop. pytest ran in quiet mode, so it printed one dot per row, three dots for three runs. Run it with -v and it lists each row with its data in the test id. When the second row fails one day, the report says which row, without opening the code. Remember Keep the data close to the test while it is small. When it grows, move it to a CSV or JSON file: JUnit has @CsvFileSource, and pytest and Mocha can read any file into the same list. Lesson 3 of 3"],[12,"failshot","A screenshot when a test fails","A red test in a report from the night run says what was expected, not what the page looked like. A screenshot taken at the moment of failure answers that. The trick is the order: the picture must be taken after the test failed but before the browser is closed. Each framework has its own place for that. A failing test that saves a screenshot before the browser closes ScreenshotOnFailure.java import java.nio.file.Files;import java.nio.file.Path;import java.nio.file.StandardCopyOption;import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.AfterTestExecutionCallback;import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtensionContext;import org.openqa.selenium.OutputType;import org.openqa.selenium.TakesScreenshot;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;publicclassScreenshotOnFailureimplementsAfterTestExecutionCallback {@Overridepublicvoid afterTestExecution(ExtensionContext context) throwsException {if (context.getExecutionException().isPresent()) {WebDriver driver = ((TitleTest) context.getRequiredTestInstance()).driver;Path shot = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE).toPath();Path target = Path.of(\"failure-\" + context.getRequiredTestMethod().getName() + \".png\");Files.copy(shot, target, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);System.out.println(\"saved \" + target + \" \" + (Files.size(target) > 0)); } }} Called right after the test method, before any AfterEach method. Present only when the test threw, so only when it failed. Name the file after the test. TitleTest.java importstatic org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;import org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach;import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;@ExtendWith(ScreenshotOnFailure.class)publicclassTitleTest {WebDriver driver;@BeforeEachvoid open() { driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\"); }@Testvoid titleIsWrongOnPurpose() { assertEquals(\"Web form!\", driver.getTitle()); }@AfterEachvoid close() { driver.quit(); }} Plug the extension into this test class. The extension reads this field. Wrong on purpose, so the test fails. Runs after the extension, so the browser is still open when the screenshot is taken. Real error from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with JUnit 5 saved failure-titleIsWrongOnPurpose.png true ╷ ├─ JUnit Platform Suite ✔ ├─ JUnit Jupiter ✔ │ └─ TitleTest ✔ │ └─ titleIsWrongOnPurpose() ✘ expected: <Web form!> but was: <Web form> └─ JUnit Vintage ✔ Failures (1): JUnit Jupiter:TitleTest:titleIsWrongOnPurpose() MethodSource [className = 'TitleTest', methodName = 'titleIsWrongOnPurpose', methodParameterTypes = ''] => org.opentest4j.AssertionFailedError: expected: <Web form!> but was: <Web form> org.junit.jupiter.api.AssertionFailureBuilder.build(AssertionFailureBuilder.java:151) org.junit.jupiter.api.AssertionFailureBuilder.buildAndThrow(AssertionFailureBuilder.java:132) org.junit.jupiter.api.AssertEquals.failNotEqual(AssertEquals.java:197) org.junit.jupiter.api.AssertEquals.assertEquals(AssertEquals.java:182) org.junit.jupiter.api.AssertEquals.assertEquals(AssertEquals.java:177) org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals(Assertions.java:1145) TitleTest.titleIsWrongOnPurpose(TitleTest.java:22) java.base/java.lang.reflect.Method.invoke(Method.java:580) java.base/java.util.ArrayList.forEach(ArrayList.java:1596) java.base/java.util.ArrayList.forEach(ArrayList.java:1596) Test run finished after 1368 ms [ 4 containers found ] [ 0 containers skipped ] [ 4 containers started ] [ 0 containers aborted ] [ 4 containers successful ] [ 0 containers failed ] [ 1 tests found ] [ 0 tests skipped ] [ 1 tests started ] [ 0 tests aborted ] [ 0 tests successful ] [ 1 tests failed ] conftest.py import osimport pytestfrom selenium import webdriver@pytest.hookimpl(wrapper=True, tryfirst=True)def pytest_runtest_makereport(item, call): report = yield setattr(item,"],[12,"failshot","A screenshot when a test fails","\"rep_\" + report.when, report)return report@pytest.fixturedef driver(request): driver = webdriver.Chrome()yield driverif request.node.rep_call.failed: name = f\"failure-{request.node.name}.png\" driver.save_screenshot(name)print(\"saved\", name, os.path.getsize(name) > 0) driver.quit() A hook that sees every test report as pytest builds it. Keep the report on the test item: rep_setup, rep_call and rep_teardown. Did the test body fail? Name the file after the test. Take the picture before quit. test_example.py from selenium import webdriverdef test_title_is_wrong_on_purpose(driver): driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")assert driver.title == \"Web form!\" The driver fixture comes from conftest.py. Wrong on purpose, so the test fails. Real error from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with pytest F [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== ________________________ test_title_is_wrong_on_purpose ________________________ driver = <selenium.webdriver.chrome.webdriver.WebDriver (session=\"25c17be969e4239e647e307513ab5469\")> def test_title_is_wrong_on_purpose(driver): driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\") > assert driver.title == \"Web form!\" E AssertionError: assert 'Web form' == 'Web form!' E E - Web form! E ? - E + Web form test_example.py:6: AssertionError --------------------------- Captured stdout teardown --------------------------- saved failure-test_title_is_wrong_on_purpose.png True =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_example.py::test_title_is_wrong_on_purpose - AssertionError: asse... 1 failed in 0.90s example.spec.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const assert = require('assert');const fs = require('fs');describe('Title', function () {let driver; beforeEach(asyncfunction () { driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html'); }); it('is wrong on purpose', asyncfunction () { assert.strictEqual(await driver.getTitle(), 'Web form!'); }); afterEach(asyncfunction () {if (this.currentTest.state === 'failed') {const name = 'failure-' + this.currentTest.title.replace(/ /g, '-') + '.png'; fs.writeFileSync(name, await driver.takeScreenshot(), 'base64'); console.log('saved ' + name + ' ' + (fs.statSync(name).size > 0)); }await driver.quit(); });}); Wrong on purpose, so the test fails. Mocha tells the hook how the test ended. Take the picture before quit. Real error from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with Mocha Title 1) is wrong on purpose saved failure-is-wrong-on-purpose.png true 0 passing (838ms) 1 failing 1) Title is wrong on purpose: AssertionError [ERR_ASSERTION]: Expected values to be strictly equal: + actual - expected + 'Web form' - 'Web form!' ^ + expected - actual -Web form +Web form! at Context.<anonymous> (example.spec.js:13:12) at process.processTicksAndRejections (node:internal/process/task_queues:105:5) Framework Where the screenshot code goes How it knows the test failed JUnit 5 An extension with AfterTestExecutionCallback, which runs before @AfterEach context.getExecutionException() is present pytest The fixture, after yield and before quit() A pytest_runtest_makereport hook in conftest.py stores the report on the test Mocha The afterEach hook, before quit() this.currentTest.state is \"failed\" The run on this page failed on purpose, and each language printed \"saved\" followed by true, which means the file was written and is not empty. The failure message itself shows the expected title \"Web form!\" and the real one, \"Web form\". One idea, four depths A test framework runs your Selenium code as tests: setup before, the test, teardown after, and a pass or fail for each. Start a fresh browser in the before each step and quit it in the after each step. Use parameterized tests for many rows of data and take a screenshot in the after step"],[12,"failshot","A screenshot when a test fails","when the test failed. Each framework has hooks at different points: JUnit extensions, pytest fixtures and hook functions, Mocha hooks with this.currentTest. Teardown runs whatever happened in the test, which is why quit belongs there. Fixtures with a wider scope, such as one browser per worker, trade isolation for speed. Combine them with deleteAllCookies and a fresh page in a function scoped fixture, and with parallel runners such as pytest-xdist or JUnit parallel execution."],[13,"","Page objects and page components","Page objects, page component objects and the rules for writing them. · page object · page object model · pom · page component · design pattern · maintainability · page factory Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 5: Build a test suite›Page objects Step 5 · Topic 2 of 4 · Intermediate Keep locators, waits and page checks in one class per page, so tests read like user steps and a UI change is fixed in one place. About 50 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Why page objects 2Write page objects and components 3Design choices and the limits of page objects Lesson 1 of 3"],[13,"why","Why page objects","A test that finds elements directly works fine on its own. The trouble starts at test number forty. The same By.cssSelector(\"button[type=submit]\") is now copied into forty tests, and when the developers rename the button, forty tests break and forty places need the same fix. The documentation describes a page object as an object oriented class that serves as an interface to a page of the application under test. Tests call its methods whenever they need the page. When the page changes, only the page object changes. The tests stay the same. One renamed button, with and without a page objectChoose how many tests use the page and how often each one uses the button, then step through a UI change. Step 0 of 5 Tests use Tests that use the page30 Uses of the button per test2 Minutes to fix one copy2 Step through the change. Try this Step to the end with copied locators and note the number of places to edit. Switch to a page object and step again. The tests still fail in step 3, but the fix is one edit. Raise the number of tests to 60. The page object number does not move. Without page objects With page objects Locators are copied into every test that uses the page Each locator is written once, inside the page object A test reads as a list of clicks and CSS selectors A test reads as what the user does: open the form, fill a field, submit A UI change breaks many tests in many files A UI change is fixed in one class Waiting and page checks are repeated The page object waits for its page once, in its constructor Real world scenario: the renamed button A shop has 60 checkout tests. The design team changes the Pay button from an id to a data attribute. Without page objects the change touches 60 files and two of them are missed, so the nightly run is red for a day. With a CheckoutPage class, one line changes in one file and all 60 tests pass again on the next run. Lesson 2 of 3"],[13,"pom","Write page objects and components","Below are two page objects, WebFormPage and ReceivedPage, one page component, Field, and three tests. Open each file in the example and walk it line by line. The tests never mention a locator. Two page objects, one component and three tests pages/WebFormPage.java package pages;import java.util.List;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;publicclassWebFormPage {staticfinalStringURL = \"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\";privatefinalBy fieldsBy = By.cssSelector(\"label.form-label\");privatefinalBy submitBy = By.cssSelector(\"button[type=submit]\");privatefinalWebDriver driver;publicWebFormPage(WebDriver driver) {this.driver = driver;if (!\"Web form\".equals(driver.getTitle())) {thrownewIllegalStateException(\"This is not the web form, it is: \" + driver.getTitle()); } }publicstaticWebFormPage open(WebDriver driver) { driver.get(URL);returnnewWebFormPage(driver); }publicList<Field> fields() {return driver.findElements(fieldsBy).stream().map(Field::new).toList(); }publicField field(String name) {return fields().stream().filter(f -> f.name().equals(name)).findFirst() .orElseThrow(() -> newIllegalArgumentException(\"No field called \" + name)); }publicReceivedPage submit() { driver.findElement(submitBy).click();returnnewReceivedPage(driver); }} Locators live here and nowhere else. The one check a page object should make: am I on the right page? Wrap every label element in a Field component. pages/Field.java package pages;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;publicclassField {privatefinalWebElement root;publicField(WebElement root) { this.root = root; }publicString name() { return root.getText().split(\"\\n\")[0].trim(); }privateWebElement control() { return root.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"input, textarea, select\")); }publicboolean isEnabled() { return control().isEnabled(); }publicField fill(String text) { control().clear(); control().sendKeys(text);returnthis; }} A page component: one label and the control inside it. The first line of the label is its name. Search inside this component only. Returning itself allows field(...).fill(...).fill(...). pages/ReceivedPage.java package pages;import java.time.Duration;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.ExpectedConditions;import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.WebDriverWait;publicclassReceivedPage {privatefinalWebDriver driver;publicReceivedPage(WebDriver driver) {this.driver = driver;newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(3)).until(ExpectedConditions.titleIs(\"Web form - target page\")); }publicString message() { return driver.findElement(By.id(\"message\")).getText(); }} The page arrives after a navigation, so wait for it instead of checking once. WebFormTest.java importstatic org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;importstatic org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;import java.util.List;import org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach;import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import pages.Field;import pages.ReceivedPage;import pages.WebFormPage;publicclassWebFormTest {WebDriver driver;@BeforeEachvoid start() { driver = newChromeDriver(); }@Testvoid submittingTextShowsReceived() {WebFormPage form = WebFormPage.open(driver); form.field(\"Text input\").fill(\"Selenium\");ReceivedPage received = form.submit(); assertEquals(\"Received!\", received.message()); }@Testvoid onlyOneFieldIsDisabled() {List<Field> fields = WebFormPage.open(driver).fields(); assertEquals(11, fields.size()); assertEquals(List.of(\"Disabled input\"), fields.stream().filter(f -> !f.isEnabled()).map(Field::name).toList()); }@Testvoid refusesTheWrongPage() { driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html\"); assertThrows(IllegalStateException.class, () -> newWebFormPage(driver)); }@AfterEachvoid stop() { driver.quit(); }} The test talks to pages, not to locators."],[13,"pom","Write page objects and components","submit() moves to another page, so it returns that page object. The assertion stays in the test. Eleven labelled controls on this page. The constructor checks the page and fails loudly. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with JUnit 5 ╷ ├─ JUnit Platform Suite ✔ ├─ JUnit Jupiter ✔ │ └─ WebFormTest ✔ │ ├─ refusesTheWrongPage() ✔ │ ├─ submittingTextShowsReceived() ✔ │ └─ onlyOneFieldIsDisabled() ✔ └─ JUnit Vintage ✔ Test run finished after 3086 ms [ 4 containers found ] [ 0 containers skipped ] [ 4 containers started ] [ 0 containers aborted ] [ 4 containers successful ] [ 0 containers failed ] [ 3 tests found ] [ 0 tests skipped ] [ 3 tests started ] [ 0 tests aborted ] [ 3 tests successful ] [ 0 tests failed ] pages/web_form_page.py from selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom pages.field importFieldfrom pages.received_page importReceivedPageclassWebFormPage:URL = \"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\"FIELDS = (By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"label.form-label\")SUBMIT = (By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button[type=submit]\")def __init__(self, driver): self.driver = driverif driver.title != \"Web form\":raiseValueError(\"This is not the web form, it is: \" + driver.title)@classmethoddef open(cls, driver): driver.get(cls.URL)return cls(driver)def fields(self):return [Field(e) for e in self.driver.find_elements(*self.FIELDS)]def field(self, name):for f in self.fields():if f.name() == name:return fraiseLookupError(\"No field called \" + name)def submit(self): self.driver.find_element(*self.SUBMIT).click()returnReceivedPage(self.driver) Locators live here and nowhere else. The one check a page object should make: am I on the right page? Wrap every label element in a Field component. pages/field.py from selenium.webdriver.common.by importByclassField:def __init__(self, root): self.root = rootdef name(self):return self.root.text.split(\"\\n\")[0].strip()def control(self):return self.root.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"input, textarea, select\")def is_enabled(self):return self.control().is_enabled()def fill(self, text): self.control().clear() self.control().send_keys(text)return self A page component: one label and the control inside it. The first line of the label is its name. Search inside this component only. Returning itself allows field(...).fill(...).fill(...). pages/received_page.py from selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions asECfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitclassReceivedPage:def __init__(self, driver): self.driver = driverWebDriverWait(driver, 3).until(EC.title_is(\"Web form - target page\"))def message(self):return self.driver.find_element(By.ID, \"message\").text The page arrives after a navigation, so wait for it instead of checking once. test_example.py import pytestfrom selenium import webdriverfrom pages.web_form_page importWebFormPage@pytest.fixturedef driver(): driver = webdriver.Chrome()yield driver driver.quit()def test_submitting_text_shows_received(driver): form = WebFormPage.open(driver) form.field(\"Text input\").fill(\"Selenium\") received = form.submit()assert received.message() == \"Received!\"def test_only_one_field_is_disabled(driver): fields = WebFormPage.open(driver).fields()assertlen(fields) == 11assert [f.name() for f in fields ifnot f.is_enabled()] == [\"Disabled input\"]def test_refuses_the_wrong_page(driver): driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html\")with pytest.raises(ValueError):WebFormPage(driver) The test talks to pages, not to locators. submit() moves to another page, so it returns that page object. The assertion stays in the test. Eleven labelled controls on this page. The constructor checks the page and fails loudly. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with pytest ... [100%] 3 passed in 3.20s pages/web_form_page.js const { By } = require('selenium-webdriver');const { Field } = require('./field');const { ReceivedPage } = require('./received_page');constURL ="],[13,"pom","Write page objects and components","'https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html';constFIELDS = By.css('label.form-label');constSUBMIT = By.css('button[type=submit]');classWebFormPage { constructor(driver) { this.driver = driver; } static async at(driver) {const title = await driver.getTitle();if (title !== 'Web form') thrownewError('This is not the web form, it is: ' + title);returnnewWebFormPage(driver); } static async open(driver) {await driver.get(URL);returnWebFormPage.at(driver); }async fields() {return (awaitthis.driver.findElements(FIELDS)).map(e => newField(e)); }async field(name) {for (const f ofawaitthis.fields()) if ((await f.name()) === name) return f;thrownewError('No field called ' + name); }async submit() {awaitthis.driver.findElement(SUBMIT).click();returnReceivedPage.at(this.driver); }}module.exports = { WebFormPage }; Locators live here and nowhere else. A constructor cannot await, so an async factory does the page check. Wrap every label element in a Field component. pages/field.js const { By } = require('selenium-webdriver');classField { constructor(root) { this.root = root; }async name() { return (awaitthis.root.getText()).split('\\n')[0].trim(); } control() { returnthis.root.findElement(By.css('input, textarea, select')); }async isEnabled() { return (awaitthis.control()).isEnabled(); }async fill(text) {const c = awaitthis.control();await c.clear();await c.sendKeys(text);returnthis; }}module.exports = { Field }; A page component: one label and the control inside it. The first line of the label is its name. Search inside this component only. pages/received_page.js const { By, until } = require('selenium-webdriver');classReceivedPage { constructor(driver) { this.driver = driver; } static async at(driver) {await driver.wait(until.titleIs('Web form - target page'), 3000);returnnewReceivedPage(driver); }async message() { returnthis.driver.findElement(By.id('message')).getText(); }}module.exports = { ReceivedPage }; The page arrives after a navigation, so wait for it instead of checking once. example.spec.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const assert = require('assert');const { WebFormPage } = require('./pages/web_form_page');describe('Web form with page objects', function () {let driver; beforeEach(asyncfunction () { driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build(); }); it('shows Received! after submitting text', asyncfunction () {const form = awaitWebFormPage.open(driver);await (await form.field('Text input')).fill('Selenium');const received = await form.submit(); assert.strictEqual(await received.message(), 'Received!'); }); it('has only one disabled field', asyncfunction () {const fields = await (awaitWebFormPage.open(driver)).fields(); assert.strictEqual(fields.length, 11);const disabled = [];for (const f of fields) if (!(await f.isEnabled())) disabled.push(await f.name()); assert.deepStrictEqual(disabled, ['Disabled input']); }); it('refuses the wrong page', asyncfunction () {await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/login.html');await assert.rejects(WebFormPage.at(driver), /This is not the web form/); }); afterEach(asyncfunction () { await driver.quit(); });}); The test talks to pages, not to locators. submit() moves to another page, so it returns that page object. The assertion stays in the test. Eleven labelled controls on this page. The check fails loudly. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) run with Mocha Web form with page objects ✔ shows Received! after submitting text (523ms) ✔ has only one disabled field (294ms) ✔ refuses the wrong page (80ms) 3 passing (3s) Read the three tests against the rules from the documentation. The first test only calls methods that describe what a user does, and the assertion is in the test, not in the page object. The second test uses Field objects, each built around one label element, which is what the documentation calls a page component object. The third test opens the login page and asks for a web form page object,"],[13,"pom","Write page objects and components","and the constructor refuses with an error. Rule from the documentation Where it shows in the example The public methods represent the services the page offers field, fields, submit, message Try not to expose the internals of the page Locators are private. Field.control() is private in Java Generally do not make assertions Every assert is in the test file The one allowed check: the right page is loaded The constructors check the title, or wait for it after a navigation Methods return other page objects, components or themselves submit() returns ReceivedPage, fill() returns the Field A page object need not represent an entire page Field represents one labelled control Remember ReceivedPage waits for its title, while WebFormPage checks the title once. The web form is opened with get(), which returns after the page loads. The received page appears after a click, and chapter 3.3 showed that the next command can run before the new page is there. Lesson 3 of 3"],[13,"design","Design choices and the limits of page objects","Page objects are a pattern, not a library, so a team still makes choices. These are the ones that come up most often. Choice What the documentation or practice suggests Same action, different result Write two methods, for example loginAsValidUser and loginExpectingError, each returning the page that really follows A part that appears on many pages A component object such as a header, a menu or a product card, used inside page objects Where waits go Inside the page object, so tests do not repeat them Base class Shared code such as the driver field and a wait helper. Keep it small PageFactory in Java An older helper that fills fields annotated with @FindBy. Plain By fields, as in the example, work in every language A layer above page objects is a domain specific language: methods such as shop.buy(\"backpack\") that hide whole user journeys. The documentation describes this as giving tests a language that business people can read. It sits on top of page objects and does not replace them. One idea, four depths A page object is a class for one page. Tests call its methods, so a change to the page is fixed in one place. Locators and waits live in the page object, assertions live in the test. Methods that move to another page return that page object. Repeated parts become component objects. The constructor checks that the right page is loaded, or waits for it after a navigation. Different results for the same action are modelled as different methods, as the documentation summary says. On top of page objects, a domain layer gives business level steps. Components scope their searches to a root element, which also makes them reusable in lists, tables and repeated cards."],[14,"","Good practices and things to avoid","Test design, encouraged practices and the things the documentation says to avoid. · best practices · test design · fresh browser · application state · shared state · test independence · captcha · file download · performance testing · 2fa Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 5: Build a test suite›Good practices Step 5 · Topic 3 of 4 · Intermediate Design browser tests the way the Selenium documentation recommends: short, independent and starting clean, with state prepared through APIs, and without the jobs Selenium is not made for. About 40 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1The shape of a good browser test 2Encouraged practices 3Things to avoid Lesson 1 of 3"],[14,"shape","The shape of a good browser test","The documentation starts its advice with a question: do you really need a browser for this? Browser tests are the most expensive tests you have. They need browsers, drivers and often a Grid, and they take seconds where a unit test takes milliseconds. Their strength is that they test the whole application, from the database to the screen, as a user sees it. When a browser test is the right tool, the documentation says it should do three things, and keep each short: set up the data, perform a discrete set of actions, and evaluate the results. One or two operations are enough most of the time. One grand test, or one small test per stepThe journey from the documentation. Change how often a step fails at random and which step really breaks. Step 0 of 4 Random failure per step (%)2.0 Really broken step (0 for none)3 Grand test pass rate with nothing broken: 90.4 %. Try this With a broken step 3, compare what the two reports tell you in the last step. Set no broken step and raise the random failure to 5 %. The grand test now fails more than one run in five. The documentation tells the story of Larry's unicorn shop: create an account, configure a unicorn, add it to the cart, check out and pay, give feedback. The temptation is one grand script for the whole journey. The documentation says to resist it, because such a test is slow, more exposed to timing problems, and when it fails it does not tell you at a glance what broke. Break it into small independent tests, each with one reason to exist. Testing type Question it answers Selenium fits? Acceptance Are we building the right product? Yes, by simulating what the user expects Functional Are we building the product right? Yes Integration Do the modules work together, for example order plus payment? Yes, for flows through the UI System Does the whole product work end to end, in an environment like production? Yes, a few journeys Performance (load and stress) How fast is it, and does it survive a peak? Not as the measuring tool. The documentation points to tools such as JMeter Regression Did a change break what worked? Yes, by running the existing tests again Lesson 2 of 3"],[14,"encouraged","Encouraged practices","These practices come from the encouraged behaviours section of the documentation. The first two are shown with real runs. Fresh browser per test. Start each test from a clean, known state. The documentation notes that drivers such as ChromeDriver and GeckoDriver start with a new user profile by default. The run below proves it: the second browser sees none of the cookie or storage the first one left. A new driver starts with a clean profile FreshBrowser.java import org.openqa.selenium.Cookie;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassFreshBrowser {staticfinalStringPAGE = \"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\";publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver first = newChromeDriver(); first.get(PAGE); first.manage().addCookie(newCookie(\"cart\", \"3 items\")); ((JavascriptExecutor) first).executeScript(\"localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark')\");System.out.println(\"first browser: \" + first.manage().getCookies().size() + \" cookie, storage \" + ((JavascriptExecutor) first).executeScript(\"return localStorage.length\")); first.quit();WebDriver second = newChromeDriver(); second.get(PAGE);System.out.println(\"second browser: \" + second.manage().getCookies().size() + \" cookies, storage \" + ((JavascriptExecutor) second).executeScript(\"return localStorage.length\")); second.quit(); }} State a first test leaves behind: a cookie and a value in local storage. A new driver gets a new, empty profile by default. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) first browser: 1 cookie, storage 1 second browser: 0 cookies, storage 0 main.py from selenium import webdriverPAGE = \"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\"first = webdriver.Chrome()first.get(PAGE)first.add_cookie({\"name\": \"cart\", \"value\": \"3 items\"})first.execute_script(\"localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark')\")print(\"first browser:\", len(first.get_cookies()), \"cookie, storage\", first.execute_script(\"return localStorage.length\"))first.quit()second = webdriver.Chrome()second.get(PAGE)print(\"second browser:\", len(second.get_cookies()), \"cookies, storage\", second.execute_script(\"return localStorage.length\"))second.quit() State a first test leaves behind: a cookie and a value in local storage. A new driver gets a new, empty profile by default. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) first browser: 1 cookie, storage 1 second browser: 0 cookies, storage 0 main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');constPAGE = 'https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html';(asyncfunction freshBrowser() {const first = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await first.get(PAGE);await first.manage().addCookie({ name: 'cart', value: '3 items' });await first.executeScript(\"localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark')\"); console.log('first browser: ' + (await first.manage().getCookies()).length + ' cookie, storage ' + await first.executeScript('return localStorage.length'));await first.quit();const second = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await second.get(PAGE); console.log('second browser: ' + (await second.manage().getCookies()).length + ' cookies, storage ' + await second.executeScript('return localStorage.length'));await second.quit();})(); State a first test leaves behind: a cookie and a value in local storage. A new driver gets a new, empty profile by default. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) first browser: 1 cookie, storage 1 second browser: 0 cookies, storage 0 Generate application state. Selenium should not be used to prepare a test case. Logging in through the form before every test is slow and adds a place to fail. The documentation suggests creating a method that logs in through an API and sets a cookie. Here the session cookie is set directly and the page under test reads it on load. Prepare the state without clicking through the user interface AppState.java import"],[14,"encouraged","Encouraged practices","org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.Cookie;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassAppState {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();String session = \"abc123\"; driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\"); driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie(\"session\", session)); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/cookies.html\");System.out.println(\"page shows: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"result\")).getText()); driver.quit(); }} In a real suite this value comes from a login API call, not from the login form. A small page on the domain, only to be allowed to set the cookie. Now open the page under test. It reads its cookies on load. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page shows: session=abc123 main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()session = \"abc123\"driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"session\", \"value\": session})driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/cookies.html\")print(\"page shows:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"result\").text)driver.quit() In a real suite this value comes from a login API call, not from the login form. A small page on the domain, only to be allowed to set the cookie. Now open the page under test. It reads its cookies on load. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page shows: session=abc123 main.js const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction appState() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();const session = 'abc123';await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'session', value: session });await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/cookies.html'); console.log('page shows: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('result')).getText());await driver.quit();})(); In a real suite this value comes from a login API call, not from the login form. A small page on the domain, only to be allowed to set the cookie. Now open the page under test. It reads its cookies on load. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) page shows: session=abc123 Practice What the documentation says Avoid sharing state Do not share test data, clean up stale data, and create a new WebDriver per test Test independency Write each test as its own unit, not relying on another test to complete Mock external services Removing dependencies on external services improves speed and stability Improved reporting Selenium does not report. Use the reports of your test framework, such as xUnit XML or HTML Locators Unique ids first, then a well written CSS selector. XPath is flexible but harder to debug and often slower Page objects and a fluent API Chapter 5.2. Methods can return the page for chained calls Domain specific language Business level methods on top of page objects Real world scenario: one order, two tests Two tests each query the database for a valid order and act on the first one they find. On a busy night both pick order 1042, one cancels it, and the other fails because the order is gone. The fix from the documentation: each test creates its own order through the API and cleans it up afterwards. Lesson 3 of 3"],[14,"discouraged","Things to avoid","The documentation also lists jobs that Selenium can technically do but should not, with what to do instead. Avoid Why Do instead CAPTCHAs They are built to stop automation Disable them in the test environment, or add a hook that lets tests bypass them File downloads The API does not expose download progress Find the link with Selenium and fetch it with an HTTP library such as curl, using any cookies it needs HTTP status codes WebDriver acts like a user, and a status code is rarely the useful detail of a failure Check what the user sees, such as an error page. Chapter 6.2 shows BiDi network events if you really need codes Logging in to Gmail or Facebook Against their terms, slow and unreliable Use the APIs or test account tools they provide Test dependency Tests must run in any order Make each test set up its own state Performance testing Browser start, servers and third parties add noise to every number Use performance tools, and keep Selenium for function Link spidering A browser takes seconds per page Use curl or a library such as BeautifulSoup Two factor authentication Automating it is hard and less secure A test only token, or 2FA turned off for test users, test IPs or the test environment Remember Every row has the same shape: Selenium is a tool for what a user does in a browser. When the job is not that, a lighter tool is faster and more reliable. One idea, four depths Use a browser test only when you need one, keep it short, start it clean, and do not make Selenium do jobs other tools do better. Each test sets up its data, performs a few actions and checks the result. Fresh browser per test, no shared data, state prepared through APIs and cookies, reports from the framework. The encouraged and discouraged lists in the documentation all serve two goals: tests that are independent, so they can run in any order and in parallel, and tests that fail for one clear reason. Large suites add test data factories, service mocks at the network edge, contract tests for APIs and a small set of end to end browser journeys on top of many fast lower level tests."],[15,"","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","Common errors, stale elements and logging in Java, Python and JavaScript. · troubleshooting · errors · exceptions · stale element · no such element · logging · debug · chromedriver log · session not created Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 5: Build a test suite›Troubleshooting Step 5 · Topic 4 of 4 · Intermediate Read an error the right way, understand why element references go stale or unknown, and turn on the client and driver logs to see exactly what was sent. About 50 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1Read an error the right way 2Stale elements and the element store 3Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Lesson 1 of 3"],[15,"errors","Read an error the right way","Every failure from a driver is one of the errors named in the W3C specification, such as no such element. Each language turns it into its own class, and the names are not always the same. The documentation keeps a table of them. These are the ones you will meet most: W3C error Java Python JavaScript Usual cause and fix no such element NoSuchElementException NoSuchElementException NoSuchElementError Wrong place, wrong time or a changed locator. Check the page, add an explicit wait, update the locator. stale element reference StaleElementReferenceException StaleElementReferenceException StaleElementReferenceError The node was replaced, for example by a refresh or a re render. Find it again. element click intercepted ElementClickInterceptedException ElementClickInterceptedException ElementClickInterceptedError Something covers the element: a sticky bar, a modal, an animation. Wait for it to go, or scroll. element not interactable ElementNotInteractableException ElementNotInteractableException ElementNotInteractableError Hidden, zero size or not ready. Wait until visible and enabled. invalid selector InvalidSelectorException InvalidSelectorException InvalidSelectorError The CSS or XPath is broken, or an XPath was passed to a CSS locator. timeout TimeoutException TimeoutException TimeoutError An explicit wait ran out. The message says which condition. script timeout ScriptTimeoutException TimeoutException ScriptTimeoutError A script took longer than the script timeout. unexpected alert open UnhandledAlertException UnexpectedAlertPresentException UnexpectedAlertOpenError An alert was left open. Handle it where it opens. no such window NoSuchWindowException NoSuchWindowException NoSuchWindowError The window was closed and the test did not switch back. invalid session id NoSuchSessionException InvalidSessionIdException NoSuchSessionError The session was quit or crashed. Look for a quit earlier in the test. session not created SessionNotCreatedException SessionNotCreatedException SessionNotCreatedError Driver and browser versions do not match, or the browser could not start. Read a failure in this order: the error name, then the message, then the line of your code in the stack trace. The error name narrows the cause to one row above. The message often names the locator or the condition. The line tells you which step of the test failed, which can be one step after the real cause, as the forgotten alert in chapter 4.2 showed. A driver that is too old for the browser (recorded in chapter 1.3) python main.py What the Python example printed from the exception message session not created: This version of ChromeDriver only supports Chrome version 147 Remember Search the error name together with the first line of the message. Ignore the long list of numbers under Stacktrace in Chrome messages. Those are addresses inside ChromeDriver, not your code. Lesson 2 of 3"],[15,"stale","Stale elements and the element store","When you find an element, the driver does not send the element to your code. It stores the node in a table that belongs to the current browsing context, the window or frame you are in, and sends back an id for it. Every later command with that element sends the id back, and the driver looks it up in the table of the current context. Make an element reference fail three ways, and fix each one Stale.java import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.NoSuchElementException;import org.openqa.selenium.StaleElementReferenceException;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassStale {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");WebElement box = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\")); driver.navigate().refresh();try { box.sendKeys(\"x\"); } catch (StaleElementReferenceException e) {System.out.println(\"1. after refresh: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName()); } box = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\")); box.sendKeys(\"found again\");System.out.println(\" fixed: \" + box.getAttribute(\"value\")); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/iframes.html\"); driver.switchTo().frame(\"iframe1\");WebElement email = driver.findElement(By.id(\"email\")); driver.switchTo().defaultContent();try { email.getAttribute(\"type\"); } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {System.out.println(\"2. outside its frame: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName()); } driver.switchTo().frame(\"iframe1\");System.out.println(\" fixed: \" + email.getAttribute(\"type\")); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\");try { email.getAttribute(\"type\"); } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {System.out.println(\"3. after leaving the page: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName()); } driver.quit(); }} The driver gives back a reference to this exact node. The page is rebuilt. The old node is gone, a new one with the same name takes its place. The fix: find it again. Found inside the frame. The reference is fine, but this context does not know it. The fix: switch back to the context where it was found. Leave the page completely. Go back to the page and find it again. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) 1. after refresh: StaleElementReferenceException fixed: found again 2. outside its frame: NoSuchElementException fixed: email 3. after leaving the page: NoSuchElementException main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.common.exceptions importNoSuchElementException, StaleElementReferenceExceptionfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importBydriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")box = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")driver.refresh()try: box.send_keys(\"x\")exceptStaleElementReferenceExceptionas e:print(\"1. after refresh:\", type(e).__name__)box = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")box.send_keys(\"found again\")print(\" fixed:\", box.get_attribute(\"value\"))driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/iframes.html\")driver.switch_to.frame(\"iframe1\")email = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"email\")driver.switch_to.default_content()try: email.get_attribute(\"type\")exceptNoSuchElementExceptionas e:print(\"2. outside its frame:\", type(e).__name__)driver.switch_to.frame(\"iframe1\")print(\" fixed:\", email.get_attribute(\"type\"))driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")try: email.get_attribute(\"type\")exceptNoSuchElementExceptionas e:print(\"3. after leaving the page:\", type(e).__name__)driver.quit() The driver gives back a reference to this exact node. The page is rebuilt. The old node is gone, a new one with the same name takes its place. The fix: find it again. Found inside the frame. The reference is fine, but this context does not know it. The fix: switch back to the context where it was found. Leave the page completely. Go back to the page and find it again. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with"],[15,"stale","Stale elements and the element store","Chrome 153) 1. after refresh: StaleElementReferenceException fixed: found again 2. outside its frame: NoSuchElementException fixed: email 3. after leaving the page: NoSuchElementException main.js const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction stale() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');let box = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));await driver.navigate().refresh();try {await box.sendKeys('x'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.StaleElementReferenceError)) throw e; console.log('1. after refresh: ' + e.name); } box = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));await box.sendKeys('found again'); console.log(' fixed: ' + await box.getAttribute('value'));await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/iframes.html');const frame = await driver.findElement(By.id('iframe1'));await driver.switchTo().frame(frame);const email = await driver.findElement(By.id('email'));await driver.switchTo().defaultContent();try {await email.getAttribute('type'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.NoSuchElementError)) throw e; console.log('2. outside its frame: ' + e.name); }await driver.switchTo().frame(frame); console.log(' fixed: ' + await email.getAttribute('type'));await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');try {await email.getAttribute('type'); } catch (e) {if (!(e instanceof error.NoSuchElementError)) throw e; console.log('3. after leaving the page: ' + e.name); }await driver.quit();})(); The driver gives back a reference to this exact node. The page is rebuilt. The old node is gone, a new one with the same name takes its place. The fix: find it again. Found inside the frame. The reference is fine, but this context does not know it. The fix: switch back to the context where it was found. Leave the page completely. Go back to the page and find it again. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) 1. after refresh: StaleElementReferenceError fixed: found again 2. outside its frame: NoSuchElementError fixed: email 3. after leaving the page: NoSuchElementError The run shows three different failures. After a refresh the id is still known, but its node has been removed from the page, so the driver reports stale element reference. Outside its frame, and after leaving the page, Chrome 153 reported no such element, because the id is looked up in a context that never stored it. The documentation groups all three under stale elements and gives the same fixes: find it again, or switch back to the right context first. What happened Error in this run Fix Refresh, re render, list re sorted stale element reference Find the element again just before you use it Switched to another frame or window no such element Switch back to the context where it was found Navigated to another page no such element Go back to the page and find it again Remember Page objects that find elements inside their methods, as in chapter 5.2, rarely meet stale elements, because they look the element up fresh each time. Lesson 3 of 3"],[15,"logs","Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did","When an error makes no sense, look at the conversation itself. Two logs show it from both ends: the client log from your Selenium library, and the driver log from ChromeDriver. Turn on the client log and see every command ClientLog.java import java.util.logging.Handler;import java.util.logging.Level;import java.util.logging.LogRecord;import java.util.logging.Logger;import java.util.logging.SimpleFormatter;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassClientLog {staticfinalLoggerHTTP = Logger.getLogger(\"org.openqa.selenium.remote.http.jdk.JdkHttpClient\");publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {HTTP.setLevel(Level.FINE);HTTP.addHandler(newHandler() {publicvoid publish(LogRecord r) {String m = newSimpleFormatter().formatMessage(r);if (m.startsWith(\"Executing request: \")) {String line = m.substring(19).replaceFirst(\"^\\\\((\\\\w+)\\\\)\", \"$1\").replaceAll(\"/session/[0-9a-f]{32}\", \"/session/{id}\");System.out.println(line.split(\" \\\\{\")[0]); } }publicvoid flush() {}publicvoid close() {} });WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\"); driver.getTitle(); driver.quit(); }} Keep a reference, or Java may forget the level you set. Java logs at INFO by default. Request lines are written at FINE. A tiny handler that prints one line per request. The record holds a pattern plus values, so format it first. Hide the session id, which changes every run. Keep the method and path, drop the JSON body. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) POST /session GET /json/version POST /session/{id}/url GET /session/{id}/title DELETE /session/{id} main.py import loggingimport refrom selenium import webdriverclassRequests(logging.Handler):def emit(self, record): m = re.match(r\"(GET|POST|DELETE) http://[^/]+(\\S*)\", record.getMessage())if m:print(m.group(1), re.sub(\"/session/[0-9a-f]{32}\", \"/session/{id}\", m.group(2)))log = logging.getLogger(\"selenium\")log.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)log.addHandler(Requests())driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")driver.titledriver.quit() A tiny handler that prints one line per request. Request lines start with the method and the URL. Hide the session id, which changes every run. The parent of every Selenium logger in Python. Python logs at WARNING by default. Requests are written at DEBUG. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) POST /session POST /session/{id}/url GET /session/{id}/title DELETE /session/{id} main.js const { Builder, Browser, logging } = require('selenium-webdriver');const http = logging.getLogger('driver.http.Executor');http.setLevel(logging.Level.FINER);http.addHandler(entry => {const m = /^\\[[\\w.]+\\] >>> (\\w+) (\\S+)$/.exec(entry.message);if (m) console.log(m[1] + ' ' + m[2].replace(/\\/session\\/[0-9a-f]{32}/, '/session/{id}'));});(asyncfunction clientLog() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');await driver.getTitle();await driver.quit();})(); In selenium-webdriver 4.51 the HTTP lines come from this logger. Request lines are written at FINER. A tiny handler that prints one line per request. Each message starts with the logger name in square brackets. Hide the session id, which changes every run. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) GET /status POST /session POST /session/{id}/url GET /session/{id}/title DELETE /session/{id} All three logs show the same W3C conversation as chapter 1.2: New Session, then one request per command, then Delete Session. Two differences are visible. JavaScript first asked the driver GET /status to check it was ready. Java asked the browser DevTools endpoint for GET /json/version right after New Session, which the Java binding does for Chrome sessions. Each language uses its own logging system: Language Logger Default level Level that shows requests Java java.util.logging, for"],[15,"logs","Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did","example org.openqa.selenium.remote.http.jdk.JdkHttpClient INFO FINE Python logging, under the name selenium WARNING DEBUG JavaScript selenium-webdriver logging, driver.http.Executor in 4.51 OFF until set FINER Common mistake The documentation shows the JavaScript logger as getLogger(\"webdriver\"). In selenium-webdriver 4.51 that name printed nothing in our run, while driver.http and driver.http.Executor printed every request. In pytest, the documentation adds that logs of passing tests are hidden unless you run pytest -s -p no:logging. Ask ChromeDriver for its own log DriverLog.java import java.io.File;import java.nio.file.Files;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriverService;import org.openqa.selenium.chromium.ChromiumDriverLogLevel;publicclassDriverLog {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {File log = newFile(\"chromedriver.log\");ChromeDriverService service = newChromeDriverService.Builder() .withLogFile(log) .withLogLevel(ChromiumDriverLogLevel.DEBUG) .build();WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(service); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\"); driver.quit();for (String line : Files.readAllLines(log.toPath())) {if (line.contains(\"] COMMAND \")) System.out.println(line.split(\"] COMMAND \")[1].split(\" \")[0]); } }} Where ChromeDriver writes its log. More detail than the default. Print the name of each command the driver received. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) InitSession Navigate Quit main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.chrome.service importServiceservice = Service(log_output=\"chromedriver.log\", service_args=[\"--log-level=DEBUG\"])driver = webdriver.Chrome(service=service)driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")driver.quit()for line in open(\"chromedriver.log\"):if\"] COMMAND \"in line:print(line.split(\"] COMMAND \")[1].split(\" \")[0]) Where ChromeDriver writes its log, and how much. Print the name of each command the driver received. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) InitSession Navigate Quit main.js const fs = require('fs');const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');(asyncfunction driverLog() {const service = new chrome.ServiceBuilder().loggingTo('chromedriver.log').enableVerboseLogging();const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeService(service).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');await driver.quit();for (const line of fs.readFileSync('chromedriver.log', 'utf8').split('\\n')) {if (line.includes('] COMMAND ')) console.log(line.split('] COMMAND ')[1].split(' ')[0]); }})(); Where ChromeDriver writes its log, with full detail. Print the name of each command the driver received. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) InitSession Navigate Quit One idea, four depths An error name tells you what kind of problem it is. Most failures are a locator, a missing wait, something covering the element, or a page that changed. Match the error to its cause: no such element, stale, intercepted, not interactable, timeout. Find elements again after the page changes and switch back to the right context. Turn on logs when the cause is unclear. The driver keeps an element store per browsing context. An id from another context is unknown there, and an id whose node left the document is stale. The client log shows each W3C request, the driver log shows each command the driver received. The Grid adds its own logs per component, and BiDi can stream console messages and JavaScript errors from the page into the test, as chapter 6.2 shows."],[16,"","Selenium Grid 4","Selenium Grid 4 components, roles, remote sessions and parallel runs. · selenium grid · grid 4 · remote webdriver · router · distributor · node · hub · standalone · parallel · se:name · file detector Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 6: Scale out with Grid›Selenium Grid Step 6 · Topic 1 of 2 · Advanced Run the same tests on other machines and many at once: the six Grid components, the standalone, hub and node and distributed roles, remote sessions with metadata and file uploads, and the session queue, all from real runs against a Grid. About 55 min3 lessons1 animation1 formula6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1When and why to use a Grid 2Components, roles and ports 3Remote sessions, metadata and files Lesson 1 of 3"],[16,"why","When and why to use a Grid","So far every test started a browser on the same machine. Selenium Grid lets the same tests run on other machines, called nodes, many at the same time. The documentation names two reasons to use it: run tests in parallel against different browsers, versions and operating systems, and cut the time a suite takes. Suite time on a GridExam favourite total time = number of tests × average test timenumber of nodes From the documentation: 15 tests of 45 s take 11 min 15 s on one node, 2 min 15 s on 5 nodes and 45 s on 15 nodes. A new session travels through the GridChoose how many nodes and slots the Grid has and how many tests ask for a browser at once. Step 0 of 5 Nodes2 Slots per node1 Tests asking at once3 Running: 0, waiting in the queue: 0. Try this With 2 slots and 3 tests, step 2 leaves one request in the queue. Watch step 5 to see when it gets a slot. Give the Grid as many slots as tests. The queue empties in one step. Step 4 is a command for a running session. It skips the queue and the Distributor completely. For a test, the Grid looks like one big remote driver. The test sends the same W3C requests as before, only to http://localhost:4444 or wherever the Grid runs. The Grid finds a node with a free slot for the requested browser, starts the session there, and passes every later command to that node. Ask the Grid if it is ready and what it offers GridStatus.java import java.net.URI;import java.net.http.HttpClient;import java.net.http.HttpRequest;import java.net.http.HttpResponse;import java.util.List;import java.util.Map;import org.openqa.selenium.json.Json;publicclassGridStatus {@SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {HttpResponse<String> res = HttpClient.newHttpClient().send(HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(\"http://localhost:4444/status\")).build(),HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());Map<String, Object> body = newJson().toType(res.body(), Json.MAP_TYPE);Map<String, Object> value = (Map<String, Object>) body.get(\"value\");System.out.println(\"ready: \" + value.get(\"ready\") + \", \" + value.get(\"message\"));List<Map<String, Object>> nodes = (List<Map<String, Object>>) value.get(\"nodes\");Map<String, Object> node = nodes.get(0);System.out.println(\"nodes: \" + nodes.size() + \", max sessions: \" + node.get(\"maxSessions\"));for (Map<String, Object> slot : (List<Map<String, Object>>) node.get(\"slots\")) {System.out.println(\"slot: \" + ((Map<String, Object>) slot.get(\"stereotype\")).get(\"browserName\")); } }} JSON gives back plain objects, so the casts are unchecked. The same endpoint a driver has, now answered by the Grid. Selenium ships its own JSON reader. Each slot can hold one session of this browser. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) ready: true, Selenium Grid ready. nodes: 1, max sessions: 2 slot: chrome slot: MicrosoftEdge slot: chrome slot: MicrosoftEdge slot: firefox slot: firefox main.py import jsonimport urllib.requestwith urllib.request.urlopen(\"http://localhost:4444/status\") as res: value = json.load(res)[\"value\"]print(f\"ready: {value['ready']}, {value['message']}\")node = value[\"nodes\"][0]print(f\"nodes: {len(value['nodes'])}, max sessions: {node['maxSessions']}\")for slot in node[\"slots\"]:print(\"slot:\", slot[\"stereotype\"][\"browserName\"]) The same endpoint a driver has, now answered by the Grid. Each slot can hold one session of this browser. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) ready: True, Selenium Grid ready. nodes: 1, max sessions: 2 slot: firefox slot: MicrosoftEdge slot: MicrosoftEdge slot: firefox slot: chrome slot: chrome main.js (asyncfunction gridStatus() {const res = await fetch('http://localhost:4444/status');const value = (await res.json()).value; console.log('ready: ' + value.ready + ', ' + value.message);const node = value.nodes[0]; console.log('nodes: ' + value.nodes.length + ', max sessions: ' + node.maxSessions);for (const slot of node.slots) { console.log('slot: ' + slot.stereotype.browserName); }})(); The same endpoint"],[16,"why","When and why to use a Grid","a driver has, now answered by the Grid. Each slot can hold one session of this browser. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) ready: true, Selenium Grid ready. nodes: 1, max sessions: 2 slot: chrome slot: MicrosoftEdge slot: chrome slot: MicrosoftEdge slot: firefox slot: firefox This Grid runs in standalone mode with --max-sessions 2. Its single node offered two slots each for Chrome, Firefox and Edge, but at most two sessions can run at the same time. Real world scenario: Black Friday regression A shop has 600 browser tests of about 40 seconds. On one machine the run takes 6 hours 40 minutes, too long to run before every release. On a Grid of 20 small nodes it takes about 20 minutes, so the team runs it on every merge, in Chrome, Firefox and Edge. Lesson 2 of 3"],[16,"components","Components, roles and ports","Grid 4 is built from six components. In a small Grid they all run in one process. In a large one each runs on its own machine. The documentation describes them like this: Component Job Default port Router Entry point for every request. New sessions go to the queue. Commands for existing sessions go straight to the right node, found through the Session Map 4444 New Session Queue Holds new session requests in first in, first out order, with a timeout 5559 Distributor Knows every node and its slots. Takes requests from the queue, finds a matching free slot and creates the session 5553 Session Map Remembers which node runs which session id 5556 Node Runs the browsers. By default one slot per CPU for Chromium browsers and Firefox, one for Safari 5555 Event Bus Messages between the other components, cheaper than HTTP. Started first in a distributed Grid 4442, 4443 and 5557 Role Command Use it for Standalone java -jar selenium-server-<version>.jar standalone All components in one process on one machine. Local debugging, quick suites, CI Hub and Node ... hub and ... node --hub http://<hub-ip>:4444 The most used role. Combine machines with different systems and browsers behind one address Distributed ... event-bus, sessionqueue, sessions, distributor, router, node Large Grids with more than 100 nodes, each component scaled on its own Remember A node registers by sending a message on the Event Bus, and the Distributor then calls it over HTTP to confirm. When hub and node are on different machines, the Event Bus ports 4442 and 4443 and the node port must be reachable. Three tests, two slots: watch the queue Queue.java import java.net.URL;import java.util.ArrayList;import java.util.List;import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;import java.util.concurrent.Executors;import java.util.concurrent.Future;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;import org.openqa.selenium.remote.RemoteWebDriver;publicclassQueue {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {long t0 = System.currentTimeMillis();ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);List<Future<String>> results = newArrayList<>();for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {int n = i; results.add(pool.submit(() -> {RemoteWebDriver driver = newRemoteWebDriver(newURL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), newChromeOptions());long got = Math.round((System.currentTimeMillis() - t0) / 1000.0);Thread.sleep(3000); driver.quit();return got + \" s\"; })); }List<String> times = newArrayList<>();for (Future<String> f : results) times.add(f.get()); pool.shutdown(); times.sort(null);System.out.println(\"got a browser after: \" + times); }} Three threads, so three tests start at the same moment. Returns when the Grid has given this test a slot. Pretend the test takes 3 seconds. Frees the slot for the next request in the queue. Two at once, the third only after a slot was free. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) got a browser after: [2 s, 2 s, 5 s] main.py import timefrom concurrent.futures importThreadPoolExecutorfrom selenium import webdrivert0 = time.time()def one_test(n): driver = webdriver.Remote(\"http://localhost:4444\", options=webdriver.ChromeOptions()) got = round(time.time() - t0) time.sleep(3) driver.quit()return gotwithThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) as pool: times = sorted(pool.map(one_test, [1, 2, 3]))print(\"got a browser after:\", [f\"{t} s\"for t in times]) Returns when the Grid has given this test a slot. Pretend the test takes 3 seconds. Frees the slot for the next request in the queue. Three threads, so three tests start at the same moment. Two at once, the third only after a slot was free. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) got a browser after: ['1 s', '1 s', '4 s'] main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const t0 = Date.now();asyncfunction oneTest() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).usingServer('http://localhost:4444').build();const got = Math.round((Date.now() - t0) /"],[16,"components","Components, roles and ports","1000);awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 3000));await driver.quit();return got;}(asyncfunction queue() {const times = (awaitPromise.all([oneTest(), oneTest(), oneTest()])).sort((a, b) => a - b); console.log('got a browser after: ' + JSON.stringify(times.map(t => t + ' s')));})(); Resolves when the Grid has given this test a slot. Pretend the test takes 3 seconds. Frees the slot for the next request in the queue. Three tests start at the same moment. Two at once, the third only after a slot was free. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) got a browser after: [\"1 s\",\"1 s\",\"4 s\"] The Grid only had two free slots for three tests. Two got a browser straight away. The third waited in the New Session Queue until a test called quit and freed a slot. This is why every test must quit its session, pass or fail: a session left open keeps its slot until the session timeout, 300 seconds on this node. Lesson 3 of 3"],[16,"remote","Remote sessions, metadata and files","Moving a test to the Grid changes one line: the driver is created with the Grid address instead of a local browser. Options still choose the browser and its settings. Capabilities that start with se: are Grid metadata. se:name shows the test name in the Grid UI instead of the session id. Run a test on the Grid with a name and a file upload OnTheGrid.java import java.net.URL;import java.nio.file.Files;import java.nio.file.Path;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;import org.openqa.selenium.remote.LocalFileDetector;import org.openqa.selenium.remote.RemoteWebDriver;publicclassOnTheGrid {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions(); options.setCapability(\"se:name\", \"upload on the grid\");RemoteWebDriver driver = newRemoteWebDriver(newURL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), options);for (String k : newString[] {\"browserName\", \"platformName\", \"se:name\", \"se:cdp\"}) {Object v = driver.getCapabilities().getCapability(k);System.out.println(k + \": \" + (k.equals(\"se:cdp\") ? String.valueOf(v).replaceAll(\"[0-9a-f]{32}\", \"{id}\") : v)); }Path file = Files.writeString(Files.createTempDirectory(\"up\").resolve(\"report.txt\"), \"hello grid\"); driver.setFileDetector(newLocalFileDetector()); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/upload.html\");WebElement input = driver.findElement(By.id(\"upload\")); input.sendKeys(file.toAbsolutePath().toString());System.out.println(\"attached: \" + driver.executeScript(\"return arguments[0].files[0].name\", input)); driver.quit(); }} Options say which browser you want. The Grid picks a node that has it. se: capabilities are Grid metadata. se:name shows in the Grid UI. The only change from a local test: a URL instead of a local driver. se:cdp is a DevTools address that goes through the Grid. Sends the local file to the node, which may be another machine. The browser on the node has the file. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browserName: chrome platformName: linux se:name: upload on the grid se:cdp: ws://localhost:4444/session/{id}/se/cdp attached: report.txt main.py import osimport tempfilefrom selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.remote.file_detector importLocalFileDetectoroptions = webdriver.ChromeOptions()options.set_capability(\"se:name\", \"upload on the grid\")driver = webdriver.Remote(\"http://localhost:4444\", options=options)for k in [\"browserName\", \"platformName\", \"se:name\", \"se:cdp\"]: v = driver.capabilities.get(k)print(k + \":\", v if k != \"se:cdp\"else v.replace(driver.session_id, \"{id}\"))path = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), \"report.txt\")with open(path, \"w\") as f: f.write(\"hello grid\")driver.file_detector = LocalFileDetector()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/upload.html\")upload = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"upload\")upload.send_keys(path)print(\"attached:\", driver.execute_script(\"return arguments[0].files[0].name\", upload))driver.quit() Options say which browser you want. The Grid picks a node that has it. se: capabilities are Grid metadata. se:name shows in the Grid UI. The only change from a local test: a URL instead of a local driver. se:cdp is a DevTools address that goes through the Grid. Sends the local file to the node, which may be another machine. The browser on the node has the file. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browserName: chrome platformName: linux se:name: upload on the grid se:cdp: ws://localhost:4444/session/{id}/se/cdp attached: report.txt main.js const fs = require('fs');const os = require('os');const path = require('path');const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');const remote = require('selenium-webdriver/remote');(asyncfunction onTheGrid() {const options = new chrome.Options(); options.set('se:name', 'upload on the grid');const driver ="],[16,"remote","Remote sessions, metadata and files","awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(options).usingServer('http://localhost:4444').build();const caps = await driver.getCapabilities();const id = (await driver.getSession()).getId();for (const k of ['browserName', 'platformName', 'se:name', 'se:cdp']) {const v = caps.get(k); console.log(k + ': ' + (k === 'se:cdp' ? v.replace(id, '{id}') : v)); }const file = path.join(fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'up-')), 'report.txt'); fs.writeFileSync(file, 'hello grid'); driver.setFileDetector(new remote.FileDetector());await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/upload.html');const upload = await driver.findElement(By.id('upload'));await upload.sendKeys(file); console.log('attached: ' + await driver.executeScript('return arguments[0].files[0].name', upload));await driver.quit();})(); Options say which browser you want. The Grid picks a node that has it. se: capabilities are Grid metadata. se:name shows in the Grid UI. The only change from a local test: a server URL. se:cdp is a DevTools address that goes through the Grid. Sends the local file to the node, which may be another machine. The browser on the node has the file. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) browserName: chrome platformName: linux se:name: upload on the grid se:cdp: ws://localhost:4444/session/{id}/se/cdp attached: report.txt Two capabilities came back from the Grid that a local driver does not return. se:name echoes the metadata. se:cdp is a DevTools address that runs through the Grid itself, so tools that need DevTools work even when the browser is on another machine. Chapter 6.2 uses it. The file detector matters as soon as the node is another machine. sendKeys with a path on your laptop means nothing to a node in a data centre. With a local file detector the binding zips the file, sends it to the node, and types the path of the copy. On this Grid the node was the same machine, so it would also have worked without it. On a real Grid it would not. Language Remote driver File detector Java new RemoteWebDriver(new URL(gridUrl), options) setFileDetector(new LocalFileDetector()) Python webdriver.Remote(gridUrl, options=options) driver.file_detector = LocalFileDetector() JavaScript new Builder().usingServer(gridUrl) setFileDetector(new remote.FileDetector()) One idea, four depths A Grid runs your tests on other machines, many at once. Your test only changes the address it connects to. The Router takes requests, the queue holds new ones, the Distributor finds a free slot on a node and the Session Map remembers where each session runs. Standalone, hub and node, or distributed decide how the components are deployed. Slots are matched by capabilities. A request waits in the queue until a slot is free or the queue timeout passes. se: capabilities carry metadata, and se:cdp exposes DevTools through the Router. The documentation recommends many small nodes over one large one, about one CPU and 1 GB of RAM per browser, and Docker for isolation. Large Grids use the distributed role, with GraphQL and observability from chapter 6.2."],[17,"","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","WebDriver BiDi events, Chrome DevTools Protocol commands and Grid GraphQL. · bidi · webdriver bidi · cdp · devtools · websocket · events · log inspector · graphql · grid endpoints · drain Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 6: Scale out with Grid›BiDi, CDP, GraphQL Step 6 · Topic 2 of 2 · Advanced Let the browser talk back with WebDriver BiDi events, reach Chromium extras through the Chrome DevTools Protocol, and look inside a running Grid with GraphQL. About 50 min3 lessons1 animation6 worked problems10 quiz questions2 assignments 1WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back 2The Chrome DevTools Protocol 3Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints Lesson 1 of 3"],[17,"bidi","WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back","Everything so far followed one pattern: the test asks, the driver answers. The documentation calls this strict request and response, and it is exactly what you want for clicks and reads. It cannot tell you about something you did not ask for, such as a message the page logged or an error it threw a second ago. WebDriver BiDi is a newer W3C protocol that adds a WebSocket next to the HTTP connection. Over it the browser can push events to the test the moment they happen. The Selenium team and the browser vendors build it together as a stable, cross browser replacement for the Chrome DevTools Protocol. Asking again and again, or being toldThe page logs three things over six seconds. Compare a test that polls over HTTP with a test that listens over the BiDi WebSocket. Step 0 of 6 HTTP test asks every (s)2 HTTP requests: 0, events known by polling: 0, by BiDi: 0. Try this Ask every second. The HTTP test learns sooner, but it sends six requests and most come back empty. Ask every 3 seconds. Fewer requests, but the error at 2.4 s stays unseen until 3 s. Look at the BiDi lane at every step. Each event arrives at the moment it happens, with no request from the test. Hear console messages and page errors as they happen BidiLog.java import java.util.List;import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;import org.openqa.selenium.By;import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.bidi.module.LogInspector;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;publicclassBidiLog {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions(); options.setCapability(\"webSocketUrl\", true);WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver(options);List<String> heard = newCopyOnWriteArrayList<>();try (LogInspector inspector = newLogInspector(driver)) { inspector.onConsoleEntry(e -> heard.add(\"console \" + e.getLevel() + \": \" + e.getText())); inspector.onJavaScriptException(e -> heard.add(\"page error: \" + e.getText())); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/bidi/logEntryAdded.html\");for (String id : newString[] {\"consoleLog\", \"consoleError\", \"jsException\"}) { driver.findElement(By.id(id)).click(); }long end = System.currentTimeMillis() + 5000;while (heard.size() < 3 && System.currentTimeMillis() < end) Thread.sleep(50); } heard.forEach(System.out::println); driver.quit(); }} Ask for a BiDi WebSocket next to the normal HTTP connection. Events arrive on another thread, so use a thread safe list. Subscribes to log events. Closing it unsubscribes. Called for every console.log, console.error and so on. Called for every uncaught error in the page. Events are pushed, not returned, so wait until all three arrived. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) console info: Hello, world! console error: I am console error page error: Error: Not working main.py from selenium import webdriverfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by importByfrom selenium.webdriver.support.wait importWebDriverWaitoptions = webdriver.ChromeOptions()options.enable_bidi = Truedriver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options)heard = []driver.script.add_console_message_handler(lambda e: heard.append(f\"console {e.level}: {e.text}\"))driver.script.add_javascript_error_handler(lambda e: heard.append(f\"page error: {e.text}\"))driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/bidi/logEntryAdded.html\")for button in [\"consoleLog\", \"consoleError\", \"jsException\"]: driver.find_element(By.ID, button).click()WebDriverWait(driver, 5).until(lambda _: len(heard) == 3)print(\"\\n\".join(heard))driver.quit() Ask for a BiDi WebSocket next to the normal HTTP connection. Called for every console.log, console.error and so on. Called for every uncaught error in the page. Events are pushed, not returned, so wait until all three arrived. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) console info: Hello, world! console error: I am console error page error: Error: Not working main.js const { Builder, Browser, By }"],[17,"bidi","WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back","= require('selenium-webdriver');const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');constLogInspector = require('selenium-webdriver/bidi/logInspector');(asyncfunction bidiLog() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(new chrome.Options().enableBidi()).build();const heard = [];const inspector = awaitLogInspector(driver);await inspector.onConsoleEntry(e => heard.push(`console ${e.level}: ${e.text}`));await inspector.onJavascriptException(e => heard.push(`page error: ${e.text}`));await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/bidi/logEntryAdded.html');for (const id of ['consoleLog', 'consoleError', 'jsException']) {await driver.findElement(By.id(id)).click(); }await driver.wait(() => heard.length === 3, 5000); console.log(heard.join('\\n'));await inspector.close();await driver.quit();})(); Ask for a BiDi WebSocket next to the normal HTTP connection. Subscribes to log events. Called for every console.log, console.error and so on. Called for every uncaught error in the page. Events are pushed, not returned, so wait until all three arrived. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) console info: Hello, world! console error: I am console error page error: Error: Not working The test never asked for a log. It subscribed once, and each click made the browser push an event. Three different events arrived: a console message at level info, one at level error, and an uncaught JavaScript error. A test can now fail on any page error, even when the page still looks fine. How to switch it on Java Python JavaScript Capability options.setCapability(\"webSocketUrl\", true) options.enable_bidi = True new chrome.Options().enableBidi() Console and page errors new LogInspector(driver) driver.script.add_console_message_handler await LogInspector(driver) Common mistake BiDi is still growing. The documentation lists log, network, script and browsing context features, and some network APIs are marked as not yet stable in some bindings, for example the Python docs example uses an underscore method for intercepts. Check the documentation page for your binding before you build on a feature. Lesson 2 of 3"],[17,"cdp","The Chrome DevTools Protocol","Before BiDi existed, Selenium gave access to the Chrome DevTools Protocol, the protocol Chrome DevTools uses to debug pages. The documentation is careful about it: CDP was not designed for testing and has no stable API, so features depend on the browser version. Selenium supports the three most recent Chrome versions for its generated CDP classes. The Java jars in this course carry v153, v154 and v155. Send Chrome DevTools commands: read the version and change the time zone Cdp.java import java.util.Map;import org.openqa.selenium.JavascriptExecutor;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;publicclassCdp {publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) {ChromeDriver driver = newChromeDriver(); driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\");Map<String, Object> version = driver.executeCdpCommand(\"Browser.getVersion\", Map.of());System.out.println(\"protocol \" + version.get(\"protocolVersion\") + \", \" + version.get(\"product\"));String zone = \"return Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone\";System.out.println(\"time zone before: \" + ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(zone)); driver.executeCdpCommand(\"Emulation.setTimezoneOverride\", Map.of(\"timezoneId\", \"Asia/Kolkata\"));System.out.println(\"time zone after: \" + ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(zone)); driver.quit(); }} executeCdpCommand exists on Chrome and Edge drivers only. Any CDP method by name, with a map of parameters. Something WebDriver Classic has no command for. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) protocol 1.3, Chrome/153.0.8010.12 time zone before: UTC time zone after: Asia/Calcutta main.py from selenium import webdriverdriver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")version = driver.execute_cdp_cmd(\"Browser.getVersion\", {})print(f\"protocol {version['protocolVersion']}, {version['product']}\")zone = \"return Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone\"print(\"time zone before:\", driver.execute_script(zone))driver.execute_cdp_cmd(\"Emulation.setTimezoneOverride\", {\"timezoneId\": \"Asia/Kolkata\"})print(\"time zone after:\", driver.execute_script(zone))driver.quit() execute_cdp_cmd exists on Chrome and Edge drivers only. Any CDP method by name, with a dict of parameters. Something WebDriver Classic has no command for. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) protocol 1.3, Chrome/153.0.8010.12 time zone before: UTC time zone after: Asia/Calcutta main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');(asyncfunction cdp() {const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');const version = await driver.sendAndGetDevToolsCommand('Browser.getVersion', {}); console.log(`protocol ${version.protocolVersion}, ${version.product}`);const zone = 'return Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone'; console.log('time zone before: ' + await driver.executeScript(zone));await driver.sendAndGetDevToolsCommand('Emulation.setTimezoneOverride', { timezoneId: 'Asia/Kolkata' }); console.log('time zone after: ' + await driver.executeScript(zone));await driver.quit();})(); sendAndGetDevToolsCommand exists on Chrome and Edge drivers only. Any CDP method by name, with an object of parameters. Something WebDriver Classic has no command for. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) protocol 1.3, Chrome/153.0.8010.12 time zone before: UTC time zone after: Asia/Calcutta Two lines show the point. Browser.getVersion returns information no W3C command gives. Emulation.setTimezoneOverride changed what the page believes its time zone is, without touching the machine. The page then reports Asia/Calcutta, because Chrome uses the older canonical name for the zone you set as Asia/Kolkata. This is how a test can check how a page shows times in India from a server in another country. WebDriver Classic CDP WebDriver BiDi Standard W3C Recommendation Chrome only, no stability promise"],[17,"cdp","The Chrome DevTools Protocol","W3C, in progress Browsers All Chrome and Edge Chrome, Edge and Firefox, growing Style Request and response over HTTP Commands and events over WebSocket Commands and events over WebSocket Use it for Every normal test step Chromium features with no other API Events, logs, network, and the future of Selenium Remember The documentation calls out two popular CDP examples with limited value: emulated geolocation, because most sites use the IP address, and overriding device metrics, because Chrome mobile emulation in the options is the better tool. Lesson 3 of 3"],[17,"gridplus","Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints","A Grid with many nodes needs a way to see what is going on. Besides the /status endpoint from chapter 6.1, the Grid answers GraphQL queries at /graphql. GraphQL lets you ask for exactly the fields you need, nothing more. Ask the Grid exactly what you need with GraphQL GridQuery.java import java.net.URI;import java.net.URL;import java.net.http.HttpClient;import java.net.http.HttpRequest;import java.net.http.HttpResponse;import java.util.List;import java.util.Map;import java.util.stream.Collectors;import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeOptions;import org.openqa.selenium.json.Json;import org.openqa.selenium.remote.RemoteWebDriver;@SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")publicclassGridQuery {staticfinalJsonJSON = newJson();staticMap<String, Object> ask(String query) throwsException {String body = \"{\\\"query\\\": \\\"\" + query + \"\\\"}\";String text = HttpClient.newHttpClient().send(HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(\"http://localhost:4444/graphql\")).POST(HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(body)).build(),HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()).body();Map<String, Object> reply = JSON.toType(text, Json.MAP_TYPE);return (Map<String, Object>) reply.get(\"data\"); }publicstaticvoid main(String[] args) throwsException {String q = \"{ grid { nodeCount, totalSlots, maxSession, sessionCount, sessionQueueSize } }\";System.out.println(\"idle: \" + ask(q).get(\"grid\"));ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions(); options.setCapability(\"se:name\", \"graphql demo\");RemoteWebDriver driver = newRemoteWebDriver(newURL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), options);System.out.println(\"busy: \" + ask(q).get(\"grid\"));Map<String, Object> info = (Map<String, Object>) ask(\"{ sessionsInfo { sessions { capabilities } } }\").get(\"sessionsInfo\");List<Map<String, Object>> sessions = (List<Map<String, Object>>) info.get(\"sessions\");System.out.println(\"names: \" + sessions.stream() .map(x -> JSON.<Map<String, Object>>toType((String) x.get(\"capabilities\"), Json.MAP_TYPE).get(\"se:name\")) .collect(Collectors.toList())); driver.quit(); }} Selenium ships its own JSON reader. GraphQL over HTTP: one POST with the query as a string. The answer sits under data. Ask only for these fields. One session is running now. The se:name metadata is in the session capabilities. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) idle: {nodeCount=1, totalSlots=6, maxSession=2, sessionCount=0, sessionQueueSize=0} busy: {nodeCount=1, totalSlots=6, maxSession=2, sessionCount=1, sessionQueueSize=0} names: [graphql demo] main.py import jsonimport urllib.requestfrom selenium import webdriverdef ask(query): req = urllib.request.Request(\"http://localhost:4444/graphql\", data=json.dumps({\"query\": query}).encode(), method=\"POST\")with urllib.request.urlopen(req) as res:return json.load(res)[\"data\"]q = \"{ grid { nodeCount, totalSlots, maxSession, sessionCount, sessionQueueSize } }\"print(\"idle:\", ask(q)[\"grid\"])options = webdriver.ChromeOptions()options.set_capability(\"se:name\", \"graphql demo\")driver = webdriver.Remote(\"http://localhost:4444\", options=options)print(\"busy:\", ask(q)[\"grid\"])sessions = ask(\"{ sessionsInfo { sessions { capabilities } } }\")[\"sessionsInfo\"][\"sessions\"]print(\"names:\", [json.loads(s[\"capabilities\"]).get(\"se:name\") for s in sessions])driver.quit() GraphQL over HTTP: one POST with the query as a string. Ask only for these fields. One session is running now. The se:name metadata is in the session capabilities. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) idle: {'nodeCount': 1, 'totalSlots': 6, 'maxSession': 2, 'sessionCount': 0, 'sessionQueueSize': 0} busy: {'nodeCount': 1, 'totalSlots': 6, 'maxSession': 2, 'sessionCount': 1, 'sessionQueueSize': 0} names: ['graphql demo'] main.js const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');asyncfunction ask(query) {const res = await fetch('http://localhost:4444/graphql', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ query }) });return (await res.json()).data;}(asyncfunction gridQuery() {const q = '{"],[17,"gridplus","Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints","grid { nodeCount, totalSlots, maxSession, sessionCount, sessionQueueSize } }'; console.log('idle: ' + JSON.stringify((await ask(q)).grid));const options = new chrome.Options(); options.set('se:name', 'graphql demo');const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(options).usingServer('http://localhost:4444').build(); console.log('busy: ' + JSON.stringify((await ask(q)).grid));const sessions = (await ask('{ sessionsInfo { sessions { capabilities } } }')).sessionsInfo.sessions; console.log('names: ' + JSON.stringify(sessions.map(s => JSON.parse(s.capabilities)['se:name'])));await driver.quit();})(); GraphQL over HTTP: one POST with the query as a string. Ask only for these fields. One session is running now. The se:name metadata is in the session capabilities. Real output from this run (Selenium 4.51.0 with Chrome 153) idle: {\"nodeCount\":1,\"totalSlots\":6,\"maxSession\":2,\"sessionCount\":0,\"sessionQueueSize\":0} busy: {\"nodeCount\":1,\"totalSlots\":6,\"maxSession\":2,\"sessionCount\":1,\"sessionQueueSize\":0} names: [\"graphql demo\"] The run shows the Grid idle, then with one session, and the se:name metadata travelling in the session capabilities. The queue size field is how a CI system can notice that tests are waiting for slots and start more nodes. Endpoint What it does GET /status Current state of the Grid and every node, with slots and sessions POST /graphql Ask for exactly the fields you need: grid, nodesInfo, sessionsInfo, session(id) DELETE /session/<id> End a session and free its slot POST /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id>/drain Stop a node after its running sessions end, with no new sessions. Needs the registration secret header DELETE /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id> Remove a node from the Distributor. Its running sessions continue One idea, four depths BiDi lets the browser tell the test about events, such as console messages and page errors, the moment they happen. CDP gives Chrome only extras. GraphQL tells you what the Grid is doing. Turn on BiDi with the webSocketUrl capability, then subscribe to log events. Use CDP commands by name for Chromium features with no W3C command. Query the Grid at /graphql for counts, sessions and the queue. BiDi and CDP both use a WebSocket with commands and events, but BiDi is a W3C standard across browsers while CDP has no stability promise. On a Grid, se:cdp in the capabilities is the DevTools address routed through the Grid. Selenium is moving its own implementation from Classic to BiDi while keeping compatibility. Network interception, authentication handlers and preload scripts are the next layer, and the Grid adds observability with tracing for each component."],[18,"","Revision: predict, debug and design","Predict the output, find the bug, design and sizing questions, a cheat sheet in three languages and a mock quiz. · revision · exam · interview · mock quiz · cheat sheet · predict output · find the bug · framework design · grid sizing Brainy BytesSelenium learning path Selenium›Step 7: Revise and practise›Revision Step 7 · Topic 1 of 1 · All levels Practise the question shapes you will meet in interviews and reviews: predict what a test prints, find why a test fails, design a framework and size a Grid, all with worked answers. About 60 min5 lessons5 formulas9 worked problems16 quiz questions 1How Selenium skills are tested 2Predict the output 3Find the bug 4Design and sizing questions 5Cheat sheet in three languages Lesson 1 of 5"],[18,"map","How Selenium skills are tested","This chapter is for the week before an interview, a certification style test or a code review of your own framework. It teaches nothing new. It shows the four shapes Selenium questions take, with worked answers, and points back to the chapter where each idea is taught. The questions are original practice questions written for this course. Question shape What it looks like What earns the marks Lesson Predict the output A short program and the question: what does it print, or which error comes back Knowing what the driver really does, not what the code seems to say 2 Find the bug A test that is slow, flaky or fails, and the question: why, and how do you fix it Naming the cause first, then a fix that removes it 3 Design and sizing Plan a framework, size a Grid, triage a flaky suite Clear layers, numbers from a formula, a reason for each choice 4 Write it in another language The same step in Java, Python or JavaScript The right method name and the right unit for time 5"],[18,"map","The twenty ideas asked about most","Selenium is a family: WebDriver, IDE and Grid (chapter 1.1). Every command is an HTTP request to a driver, which answers with JSON (chapter 1.2). Selenium Manager finds or downloads the right driver (chapter 1.3). Options and capabilities, page load strategies and the three timeouts (chapter 2.1). get, back, forward, refresh and screenshots (chapter 2.2). The eight locator strategies, CSS against XPath, relative locators (chapter 3.1). find_element against find_elements, searching inside an element, shadow roots (chapter 3.2). click, send keys, clear, select lists and what the driver checks before a click (chapter 3.3). Why sleeps fail, implicit against explicit waits, and why the two must not be mixed (chapter 4.1). Window handles, frames and alerts (chapter 4.2). The Actions API: ticks, pointer, keys and wheel (chapter 4.3). execute_script return types, async scripts and cookies (chapter 4.4). JUnit, pytest and Mocha set up and tear down, a screenshot on failure (chapter 5.1). Page objects and components (chapter 5.2). A fresh browser per test, set up state through an API, small tests (chapter 5.3). Stale elements, no such element and reading the logs (chapter 5.4). Grid roles: Router, Session Queue, Distributor, Session Map, Node and Event Bus (chapter 6.1). RemoteWebDriver and parallel runs, the sizing formula (chapter 6.1). WebDriver BiDi events against classic request and response (chapter 6.2). CDP for Chromium extras and GraphQL to look inside a Grid (chapter 6.2). Remember Use the Revise lessons in order the first time, then use the Quiz and Worked problems tabs as a timed mock test. Each answer says which chapter to reread if you got it wrong. Lesson 2 of 5"],[18,"trace","Predict the output","These questions show a few lines of code and ask what happens. Every answer below is what a real run printed in an earlier chapter, so you can trust it more than intuition. Question 1. The pointer is moved to (8, 12) in the viewport, then moved by an offset of (13, 15). What does the page report? move to location sets an absolute point, so the pointer is at (8, 12). move by offset adds to where the pointer already is. 8 + 13 = 21 and 12 + 15 = 27, so the page printed 21, 27 (chapter 4.3). Question 2 ActionChains(driver).key_down(Keys.SHIFT).send_keys(\"abc\").key_up(Keys.SHIFT).send_keys(\"def\").perform()print(field.get_attribute(\"value\")) Question 2. What is printed? Shift is held while a, b and c are typed, so they arrive as capitals. Shift is released before d, e and f. The real run printed ABCdef (chapter 4.3). Question 3 driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"k\", \"value\": \"v\"}) Question 3. What happens? A new session sits on a blank page with no domain. A cookie belongs to a domain, so there is nothing to attach it to. The driver answers with the invalid cookie domain error. Navigate to the site first, then add the cookie (chapter 4.4). Question 4. execute_script(\"return 7\") is called in each language. What type comes back? The browser sends back a JSON number. Each binding maps it to its own type: Java gives a Long, Python an int and JavaScript a number. A decimal such as 7.5 comes back as a Double in Java and a float in Python (chapter 4.4). Question 5 el = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"my-text-id\")driver.refresh()el.send_keys(\"hi\") Question 5. What happens on the last line? refresh builds a brand new document. The reference in el points to an element of the old document. The run raised StaleElementReferenceException. Find the element again after the refresh (chapter 5.4). Question 6. A test switches into an iframe, then looks for a heading that is in the main page. What happens? After the switch, every find searches only inside the frame. The heading is not in the frame, so the search finds nothing. The real run raised NoSuchElementException. Switch back with default content first (chapters 4.2 and 5.4). Question 7. A test asks for a cookie that does not exist. What comes back? Java returned null. Python returned None. JavaScript threw NoSuchCookieError. The same question has three answers, so know your binding (chapter 4.4). Lesson 3 of 5"],[18,"trace","Find the bug","Each test below runs, but it is slow, flaky or wrong. Say the cause first, then the fix. The cause is what earns the marks."],[18,"trace","Bug 1. A sleep before the click","driver.find_element(By.ID, \"search\").click()time.sleep(5)driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \".result\").click() Cause and fix Cause: the sleep always waits 5 seconds. When results come in 1 second it wastes 4. When they need 6 seconds the test still fails. Fix: wait for the condition you need, for example WebDriverWait(driver, 10).until(EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.CSS_SELECTOR, \".result\"))). The explicit wait returns as soon as the condition holds, checking every 0.5 seconds in Python and Java (chapter 4.1)."],[18,"trace","Bug 2. Implicit and explicit waits together","driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(10));newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(15)).until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id(\"late\"))); Cause and fix Cause: every check inside the explicit wait is a find that itself blocks for up to 10 seconds. With the numbers from the documentation the wait gave up after about 20.5 seconds, not 15. Fix: keep the implicit wait at 0 and use explicit waits only. The documentation says do not mix them (chapter 4.1)."],[18,"trace","Bug 3. A test that never closes its browser","driver = webdriver.Chrome()driver.get(url)assert driver.title == \"Web form\"driver.quit() Cause and fix Cause: when the assert fails, the line with quit never runs, so Chrome and the driver stay open. On a long run these leftovers fill the memory of the machine. Fix: quit in a finally block, or in the tear down of the test framework (chapters 1.3 and 5.1)."],[18,"trace","Bug 4. An absolute XPath","driver.find_element(By.XPATH, \"/html/body/main/div[2]/form/div[1]/input\") Cause and fix Cause: the locator depends on six levels of page structure. Any change to any level breaks it. Fix: use the id or name if there is one, otherwise a short CSS selector tied to a stable attribute. Keep the locator in a page object so a change is fixed in one place (chapters 3.1 and 5.2)."],[18,"trace","Bug 5. Logging in through the form in every test","Cause and fix Cause: each test spends seconds typing a user name and password that are not what the test checks. Fix: get a session through an API or set the session cookie, then open the page under test. Keep one test that logs in through the form, because that form is a feature too (chapter 5.3). Common mistake A good bug answer has three parts: what the code really does, why that breaks, and a fix that removes the cause. A retry that hides the failure is not a fix. Lesson 4 of 5"],[18,"design","Design and sizing questions","Senior interviews and code reviews ask you to plan, not to recite. Answer with layers, numbers and reasons."],[18,"design","Question 1. Design a test framework for a shop","TestsRead like a story: open the product, add to cart, check the total. Page objects and componentsOne class per page and per repeated part, holding the locators and the actions. Driver factoryBuilds a local or remote driver from configuration, with the options for each browser. SupportAPI helpers for set up, test data, a screenshot on failure, logs. Say why each layer exists. Tests change when the behaviour changes. Page objects change when the page changes. The driver factory changes when the place you run changes, for example from a laptop to a Grid. Each change then touches one layer only."],[18,"design","Question 2. Size a Grid","Suite time on a GridExam favourite total time = number of tests × average test timenumber of nodes From the Grid documentation. Each node here runs one test at a time. 900 tests of 30 s must finish in 10 minutes. How many one slot nodes are needed? Rearrange the formula: nodes = tests × average time ÷ allowed time. The allowed time is 10 × 60 = 600 s. nodes = 900 × 30 ÷ 600 = 45 The documentation suggests about one CPU and 1 GB of RAM per browser, so plan about 45 CPUs and 45 GB across the nodes (chapter 6.1)."],[18,"design","Question 3. Triage a flaky suite","A suite fails a few different tests on each run. What do you do? Group the failures by error. no such element and stale element usually mean timing or a page that changed under the test. Read the screenshot and the logs of one failure from each group before changing code. Replace sleeps with explicit waits for the exact condition, and find elements again after the page changes. Make each test start from a fresh browser and its own data, so tests cannot affect each other. Track the failure rate after the fix. A retry may keep a release moving, but it does not count as the fix (chapters 5.3 and 5.4). One idea, four depths Selenium tests are small programs. Revise by predicting what they do, finding what is wrong, and planning how many machines they need. Know the layers of a framework, the waits and the errors well enough to explain a failure from its message alone. Size a Grid from the formula and the resources per browser, and read the queue size to scale it. Compare WebDriver Classic, CDP and BiDi as protocols, and explain why events need a two way connection. Lesson 5 of 5"],[18,"sheet","Cheat sheet in three languages","The same common steps in each binding. Time is a Duration in Java, seconds in Python and milliseconds in JavaScript. Step Java Python JavaScript Start Chrome new ChromeDriver() webdriver.Chrome() await new Builder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build() Open a page driver.get(url) driver.get(url) await driver.get(url) Find one element driver.findElement(By.id(\"q\")) driver.find_element(By.ID, \"q\") await driver.findElement(By.id(\"q\")) Type el.sendKeys(\"text\") el.send_keys(\"text\") await el.sendKeys(\"text\") Wait for an element new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(5)).until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id(\"q\"))) WebDriverWait(driver, 5).until(EC.visibility_of_element_located((By.ID, \"q\"))) await driver.wait(until.elementLocated(By.id(\"q\")), 5000) Into a frame driver.switchTo().frame(el) driver.switch_to.frame(el) await driver.switchTo().frame(el) Back to the page driver.switchTo().defaultContent() driver.switch_to.default_content() await driver.switchTo().defaultContent() Run a script ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(\"return 1\") driver.execute_script(\"return 1\") await driver.executeScript(\"return 1\") Add a cookie driver.manage().addCookie(new Cookie(\"k\", \"v\")) driver.add_cookie({{\"name\": \"k\", \"value\": \"v\"}}) await driver.manage().addCookie({{name: \"k\", value: \"v\"}}) Use a Grid new RemoteWebDriver(new URL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), new ChromeOptions()) webdriver.Remote(command_executor=\"http://localhost:4444\", options=webdriver.ChromeOptions()) await new Builder().usingServer(\"http://localhost:4444\").forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build() End the session driver.quit() driver.quit() await driver.quit() Explicit wait pollExam favourite 500 ms The default check interval in Java and Python. Page load timeout 300,000 ms Five minutes by default. Script timeout 30,000 ms Thirty seconds by default. Implicit waitExam favourite 0 The default. Keep it there when you use explicit waits."],[19,"","Pick your cheatsheet","Choose the language you write tests in. Each language has its own full and compact Selenium cheatsheet with a PDF, and shows only that language's code. · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · revision · PDF · download · quick reference"],[19,"","Java","Only Java code, from all 19 chapters. Full cheatsheetPDF, 42 pages, 1582 KB Compact cheatsheetPDF, 6 pages, 538 KB"],[19,"","Python","Only Python code, from all 19 chapters. Full cheatsheetPDF, 41 pages, 1572 KB Compact cheatsheetPDF, 6 pages, 530 KB"],[19,"","JavaScript","Only JavaScript code, from all 19 chapters. Full cheatsheetPDF, 41 pages, 1533 KB Compact cheatsheetPDF, 6 pages, 533 KB"],[20,"","Java cheatsheet","Every key idea, Java example, table and common mistake from the Selenium chapters, with only Java code. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · Java cheatsheet · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · revision · PDF · download · Java · WebDriver · locators · waits · Grid"],[20,"step-1","Step 1Meet Selenium","1.1"],[20,"what-is-selenium","What Selenium is and where it came from","Open chapter What Selenium does, its three parts, the key words, its history to the W3C standard and a first real program. Key ideas What Selenium is An umbrella project of tools and libraries that automate real browsers the way a user would. At its core, a browser user agent library. Three parts WebDriver (library plus browser driver, for code), IDE (record and play back extension), Grid (many sessions on many machines). Not a test framework Selenium drives the browser. Assertions, test structure and reports come from JUnit, pytest, Mocha and similar tools. Key dates Selenium 2.0 on 8 July 2011, 3.0 on 13 October 2016, W3C Recommendation on 5 June 2018, Selenium 4.0 on 13 October 2021. Program shape Start a session, send the browser somewhere, ask or act, and always quit at the end. Code A first look: open a page, read its title, close the browser import org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver;Bring in the WebDriver interface. Every browser driver in Java speaks through it. import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;ChromeDriver is the class that starts and talks to Chrome. publicclassFirstLook {A plain Java class. Java runs the main method below. WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();Start Chrome. Selenium Manager finds a matching chromedriver for you, then a new browser session begins. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");Ask the browser to open the page and wait until it has loaded. System.out.println(\"Title: \" + driver.getTitle());Ask the browser for the page title and print it. System.out.println(\"URL: \" + driver.getCurrentUrl());Ask which address the browser is showing now. driver.quit();End the session. This closes every window and stops the driver process. Lesson: A first look at real code Tables Table: what people use selenium for · an everyday example What people use Selenium for An everyday example Testing web applications Before a bank releases a new version of its login page, a test opens the page in Chrome, Firefox and Edge, logs in with a test account and checks the balance appears. Repetitive tasks A script logs into a supplier portal every morning at 6 and downloads the order report for the day. Collecting data from pages A researcher reads prices from a site that has no API. The docs ask you to check the terms of service of the site first, because some sites forbid it and some block Selenium. Lesson: What Selenium is Table: part · what it is · use it when Part What it is Use it when WebDriver A library for your programming language plus a driver for each browser. Your code sends commands, the driver makes the browser carry them out. You write automated tests or scripts in Java, Python, JavaScript, C# or Ruby. This is what most of this subject teaches. IDE A Chrome and Firefox extension that records what you do in the browser and plays it back as Selenium commands. You want a quick recording, or you are learning which command matches which action. Grid A server that spreads WebDriver sessions over many machines, browsers and operating systems. One machine is too slow, or you must test on browser and operating system pairs you do not have. Lesson: WebDriver, IDE and Grid Table: word · meaning in the selenium documentation Word Meaning in the Selenium documentation API The set of commands you use to control WebDriver, such as get, findElement and click. Library The code module that holds those commands for one language: a .jar file for Java, a pip package for Python, an npm package for JavaScript. Driver The program that controls one browser, such as chromedriver for Chrome and geckodriver for Firefox. Browser vendors write most of them. It usually runs on the same machine as the browser. Framework An extra library that supports a test suite: test runners such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha, or tools such as Cucumber. Lesson: WebDriver, IDE and Grid Table: version · released · what changed Version Released What changed Selenium 2.0 8 July 2011 The WebDriver APIs became the heart of Selenium."],[20,"what-is-selenium","What Selenium is and where it came from","Selenium 1 code still ran. Selenium 3.0 13 October 2016 The original Selenium Core implementation was removed. Every major browser vendor now ships its own driver. W3C WebDriver 5 June 2018 WebDriver became a W3C Recommendation, a standard every browser can implement the same way. Selenium 4.0 13 October 2021 Support for the old JSON Wire Protocol was removed. Only the W3C protocol is used. Relative locators and a rebuilt Grid arrived. Lesson: How Selenium grew Tips and traps Common mistake Selenium only controls the browser. It does not decide whether a test passed, it does not write reports and it does not give you Given, When, Then syntax. Those come from a test framework such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha. Chapter 5.1 joins the two.Lesson: What Selenium is In the exam Interviewers like to ask what changed in Selenium 4. The short answer: W3C protocol only, so capabilities must follow the W3C names (browserVersion, platformName) or carry a vendor prefix such as goog:chromeOptions. Options classes replaced DesiredCapabilities, Java lost the findElementById style helpers, and relative locators and Grid 4 arrived. Selenium Manager came later, in 4.6.Lesson: How Selenium grew Remember Always end with quit. If a program stops without it, the driver process and the browser keep running in the background and hold memory and ports. On a busy test machine those leftovers pile up quickly.Lesson: A first look at real code 1.2"],[20,"architecture","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","Open chapter How a command travels from your code to the browser over the W3C WebDriver protocol, shown with real HTTP traffic. Key ideas Three programs Your code and the binding (local end), the driver (remote end), the browser. The driver is written by the browser vendor. HTTP and JSON Every command is one HTTP request with a fixed method and path. Answers are {\"value\": ...} on success or {\"value\": {\"error\", \"message\", \"stacktrace\"}} on failure. Session id POST /session returns a session id. Every later path contains it. DELETE /session/{id} ends it. Element reference Elements travel as {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"id\"}. Bindings turn id, name and class name into CSS selectors. BiDi Classic WebDriver is request and response. WebDriver BiDi adds a WebSocket so the browser can send events. CDP access is temporary until BiDi covers it. Code Drive Chrome with plain HTTP and no Selenium library at all import java.net.URI;URI turns a text address into an address object the HTTP client accepts. import java.net.http.HttpClient;The HTTP client built into Java 11 and later. staticHttpClient http = HttpClient.newHttpClient();One HTTP client, reused for every command. staticString call(String method, String path, String body) throwsException {Send one W3C command and return the JSON answer as text. HttpRequest.Builder req = HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(\"http://localhost:9515\" + path))chromedriver listens on port 9515 unless told otherwise. .header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\");The protocol speaks JSON in both directions. req.method(method, body == null ? HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.noBody() : HttpRequest.BodyPublishers.ofString(body));GET and DELETE carry no body. POST carries a JSON object. String reply = http.send(req.build(), HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()).body();Send it and wait for the answer. System.out.println(method + \" \" + path + \" -> \" + (reply.length() > 70 ? reply.substring(0, 70) + \"...\" : reply));Print the request and the start of the answer. staticString value(String json, String key) {A tiny helper that pulls one string out of the JSON answer. Process driver = newProcessBuilder(\"chromedriver\", \"--port=9515\").start();Start the driver program yourself, the job Selenium usually does for you. Thread.sleep(1000);Give chromedriver a moment to open its port. String created = call(\"POST\", \"/session\", \"{\\\"capabilities\\\":{\\\"alwaysMatch\\\":{\\\"browserName\\\":\\\"chrome\\\"}}}\");New Session. Chrome opens. String s = \"/session/\" + value(created, \"sessionId\");Every later command names this session in its path. call(\"POST\", s + \"/timeouts\", \"{\\\"implicit\\\":2000}\");Set Timeouts: let Find Element keep looking for up to 2 seconds, so the next page has time to load. call(\"POST\", s + \"/url\", \"{\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\\\"}\");Navigate To. call(\"GET\", s + \"/title\", null);Get Title. String box = value(call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", \"{\\\"using\\\":\\\"css selector\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"[name='my-text']\\\"}\"), \"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\");Find Element returns a reference under this fixed key. call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + box + \"/value\", \"{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Selenium\\\"}\");Element Send Keys types into that element. call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + button + \"/click\", \"{}\");Element Click submits the form. call(\"GET\", s + \"/element/\" + message + \"/text\", null);Get Element Text on the next page. call(\"DELETE\", s, null);Delete Session closes Chrome. driver.destroy();Stop chromedriver. Lesson: See the wire for yourself Numbers to remember SuccessExam favourite HTTP 200 and {\"value\": result} The result is null for commands that only act, a string for text and titles, a list for Find Elements. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol ErrorExam favourite HTTP 4xx or 5xx and {\"value\": {\"error\", \"message\", \"stacktrace\"}} error is a fixed code such as no such element. The binding turns it into an exception. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Element reference {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.C6…e.6\"} A web element"],[20,"architecture","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","travels as an object with this one fixed key. The value is an opaque id made by the driver. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Tables Table: spec word · in practice · example Spec word In practice Example Local end The client side, usually a language library on top of the protocol selenium for Python, selenium-java, selenium-webdriver for npm Remote end The server side that carries out the commands chromedriver, or Selenium Grid Intermediary node A remote end that passes commands on to another remote end Selenium Grid, which forwards to a node Endpoint node The last remote end in the chain, built into or next to the browser chromedriver driving Chrome Lesson: The pieces between your code and the browser Table: command · method · path Command Method Path New Session POST /session Delete Session DELETE /session/{session id} Navigate To POST /session/{id}/url Get Title GET /session/{id}/title Find Element POST /session/{id}/element Find Elements POST /session/{id}/elements Element Click POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/click Element Send Keys POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/value Get Element Text GET /session/{id}/element/{element id}/text Set Timeouts POST /session/{id}/timeouts Execute Script POST /session/{id}/execute/sync Take Screenshot GET /session/{id}/screenshot Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Table: error code · http status Error code HTTP status no such element 404 stale element reference 404 invalid session id 404 element not interactable 400 element click intercepted 400 invalid selector 400 timeout 500 unexpected alert open 500 session not created 500 Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Table: layout · where things run · code difference Layout Where things run Code difference Direct Your code, the driver and the browser on one machine. new ChromeDriver(), webdriver.Chrome() Remote Your code on one machine, driver and browser on another, reached through Selenium Server or RemoteWebDriver. new RemoteWebDriver(url, options), webdriver.Remote(url, options=...) Grid Selenium Grid receives the commands and relays them to the driver on a node machine. Same as remote, the URL points at the Grid Lesson: Local, remote and Grid, plus BiDi Tips and traps Remember The binding changes some locators before they leave your machine. Look for it in the log in the next lesson. That is why the W3C spec only needs five strategies: css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath.Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Remember You will never write raw HTTP in real tests. The point is to see that nothing magic happens: Selenium is a polite HTTP client with good manners about waiting, errors and types. When a test fails with a strange message, picture the request that was sent and the error code that came back.Lesson: See the wire for yourself 1.3"],[20,"setup-first-script","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","Open chapter Install Selenium, let Selenium Manager match the driver, and write and tidy up your first real script. Key ideas Install Java: selenium-java with Maven or Gradle (Java 11+). Selenium Manager Ships with Selenium since 4.6. A fallback that discovers the browser version, resolves, downloads and caches the driver in ~/.cache/selenium, and since 4.11 can download browsers. Driver mismatch An old driver on PATH or given by hand gives session not created. Delete it and let Selenium Manager match the driver. Eight components Start session, act on browser, ask the browser, wait strategy, find element, act on element, ask the element, end session. Code The eight basic components of a Selenium script import java.time.Duration;Duration describes a length of time, used for the wait below. import org.openqa.selenium.By;By holds the ways to locate an element: by name, by CSS selector and more. import org.openqa.selenium.WebElement;A WebElement is your handle on one element of the page. WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();1. Start the session. Chrome opens with a fresh profile. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");2. Take action on the browser: go to the practice form. String title = driver.getTitle();3. Request browser information: the page title. driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofMillis(500));4. Establish a waiting strategy. Find calls will now retry for up to half a second. WebElement textBox = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\"));5. Find an element: the text box whose name attribute is my-text. WebElement submitButton = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\"));5. Find another: the first button on the page. textBox.sendKeys(\"Selenium\");6. Take action on an element: type into the box. submitButton.click();6. Click Submit. The browser loads the next page. WebElement message = driver.findElement(By.id(\"message\"));Find the message on the new page. The implicit wait gives it time to appear. String value = message.getText();7. Request element information: its visible text. driver.quit();8. End the session. The browser and the driver both close. Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Quit even when something fails WebDriver driver = newChromeDriver();Start the session outside the try, so driver is known in finally. try {Everything that might fail goes inside try. driver.findElement(By.id(\"no-such-id\"));This element does not exist, so this line throws. System.out.println(\"never printed\");Java jumps straight to catch and never reaches this line. } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {Catch the error so we can report it. System.out.println(\"Failed: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]);Print only the first line of the message. } finally {finally runs whether the try worked or failed. driver.quit();So the browser always closes. Lesson: Always end the session Tables Table: language · minimum version for selenium 4.51 · how you add selenium Language Minimum version for Selenium 4.51 How you add Selenium Java Java 11 (the jars are built for Java 11) A build tool. Add org.seleniumhq.selenium:selenium-java to Maven or Gradle. Lesson: Install Selenium for your language Table: setting · default · what it does Setting Default What it does cache-path ~/.cache/selenium Where drivers, browsers and metadata are stored ttl 3600 seconds How long resolved versions are trusted before asking the vendor again offline false No network requests or downloads at all avoid-stats false Selenium Manager sends anonymous usage numbers to Plausible once a day. Set SE_AVOID_STATS=true to stop it. proxy none An HTTP proxy for the downloads Lesson: Selenium Manager finds the driver Table: step · what it means · in this example Step What it means In this example 1. Start the session Create a driver. A browser opens. Chrome 2. Take action on the browser Navigate, go back, resize and so on. Open the web form 3. Request browser information Title, address, window handles, cookies. Read the title 4. Establish a waiting"],[20,"setup-first-script","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","strategy Make sure an element is present before you look for it. Implicit wait of 500 ms 5. Find an element Most commands are about elements. The text box and the button 6. Take action on an element Type, click, clear. Type Selenium, click Submit 7. Request element information Text, attributes, size, state. Read the message 8. End the session Quit the driver and close the browser. quit Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Table: quit · close quit close Closes Every window and tab of the session Only the current window or tab Driver process Stopped Keeps running while other windows are open After it The driver object cannot be used again Switch to another window to carry on Lesson: Always end the session Tips and traps Common mistake If you pass a driver path yourself, Selenium trusts you and never calls Selenium Manager. The documentation says that with Selenium 4.6 or newer you should not need to set a driver location at all. Delete old drivers from PATH and let Selenium Manager do the job.Lesson: Selenium Manager finds the driver Common mistake The documentation itself warns that an implicit wait is rarely the best solution. It is used here because it is the easiest to show. Chapter 4.1 replaces it with explicit waits.Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Remember In a test framework the setup and teardown hooks do this job: start the driver before each test and quit it after each test, pass or fail. Chapter 5.1 shows the hooks in JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha.Lesson: Always end the session"],[20,"step-2","Step 2Control the browser","2.1"],[20,"sessions-options","Sessions, options and timeouts","Open chapter Options and capabilities, page load strategy, the three session timeouts and the driver service, shown with real runs. Key ideas Options Since Selenium 4, describe sessions with Options classes. They become W3C capabilities, and browser specific ones carry a prefix such as goog:chromeOptions. Page load strategy normal waits for complete (load), eager for interactive (DOMContentLoaded), none for the first download. It covers navigation by address, not clicks. Default timeouts Implicit 0, page load 300,000 ms, script 30,000 ms. Java prints PT0S, PT5M and PT30S. Service Starts and stops a local driver: file, port, arguments and logs. Not used for remote sessions. Granted capabilities Read them after New Session to learn the real browser version, platform and defaults such as dismiss and notify. Code Ask for a session with options, then read what the browser agreed to ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions();An Options object describes the session you want. browserName is already set to chrome. options.addArguments(\"--headless=new\");A Chrome command line switch: run without a visible window. options.addArguments(\"--window-size=1280,800\");Another switch: the size of the browser window. options.setPageLoadStrategy(PageLoadStrategy.EAGER);A W3C capability: get() returns at DOMContentLoaded. ChromeDriver driver = newChromeDriver(options);New Session is sent with these capabilities. Capabilities caps = driver.getCapabilities();The capabilities the driver actually granted. System.out.println(\"browserVersion: \" + caps.getBrowserVersion());The exact version of the browser that started. System.out.println(\"platformName: \" + caps.getPlatformName());The operating system at the remote end. System.out.println(\"pageLoadStrategy: \" + caps.getCapability(\"pageLoadStrategy\"));Our request was honoured. System.out.println(\"unhandledPromptBehavior: \" + caps.getCapability(\"unhandledPromptBehavior\"));A default we did not set. Lesson: Options and capabilities Read the three session timeouts, then trip one on purpose WebDriver.Timeouts t = driver.manage().timeouts();The timeouts of this session. System.out.println(\"implicit: \" + t.getImplicitWaitTimeout());Java prints a Duration. PT0S means zero seconds. System.out.println(\"pageLoad: \" + t.getPageLoadTimeout());PT5M is five minutes, which is 300,000 ms. System.out.println(\"script: \" + t.getScriptTimeout());PT30S is thirty seconds. t.scriptTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1));Allow scripts only one second from now on. ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeAsyncScript(\"/* never calls its callback */\");An async script must call its callback. This one never does. } catch (ScriptTimeoutException e) {After one second the driver gives up with the W3C error script timeout. Lesson: The three session timeouts Start the driver on a port you choose and ask if it is ready ChromeDriverService service = newChromeDriverService.Builder().usingPort(9600).build();A Service describes how to start chromedriver: here, on port 9600. ChromeDriver driver = newChromeDriver(service);Selenium starts chromedriver on that port, then opens a session. System.out.println(\"Driver URL: \" + service.getUrl());The address the binding sends its HTTP commands to. HttpRequest status = HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(\"http://localhost:9600/status\")).build();GET /status is a W3C command that needs no session. System.out.println(\"ready: \" + body.contains(\"\\\"ready\\\":true\"));The driver says whether it can start new sessions. driver.quit();Ends the session and stops the service. Lesson: The driver service Numbers to remember Implicit wait 0 How long Find Element keeps looking before it gives up with no such element. Zero means it checks once. Lesson: The three session timeouts Page loadExam favourite 300,000 ms Five minutes. How long a navigation may take before a timeout error. Lesson: The three session timeouts Script 30,000 ms Thirty seconds. How long an injected script may run before the script timeout error. Lesson: The three session"],[20,"sessions-options","Sessions, options and timeouts","timeouts Tables Table: capability · what it controls · default Capability What it controls Default browserName Which browser. Set for you by each Options class. set by the class browserVersion Which version. Selenium Manager can download it if missing. whatever is installed platformName The operating system at the remote end. Matters most on Grid and in the cloud. the local OS pageLoadStrategy When navigation commands return: normal, eager or none. normal acceptInsecureCerts Trust expired or self signed TLS certificates. false timeouts Script, page load and implicit wait. 30 s, 300 s, 0 unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to an alert your code did not handle. dismiss and notify proxy Send browser traffic through a proxy server. none setWindowRect Whether the browser supports resizing and moving windows. reported by the driver strictFileInteractability Strict checks for hidden file inputs. off Lesson: Options and capabilities Table: chrome switch · what it does Chrome switch What it does --headless=new No visible window. Common on build servers. --start-maximized Open the window maximised. --window-size=1280,800 A fixed window size, so pages lay out the same on every machine. --user-data-dir=... Use a chosen profile folder instead of a fresh temporary one. Lesson: Options and capabilities Table: strategy · waits until readystate is · browser event · use it when Strategy Waits until readyState is Browser event Use it when normal (default) complete load: every image, font and style sheet is in You want the safest behaviour eager interactive DOMContentLoaded: the HTML is parsed, images may still load Large images or ads slow the page and the test does not need them none any only the first download of the page You control every wait yourself Lesson: Page load strategy Table: language · set the page load timeout to 30 s Language Set the page load timeout to 30 s Java driver.manage().timeouts().pageLoadTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(30)) Lesson: The three session timeouts Table: service setting · why you would change it Service setting Why you would change it Driver location Only with Selenium older than 4.6 or a special setup. Otherwise let Selenium Manager find it. Port A firewall allows only certain ports, or you want to talk to the driver yourself. Log output and level Driver logs are thrown away unless you send them somewhere. Levels are ALL, DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, SEVERE and OFF. Arguments Pass chromedriver switches, such as appending to a log file. Lesson: The driver service Tips and traps Remember Each Options class can also take the binary path of a Chromium based browser, extensions, and excludeSwitches to turn off a default switch of chromedriver. The documentation lists them on the Chrome page. Use them only when you need them, because every extra setting is one more difference from what real users run.Lesson: Options and capabilities Common mistake Switching to eager or none to make tests faster is a trade. Every element that arrives later now needs an explicit wait, or the test becomes flaky. Chapter 4.1 shows how to write those waits.Lesson: Page load strategy In the exam These timeouts belong to the browser session. Explicit waits such as WebDriverWait are different: they run in your code and poll the browser. Mixing an implicit wait with explicit waits can give surprising wait times, which chapter 4.1 explains.Lesson: The three session timeouts 2.2"],[20,"navigate-screenshots","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","Open chapter Navigation and history, title and URL, window size and position, screenshots and printing pages to PDF. Key ideas Navigation get or navigate().to, back, forward, refresh. Where am I Title and current URL. Check them when a test depends on a redirect or on history, because back on the first page raises no error. Window rect Set and get size and position. An exact size is more portable than maximize, which needs a window manager. Screenshots Take Screenshot (viewport) and Take Element Screenshot return base64 PNG. Save one when a test fails, before quit. Print Page Returns a base64 PDF. Use PrintOptions for orientation, ranges, size, margins and scale. Chromium needs headless. Code Go somewhere, go back, go forward, reload driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\");Navigate To. The short form of driver.navigate().to(...). driver.navigate().to(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html\");The long form does the same thing. System.out.println(\"2 \" + driver.getTitle() + \" at \" + driver.getCurrentUrl());Title and address of the page now showing. driver.navigate().back();Press the Back button. driver.navigate().forward();Press the Forward button. driver.navigate().refresh();Reload the current page. Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Set the window size and position, then read them back WebDriver.Window window = driver.manage().window();Every window command lives under manage().window(). window.setSize(newDimension(1024, 768));Restore the window and make it 1024 by 768 pixels. window.setPosition(newPoint(40, 30));Move its top left corner to x 40, y 30 on the screen. Dimension size = window.getSize();Ask the browser for the size it really has. Lesson: Window size and position Save a screenshot of the page and of one element File page = ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);Take Screenshot of the viewport. Selenium writes a temporary PNG file. Files.copy(page.toPath(), Path.of(\"page.png\"));Copy it somewhere you will find it. File only = button.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE);Take Element Screenshot: just the area of the button. BufferedImage img = ImageIO.read(newFile(name));Open the PNG to read its size in pixels. Lesson: Screenshots Print the page to a PDF file ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions().addArguments(\"--headless=new\");Chromium prints to PDF only in headless mode. PrintOptions print = newPrintOptions();Paper settings: orientation, page ranges, size, margins, scale, background. Pdf pdf = ((PrintsPage) driver).print(print);The W3C Print Page command. The PDF comes back as base64 text. byte[] bytes = Base64.getDecoder().decode(pdf.getContent());Decode it to the real bytes. System.out.println(\"page.pdf starts with \" + newString(bytes, 0, 5));Every PDF file starts with %PDF-. Lesson: Print a page to PDF Tables Table: what you want · java What you want Java Open an address driver.get(url) or navigate().to(url) Back navigate().back() Forward navigate().forward() Reload navigate().refresh() Page title getTitle() Current address getCurrentUrl() Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Table: command · what it does Command What it does Set window size Restores the window if it was maximised, then sets width and height in pixels Set window position Moves the top left corner to x and y on the screen Get window rect Width, height, x and y in one answer Maximize Makes the window fill the screen, without covering the system menus Minimize Hides the window, usually to the taskbar. The exact result depends on the window manager. Selenium 4 and later. Fullscreen Fills the whole screen, like pressing F11 Lesson: Window size and position Table: language · page screenshot to file · element screenshot to file Language Page screenshot to file Element screenshot to file Java ((TakesScreenshot) driver).getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE) element.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE) Lesson: Screenshots Table: printoptions setting · example PrintOptions setting Example Orientation"],[20,"navigate-screenshots","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","portrait or landscape Page ranges \"2-4\" Page size A4, Legal and others Margins top, bottom, left, right Scale 1.0 is 100 %, 0.25 is 25 % Background whether background colours and images print Shrink to fit whether wide content is shrunk to the page Lesson: Print a page to PDF Tips and traps Common mistake After refresh or any navigation, the elements you found before belong to the old page. Using them raises stale element reference. Find them again on the new page. Chapter 5.4 covers this error in depth.Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Common mistake Maximize and minimize need a desktop window manager. On a bare Linux build server they can fail with an unknown error, which is what happened when this page was built. Setting an exact size with Set Window Rect or the --window-size switch works everywhere and gives the same layout on every machine.Lesson: Window size and position Remember Take a screenshot in the teardown of a failing test, before quit. Name the file after the test, so the report can link to it. Chapter 5.1 wires this into JUnit, pytest and Mocha.Lesson: Screenshots"],[20,"step-3","Step 3Find and use elements","3.1"],[20,"locators","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","Open chapter All eight locator strategies, CSS and XPath, relative locators and how to choose a locator that lasts. Key ideas Eight strategies id, name, class name, css selector, xpath, tag name, link text, partial link text. find element returns the first match. Five on the wire W3C defines css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath. Bindings turn id, name and class name into CSS. CSS and XPath CSS: #id, .class, tag[attr=\"v\"], parent > child. XPath adds text matching and moving up to a parent. Prefer relative XPath (//). Relative locators above, below, toLeftOf, toRightOf and near (50 px), built on getBoundingClientRect. They depend on layout, so check what they return. Preference Unique stable id, then a short CSS selector, then XPath. Avoid absolute paths, styling classes and generated ids. Consider data-test attributes. Code All eight locator strategies on the documentation example page staticvoid show(String how, WebElement e) {A helper that prints which element a locator found. show(\"className\", driver.findElement(By.className(\"information\")));The first element whose class list contains information. show(\"cssSelector\", driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"#fname\")));CSS: # means id, so the input with id fname. show(\"id\", driver.findElement(By.id(\"lname\")));The element whose id attribute is lname. show(\"name\", driver.findElement(By.name(\"newsletter\")));The element whose name attribute is newsletter. show(\"linkText\", driver.findElement(By.linkText(\"Selenium Official Page\")));A link whose whole visible text matches. show(\"partialLinkText\", driver.findElement(By.partialLinkText(\"Official\")));A link whose visible text contains Official. show(\"tagName\", driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"a\")));The first a element on the page. show(\"xpath\", driver.findElement(By.xpath(\"//input[@value='f']\")));XPath: any input whose value attribute is f. Lesson: The eight locator strategies Relative locators: find an element by where it sits importstatic org.openqa.selenium.support.locators.RelativeLocator.with;with() starts a relative locator. System.out.println(how + \" -> \" + e.getAttribute(\"outerHTML\"));outerHTML shows the whole tag that was found. show(\"below last name label\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).below(By.cssSelector(\"label[for='lname']\"))));An input below the Last name label. The closest one wins. show(\"above lname\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).above(By.id(\"lname\"))));An input above the lname box. show(\"right of Male\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).toRightOf(By.cssSelector(\"input[value='m']\"))));An input to the right of the Male radio. show(\"below fname and above the checkbox\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).below(By.id(\"fname\")).above(By.name(\"newsletter\"))));Chained: both conditions must hold. show(\"near Newsletter label\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).near(By.cssSelector(\"label[for='newsletter']\"))));near means at most 50 pixels away. Look at what it really returned. show(\"left of password\", driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).toLeftOf(By.id(\"password-field\"))));On the login page the three inputs sit in one row. Lesson: Relative locators Tables Table: strategy · finds · example Strategy Finds Example id The element whose id attribute matches lname name Elements whose name attribute matches newsletter class name Elements whose class list contains the value. Compound names such as \"a b\" are not allowed. information css selector Elements matching a CSS selector #fname xpath Elements matching an XPath expression //input[@value='f'] tag name Elements with that tag a link text Links whose visible text is exactly the value Selenium Official Page partial link text Links whose visible text contains the value Official Lesson: The eight locator strategies Table: to find · css selector · xpath To find CSS selector XPath By id #fname //*[@id=\"fname\"] By class .information //*[contains(@class,\"information\")] Tag with attribute"],[20,"locators","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","input[name=\"fname\"] //input[@name=\"fname\"] Attribute starts with a[href^=\"https\"] //a[starts-with(@href,\"https\")] Attribute contains a[href*=\"selenium\"] //a[contains(@href,\"selenium\")] Direct child form > input //form/input Any descendant form input //form//input Second matching input input:nth-of-type(2) (//input)[2] By visible text not possible //label[text()=\"Last name:\"] The parent not possible //input[@id=\"lname\"]/.. Next sibling label + input //label[@for=\"lname\"]/following-sibling::input[1] Lesson: CSS selectors and XPath Table: method · finds an element that is · note Method Finds an element that is Note above higher on the page than the anchor below lower on the page than the anchor toLeftOf / to_left_of left of the anchor toRightOf / to_right_of right of the anchor near at most 50 pixels from the anchor Useful for an input next to its label Lesson: Relative locators Table: fragile locator · why it breaks · better Fragile locator Why it breaks Better /html/body/div[2]/form/input[3] Any new div or input shifts the path #lname .btn.btn-primary.mt-3 Styling classes change when the design changes [data-test=\"submit\"] //button[text()=\"Submit\"] Breaks when the text is translated or reworded An id or a data attribute #ember1234 Generated ids change on every page load A stable attribute near it Lesson: Choosing a good locator Tips and traps Remember find element (singular) returns the first match or raises no such element. find elements (plural) returns every match in page order, or an empty list. Chapter 3.2 covers the difference in detail.Lesson: The eight locator strategies Common mistake Each relative locator sends the page a script to run, so it is slower than a plain CSS selector. Prefer an id or a short CSS selector, and keep relative locators for elements with no good attributes.Lesson: Relative locators In the exam Keep locators in one place, apart from the steps that use them. The documentation recommends declaring them separately, which is exactly what a page object does. Chapter 5.2 shows the pattern.Lesson: Choosing a good locator 3.2"],[20,"finding-elements","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","Open chapter Singular and plural finds, scoped searches and the XPath trap, shadow DOM and the active element. Key ideas One or many find element returns the first match or raises no such element. find elements returns all matches or an empty list. Search context You can search from the driver, from an element or from a shadow root. CSS from an element stays inside it. XPath trap An XPath starting with // always starts at the document, even when called on an element. Use .// to search below the element. Shadow DOM Find the host, get its shadow root, then search with a CSS selector. Chrome refuses XPath there. Active element switchTo().activeElement() returns the focused element. Use it to test keyboard flow such as Tab order. Code One element, all elements, and what happens when nothing matches List<WebElement> inputs = driver.findElements(By.tagName(\"input\"));Plural: a List of every input, in page order. for (WebElement e : inputs) {Walk the list like any Java list. List<WebElement> none = driver.findElements(By.id(\"email\"));No match: an empty list, never an error. driver.findElement(By.id(\"email\"));Singular with no match: an exception. Lesson: Find one or find many Search inside one element, and the XPath trap WebElement form = driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"form\"));First find the parent element. System.out.println(\"inputs in form: \" + form.findElements(By.cssSelector(\"input\")).size());Called on the form, CSS only looks inside it. System.out.println(\"links via //a: \" + form.findElements(By.xpath(\"//a\")).size());The trap: // starts at the top of the document, so the link outside the form is found. System.out.println(\"links via .//a: \" + form.findElements(By.xpath(\".//a\")).size());The dot means start here. Now nothing outside the form counts. WebElement last = form.findElement(By.id(\"lname\"));Two commands: find the form, then find inside it. WebElement same = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"form #lname\"));The optimised version: one CSS selector, one command. System.out.println(\"same element: \" + last.equals(same));Both references point at the same element. Lesson: Search inside an element Reach into a shadow root System.out.println(\"from the page: \" + driver.findElements(By.cssSelector(box)).size());The checkbox lives in a shadow tree, so a normal search finds nothing. WebElement host = driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"custom-checkbox-element\"));The shadow host: the element the hidden tree is attached to. SearchContext shadowRoot = host.getShadowRoot();Get Element Shadow Root. A SearchContext, like the driver and an element. WebElement checkbox = shadowRoot.findElement(By.cssSelector(box));Search inside the shadow tree. shadowRoot.findElement(By.xpath(\".//input\"));XPath inside a shadow root. System.out.println(\"xpath in shadow root: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]);Chrome refuses it. Lesson: The shadow DOM Which element has the focus? System.out.println(\"at start: \" + driver.switchTo().activeElement().getTagName());Nothing is focused yet, so the active element is the body. driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\")).click();Clicking a text box gives it the focus. driver.switchTo().activeElement().sendKeys(Keys.TAB);Press Tab on whatever has the focus, as a user would. System.out.println(\"after Tab: \" + driver.switchTo().activeElement().getAttribute(\"name\"));The focus moved to the next field. Lesson: The active element Tables Table: find element (singular) · find elements (plural) find element (singular) find elements (plural) Returns One element: the first match in page order A list of every match, in page order No match Raises no such element Returns an empty list W3C command Find Element Find Elements Use it for An element that must be there Counting, looping, or checking that something is absent Lesson: Find one or find many Tips and traps Common mistake find elements never waits for the first element to appear unless an implicit wait is set, and then it returns as soon as at least one matches. An empty list can mean the"],[20,"finding-elements","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","page is not ready yet. Chapter 4.1 shows how to wait properly.Lesson: Find one or find many Remember Searching inside a container is still very useful when the page repeats a pattern: the Delete button inside the third row of a table, or the price inside one product card. Find the row or card first, then the button inside it.Lesson: Search inside an element Remember A shadow root can contain another shadow host. Then you repeat the two steps: get that host, get its shadow root, search again. Each level is one more pair of commands.Lesson: The shadow DOM 3.3"],[20,"interacting","Interacting with elements and reading them","Open chapter Click, type, clear, the checks before every action and their errors, element information, select lists and uploads. Key ideas Five commands click, send keys, clear, submit and select. Prefer clicking the submit button over submit(). send keys appends, so clear first. Built in checks Before acting the driver scrolls the element into view and checks it is interactable. Errors: element not interactable, invalid element state, element click intercepted. Attribute vs property DOM attribute is the HTML, DOM property is the live value. The classic getAttribute mixes both. Select and upload Select works only on select elements: by visible text, value or index. Upload by sending the full path to input type=\"file\". Code Fill in a form: clear, type, tick and click driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(2));As in chapter 1.3: let finds retry for up to 2 seconds, so the page after Submit has time to load. text.sendKeys(\"first try\");Element Send Keys: types into the box, adding to what is already there. text.clear();Element Clear: empties the box. text.sendKeys(\"Selenium\");Type the real value. System.out.println(\"check 2 before: \" + check2.isSelected());isSelected reads whether a checkbox is ticked. check2.click();Element Click: ticks it, as a mouse click would. driver.findElement(By.id(\"my-radio-2\")).click();Clicking the second radio button unselects the first one. driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")).click();Click Submit. The documentation prefers this to the submit() method. Lesson: Click, type and clear The three errors interactions give, made on purpose staticvoid attempt(String what, Runnable step) {Run one step and report the error it raises, if any. attempt(\"type into disabled\", () -> driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-disabled\")).sendKeys(\"x\"));A disabled input cannot take keyboard input. attempt(\"clear readonly\", () -> driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-readonly\")).clear());A readonly input cannot be reset. ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(Add a cookie banner that covers the bottom of the page, as many real sites do. attempt(\"click under banner\", () -> driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")).click());The banner sits over the centre of the button. Lesson: Click, type and clear Ask an element about itself text.sendKeys(\"hello\");Type, so the live value differs from the HTML. System.out.println(\"disabled box enabled: \" + driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-disabled\")).isEnabled());The disabled input reports false. System.out.println(\"tag: \" + text.getTagName() + \", rect: \" + text.getRect().getWidth() + \" x \" + text.getRect().getHeight());Get Element Rect: position and size in pixels. System.out.println(\"getAttribute(value): \" + text.getAttribute(\"value\"));The classic method: property first, then attribute. System.out.println(\"getDomAttribute(value): \" + text.getDomAttribute(\"value\"));Only what the HTML says. The HTML has no value attribute. System.out.println(\"getDomProperty(value): \" + text.getDomProperty(\"value\"));The live JavaScript property: what is typed now. System.out.println(\"getDomAttribute(myprop): \" + text.getDomAttribute(\"myprop\"));A made up attribute in the HTML. System.out.println(\"button colour: \" + driver.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")).getCssValue(\"color\"));The computed CSS value. System.out.println(\"heading text: \" + driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"h1\")).getText());The rendered, visible text. Lesson: Read information about an element Work with single and multiple select lists import org.openqa.selenium.support.ui.Select;Select lives in the support package. Select single = newSelect(driver.findElement(By.name(\"selectomatic\")));Wrap a select element in a Select object. System.out.println(\"options: \" + single.getOptions().size());getOptions lists every option element. single.selectByVisibleText(\"Four\");Pick by the text the user sees. single.selectByValue(\"two\");Pick by the value attribute. single.selectByIndex(0);Pick by position, counting from 0. Select multi ="],[20,"interacting","Interacting with elements and reading them","newSelect(driver.findElement(By.id(\"multi\")));A select with the multiple attribute. System.out.println(\"multi starts with: \" + multi.getAllSelectedOptions().size() + \" selected\");Eggs and Sausages are selected in the HTML. multi.deselectByValue(\"eggs\");Only multiple lists can deselect. Lesson: Select lists Upload a file without the file dialog Path file = Files.writeString(Path.of(\"report.txt\"), \"quarterly numbers\");Make a small file to upload. WebElement input = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-file\"));The input with type=\"file\". input.sendKeys(file.toAbsolutePath().toString());Send the full path. No dialog opens, the file is simply attached. System.out.println(\"browser shows: \" + input.getDomProperty(\"value\"));Browsers hide the real folder and show a made up folder called fakepath. Lesson: File upload Tables Table: command · works on · watch out for Command Works on Watch out for click Any element It clicks the centre of the element. If something else is on top there, you get element click intercepted. send keys Text fields and other keyboard interactable elements It adds to the existing text. Clear first if you want to replace it. clear Editable, resettable elements: text inputs and content editable Readonly and disabled inputs give invalid element state. submit Form elements In Selenium 4 it runs a script, so the documentation recommends clicking the submit button instead. select select and option elements Use the Select class, covered below. Lesson: Click, type and clear Table: question · java Question Java Is it displayed? isDisplayed() Is it enabled? isEnabled() Is it selected? isSelected() Tag name getTagName() Position and size getRect() Computed CSS getCssValue(name) Visible text getText() Lesson: Read information about an element Table: method · reads · use it for Method Reads Use it for get DOM attribute Only the attribute in the HTML Checking what the server sent, such as a data-test value get DOM property Only the live JavaScript property What is in a field now, whether a box is checked now get attribute (classic) The property if there is one, otherwise the attribute. Selenium does this with a JavaScript atom Old code. Prefer the two precise methods. Lesson: Read information about an element Table: rule from the documentation · what it means for you Rule from the documentation What it means for you Select only works on select and option elements Drop downs built from div or li with JavaScript need normal clicks instead Since Selenium 4.5 a Select cannot be created on a disabled select Check isEnabled first if the list may be disabled Disabled options cannot be selected Trying raises an error Only multiple lists can deselect deselect on a single list raises an error Lesson: Select lists Tips and traps Remember When the test runs on a Grid, the file must reach the remote machine. The Java, Python and JavaScript bindings handle this with a file detector that uploads the file to the node first. Chapter 6.1 shows it.Lesson: File upload"],[20,"interacting","Step 4Synchronise and control","4.1"],[20,"waits","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","Open chapter Why tests race the page, implicit and explicit waits, expected conditions, custom waits and why not to mix them. Key ideas Implicit wait A session setting, default 0. Every find keeps looking up to that long and returns as soon as the element appears. It only waits for presence. Explicit wait A loop with a condition, timeout and polling interval. Defaults: Java 500 ms. Expected conditions presence, visibility, clickable, text present, title contains, staleness. Wait for visibility before typing. Never mix Implicit plus explicit makes waits unpredictable: 10 s with 15 s can time out after about 20 s. Keep implicit at 0 and use explicit waits. Code The race: look for the box straight after the click driver.findElement(By.id(\"adder\")).click();The page adds a red box, but only 1 second after the click. driver.findElement(By.id(\"box0\"));Our code asks at once. No wait is set, so the driver checks only once. System.out.println(\"too early: no such element\");Selenium was faster than the page. Lesson: The race between your code and the page Implicit wait: every find keeps looking driver.manage().timeouts().implicitlyWait(Duration.ofSeconds(2));For the whole session: a find may keep looking for up to 2 seconds. WebElement box = driver.findElement(By.id(\"box0\"));Not there yet, so the driver keeps checking until the box appears. System.out.println(\"found \" + box.getAttribute(\"class\") + \" after about \" + Math.round(seconds) + \" s\");Returns as soon as it is found, not after the full 2 seconds. Lesson: Implicit waits Explicit wait: wait for exactly the condition you need WebElement revealed = driver.findElement(By.id(\"revealed\"));The input is in the page already, just hidden. driver.findElement(By.id(\"reveal\")).click();The page shows it 1 second later. WebDriverWait wait = newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2));Wait at most 2 seconds. Java checks every 500 ms. wait.until(d -> revealed.isDisplayed());A lambda: keep calling it until it returns true. revealed.sendKeys(\"Displayed\");Safe now: the input is displayed, so it can take keys. Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions Ready made conditions, and what a timeout looks like WebElement box = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.presenceOfElementLocated(By.id(\"box0\")));A ready made condition. It returns the element it waited for. WebElement input = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id(\"revealed\")));Present is not enough for typing. Wait until it is visible. newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(1)).withMessage(\"the second box never came\")Our own message makes the failure readable. .until(ExpectedConditions.presenceOfElementLocated(By.id(\"box1\")));Nobody clicked again, so box1 never appears. Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions A customised wait: poll faster and retry on an error Wait<WebDriver> wait = newFluentWait<WebDriver>(driver)FluentWait lets you set every part of the loop. .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))Give up after 2 seconds. .pollingEvery(Duration.ofMillis(300))Check every 300 ms instead of 500. .ignoring(ElementNotInteractableException.class);Treat this error as not yet and try again. wait.until(d -> {The condition does the action itself and returns true when it worked. Lesson: Customising waits, and never mixing them Tables Table: strength · weakness Strength Weakness One line covers every find in the session It only waits for presence. A present element can still be hidden, covered or disabled Finds return as soon as the element appears Every check that something is absent now takes the full wait Easy to show in a first script It cannot wait for text, a title, a count or anything that is not a find Lesson: Implicit waits Table: default · java webdriverwait Default Java WebDriverWait Polling interval 500 ms Errors ignored while looping NotFoundException (such as no such element) Timeout you must give one Error at the end TimeoutException Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions Table: wait for · java Wait for"],[20,"waits","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","Java Element exists presenceOfElementLocated Element visible visibilityOfElementLocated Element clickable elementToBeClickable Text present textToBePresentInElement Title contains titleContains Element gone stale stalenessOf Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions 4.2"],[20,"windows-frames-alerts","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","Open chapter Switching windows and tabs, working inside iframes, and handling alerts, confirms and prompts. Key ideas Windows Each window or tab has a handle. Save the original, switch to the new one, and switch back after close(). newWindow(\"tab\") opens and switches in one step. Frames Switch into an iframe by element, name or id, or index before finding inside it. defaultContent goes to the top, parentFrame up one level. Title in frames Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. Alerts alert, confirm and prompt: getText, accept, dismiss, sendKeys. Wait with alert is present when it appears late. Forgotten pop ups unhandledPromptBehavior defaults to dismiss and notify: the pop up is closed and the next command raises unexpected alert open. Code Follow a link into a new window, come back, open a new tab String original = driver.getWindowHandle();The id of the current window. It stays the same for the whole session. driver.findElement(By.name(\"windowTwo\")).click();This link has target=\"_blank\", so it opens a new window. newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(ExpectedConditions.numberOfWindowsToBe(2));Wait until the second window exists. for (String handle : driver.getWindowHandles()) {Every open window, in the order they were opened. if (!handle.equals(original)) driver.switchTo().window(handle);Switch to the one that is not the original. driver.close();Close only this window. driver.switchTo().window(original);Switch back, or the next command fails with no such window. driver.switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB);Selenium 4: open a new tab and switch to it in one step. driver.quit();Closes every window and tab at once. Lesson: Windows and tabs Switch into an iframe three ways and back out System.out.println(\"email boxes seen from the top: \" + driver.findElements(By.id(\"email\")).size());The form lives inside the iframe, so the top document sees none. driver.switchTo().frame(frame);1. Switch with a WebElement: the most flexible way. driver.switchTo().defaultContent();Leave the frame: back to the top document. driver.switchTo().frame(\"iframe1-name\");2. Switch with the frame name or id. driver.switchTo().frame(0);3. Switch with an index: the first frame of the page. System.out.println(\"title inside the frame: \" + driver.getTitle());Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. System.out.println(\"heading: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"iframe_page_heading\")).getText());Outside elements are reachable again. Lesson: Frames and iframes Alert, confirm, prompt, a slow alert and one left open driver.findElement(By.id(\"alert\")).click();The page calls alert('cheese'). Alert alert = wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent());Wait for the alert, then get a handle on it. alert.accept();Press OK. driver.findElement(By.id(\"confirm\")).click();A confirm box: OK or Cancel. Alert confirm = driver.switchTo().alert();Switch to it without waiting, because it opens at once. confirm.dismiss();Press Cancel. driver.findElement(By.id(\"prompt\")).click();A prompt asks for text. prompt.sendKeys(\"Selenium\");Type the answer into the prompt. System.out.println(\"page shows: \" + driver.findElement(By.id(\"text\")).getText());The page wrote our answer into a paragraph. driver.findElement(By.id(\"slow-alert\")).click();This one opens 200 ms later. System.out.println(\"slow: \" + wait.until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent()).getText());switchTo().alert() now would fail, so wait. driver.findElement(By.id(\"alert\")).click();Open one more alert and forget about it. driver.getTitle();Any command while an alert is open. System.out.println(\"left open: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]);The default behaviour dismisses it and reports an error. Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Tables Table: what you want · java What you want Java Current handle getWindowHandle() All handles getWindowHandles() Switch switchTo().window(h) New tab or window switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB) Close current close() Lesson:"],[20,"windows-frames-alerts","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","Windows and tabs Table: switch by · example · when to use Switch by Example When to use WebElement switchTo().frame(element) Most flexible. Find the frame with any locator. Name or id switchTo().frame(\"iframe1-name\") The frame has a stable name or id. If several match, the first one is used. Java and Python accept the string. JavaScript needs the element. Index switchTo().frame(0) Position in window.frames. Breaks if frames are added. Leave all frames switchTo().defaultContent() Back to the top document. Up one level switchTo().parentFrame() For frames inside frames. Lesson: Frames and iframes Table: pop up · buttons · what you can do Pop up Buttons What you can do alert OK Read the text, accept confirm OK and Cancel Read the text, accept or dismiss prompt OK and Cancel, plus a text box Read the text, send keys, accept or dismiss. Sending keys replaces the placeholder text Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Table: unhandledpromptbehavior · what happens to a forgotten pop up unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to a forgotten pop up dismiss and notify (default) Dismissed, and the command raises unexpected alert open accept and notify Accepted, and the command raises unexpected alert open dismiss Dismissed silently accept Accepted silently ignore Left open, and the command raises unexpected alert open Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Tips and traps Common mistake After close() you are still pointing at the closed window. The documentation warns that the next command raises no such window. Always switch back to a handle you saved before.Lesson: Windows and tabs Remember Recent drivers dismiss beforeunload prompts, the \"Leave site?\" box, by default. Chapter 2.1 showed where unhandledPromptBehavior is set.Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts 4.3"],[20,"actions","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","Open chapter Mouse, keyboard and wheel actions, and how a chain becomes ticks. Key ideas Build then perform An action chain is sent in one request when perform() is called. Nothing happens before that. Ticks Each input source has one action per tick. A tick lasts as long as its slowest action. Mouse doubleClick, contextClick, moveToElement, clickAndHold, dragAndDrop, moveByOffset. The driver remembers the pointer position between chains. Keyboard keyDown and keyUp for modifiers, sendKeys for text. Use Command on a Mac and Control elsewhere. Release every key you press. Wheel scrollToElement, scrollByAmount, scrollFromOrigin. Wait for the scroll result, because it can land after perform() returns. Code Double click, right click, hover, drag and drop, and move to a point newActions(driver).doubleClick(box).perform();Build a chain, then perform() sends it to the browser in one request. newActions(driver).contextClick(box).perform();A right click. newActions(driver).moveToElement(driver.findElement(By.id(\"hover\"))).perform();Move to the centre of the element. Nothing is clicked. newActions(driver).dragAndDrop(from, to).perform();Move to from, press, move to to, release. newActions(driver).moveToLocation(8, 12).perform();A point measured from the top left of the viewport. newActions(driver).moveByOffset(13, 15).perform();From wherever the pointer is now. The driver remembered it between chains. Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Hold Shift, select all with the right modifier, and type into an element .keyDown(Keys.SHIFT)Press Shift and keep holding it. .sendKeys(\"abc\")Goes to the element that has focus. The page focuses this box on load. .keyUp(Keys.SHIFT)Let go of Shift. Keys cmdCtrl = Platform.getCurrent().is(Platform.MAC) ? Keys.COMMAND : Keys.CONTROL;Select all is Command A on a Mac and Control A elsewhere. newActions(driver).sendKeys(input, \"Selenium!\").perform();Clicks the element first, then types. Lesson: Keyboard actions Scroll to an element, by an amount, and inside a frame WebElement frame = driver.findElement(By.tagName(\"iframe\"));The page starts with a 5000 px tall block, so the iframe is far below. newActions(driver).scrollToElement(frame).perform();Scroll until the bottom of the element is in view. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html\");Load it again. A refresh would keep the scroll position. newActions(driver).scrollByAmount(0, 300).perform();Like turning the wheel: 300 px down from the top left of the viewport. newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(d -> ((Number) js.executeScript(\"return window.scrollY\")).intValue() >= 300);The scroll can land after perform returns, so wait for it. newActions(driver).scrollFromOrigin(WheelInput.ScrollOrigin.fromElement(inner), 0, 200).perform();Turn the wheel with the pointer over the frame, so the frame scrolls. Lesson: Scroll wheel actions Tables Table: mouse action · java Mouse action Java Double click doubleClick(el) Right click contextClick(el) Hover moveToElement(el) Press and hold clickAndHold(el) Drag and drop dragAndDrop(a, b) Point in the viewport moveToLocation(x, y) From the current point moveByOffset(x, y) Release everything ((RemoteWebDriver) driver).resetInputState() Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Table: key method · what it does Key method What it does keyDown(key) Press a key and keep it down. Meant for modifier keys. keyUp(key) Release it. Forget this and the key stays down for the next chain. sendKeys(text) Type into whatever has focus, one key down and key up per character. sendKeys(element, text) Click the element first, then type. Java and Python only. Lesson: Keyboard actions Table: wheel action · java Wheel action Java Into view scrollToElement(el) By an amount scrollByAmount(0, 300) From an element scrollFromOrigin(ScrollOrigin.fromElement(el), 0, 200) Lesson: Scroll wheel actions Tips and traps Common mistake Actions type into the focused element."],[20,"actions","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","If nothing has focus, the keys go to the page body and the test seems to type into nothing. Element sendKeys focuses the element itself, which is why it is the better choice for plain typing.Lesson: Keyboard actions 4.4"],[20,"javascript-cookies","JavaScript execution and cookies","Open chapter Running JavaScript in the page, async scripts, and working with cookies. Key ideas executeScript The script runs as a function body. Use return to get a value and arguments[n] to read what you pass in. Elements go in and come back as WebElements. Return types Whole numbers are Long in Java and int in Python, objects are Map and dict, undefined is null or None. Async scripts executeAsyncScript adds a callback as the last argument. A returned promise is also awaited. The script timeout, 30 s by default, limits both. Cookies Visit the domain first, then add. httpOnly hides a cookie from page scripts. deleteAllCookies gives a clean start. Code Run JavaScript, pass an element in, and see what type comes back staticString type(Object o) { return o == null ? \"null\" : o.getClass().getSimpleName(); }The Java class of whatever came back. JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver;Every driver class implements JavascriptExecutor, so this cast is safe. System.out.println(script + \" -> \" + type(js.executeScript(script)));Run the script as the body of a function and convert the result. System.out.println(\"arguments: \" + js.executeScript(\"return arguments[0].innerText + ' / ' + arguments[1]\", dinner, 7));Extra arguments arrive in the script as arguments[0], arguments[1] and so on. js.executeScript(\"arguments[0].click();\", driver.findElement(By.id(\"updatediv\")));A click done by JavaScript. It skips the checks a real click makes. Lesson: Run JavaScript in the page Asynchronous scripts, promises, the script timeout and a JavaScript error Object waited = js.executeAsyncScript(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('waited'), 300);\");Selenium adds a callback as the last argument. The command returns when the script calls it. System.out.println(\"promise: \" + js.executeScript(\"return new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('from promise'), 200));\"));A returned promise is awaited by the browser before it answers. driver.manage().timeouts().scriptTimeout(Duration.ofMillis(200));How long a script may run. The default is 30 seconds. System.out.println(\"too slow: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]);The callback came after 500 ms, the limit was 200 ms. System.out.println(\"broken script: \" + e.getMessage().split(\"\\n\")[0]);A JavaScript error in the page becomes javascript error. Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout Add, read, protect and delete cookies driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie(\"key\", \"value\"));The browser is still on an empty start page with no domain. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\");Visit the domain first. A small page is enough. Cookie c = driver.manage().getCookieNamed(\"key\");Read one cookie by name. driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie.Builder(\"foo\", \"bar\").sameSite(\"Strict\").build());The builder sets the other fields. driver.manage().addCookie(newCookie.Builder(\"secret\", \"s\").isHttpOnly(true).build());httpOnly hides the cookie from page JavaScript. System.out.println(\"missing cookie: \" + driver.manage().getCookieNamed(\"foo\"));Asking for a cookie that is not there gives null, not an error. driver.manage().deleteAllCookies();A clean start, for example before logging in as another user. Lesson: Cookies Tables Table: error · java Error Java The script took longer than the script timeout ScriptTimeoutException The script threw an error JavascriptException Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout Table: field · meaning Field Meaning name, value Required. The data. domain, path Where the cookie is sent. They must match the current page. secure Only sent over https. httpOnly Hidden from page JavaScript. sameSite Strict, Lax or None. Whether the cookie goes with requests started by other sites. expiry Seconds since 1970. With no expiry the cookie lasts for the session. Lesson: Cookies Tips and traps Remember The script timeout is 30 seconds by default, as chapter 2.1 showed. It covers both sync and async scripts. A short timeout makes a broken"],[20,"javascript-cookies","JavaScript execution and cookies","async script fail fast.Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout"],[20,"javascript-cookies","Step 5Build a test suite","5.1"],[20,"test-frameworks","Test frameworks: JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha","Open chapter Running Selenium inside JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha, with data-driven tests and screenshots on failure. Key ideas Framework jobs Selenium drives the browser. JUnit 5, pytest or Mocha find the tests, run setup and teardown, assert and report. Fresh browser Start the driver in the before each step and quit it in the after each step, so tests stay independent and can run in any order. Teardown always runs @AfterEach, code after yield and afterEach run also when the test failed, so quit belongs there. Screenshot on failure Take it after the test failed and before quit: a JUnit AfterTestExecutionCallback. Code A first test class: a fresh browser for every test WebDriver driver;A field, so every method of this test can reach the driver. @BeforeEachRuns before every test method. @TestOne test: one behaviour, one reason to fail. assertEquals(\"Web form\", driver.getTitle());Expected value first in JUnit, then the actual one. @AfterEachRuns after every test, also when the test failed. Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework One test, three rows of data @ParameterizedTest(name = \"{0} / {1}\")The name shows the data of each run in the report. @CsvSource({Each line is one run of the test: username, password, expected message. void login(String user, String password, String expected) {JUnit passes the columns in as parameters. Alert alert = newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(2)).until(ExpectedConditions.alertIsPresent());The page answers with an alert. Lesson: Data-driven tests A failing test that saves a screenshot before the browser closes publicclassScreenshotOnFailureimplementsAfterTestExecutionCallback {Called right after the test method, before any AfterEach method. if (context.getExecutionException().isPresent()) {Present only when the test threw, so only when it failed. Path target = Path.of(\"failure-\" + context.getRequiredTestMethod().getName() + \".png\");Name the file after the test. @ExtendWith(ScreenshotOnFailure.class)Plug the extension into this test class. WebDriver driver;The extension reads this field. assertEquals(\"Web form!\", driver.getTitle());Wrong on purpose, so the test fails. @AfterEachRuns after the extension, so the browser is still open when the screenshot is taken. Lesson: A screenshot when a test fails Tables Table: job · junit 5 Job JUnit 5 Before every test @BeforeEach After every test @AfterEach Once for the class or file @BeforeAll, @AfterAll Check a value assertEquals(expected, actual) Run it build tool or console launcher Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework Table: framework · where the screenshot code goes · how it knows the test failed Framework Where the screenshot code goes How it knows the test failed JUnit 5 An extension with AfterTestExecutionCallback, which runs before @AfterEach context.getExecutionException() is present Lesson: A screenshot when a test fails Tips and traps Remember The Selenium documentation recommends a fresh browser for every test. Then no cookie, login or open dialog can leak from one test into the next, and the tests can run in any order or in parallel. It costs a browser start per test, a price that is almost always worth paying.Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework Remember Keep the data close to the test while it is small. When it grows, move it to a CSV or JSON file: JUnit has @CsvFileSource, and pytest and Mocha can read any file into the same list.Lesson: Data-driven tests 5.2"],[20,"page-objects","Page objects and page components","Open chapter Page objects, page component objects and the rules for writing them. Key ideas Page object A class that is the interface to one page. Tests call its methods, so a change to the page is fixed in one place. No assertions inside Assertions belong to the test. The only check in a page object is that the right page is loaded. Return the next page A method that moves to another page returns that page object. A method that stays may return itself. Components A page component object models a repeated part, such as a field or a product card, and searches only inside its root element. Waits inside Put waits in the page object, for example a constructor that waits for its title after a navigation. Code Two page objects, one component and three tests privatefinalBy fieldsBy = By.cssSelector(\"label.form-label\");Locators live here and nowhere else. if (!\"Web form\".equals(driver.getTitle())) {The one check a page object should make: am I on the right page? return driver.findElements(fieldsBy).stream().map(Field::new).toList();Wrap every label element in a Field component. publicclassField {A page component: one label and the control inside it. publicString name() { return root.getText().split(\"\\n\")[0].trim(); }The first line of the label is its name. privateWebElement control() { return root.findElement(By.cssSelector(\"input, textarea, select\")); }Search inside this component only. returnthis;Returning itself allows field(...).fill(...).fill(...). newWebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(3)).until(ExpectedConditions.titleIs(\"Web form - target page\"));The page arrives after a navigation, so wait for it instead of checking once. WebFormPage form = WebFormPage.open(driver);The test talks to pages, not to locators. ReceivedPage received = form.submit();submit() moves to another page, so it returns that page object. assertEquals(\"Received!\", received.message());The assertion stays in the test. assertEquals(11, fields.size());Eleven labelled controls on this page. assertThrows(IllegalStateException.class, () -> newWebFormPage(driver));The constructor checks the page and fails loudly. Lesson: Write page objects and components Tables Table: without page objects · with page objects Without page objects With page objects Locators are copied into every test that uses the page Each locator is written once, inside the page object A test reads as a list of clicks and CSS selectors A test reads as what the user does: open the form, fill a field, submit A UI change breaks many tests in many files A UI change is fixed in one class Waiting and page checks are repeated The page object waits for its page once, in its constructor Lesson: Why page objects Table: rule from the documentation · where it shows in the example Rule from the documentation Where it shows in the example The public methods represent the services the page offers field, fields, submit, message Try not to expose the internals of the page Locators are private. Field.control() is private in Java Generally do not make assertions Every assert is in the test file The one allowed check: the right page is loaded The constructors check the title, or wait for it after a navigation Methods return other page objects, components or themselves submit() returns ReceivedPage, fill() returns the Field A page object need not represent an entire page Field represents one labelled control Lesson: Write page objects and components Table: choice · what the documentation or practice suggests Choice What the documentation or practice suggests Same action, different result Write two methods, for example loginAsValidUser and loginExpectingError, each returning the page that really follows A part that appears on many pages A component object such as a header, a menu or a product card, used inside page objects Where waits go Inside the page object, so tests do not repeat them Base class Shared code such as the driver field and a wait helper. Keep it small PageFactory in Java An older helper that fills"],[20,"page-objects","Page objects and page components","fields annotated with @FindBy. Plain By fields, as in the example, work in every language Lesson: Design choices and the limits of page objects Tips and traps Remember ReceivedPage waits for its title, while WebFormPage checks the title once. The web form is opened with get(), which returns after the page loads. The received page appears after a click, and chapter 3.3 showed that the next command can run before the new page is there.Lesson: Write page objects and components 5.3"],[20,"practices","Good practices and things to avoid","Open chapter Test design, encouraged practices and the things the documentation says to avoid. Key ideas Need a browser? Ask first whether a lighter test can do the job. Browser tests are the most expensive kind. Short and focused Set up the data, perform a few actions, evaluate the result. Many small independent tests beat one grand journey. Clean start A new driver starts with a new profile. Use a fresh browser per test and never share test data. Prepare state outside the UI Log in and create data through APIs, then set cookies, instead of clicking through forms. Avoid CAPTCHAs, download progress, status codes, third party logins, test order, performance numbers, spidering and 2FA are jobs for other tools or test hooks. Code A new driver starts with a clean profile first.manage().addCookie(newCookie(\"cart\", \"3 items\"));State a first test leaves behind: a cookie ((JavascriptExecutor) first).executeScript(\"localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark')\");and a value in local storage. WebDriver second = newChromeDriver();A new driver gets a new, empty profile by default. Lesson: Encouraged practices Prepare the state without clicking through the user interface String session = \"abc123\";In a real suite this value comes from a login API call, not from the login form. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\");A small page on the domain, only to be allowed to set the cookie. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/cookies.html\");Now open the page under test. It reads its cookies on load. Lesson: Encouraged practices Tables Table: testing type · question it answers · selenium fits? Testing type Question it answers Selenium fits? Acceptance Are we building the right product? Yes, by simulating what the user expects Functional Are we building the product right? Yes Integration Do the modules work together, for example order plus payment? Yes, for flows through the UI System Does the whole product work end to end, in an environment like production? Yes, a few journeys Performance (load and stress) How fast is it, and does it survive a peak? Not as the measuring tool. The documentation points to tools such as JMeter Regression Did a change break what worked? Yes, by running the existing tests again Lesson: The shape of a good browser test Table: practice · what the documentation says Practice What the documentation says Avoid sharing state Do not share test data, clean up stale data, and create a new WebDriver per test Test independency Write each test as its own unit, not relying on another test to complete Mock external services Removing dependencies on external services improves speed and stability Improved reporting Selenium does not report. Use the reports of your test framework, such as xUnit XML or HTML Locators Unique ids first, then a well written CSS selector. XPath is flexible but harder to debug and often slower Page objects and a fluent API Chapter 5.2. Methods can return the page for chained calls Domain specific language Business level methods on top of page objects Lesson: Encouraged practices Table: avoid · why · do instead Avoid Why Do instead CAPTCHAs They are built to stop automation Disable them in the test environment, or add a hook that lets tests bypass them File downloads The API does not expose download progress Find the link with Selenium and fetch it with an HTTP library such as curl, using any cookies it needs HTTP status codes WebDriver acts like a user, and a status code is rarely the useful detail of a failure Check what the user sees, such as an error page. Chapter 6.2 shows BiDi network events if you really need codes Logging in to Gmail or Facebook Against their terms, slow and unreliable Use the APIs or test account tools they provide Test dependency Tests must run in any order Make each test set up its own state Performance testing Browser start, servers and third parties add noise to every number Use performance tools, and keep Selenium for function Link spidering A browser"],[20,"practices","Good practices and things to avoid","takes seconds per page Use curl or a library such as BeautifulSoup Two factor authentication Automating it is hard and less secure A test only token, or 2FA turned off for test users, test IPs or the test environment Lesson: Things to avoid Tips and traps Remember Every row has the same shape: Selenium is a tool for what a user does in a browser. When the job is not that, a lighter tool is faster and more reliable.Lesson: Things to avoid 5.4"],[20,"troubleshooting","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","Open chapter Common errors, stale elements and logging in Java, Python and JavaScript. Key ideas Error names Each W3C error has a class per language. Read the name, then the message, then your line in the trace. Stale element The node behind a reference was replaced, for example by a refresh. Find the element again. Context An element found in one frame or window is unknown in another. Switch back before using it. Client log Java: java.util.logging at FINE. Driver log ChromeDriver writes COMMAND and RESPONSE lines when its service is given a log file and a verbose level. Code Make an element reference fail three ways, and fix each one WebElement box = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\"));The driver gives back a reference to this exact node. driver.navigate().refresh();The page is rebuilt. The old node is gone, a new one with the same name takes its place. box = driver.findElement(By.name(\"my-text\"));The fix: find it again. WebElement email = driver.findElement(By.id(\"email\"));Found inside the frame. System.out.println(\"2. outside its frame: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName());The reference is fine, but this context does not know it. driver.switchTo().frame(\"iframe1\");The fix: switch back to the context where it was found. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\");Leave the page completely. System.out.println(\"3. after leaving the page: \" + e.getClass().getSimpleName());Go back to the page and find it again. Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Turn on the client log and see every command staticfinalLoggerHTTP = Logger.getLogger(\"org.openqa.selenium.remote.http.jdk.JdkHttpClient\");Keep a reference, or Java may forget the level you set. HTTP.setLevel(Level.FINE);Java logs at INFO by default. Request lines are written at FINE. HTTP.addHandler(newHandler() {A tiny handler that prints one line per request. String m = newSimpleFormatter().formatMessage(r);The record holds a pattern plus values, so format it first. String line = m.substring(19).replaceFirst(\"^\\\\((\\\\w+)\\\\)\", \"$1\").replaceAll(\"/session/[0-9a-f]{32}\", \"/session/{id}\");Hide the session id, which changes every run. System.out.println(line.split(\" \\\\{\")[0]);Keep the method and path, drop the JSON body. Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Ask ChromeDriver for its own log .withLogFile(log)Where ChromeDriver writes its log. .withLogLevel(ChromiumDriverLogLevel.DEBUG)More detail than the default. if (line.contains(\"] COMMAND \")) System.out.println(line.split(\"] COMMAND \")[1].split(\" \")[0]);Print the name of each command the driver received. Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Tables Table: w3c error · java · usual cause and fix W3C error Java Usual cause and fix no such element NoSuchElementException Wrong place, wrong time or a changed locator. Check the page, add an explicit wait, update the locator. stale element reference StaleElementReferenceException The node was replaced, for example by a refresh or a re render. Find it again. element click intercepted ElementClickInterceptedException Something covers the element: a sticky bar, a modal, an animation. Wait for it to go, or scroll. element not interactable ElementNotInteractableException Hidden, zero size or not ready. Wait until visible and enabled. invalid selector InvalidSelectorException The CSS or XPath is broken, or an XPath was passed to a CSS locator. timeout TimeoutException An explicit wait ran out. The message says which condition. script timeout ScriptTimeoutException A script took longer than the script timeout. unexpected alert open UnhandledAlertException An alert was left open. Handle it where it opens. no such window NoSuchWindowException The window was closed and the test did not switch back. invalid session id NoSuchSessionException The session was quit or crashed. Look for a quit earlier in the test. session not created SessionNotCreatedException Driver and browser versions do not match, or the browser could not start. Lesson:"],[20,"troubleshooting","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","Read an error the right way Table: what happened · error in this run · fix What happened Error in this run Fix Refresh, re render, list re sorted stale element reference Find the element again just before you use it Switched to another frame or window no such element Switch back to the context where it was found Navigated to another page no such element Go back to the page and find it again Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Table: language · logger · default level · level that shows requests Language Logger Default level Level that shows requests Java java.util.logging, for example org.openqa.selenium.remote.http.jdk.JdkHttpClient INFO FINE Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Tips and traps Remember Search the error name together with the first line of the message. Ignore the long list of numbers under Stacktrace in Chrome messages. Those are addresses inside ChromeDriver, not your code.Lesson: Read an error the right way Remember Page objects that find elements inside their methods, as in chapter 5.2, rarely meet stale elements, because they look the element up fresh each time.Lesson: Stale elements and the element store"],[20,"step-6","Step 6Scale out with Grid","6.1"],[20,"grid","Selenium Grid 4","Open chapter Selenium Grid 4 components, roles, remote sessions and parallel runs. Key ideas Why a Grid Parallel runs on many browsers, versions and systems, and shorter suites: tests × average time ÷ nodes. Components Router (4444), New Session Queue, Distributor, Session Map, Node (5555) and Event Bus (4442, 4443). Roles Standalone for one machine, Hub and Node for most Grids, Distributed for very large ones. Remote driver Only the driver creation changes. se:name adds metadata, se:cdp gives DevTools through the Grid, and a local file detector sends upload files to the node. Queue and quit New sessions wait in the queue for a free slot. Always quit, or the slot stays busy until the session timeout. Code Ask the Grid if it is ready and what it offers @SuppressWarnings(\"unchecked\")JSON gives back plain objects, so the casts are unchecked. HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(\"http://localhost:4444/status\")).build(),The same endpoint a driver has, now answered by the Grid. Map<String, Object> body = newJson().toType(res.body(), Json.MAP_TYPE);Selenium ships its own JSON reader. System.out.println(\"slot: \" + ((Map<String, Object>) slot.get(\"stereotype\")).get(\"browserName\"));Each slot can hold one session of this browser. Lesson: When and why to use a Grid Three tests, two slots: watch the queue ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);Three threads, so three tests start at the same moment. RemoteWebDriver driver = newRemoteWebDriver(newURL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), newChromeOptions());Returns when the Grid has given this test a slot. Thread.sleep(3000);Pretend the test takes 3 seconds. driver.quit();Frees the slot for the next request in the queue. System.out.println(\"got a browser after: \" + times);Two at once, the third only after a slot was free. Lesson: Components, roles and ports Run a test on the Grid with a name and a file upload ChromeOptions options = newChromeOptions();Options say which browser you want. The Grid picks a node that has it. options.setCapability(\"se:name\", \"upload on the grid\");se: capabilities are Grid metadata. se:name shows in the Grid UI. RemoteWebDriver driver = newRemoteWebDriver(newURL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), options);The only change from a local test: a URL instead of a local driver. System.out.println(k + \": \" + (k.equals(\"se:cdp\") ? String.valueOf(v).replaceAll(\"[0-9a-f]{32}\", \"{id}\") : v));se:cdp is a DevTools address that goes through the Grid. driver.setFileDetector(newLocalFileDetector());Sends the local file to the node, which may be another machine. System.out.println(\"attached: \" + driver.executeScript(\"return arguments[0].files[0].name\", input));The browser on the node has the file. Lesson: Remote sessions, metadata and files Numbers to remember Suite time on a GridExam favourite total time = number of tests × average test timenumber of nodes From the documentation: 15 tests of 45 s take 11 min 15 s on one node, 2 min 15 s on 5 nodes and 45 s on 15 nodes. Lesson: When and why to use a Grid Tables Table: component · job · default port Component Job Default port Router Entry point for every request. New sessions go to the queue. Commands for existing sessions go straight to the right node, found through the Session Map 4444 New Session Queue Holds new session requests in first in, first out order, with a timeout 5559 Distributor Knows every node and its slots. Takes requests from the queue, finds a matching free slot and creates the session 5553 Session Map Remembers which node runs which session id 5556 Node Runs the browsers. By default one slot per CPU for Chromium browsers and Firefox, one for Safari 5555 Event Bus Messages between the other components, cheaper than HTTP. Started first in a distributed Grid 4442, 4443 and 5557 Lesson: Components, roles and ports Table: role · command · use it for Role Command Use it for Standalone java -jar selenium-server-<version>.jar standalone All components in one process on one machine. Local debugging, quick suites, CI Hub and Node"],[20,"grid","Selenium Grid 4","... hub and ... node --hub http://<hub-ip>:4444 The most used role. Combine machines with different systems and browsers behind one address Distributed ... event-bus, sessionqueue, sessions, distributor, router, node Large Grids with more than 100 nodes, each component scaled on its own Lesson: Components, roles and ports Table: language · remote driver · file detector Language Remote driver File detector Java new RemoteWebDriver(new URL(gridUrl), options) setFileDetector(new LocalFileDetector()) Lesson: Remote sessions, metadata and files Tips and traps Remember A node registers by sending a message on the Event Bus, and the Distributor then calls it over HTTP to confirm. When hub and node are on different machines, the Event Bus ports 4442 and 4443 and the node port must be reachable.Lesson: Components, roles and ports 6.2"],[20,"bidi-cdp","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","Open chapter WebDriver BiDi events, Chrome DevTools Protocol commands and Grid GraphQL. Key ideas BiDi A W3C protocol with a WebSocket, so the browser can push events. Turn it on with webSocketUrl, then subscribe to console and page errors. Events, not polling Handlers are called when events arrive. Wait for them, because they come after the action that caused them. CDP Chrome and Edge only, no stability promise, the three newest Chrome versions. Use it for Chromium features with no other API. GraphQL POST a query to /graphql on the Grid for nodes, slots, sessions and the queue size. Grid endpoints /status, DELETE /session, and drain or remove a node through the Distributor endpoints. Code Hear console messages and page errors as they happen options.setCapability(\"webSocketUrl\", true);Ask for a BiDi WebSocket next to the normal HTTP connection. List<String> heard = newCopyOnWriteArrayList<>();Events arrive on another thread, so use a thread safe list. try (LogInspector inspector = newLogInspector(driver)) {Subscribes to log events. Closing it unsubscribes. inspector.onConsoleEntry(e -> heard.add(\"console \" + e.getLevel() + \": \" + e.getText()));Called for every console.log, console.error and so on. inspector.onJavaScriptException(e -> heard.add(\"page error: \" + e.getText()));Called for every uncaught error in the page. while (heard.size() < 3 && System.currentTimeMillis() < end) Thread.sleep(50);Events are pushed, not returned, so wait until all three arrived. Lesson: WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back Send Chrome DevTools commands: read the version and change the time zone ChromeDriver driver = newChromeDriver();executeCdpCommand exists on Chrome and Edge drivers only. Map<String, Object> version = driver.executeCdpCommand(\"Browser.getVersion\", Map.of());Any CDP method by name, with a map of parameters. driver.executeCdpCommand(\"Emulation.setTimezoneOverride\", Map.of(\"timezoneId\", \"Asia/Kolkata\"));Something WebDriver Classic has no command for. Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol Ask the Grid exactly what you need with GraphQL staticfinalJsonJSON = newJson();Selenium ships its own JSON reader. String body = \"{\\\"query\\\": \\\"\" + query + \"\\\"}\";GraphQL over HTTP: one POST with the query as a string. return (Map<String, Object>) reply.get(\"data\");The answer sits under data. String q = \"{ grid { nodeCount, totalSlots, maxSession, sessionCount, sessionQueueSize } }\";Ask only for these fields. System.out.println(\"busy: \" + ask(q).get(\"grid\"));One session is running now. .map(x -> JSON.<Map<String, Object>>toType((String) x.get(\"capabilities\"), Json.MAP_TYPE).get(\"se:name\"))The se:name metadata is in the session capabilities. Lesson: Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints Tables Table: how to switch it on · java How to switch it on Java Capability options.setCapability(\"webSocketUrl\", true) Console and page errors new LogInspector(driver) Lesson: WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back Table: webdriver classic · cdp · webdriver bidi WebDriver Classic CDP WebDriver BiDi Standard W3C Recommendation Chrome only, no stability promise W3C, in progress Browsers All Chrome and Edge Chrome, Edge and Firefox, growing Style Request and response over HTTP Commands and events over WebSocket Commands and events over WebSocket Use it for Every normal test step Chromium features with no other API Events, logs, network, and the future of Selenium Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol Table: endpoint · what it does Endpoint What it does GET /status Current state of the Grid and every node, with slots and sessions POST /graphql Ask for exactly the fields you need: grid, nodesInfo, sessionsInfo, session(id) DELETE /session/<id> End a session and free its slot POST /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id>/drain Stop a node after its running sessions end, with no new sessions. Needs the registration secret header DELETE /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id> Remove a node from the Distributor. Its running sessions continue Lesson: Grid features: GraphQL and"],[20,"bidi-cdp","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","endpoints Tips and traps Remember The documentation calls out two popular CDP examples with limited value: emulated geolocation, because most sites use the IP address, and overriding device metrics, because Chrome mobile emulation in the options is the better tool.Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol"],[20,"step-7","Step 7Revise and practise","7.1"],[20,"revision","Revision: predict, debug and design","Open chapter Predict the output, find the bug, design and sizing questions, a cheat sheet in three languages and a mock quiz. Key ideas Predict, then run The driver does what the protocol says, not what the code seems to say. Offsets add, Shift changes case, a refresh makes references stale. Cause before fix Name why a test is slow or flaky before changing it. Sleeps, mixed waits and missing quit calls are the usual causes. Layers Tests, page objects and components, a driver factory and support code. Each change should touch one layer. Size with the formula Suite time = tests × average time ÷ nodes, with about one CPU and 1 GB of RAM per browser. Know your binding Time is a Duration in Java, seconds in Python and milliseconds in JavaScript, and a missing cookie gives null, None or an error. Numbers to remember Explicit wait pollExam favourite 500 ms The default check interval in Java and Python. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Page load timeout 300,000 ms Five minutes by default. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Script timeout 30,000 ms Thirty seconds by default. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Tables Table: question shape · what it looks like · what earns the marks · lesson Question shape What it looks like What earns the marks Lesson Predict the output A short program and the question: what does it print, or which error comes back Knowing what the driver really does, not what the code seems to say 2 Find the bug A test that is slow, flaky or fails, and the question: why, and how do you fix it Naming the cause first, then a fix that removes it 3 Design and sizing Plan a framework, size a Grid, triage a flaky suite Clear layers, numbers from a formula, a reason for each choice 4 Write it in another language The same step in Java, Python or JavaScript The right method name and the right unit for time 5 Lesson: How Selenium skills are tested Table: step · java Step Java Start Chrome new ChromeDriver() Open a page driver.get(url) Find one element driver.findElement(By.id(\"q\")) Type el.sendKeys(\"text\") Wait for an element new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(5)).until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id(\"q\"))) Into a frame driver.switchTo().frame(el) Back to the page driver.switchTo().defaultContent() Run a script ((JavascriptExecutor) driver).executeScript(\"return 1\") Add a cookie driver.manage().addCookie(new Cookie(\"k\", \"v\")) Use a Grid new RemoteWebDriver(new URL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), new ChromeOptions()) End the session driver.quit() Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Tips and traps Remember Use the Revise lessons in order the first time, then use the Quiz and Worked problems tabs as a timed mock test. Each answer says which chapter to reread if you got it wrong.Lesson: How Selenium skills are tested Common mistake A good bug answer has three parts: what the code really does, why that breaks, and a fix that removes the cause. A retry that hides the failure is not a fix.Lesson: Find the bug"],[21,"","Python cheatsheet","Every key idea, Python example, table and common mistake from the Selenium chapters, with only Python code. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · Python cheatsheet · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · revision · PDF · download · Python · WebDriver · locators · waits · Grid"],[21,"step-1","Step 1Meet Selenium","1.1"],[21,"what-is-selenium","What Selenium is and where it came from","Open chapter What Selenium does, its three parts, the key words, its history to the W3C standard and a first real program. Key ideas What Selenium is An umbrella project of tools and libraries that automate real browsers the way a user would. At its core, a browser user agent library. Three parts WebDriver (library plus browser driver, for code), IDE (record and play back extension), Grid (many sessions on many machines). Not a test framework Selenium drives the browser. Assertions, test structure and reports come from JUnit, pytest, Mocha and similar tools. Key dates Selenium 2.0 on 8 July 2011, 3.0 on 13 October 2016, W3C Recommendation on 5 June 2018, Selenium 4.0 on 13 October 2021. Program shape Start a session, send the browser somewhere, ask or act, and always quit at the end. Code A first look: open a page, read its title, close the browser from selenium import webdriverImport the webdriver package, which holds one class per browser. driver = webdriver.Chrome()Start Chrome. Selenium Manager finds a matching chromedriver, then a new session begins. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")Open the page and wait until it has loaded. print(\"Title:\", driver.title)driver.title asks the browser for the page title. print(\"URL:\", driver.current_url)current_url asks which address the browser is showing. driver.quit()End the session, close every window and stop chromedriver. Lesson: A first look at real code Tables Table: what people use selenium for · an everyday example What people use Selenium for An everyday example Testing web applications Before a bank releases a new version of its login page, a test opens the page in Chrome, Firefox and Edge, logs in with a test account and checks the balance appears. Repetitive tasks A script logs into a supplier portal every morning at 6 and downloads the order report for the day. Collecting data from pages A researcher reads prices from a site that has no API. The docs ask you to check the terms of service of the site first, because some sites forbid it and some block Selenium. Lesson: What Selenium is Table: part · what it is · use it when Part What it is Use it when WebDriver A library for your programming language plus a driver for each browser. Your code sends commands, the driver makes the browser carry them out. You write automated tests or scripts in Java, Python, JavaScript, C# or Ruby. This is what most of this subject teaches. IDE A Chrome and Firefox extension that records what you do in the browser and plays it back as Selenium commands. You want a quick recording, or you are learning which command matches which action. Grid A server that spreads WebDriver sessions over many machines, browsers and operating systems. One machine is too slow, or you must test on browser and operating system pairs you do not have. Lesson: WebDriver, IDE and Grid Table: word · meaning in the selenium documentation Word Meaning in the Selenium documentation API The set of commands you use to control WebDriver, such as get, findElement and click. Library The code module that holds those commands for one language: a .jar file for Java, a pip package for Python, an npm package for JavaScript. Driver The program that controls one browser, such as chromedriver for Chrome and geckodriver for Firefox. Browser vendors write most of them. It usually runs on the same machine as the browser. Framework An extra library that supports a test suite: test runners such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha, or tools such as Cucumber. Lesson: WebDriver, IDE and Grid Table: version · released · what changed Version Released What changed Selenium 2.0 8 July 2011 The WebDriver APIs became the heart of Selenium. Selenium 1 code still ran. Selenium 3.0 13 October 2016 The original Selenium Core implementation was removed. Every major browser vendor now ships its own driver. W3C WebDriver 5 June 2018 WebDriver became a W3C Recommendation, a standard every browser can implement the same way. Selenium 4.0 13"],[21,"what-is-selenium","What Selenium is and where it came from","October 2021 Support for the old JSON Wire Protocol was removed. Only the W3C protocol is used. Relative locators and a rebuilt Grid arrived. Lesson: How Selenium grew Tips and traps Common mistake Selenium only controls the browser. It does not decide whether a test passed, it does not write reports and it does not give you Given, When, Then syntax. Those come from a test framework such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha. Chapter 5.1 joins the two.Lesson: What Selenium is Remember Always end with quit. If a program stops without it, the driver process and the browser keep running in the background and hold memory and ports. On a busy test machine those leftovers pile up quickly.Lesson: A first look at real code 1.2"],[21,"architecture","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","Open chapter How a command travels from your code to the browser over the W3C WebDriver protocol, shown with real HTTP traffic. Key ideas Three programs Your code and the binding (local end), the driver (remote end), the browser. The driver is written by the browser vendor. HTTP and JSON Every command is one HTTP request with a fixed method and path. Answers are {\"value\": ...} on success or {\"value\": {\"error\", \"message\", \"stacktrace\"}} on failure. Session id POST /session returns a session id. Every later path contains it. DELETE /session/{id} ends it. Element reference Elements travel as {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"id\"}. Bindings turn id, name and class name into CSS selectors. BiDi Classic WebDriver is request and response. WebDriver BiDi adds a WebSocket so the browser can send events. CDP access is temporary until BiDi covers it. Code Drive Chrome with plain HTTP and no Selenium library at all import jsonFor turning Python dictionaries into JSON text and back. import subprocessFor starting the chromedriver program. import urllib.requestPython's built in HTTP client. No Selenium library is imported anywhere. driver = subprocess.Popen([\"chromedriver\", \"--port=9515\"], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)Start the driver yourself, the job Selenium usually does for you. Its own messages are hidden. time.sleep(1)Give it a moment to open port 9515. def call(method, path, body=None):Send one W3C command and return the part of the answer under \"value\". data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body isnotNoneelseNonePOST bodies are JSON objects. GET and DELETE have none. reply = json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(req))Send it and read the JSON answer. print(method, path, \" -> \", text[:70] + (\"...\"if len(text) > 70else\"\"))Print the request and the start of the answer. return reply[\"value\"]Every success answer keeps its result under \"value\". KEY = \"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\"The fixed key the W3C spec uses for element references. s = \"/session/\" + call(\"POST\", \"/session\", {\"capabilities\": {\"alwaysMatch\": {\"browserName\": \"chrome\"}}})[\"sessionId\"]New Session. Chrome opens and we keep the session path. call(\"POST\", s + \"/timeouts\", {\"implicit\": 2000})Set Timeouts: Find Element may keep looking for 2 seconds, so the next page has time to load. call(\"POST\", s + \"/url\", {\"url\": \"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\"})Navigate To. call(\"GET\", s + \"/title\")Get Title. box = call(\"POST\", s + \"/element\", {\"using\": \"css selector\", \"value\": \"[name='my-text']\"})[KEY]Find Element. call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + box + \"/value\", {\"text\": \"Selenium\"})Element Send Keys. call(\"POST\", s + \"/element/\" + button + \"/click\", {})Element Click submits the form. call(\"GET\", s + \"/element/\" + message + \"/text\")Get Element Text on the next page. call(\"DELETE\", s)Delete Session closes Chrome. driver.terminate()Stop chromedriver. Lesson: See the wire for yourself Watch the real requests a normal Selenium script sends import loggingPython's standard logging module. logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG, format=\"%(message)s\", stream=sys.stdout)Print DEBUG messages, which include every HTTP request the binding sends. for name in (\"urllib3\", \"selenium.webdriver.common\"):Hide two noisy loggers so only the protocol messages remain. driver = webdriver.Chrome()POST /session driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")POST /session/{id}/url box = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")POST /session/{id}/element. Watch how By.NAME becomes a CSS selector. box.send_keys(\"Hi\")POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/value print(\"title is\", driver.title)GET /session/{id}/title driver.quit()DELETE /session/{id} Lesson: See the wire for yourself Numbers to remember SuccessExam favourite HTTP 200 and {\"value\": result} The result is null for commands that only act, a string for text and titles, a list for Find Elements. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol ErrorExam favourite HTTP 4xx or 5xx and {\"value\": {\"error\","],[21,"architecture","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","\"message\", \"stacktrace\"}} error is a fixed code such as no such element. The binding turns it into an exception. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Element reference {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.C6…e.6\"} A web element travels as an object with this one fixed key. The value is an opaque id made by the driver. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Tables Table: spec word · in practice · example Spec word In practice Example Local end The client side, usually a language library on top of the protocol selenium for Python, selenium-java, selenium-webdriver for npm Remote end The server side that carries out the commands chromedriver, or Selenium Grid Intermediary node A remote end that passes commands on to another remote end Selenium Grid, which forwards to a node Endpoint node The last remote end in the chain, built into or next to the browser chromedriver driving Chrome Lesson: The pieces between your code and the browser Table: command · method · path · what you write in python Command Method Path What you write in Python New Session POST /session webdriver.Chrome() Delete Session DELETE /session/{session id} driver.quit() Navigate To POST /session/{id}/url driver.get(url) Get Title GET /session/{id}/title driver.title Find Element POST /session/{id}/element driver.find_element(...) Find Elements POST /session/{id}/elements driver.find_elements(...) Element Click POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/click element.click() Element Send Keys POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/value element.send_keys(...) Get Element Text GET /session/{id}/element/{element id}/text element.text Set Timeouts POST /session/{id}/timeouts driver.implicitly_wait(2) Execute Script POST /session/{id}/execute/sync driver.execute_script(...) Take Screenshot GET /session/{id}/screenshot driver.get_screenshot_as_png() Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Table: error code · http status · python exception you see Error code HTTP status Python exception you see no such element 404 NoSuchElementException stale element reference 404 StaleElementReferenceException invalid session id 404 InvalidSessionIdException element not interactable 400 ElementNotInteractableException element click intercepted 400 ElementClickInterceptedException invalid selector 400 InvalidSelectorException timeout 500 TimeoutException unexpected alert open 500 UnexpectedAlertPresentException session not created 500 SessionNotCreatedException Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Table: layout · where things run · code difference Layout Where things run Code difference Direct Your code, the driver and the browser on one machine. new ChromeDriver(), webdriver.Chrome() Remote Your code on one machine, driver and browser on another, reached through Selenium Server or RemoteWebDriver. new RemoteWebDriver(url, options), webdriver.Remote(url, options=...) Grid Selenium Grid receives the commands and relays them to the driver on a node machine. Same as remote, the URL points at the Grid Lesson: Local, remote and Grid, plus BiDi Tips and traps Remember The binding changes some locators before they leave your machine. The Python binding sends By.ID \"x\" as the CSS selector [id=\"x\"], By.NAME as [name=\"x\"] and By.CLASS_NAME as .x. Look for it in the log in the next lesson. That is why the W3C spec only needs five strategies: css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath.Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Remember You will never write raw HTTP in real tests. The point is to see that nothing magic happens: Selenium is a polite HTTP client with good manners about waiting, errors and types. When a test fails with a strange message, picture the request that was sent and the error code that came back.Lesson: See the wire for yourself 1.3"],[21,"setup-first-script","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","Open chapter Install Selenium, let Selenium Manager match the driver, and write and tidy up your first real script. Key ideas Install Python: pip install selenium (3.10+). Selenium Manager Ships with Selenium since 4.6. A fallback that discovers the browser version, resolves, downloads and caches the driver in ~/.cache/selenium, and since 4.11 can download browsers. Driver mismatch An old driver on PATH or given by hand gives session not created. Delete it and let Selenium Manager match the driver. Eight components Start session, act on browser, ask the browser, wait strategy, find element, act on element, ask the element, end session. Always quit Put quit in finally, or use a Python with block. quit closes every window and stops the driver, close only closes one window. Code What an old driver on a new browser looks like from selenium.webdriver.chrome.service importServiceService describes how to start the driver program. service = Service(executable_path=\"/opt/drivers/chromedriver-147\")Point Selenium at an old chromedriver 147 on purpose. Selenium Manager is now skipped. driver = webdriver.Chrome(service=service)Chrome on this machine is version 153, so New Session fails. exceptSessionNotCreatedExceptionas e:The driver answered with the W3C error code session not created. print(e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])Print the first line of the driver's message. Lesson: Selenium Manager finds the driver The eight basic components of a Selenium script from selenium.webdriver.common.by importByBy holds the locator strategies: By.NAME, By.CSS_SELECTOR and more. driver = webdriver.Chrome()1. Start the session. Chrome opens with a fresh profile. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")2. Take action on the browser: go to the practice form. title = driver.title3. Request browser information: the page title. driver.implicitly_wait(0.5)4. Establish a waiting strategy. Python takes seconds, so 0.5 means half a second. text_box = driver.find_element(by=By.NAME, value=\"my-text\")5. Find an element by its name attribute. submit_button = driver.find_element(by=By.CSS_SELECTOR, value=\"button\")5. Find the first button with a CSS selector. text_box.send_keys(\"Selenium\")6. Take action on an element: type into the box. submit_button.click()6. Click Submit. The browser loads the next page. message = driver.find_element(by=By.ID, value=\"message\")Find the message on the new page. value = message.text7. Request element information: its visible text. driver.quit()8. End the session. The browser and the driver both close. Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Quit even when something fails with webdriver.Chrome() as driver:A with block calls driver.quit() for you when the block ends, even after an error. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"no-such-id\")This element does not exist, so this line raises. exceptNoSuchElementExceptionas e:Catch the error so we can report it. print(\"Failed:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])e.msg is the message from the driver. Keep its first line. print(\"Browser closed\")We are outside the with block, so quit has already run. Lesson: Always end the session Tables Table: language · minimum version for selenium 4.51 · how you add selenium Language Minimum version for Selenium 4.51 How you add Selenium Python Python 3.10 pip install selenium, or a line selenium==4.51.0 in requirements.txt Lesson: Install Selenium for your language Table: setting · default · what it does Setting Default What it does cache-path ~/.cache/selenium Where drivers, browsers and metadata are stored ttl 3600 seconds How long resolved versions are trusted before asking the vendor again offline false No network requests or downloads at all avoid-stats false Selenium Manager sends anonymous usage numbers to Plausible once a day. Set SE_AVOID_STATS=true to stop it. proxy none An HTTP proxy for the downloads Lesson: Selenium Manager finds the driver Table: step · what it means · in this example Step What it means In this example 1. Start the session Create a"],[21,"setup-first-script","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","driver. A browser opens. Chrome 2. Take action on the browser Navigate, go back, resize and so on. Open the web form 3. Request browser information Title, address, window handles, cookies. Read the title 4. Establish a waiting strategy Make sure an element is present before you look for it. Implicit wait of 500 ms 5. Find an element Most commands are about elements. The text box and the button 6. Take action on an element Type, click, clear. Type Selenium, click Submit 7. Request element information Text, attributes, size, state. Read the message 8. End the session Quit the driver and close the browser. quit Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Table: quit · close quit close Closes Every window and tab of the session Only the current window or tab Driver process Stopped Keeps running while other windows are open After it The driver object cannot be used again Switch to another window to carry on Lesson: Always end the session Tips and traps Remember Use a virtual environment in Python and a package.json in JavaScript. Each project then pins its own Selenium version, so upgrading one project never breaks another.Lesson: Install Selenium for your language Common mistake If you pass a driver path yourself, Selenium trusts you and never calls Selenium Manager. The documentation says that with Selenium 4.6 or newer you should not need to set a driver location at all. Delete old drivers from PATH and let Selenium Manager do the job.Lesson: Selenium Manager finds the driver Common mistake The documentation itself warns that an implicit wait is rarely the best solution. It is used here because it is the easiest to show. Chapter 4.1 replaces it with explicit waits.Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Remember In a test framework the setup and teardown hooks do this job: start the driver before each test and quit it after each test, pass or fail. Chapter 5.1 shows the hooks in JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha.Lesson: Always end the session"],[21,"step-2","Step 2Control the browser","2.1"],[21,"sessions-options","Sessions, options and timeouts","Open chapter Options and capabilities, page load strategy, the three session timeouts and the driver service, shown with real runs. Key ideas Options Since Selenium 4, describe sessions with Options classes. They become W3C capabilities, and browser specific ones carry a prefix such as goog:chromeOptions. Page load strategy normal waits for complete (load), eager for interactive (DOMContentLoaded), none for the first download. It covers navigation by address, not clicks. Default timeouts Implicit 0, page load 300,000 ms, script 30,000 ms. Service Starts and stops a local driver: file, port, arguments and logs. Not used for remote sessions. Granted capabilities Read them after New Session to learn the real browser version, platform and defaults such as dismiss and notify. Code Ask for a session with options, then read what the browser agreed to options = webdriver.ChromeOptions()An Options object describes the session you want. browserName is already chrome. options.add_argument(\"--headless=new\")A Chrome command line switch: run without a visible window. options.add_argument(\"--window-size=1280,800\")Another switch: the size of the browser window. options.page_load_strategy = \"eager\"A W3C capability: get() returns at DOMContentLoaded. driver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options)New Session is sent with these capabilities. caps = driver.capabilitiesA plain dict of what the driver actually granted. for key in (\"browserName\", \"browserVersion\", \"platformName\", \"pageLoadStrategy\", \"unhandledPromptBehavior\"):Print a few of the standard keys. Lesson: Options and capabilities Read the three session timeouts, then trip one on purpose t = driver.timeoutsThe timeouts of this session. Python reports them in seconds. print(\"implicit:\", t.implicit_wait)0: find_element does not wait at all unless you ask. print(\"pageLoad:\", t.page_load)300 seconds, which is 300,000 ms. print(\"script:\", t.script)30 seconds. driver.set_script_timeout(1)Allow scripts only one second from now on. driver.execute_async_script(\"/* never calls its callback */\")An async script must call its callback. This one never does. exceptTimeoutExceptionas e:Python raises TimeoutException for the W3C error script timeout. Lesson: The three session timeouts Start the driver on a port you choose and ask if it is ready service = Service(port=9600)A Service describes how to start chromedriver: here, on port 9600. driver = webdriver.Chrome(service=service)Selenium starts chromedriver on that port, then opens a session. print(\"Driver URL:\", service.service_url)The address the binding sends its HTTP commands to. with urllib.request.urlopen(\"http://localhost:9600/status\") as reply:GET /status is a W3C command that needs no session. print(\"ready:\", status[\"ready\"])The driver says whether it can start new sessions. driver.quit()Ends the session and stops the service. Lesson: The driver service Numbers to remember Implicit wait 0 How long Find Element keeps looking before it gives up with no such element. Zero means it checks once. Lesson: The three session timeouts Page loadExam favourite 300,000 ms Five minutes. How long a navigation may take before a timeout error. Lesson: The three session timeouts Script 30,000 ms Thirty seconds. How long an injected script may run before the script timeout error. Lesson: The three session timeouts Tables Table: capability · what it controls · default Capability What it controls Default browserName Which browser. Set for you by each Options class. set by the class browserVersion Which version. Selenium Manager can download it if missing. whatever is installed platformName The operating system at the remote end. Matters most on Grid and in the cloud. the local OS pageLoadStrategy When navigation commands return: normal, eager or none. normal acceptInsecureCerts Trust expired or self signed TLS certificates. false timeouts Script, page load and implicit wait. 30 s, 300 s, 0 unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to an alert your code did not handle. dismiss"],[21,"sessions-options","Sessions, options and timeouts","and notify proxy Send browser traffic through a proxy server. none setWindowRect Whether the browser supports resizing and moving windows. reported by the driver strictFileInteractability Strict checks for hidden file inputs. off Lesson: Options and capabilities Table: chrome switch · what it does Chrome switch What it does --headless=new No visible window. Common on build servers. --start-maximized Open the window maximised. --window-size=1280,800 A fixed window size, so pages lay out the same on every machine. --user-data-dir=... Use a chosen profile folder instead of a fresh temporary one. Lesson: Options and capabilities Table: strategy · waits until readystate is · browser event · use it when Strategy Waits until readyState is Browser event Use it when normal (default) complete load: every image, font and style sheet is in You want the safest behaviour eager interactive DOMContentLoaded: the HTML is parsed, images may still load Large images or ads slow the page and the test does not need them none any only the first download of the page You control every wait yourself Lesson: Page load strategy Table: language · set the page load timeout to 30 s Language Set the page load timeout to 30 s Python driver.set_page_load_timeout(30) Lesson: The three session timeouts Table: service setting · why you would change it Service setting Why you would change it Driver location Only with Selenium older than 4.6 or a special setup. Otherwise let Selenium Manager find it. Port A firewall allows only certain ports, or you want to talk to the driver yourself. Log output and level Driver logs are thrown away unless you send them somewhere. Levels are ALL, DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, SEVERE and OFF. Arguments Pass chromedriver switches, such as appending to a log file. Lesson: The driver service Tips and traps Remember Each Options class can also take the binary path of a Chromium based browser, extensions, and excludeSwitches to turn off a default switch of chromedriver. The documentation lists them on the Chrome page. Use them only when you need them, because every extra setting is one more difference from what real users run.Lesson: Options and capabilities Common mistake Switching to eager or none to make tests faster is a trade. Every element that arrives later now needs an explicit wait, or the test becomes flaky. Chapter 4.1 shows how to write those waits.Lesson: Page load strategy In the exam These timeouts belong to the browser session. Explicit waits such as WebDriverWait are different: they run in your code and poll the browser. Mixing an implicit wait with explicit waits can give surprising wait times, which chapter 4.1 explains.Lesson: The three session timeouts 2.2"],[21,"navigate-screenshots","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","Open chapter Navigation and history, title and URL, window size and position, screenshots and printing pages to PDF. Key ideas Navigation get or navigate().to, back, forward, refresh. Python spells them driver.back(), driver.forward(), driver.refresh(). Where am I Title and current URL. Check them when a test depends on a redirect or on history, because back on the first page raises no error. Window rect Set and get size and position. An exact size is more portable than maximize, which needs a window manager. Screenshots Take Screenshot (viewport) and Take Element Screenshot return base64 PNG. Save one when a test fails, before quit. Print Page Returns a base64 PDF. Use PrintOptions for orientation, ranges, size, margins and scale. Chromium needs headless. Code Go somewhere, go back, go forward, reload driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html\")Navigate To. Waits for the page load strategy. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html\")A second page. History now holds two entries. print(\"2\", driver.title, \"at\", driver.current_url)Title and address of the page now showing. driver.back()Press the Back button. driver.forward()Press the Forward button. driver.refresh()Reload the current page. Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Set the window size and position, then read them back driver.set_window_size(1024, 768)Restore the window and make it 1024 by 768 pixels. driver.set_window_position(40, 30)Move its top left corner to x 40, y 30 on the screen. size = driver.get_window_size()A dict with width and height. where = driver.get_window_position()A dict with x and y. print(\"rect\", driver.get_window_rect())One W3C command, Get Window Rect, returns all four numbers at once. Lesson: Window size and position Save a screenshot of the page and of one element def png_size(path):A PNG keeps its width and height at bytes 16 to 24 of the file. driver.save_screenshot(\"page.png\")Take Screenshot of the viewport and save it as a PNG. button.screenshot(\"button.png\")Take Element Screenshot: just the area of the button. Lesson: Screenshots Print the page to a PDF file options.add_argument(\"--headless=new\")Chromium prints to PDF only in headless mode. print_options = PrintOptions()Paper settings: orientation, page ranges, size, margins, scale, background. pdf = base64.b64decode(driver.print_page(print_options))The W3C Print Page command answers with base64, so decode it. print(\"page.pdf starts with\", pdf[:5].decode())Every PDF file starts with %PDF-. Lesson: Print a page to PDF Tables Table: what you want · python What you want Python Open an address driver.get(url) Back driver.back() Forward driver.forward() Reload driver.refresh() Page title driver.title Current address driver.current_url Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Table: command · what it does Command What it does Set window size Restores the window if it was maximised, then sets width and height in pixels Set window position Moves the top left corner to x and y on the screen Get window rect Width, height, x and y in one answer Maximize Makes the window fill the screen, without covering the system menus Minimize Hides the window, usually to the taskbar. The exact result depends on the window manager. Selenium 4 and later. Fullscreen Fills the whole screen, like pressing F11 Lesson: Window size and position Table: language · page screenshot to file · element screenshot to file Language Page screenshot to file Element screenshot to file Python driver.save_screenshot(\"page.png\") element.screenshot(\"el.png\") Lesson: Screenshots Table: printoptions setting · example PrintOptions setting Example Orientation portrait or landscape Page ranges \"2-4\" Page size A4, Legal and others Margins top, bottom, left, right Scale 1.0 is 100 %, 0.25 is 25 % Background whether background colours and images print Shrink to fit whether wide content is shrunk to the page Lesson: Print a page to PDF Tips and traps Common mistake After refresh or any navigation, the"],[21,"navigate-screenshots","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","elements you found before belong to the old page. Using them raises stale element reference. Find them again on the new page. Chapter 5.4 covers this error in depth.Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Common mistake Maximize and minimize need a desktop window manager. On a bare Linux build server they can fail with an unknown error, which is what happened when this page was built. Setting an exact size with Set Window Rect or the --window-size switch works everywhere and gives the same layout on every machine.Lesson: Window size and position Remember Take a screenshot in the teardown of a failing test, before quit. Name the file after the test, so the report can link to it. Chapter 5.1 wires this into JUnit, pytest and Mocha.Lesson: Screenshots"],[21,"step-3","Step 3Find and use elements","3.1"],[21,"locators","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","Open chapter All eight locator strategies, CSS and XPath, relative locators and how to choose a locator that lasts. Key ideas Eight strategies id, name, class name, css selector, xpath, tag name, link text, partial link text. find element returns the first match. Five on the wire W3C defines css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath. Bindings turn id, name and class name into CSS. CSS and XPath CSS: #id, .class, tag[attr=\"v\"], parent > child. XPath adds text matching and moving up to a parent. Prefer relative XPath (//). Relative locators above, below, toLeftOf, toRightOf and near (50 px), built on getBoundingClientRect. They depend on layout, so check what they return. Preference Unique stable id, then a short CSS selector, then XPath. Avoid absolute paths, styling classes and generated ids. Consider data-test attributes. Code All eight locator strategies on the documentation example page def show(how, e):A helper that prints which element a locator found. show(\"CLASS_NAME\", driver.find_element(By.CLASS_NAME, \"information\"))The first element whose class list contains information. show(\"CSS_SELECTOR\", driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"#fname\"))CSS: # means id, so the input with id fname. show(\"ID\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"lname\"))The element whose id attribute is lname. show(\"NAME\", driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"newsletter\"))The element whose name attribute is newsletter. show(\"LINK_TEXT\", driver.find_element(By.LINK_TEXT, \"Selenium Official Page\"))A link whose whole visible text matches. show(\"PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT\", driver.find_element(By.PARTIAL_LINK_TEXT, \"Official\"))A link whose visible text contains Official. show(\"TAG_NAME\", driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"a\"))The first a element on the page. show(\"XPATH\", driver.find_element(By.XPATH, \"//input[@value='f']\"))XPath: any input whose value attribute is f. Lesson: The eight locator strategies Relative locators: find an element by where it sits from selenium.webdriver.support.relative_locator import locate_withlocate_with() starts a relative locator. print(how, \"->\", e.get_attribute(\"outerHTML\"))outerHTML shows the whole tag that was found. show(\"below last name label\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").below({By.CSS_SELECTOR: \"label[for='lname']\"})))Python writes the anchor locator as a small dict. show(\"above lname\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").above({By.ID: \"lname\"})))An input above the lname box. show(\"right of Male\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").to_right_of({By.CSS_SELECTOR: \"input[value='m']\"})))An input to the right of the Male radio. show(\"below fname and above the checkbox\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").below({By.ID: \"fname\"}).above({By.NAME: \"newsletter\"})))Chained: both conditions must hold. show(\"near Newsletter label\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").near({By.CSS_SELECTOR: \"label[for='newsletter']\"})))near means at most 50 pixels away. Look at what it really returned. show(\"left of password\", driver.find_element(locate_with(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\").to_left_of({By.ID: \"password-field\"})))On the login page the three inputs sit in one row. Lesson: Relative locators Tables Table: strategy · finds · example Strategy Finds Example id The element whose id attribute matches lname name Elements whose name attribute matches newsletter class name Elements whose class list contains the value. Compound names such as \"a b\" are not allowed. information css selector Elements matching a CSS selector #fname xpath Elements matching an XPath expression //input[@value='f'] tag name Elements with that tag a link text Links whose visible text is exactly the value Selenium Official Page partial link text Links whose visible text contains the value Official Lesson: The eight locator strategies Table: to find · css selector · xpath To find CSS selector XPath By id #fname //*[@id=\"fname\"] By class .information //*[contains(@class,\"information\")] Tag"],[21,"locators","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","with attribute input[name=\"fname\"] //input[@name=\"fname\"] Attribute starts with a[href^=\"https\"] //a[starts-with(@href,\"https\")] Attribute contains a[href*=\"selenium\"] //a[contains(@href,\"selenium\")] Direct child form > input //form/input Any descendant form input //form//input Second matching input input:nth-of-type(2) (//input)[2] By visible text not possible //label[text()=\"Last name:\"] The parent not possible //input[@id=\"lname\"]/.. Next sibling label + input //label[@for=\"lname\"]/following-sibling::input[1] Lesson: CSS selectors and XPath Table: method · finds an element that is · note Method Finds an element that is Note above higher on the page than the anchor below lower on the page than the anchor toLeftOf / to_left_of left of the anchor toRightOf / to_right_of right of the anchor near at most 50 pixels from the anchor Useful for an input next to its label Lesson: Relative locators Table: fragile locator · why it breaks · better Fragile locator Why it breaks Better /html/body/div[2]/form/input[3] Any new div or input shifts the path #lname .btn.btn-primary.mt-3 Styling classes change when the design changes [data-test=\"submit\"] //button[text()=\"Submit\"] Breaks when the text is translated or reworded An id or a data attribute #ember1234 Generated ids change on every page load A stable attribute near it Lesson: Choosing a good locator Tips and traps Remember find element (singular) returns the first match or raises no such element. find elements (plural) returns every match in page order, or an empty list. Chapter 3.2 covers the difference in detail.Lesson: The eight locator strategies Common mistake Each relative locator sends the page a script to run, so it is slower than a plain CSS selector. Prefer an id or a short CSS selector, and keep relative locators for elements with no good attributes.Lesson: Relative locators In the exam Keep locators in one place, apart from the steps that use them. The documentation recommends declaring them separately, which is exactly what a page object does. Chapter 5.2 shows the pattern.Lesson: Choosing a good locator 3.2"],[21,"finding-elements","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","Open chapter Singular and plural finds, scoped searches and the XPath trap, shadow DOM and the active element. Key ideas One or many find element returns the first match or raises no such element. find elements returns all matches or an empty list. Search context You can search from the driver, from an element or from a shadow root. CSS from an element stays inside it. XPath trap An XPath starting with // always starts at the document, even when called on an element. Use .// to search below the element. Shadow DOM Find the host, get its shadow root, then search with a CSS selector. Chrome refuses XPath there. Active element switchTo().activeElement() returns the focused element. Use it to test keyboard flow such as Tab order. Code One element, all elements, and what happens when nothing matches inputs = driver.find_elements(By.TAG_NAME, \"input\")Plural: a list of every input, in page order. for e in inputs:Walk the list like any Python list. none = driver.find_elements(By.ID, \"email\")No match: an empty list, never an error. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"email\")Singular with no match: an exception. Lesson: Find one or find many Search inside one element, and the XPath trap form = driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"form\")First find the parent element. print(\"inputs in form:\", len(form.find_elements(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"input\")))Called on the form, CSS only looks inside it. print(\"links via //a:\", len(form.find_elements(By.XPATH, \"//a\")))The trap: // starts at the top of the document, so the link outside the form is found. print(\"links via .//a:\", len(form.find_elements(By.XPATH, \".//a\")))The dot means start here. Now nothing outside the form counts. last = form.find_element(By.ID, \"lname\")Two commands: find the form, then find inside it. same = driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"form #lname\")The optimised version: one CSS selector, one command. print(\"same element:\", last == same)Both references point at the same element. Lesson: Search inside an element Reach into a shadow root print(\"from the page:\", len(driver.find_elements(By.CSS_SELECTOR, box)))The checkbox lives in a shadow tree, so a normal search finds nothing. host = driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"custom-checkbox-element\")The shadow host: the element the hidden tree is attached to. shadow_root = host.shadow_rootGet Element Shadow Root. A ShadowRoot object with find_element and find_elements. checkbox = shadow_root.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, box)Search inside the shadow tree. shadow_root.find_element(By.XPATH, \".//input\")XPath inside a shadow root. print(\"xpath in shadow root:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])Chrome refuses it. Lesson: The shadow DOM Which element has the focus? print(\"at start:\", driver.switch_to.active_element.tag_name)Nothing is focused yet, so the active element is the body. driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\").click()Clicking a text box gives it the focus. driver.switch_to.active_element.send_keys(Keys.TAB)Press Tab on whatever has the focus, as a user would. print(\"after Tab:\", driver.switch_to.active_element.get_attribute(\"name\"))The focus moved to the next field. Lesson: The active element Tables Table: find element (singular) · find elements (plural) find element (singular) find elements (plural) Returns One element: the first match in page order A list of every match, in page order No match Raises no such element Returns an empty list W3C command Find Element Find Elements Use it for An element that must be there Counting, looping, or checking that something is absent Lesson: Find one or find many Tips and traps Common mistake find elements never waits for the first element to appear unless an implicit wait is set, and then it returns as soon as at least one matches. An empty list can mean the page is not ready yet. Chapter 4.1 shows how to wait properly.Lesson: Find one or find many Remember Searching inside a container is still very useful when the page repeats a pattern: the Delete button inside the third row of a table, or the price inside"],[21,"finding-elements","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","one product card. Find the row or card first, then the button inside it.Lesson: Search inside an element Remember A shadow root can contain another shadow host. Then you repeat the two steps: get that host, get its shadow root, search again. Each level is one more pair of commands.Lesson: The shadow DOM 3.3"],[21,"interacting","Interacting with elements and reading them","Open chapter Click, type, clear, the checks before every action and their errors, element information, select lists and uploads. Key ideas Five commands click, send keys, clear, submit and select. Prefer clicking the submit button over submit(). send keys appends, so clear first. Built in checks Before acting the driver scrolls the element into view and checks it is interactable. Errors: element not interactable, invalid element state, element click intercepted. Attribute vs property DOM attribute is the HTML, DOM property is the live value. The classic getAttribute mixes both. Select and upload Select works only on select elements: by visible text, value or index. Upload by sending the full path to input type=\"file\". Code Fill in a form: clear, type, tick and click driver.implicitly_wait(2)As in chapter 1.3: let finds retry for up to 2 seconds, so the page after Submit has time to load. text.send_keys(\"first try\")Element Send Keys: types into the box, adding to what is already there. text.clear()Element Clear: empties the box. text.send_keys(\"Selenium\")Type the real value. print(\"check 2 before:\", check2.is_selected())is_selected reads whether a checkbox is ticked. check2.click()Element Click: ticks it, as a mouse click would. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"my-radio-2\").click()Clicking the second radio button unselects the first one. driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\").click()Click Submit. The documentation prefers this to the submit() method. Lesson: Click, type and clear The three errors interactions give, made on purpose def attempt(what, step):Run one step and report the error it raises, if any. exceptWebDriverExceptionas e:Every Selenium error is a WebDriverException. attempt(\"type into disabled\", lambda: driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-disabled\").send_keys(\"x\"))A disabled input cannot take keyboard input. attempt(\"clear readonly\", lambda: driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-readonly\").clear())A readonly input cannot be reset. driver.execute_script(Add a cookie banner that covers the bottom of the page, as many real sites do. attempt(\"click under banner\", lambda: driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\").click())The banner sits over the centre of the button. Lesson: Click, type and clear Ask an element about itself text.send_keys(\"hello\")Type, so the live value differs from the HTML. print(\"disabled box enabled:\", driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-disabled\").is_enabled())The disabled input reports False. print(\"tag:\", text.tag_name, \", rect:\", text.rect[\"width\"], \"x\", text.rect[\"height\"])Get Element Rect: position and size in pixels. print(\"get_attribute(value):\", text.get_attribute(\"value\"))The classic method: property first, then attribute. print(\"get_dom_attribute(value):\", text.get_dom_attribute(\"value\"))Only what the HTML says. The HTML has no value attribute. print(\"get_property(value):\", text.get_property(\"value\"))The live JavaScript property: what is typed now. print(\"get_dom_attribute(myprop):\", text.get_dom_attribute(\"myprop\"))A made up attribute in the HTML. print(\"button colour:\", driver.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\").value_of_css_property(\"color\"))The computed CSS value. print(\"heading text:\", driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"h1\").text)The rendered, visible text. Lesson: Read information about an element Work with single and multiple select lists from selenium.webdriver.support.select importSelectSelect lives in the support package. single = Select(driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"selectomatic\"))Wrap a select element in a Select object. print(\"options:\", len(single.options))options lists every option element. single.select_by_visible_text(\"Four\")Pick by the text the user sees. single.select_by_value(\"two\")Pick by the value attribute. single.select_by_index(0)Pick by position, counting from 0. multi = Select(driver.find_element(By.ID, \"multi\"))A select with the multiple attribute. print(\"multi starts with:\", len(multi.all_selected_options), \"selected\")Eggs and Sausages are selected in the"],[21,"interacting","Interacting with elements and reading them","HTML. multi.deselect_by_value(\"eggs\")Only multiple lists can deselect. Lesson: Select lists Upload a file without the file dialog file.write_text(\"quarterly numbers\")Make a small file to upload. upload = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-file\")The input with type=\"file\". upload.send_keys(str(file.resolve()))Send the full path. No dialog opens, the file is simply attached. print(\"browser shows:\", upload.get_property(\"value\"))Browsers hide the real folder and show a made up folder called fakepath. Lesson: File upload Tables Table: command · works on · watch out for Command Works on Watch out for click Any element It clicks the centre of the element. If something else is on top there, you get element click intercepted. send keys Text fields and other keyboard interactable elements It adds to the existing text. Clear first if you want to replace it. clear Editable, resettable elements: text inputs and content editable Readonly and disabled inputs give invalid element state. submit Form elements In Selenium 4 it runs a script, so the documentation recommends clicking the submit button instead. select select and option elements Use the Select class, covered below. Lesson: Click, type and clear Table: question · python Question Python Is it displayed? is_displayed() Is it enabled? is_enabled() Is it selected? is_selected() Tag name tag_name Position and size rect Computed CSS value_of_css_property(name) Visible text text Lesson: Read information about an element Table: method · reads · use it for Method Reads Use it for get DOM attribute Only the attribute in the HTML Checking what the server sent, such as a data-test value get DOM property Only the live JavaScript property What is in a field now, whether a box is checked now get attribute (classic) The property if there is one, otherwise the attribute. Selenium does this with a JavaScript atom Old code. Prefer the two precise methods. Lesson: Read information about an element Table: rule from the documentation · what it means for you Rule from the documentation What it means for you Select only works on select and option elements Drop downs built from div or li with JavaScript need normal clicks instead Since Selenium 4.5 a Select cannot be created on a disabled select Check isEnabled first if the list may be disabled Disabled options cannot be selected Trying raises an error Only multiple lists can deselect deselect on a single list raises an error Lesson: Select lists Tips and traps Remember When the test runs on a Grid, the file must reach the remote machine. The Java, Python and JavaScript bindings handle this with a file detector that uploads the file to the node first. Chapter 6.1 shows it.Lesson: File upload"],[21,"interacting","Step 4Synchronise and control","4.1"],[21,"waits","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","Open chapter Why tests race the page, implicit and explicit waits, expected conditions, custom waits and why not to mix them. Key ideas Implicit wait A session setting, default 0. Every find keeps looking up to that long and returns as soon as the element appears. It only waits for presence. Explicit wait A loop with a condition, timeout and polling interval. Expected conditions presence, visibility, clickable, text present, title contains, staleness. Wait for visibility before typing. Never mix Implicit plus explicit makes waits unpredictable: 10 s with 15 s can time out after about 20 s. Keep implicit at 0 and use explicit waits. Code The race: look for the box straight after the click driver.find_element(By.ID, \"adder\").click()The page adds a red box, but only 1 second after the click. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"box0\")Our code asks at once. No wait is set, so the driver checks only once. print(\"too early: no such element\")Selenium was faster than the page. Lesson: The race between your code and the page Implicit wait: every find keeps looking driver.implicitly_wait(2)For the whole session: a find may keep looking for up to 2 seconds. box = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"box0\")Not there yet, so the driver keeps checking until the box appears. print(\"found\", box.get_attribute(\"class\"), \"after about\", round(seconds), \"s\")Returns as soon as it is found, not after the full 2 seconds. Lesson: Implicit waits Explicit wait: wait for exactly the condition you need revealed = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"revealed\")The input is in the page already, just hidden. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"reveal\").click()The page shows it 1 second later. wait = WebDriverWait(driver, timeout=2)Wait at most 2 seconds. Python checks every 0.5 s. wait.until(lambda d: revealed.is_displayed())A lambda: keep calling it until it returns something true. revealed.send_keys(\"Displayed\")Safe now: the input is displayed, so it can take keys. Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions Ready made conditions, and what a timeout looks like from selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions asECThe usual short name for the expected conditions module. box = wait.until(EC.presence_of_element_located((By.ID, \"box0\")))A ready made condition. Note the locator is one tuple. It returns the element. field = wait.until(EC.visibility_of_element_located((By.ID, \"revealed\")))Present is not enough for typing. Wait until it is visible. WebDriverWait(driver, 1).until(EC.presence_of_element_located((By.ID, \"box1\")), \"the second box never came\")Nobody clicked again, so box1 never appears. The second argument is our message. Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions A customised wait: poll faster and retry on an error errors = [ElementNotInteractableException]The errors that mean not yet, try again. wait = WebDriverWait(driver, timeout=2, poll_frequency=0.3, ignored_exceptions=errors)Give up after 2 s, check every 0.3 s. wait.until(lambda d: revealed.send_keys(\"Displayed\") orTrue)Do the action inside the condition. send_keys returns None, so \"or True\" reports success. Lesson: Customising waits, and never mixing them Proof: mixing implicit and explicit waits stretches the timeout driver.implicitly_wait(2)Implicit wait: each find may block for 2 seconds. WebDriverWait(driver, 3).until(EC.presence_of_element_located((By.ID, \"box9\")))Explicit wait of 3 seconds for an element that never comes. print(f\"gave up after {time.monotonic() - start:.1f} s, not 3 s\")Each check inside the explicit wait blocked 2 seconds on the implicit wait. Lesson: Customising waits, and never mixing them Tables Table: strength · weakness Strength Weakness One line covers every find in the session It only waits for presence. A present element can still be hidden, covered or disabled Finds return as soon as the element appears Every check that something is absent now takes the full wait Easy to show in a first script It cannot wait for text, a title, a count or anything that is not a find Lesson:"],[21,"waits","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","Implicit waits Table: default · python webdriverwait Default Python WebDriverWait Polling interval 0.5 s Errors ignored while looping NoSuchElementException Timeout you must give one Error at the end TimeoutException Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions Table: wait for · python Wait for Python Element exists presence_of_element_located Element visible visibility_of_element_located Element clickable element_to_be_clickable Text present text_to_be_present_in_element Title contains title_contains Element gone stale staleness_of Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions 4.2"],[21,"windows-frames-alerts","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","Open chapter Switching windows and tabs, working inside iframes, and handling alerts, confirms and prompts. Key ideas Windows Each window or tab has a handle. Save the original, switch to the new one, and switch back after close(). newWindow(\"tab\") opens and switches in one step. Frames Switch into an iframe by element, name or id, or index before finding inside it. defaultContent goes to the top, parentFrame up one level. Title in frames Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. Alerts alert, confirm and prompt: getText, accept, dismiss, sendKeys. Wait with alert is present when it appears late. Forgotten pop ups unhandledPromptBehavior defaults to dismiss and notify: the pop up is closed and the next command raises unexpected alert open. Code Follow a link into a new window, come back, open a new tab original = driver.current_window_handleThe id of the current window. It stays the same for the whole session. driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"windowTwo\").click()This link has target=\"_blank\", so it opens a new window. WebDriverWait(driver, 2).until(EC.number_of_windows_to_be(2))Wait until the second window exists. for handle in driver.window_handles:Every open window, in the order they were opened. driver.switch_to.window(handle)Switch to the one that is not the original. driver.close()Close only this window. driver.switch_to.window(original)Switch back, or the next command fails with no such window. driver.switch_to.new_window(\"tab\")Selenium 4: open a new tab and switch to it in one step. driver.quit()Closes every window and tab at once. Lesson: Windows and tabs Switch into an iframe three ways and back out print(\"email boxes seen from the top:\", len(driver.find_elements(By.ID, \"email\")))The form lives inside the iframe, so the top document sees none. driver.switch_to.frame(frame)1. Switch with a WebElement: the most flexible way. driver.switch_to.default_content()Leave the frame: back to the top document. driver.switch_to.frame(\"iframe1-name\")2. Switch with the frame name or id. driver.switch_to.frame(0)3. Switch with an index: the first frame of the page. print(\"title inside the frame:\", driver.title)Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. print(\"heading:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"iframe_page_heading\").text)Outside elements are reachable again. Lesson: Frames and iframes Alert, confirm, prompt, a slow alert and one left open driver.find_element(By.ID, \"alert\").click()The page calls alert('cheese'). alert = wait.until(EC.alert_is_present())Wait for the alert, then get a handle on it. alert.accept()Press OK. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"confirm\").click()A confirm box: OK or Cancel. confirm = driver.switch_to.alertSwitch to it without waiting, because it opens at once. confirm.dismiss()Press Cancel. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"prompt\").click()A prompt asks for text. prompt.send_keys(\"Selenium\")Type the answer into the prompt. print(\"page shows:\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"text\").text)The page wrote our answer into a paragraph. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"slow-alert\").click()This one opens 200 ms later. print(\"slow:\", wait.until(EC.alert_is_present()).text)switch_to.alert now would fail, so wait. driver.find_element(By.ID, \"alert\").click()Open one more alert and forget about it. driver.titleAny command while an alert is open. print(\"left open:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])The default behaviour dismisses it and reports an error. Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Tables Table: what you want · python What you want Python Current handle current_window_handle All handles window_handles Switch switch_to.window(h) New tab or window switch_to.new_window(\"tab\") Close current close() Lesson: Windows and tabs Table: switch by · example · when to use Switch by Example When to use WebElement switchTo().frame(element) Most flexible. Find the frame with any locator. Name or id switchTo().frame(\"iframe1-name\") The frame has a stable name or id. If several match, the first one is used."],[21,"windows-frames-alerts","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","Java and Python accept the string. JavaScript needs the element. Index switchTo().frame(0) Position in window.frames. Breaks if frames are added. Leave all frames switchTo().defaultContent() Back to the top document. Up one level switchTo().parentFrame() For frames inside frames. Lesson: Frames and iframes Table: pop up · buttons · what you can do Pop up Buttons What you can do alert OK Read the text, accept confirm OK and Cancel Read the text, accept or dismiss prompt OK and Cancel, plus a text box Read the text, send keys, accept or dismiss. Sending keys replaces the placeholder text Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Table: unhandledpromptbehavior · what happens to a forgotten pop up unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to a forgotten pop up dismiss and notify (default) Dismissed, and the command raises unexpected alert open accept and notify Accepted, and the command raises unexpected alert open dismiss Dismissed silently accept Accepted silently ignore Left open, and the command raises unexpected alert open Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Tips and traps Common mistake After close() you are still pointing at the closed window. The documentation warns that the next command raises no such window. Always switch back to a handle you saved before.Lesson: Windows and tabs Remember Recent drivers dismiss beforeunload prompts, the \"Leave site?\" box, by default. Chapter 2.1 showed where unhandledPromptBehavior is set.Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts 4.3"],[21,"actions","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","Open chapter Mouse, keyboard and wheel actions, and how a chain becomes ticks. Key ideas Build then perform An action chain is sent in one request when perform() is called. Nothing happens before that. Ticks Each input source has one action per tick. A tick lasts as long as its slowest action. Mouse doubleClick, contextClick, moveToElement, clickAndHold, dragAndDrop, moveByOffset. The driver remembers the pointer position between chains. Keyboard keyDown and keyUp for modifiers, sendKeys for text. Use Command on a Mac and Control elsewhere. Release every key you press. Wheel scrollToElement, scrollByAmount, scrollFromOrigin. Wait for the scroll result, because it can land after perform() returns. Code Double click, right click, hover, drag and drop, and move to a point ActionChains(driver).double_click(box).perform()Build a chain, then perform() sends it to the browser in one request. ActionChains(driver).context_click(box).perform()A right click. ActionChains(driver).move_to_element(driver.find_element(By.ID, \"hover\")).perform()Move to the centre of the element. Nothing is clicked. ActionChains(driver).drag_and_drop(source, target).perform()Move to source, press, move to target, release. builder = ActionBuilder(driver)The lower level builder. Python has no move_to_location on ActionChains. builder.pointer_action.move_to_location(8, 12)A point measured from the top left of the viewport. ActionChains(driver).move_by_offset(13, 15).perform()From wherever the pointer is now. The driver remembered it between chains. Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Hold Shift, select all with the right modifier, and type into an element .perform()Shift is held for abc only. send_keys goes to the box that has focus. cmd_ctrl = Keys.COMMANDif sys.platform == \"darwin\"elseKeys.CONTROLSelect all is Command A on a Mac and Control A elsewhere. ActionChains(driver).send_keys_to_element(box, \"Selenium!\").perform()Clicks the element first, then types. Lesson: Keyboard actions Scroll to an element, by an amount, and inside a frame frame = driver.find_element(By.TAG_NAME, \"iframe\")The page starts with a 5000 px tall block, so the iframe is far below. ActionChains(driver).scroll_to_element(frame).perform()Scroll until the bottom of the element is in view. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html\")Load it again. A refresh would keep the scroll position. ActionChains(driver).scroll_by_amount(0, 300).perform()Like turning the wheel: 300 px down from the top left of the viewport. WebDriverWait(driver, 2).until(lambda d: d.execute_script(\"return window.scrollY\") >= 300)The scroll can land after perform returns, so wait for it. ActionChains(driver).scroll_from_origin(ScrollOrigin.from_element(inner), 0, 200).perform()Turn the wheel with the pointer over the frame, so the frame scrolls. Lesson: Scroll wheel actions Tables Table: mouse action · python Mouse action Python Double click double_click(el) Right click context_click(el) Hover move_to_element(el) Press and hold click_and_hold(el) Drag and drop drag_and_drop(a, b) Point in the viewport ActionBuilder then move_to_location(x, y) From the current point move_by_offset(x, y) Release everything ActionBuilder(driver).clear_actions() Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Table: key method · what it does Key method What it does keyDown(key) Press a key and keep it down. Meant for modifier keys. keyUp(key) Release it. Forget this and the key stays down for the next chain. sendKeys(text) Type into whatever has focus, one key down and key up per character. sendKeys(element, text) Click the element first, then type. Java and Python only. Lesson: Keyboard actions Table: wheel action · python Wheel action Python Into view scroll_to_element(el) By an amount scroll_by_amount(0, 300) From an element scroll_from_origin(ScrollOrigin.from_element(el), 0, 200) Lesson: Scroll wheel actions Tips and traps Remember A pointer move has a duration. In"],[21,"actions","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","the bindings used here, Python uses 250 ms by default and JavaScript 100 ms. Some drag libraries ignore a move that takes 0 ms, so if a drag does nothing, try a chain with a longer move.Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Common mistake Actions type into the focused element. If nothing has focus, the keys go to the page body and the test seems to type into nothing. Element sendKeys focuses the element itself, which is why it is the better choice for plain typing.Lesson: Keyboard actions 4.4"],[21,"javascript-cookies","JavaScript execution and cookies","Open chapter Running JavaScript in the page, async scripts, and working with cookies. Key ideas executeScript The script runs as a function body. Use return to get a value and arguments[n] to read what you pass in. Elements go in and come back as WebElements. Return types Whole numbers are Long in Java and int in Python, objects are Map and dict, undefined is null or None. Async scripts executeAsyncScript adds a callback as the last argument. A returned promise is also awaited. The script timeout, 30 s by default, limits both. Cookies Visit the domain first, then add. httpOnly hides a cookie from page scripts. deleteAllCookies gives a clean start. Code Run JavaScript, pass an element in, and see what type comes back print(script, \"->\", type(driver.execute_script(script)).__name__)Run the script as the body of a function and convert the result. print(\"arguments:\", driver.execute_script(\"return arguments[0].innerText + ' / ' + arguments[1]\", dinner, 7))Extra arguments arrive in the script as arguments[0], arguments[1] and so on. driver.execute_script(\"arguments[0].click();\", driver.find_element(By.ID, \"updatediv\"))A click done by JavaScript. It skips the checks a real click makes. Lesson: Run JavaScript in the page Asynchronous scripts, promises, the script timeout and a JavaScript error waited = driver.execute_async_script(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('waited'), 300);\")Selenium adds a callback as the last argument. The command returns when the script calls it. print(\"promise:\", driver.execute_script(\"return new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('from promise'), 200));\"))A returned promise is awaited by the browser before it answers. driver.set_script_timeout(0.2)How long a script may run, in seconds. The default is 30. print(\"too slow:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])The callback came after 500 ms, the limit was 200 ms. print(\"broken script:\", e.msg.split(\"\\n\")[0])A JavaScript error in the page becomes javascript error. Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout Add, read, protect and delete cookies driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"key\", \"value\": \"value\"})The browser is still on an empty start page with no domain. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")Visit the domain first. A small page is enough. c = driver.get_cookie(\"key\")Read one cookie by name. It comes back as a dict. driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"foo\", \"value\": \"bar\", \"sameSite\": \"Strict\"})Any other field goes in the same dict. driver.add_cookie({\"name\": \"secret\", \"value\": \"s\", \"httpOnly\": True})httpOnly hides the cookie from page JavaScript. print(\"missing cookie:\", driver.get_cookie(\"foo\"))Asking for a cookie that is not there gives None, not an error. driver.delete_all_cookies()A clean start, for example before logging in as another user. Lesson: Cookies Tables Table: error · python Error Python The script took longer than the script timeout TimeoutException The script threw an error JavascriptException Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout Table: field · meaning Field Meaning name, value Required. The data. domain, path Where the cookie is sent. They must match the current page. secure Only sent over https. httpOnly Hidden from page JavaScript. sameSite Strict, Lax or None. Whether the cookie goes with requests started by other sites. expiry Seconds since 1970. With no expiry the cookie lasts for the session. Lesson: Cookies Tips and traps Remember The script timeout is 30 seconds by default, as chapter 2.1 showed. It covers both sync and async scripts. A short timeout makes a broken async script fail fast.Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout"],[21,"javascript-cookies","Step 5Build a test suite","5.1"],[21,"test-frameworks","Test frameworks: JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha","Open chapter Running Selenium inside JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha, with data-driven tests and screenshots on failure. Key ideas Framework jobs Selenium drives the browser. JUnit 5, pytest or Mocha find the tests, run setup and teardown, assert and report. Fresh browser Start the driver in the before each step and quit it in the after each step, so tests stay independent and can run in any order. Teardown always runs @AfterEach, code after yield and afterEach run also when the test failed, so quit belongs there. Data-driven @ParameterizedTest with @CsvSource, @pytest.mark.parametrize, or a loop around it() in Mocha. One result per row. Screenshot on failure Take it after the test failed and before quit: a pytest makereport hook plus fixture. Code A first test class: a fresh browser for every test @pytest.fixtureA fixture is pytest's setup and teardown in one function. yield driverEverything before yield is setup. The test runs here. driver.quit()Everything after yield is teardown, and runs also when the test failed. def test_page_has_the_right_title(driver):Asking for a parameter called driver makes pytest run the fixture. assert driver.title == \"Web form\"A plain assert. pytest shows both sides when it fails. Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework One test, three rows of data @pytest.mark.parametrize(\"user, password, expected\", [Each tuple is one run of the test. def test_login(driver, user, password, expected):pytest passes the columns in as parameters, next to the fixture. alert = WebDriverWait(driver, 2).until(EC.alert_is_present())The page answers with an alert. Lesson: Data-driven tests A failing test that saves a screenshot before the browser closes @pytest.hookimpl(wrapper=True, tryfirst=True)A hook that sees every test report as pytest builds it. setattr(item, \"rep_\" + report.when, report)Keep the report on the test item: rep_setup, rep_call and rep_teardown. if request.node.rep_call.failed:Did the test body fail? name = f\"failure-{request.node.name}.png\"Name the file after the test. driver.save_screenshot(name)Take the picture before quit. def test_title_is_wrong_on_purpose(driver):The driver fixture comes from conftest.py. assert driver.title == \"Web form!\"Wrong on purpose, so the test fails. Lesson: A screenshot when a test fails Tables Table: job · pytest Job pytest Before every test fixture code before yield After every test fixture code after yield Once for the class or file scope=\"module\" or \"session\" Check a value assert actual == expected Run it pytest Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework Table: framework · where the screenshot code goes · how it knows the test failed Framework Where the screenshot code goes How it knows the test failed pytest The fixture, after yield and before quit() A pytest_runtest_makereport hook in conftest.py stores the report on the test Lesson: A screenshot when a test fails Tips and traps Remember The Selenium documentation recommends a fresh browser for every test. Then no cookie, login or open dialog can leak from one test into the next, and the tests can run in any order or in parallel. It costs a browser start per test, a price that is almost always worth paying.Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework Remember Keep the data close to the test while it is small. When it grows, move it to a CSV or JSON file: JUnit has @CsvFileSource, and pytest and Mocha can read any file into the same list.Lesson: Data-driven tests 5.2"],[21,"page-objects","Page objects and page components","Open chapter Page objects, page component objects and the rules for writing them. Key ideas Page object A class that is the interface to one page. Tests call its methods, so a change to the page is fixed in one place. No assertions inside Assertions belong to the test. The only check in a page object is that the right page is loaded. Return the next page A method that moves to another page returns that page object. A method that stays may return itself. Components A page component object models a repeated part, such as a field or a product card, and searches only inside its root element. Waits inside Put waits in the page object, for example a constructor that waits for its title after a navigation. Code Two page objects, one component and three tests FIELDS = (By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"label.form-label\")Locators live here and nowhere else. if driver.title != \"Web form\":The one check a page object should make: am I on the right page? return [Field(e) for e in self.driver.find_elements(*self.FIELDS)]Wrap every label element in a Field component. classField:A page component: one label and the control inside it. return self.root.text.split(\"\\n\")[0].strip()The first line of the label is its name. return self.root.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"input, textarea, select\")Search inside this component only. return selfReturning itself allows field(...).fill(...).fill(...). WebDriverWait(driver, 3).until(EC.title_is(\"Web form - target page\"))The page arrives after a navigation, so wait for it instead of checking once. form = WebFormPage.open(driver)The test talks to pages, not to locators. received = form.submit()submit() moves to another page, so it returns that page object. assert received.message() == \"Received!\"The assertion stays in the test. assert len(fields) == 11Eleven labelled controls on this page. with pytest.raises(ValueError):The constructor checks the page and fails loudly. Lesson: Write page objects and components Tables Table: without page objects · with page objects Without page objects With page objects Locators are copied into every test that uses the page Each locator is written once, inside the page object A test reads as a list of clicks and CSS selectors A test reads as what the user does: open the form, fill a field, submit A UI change breaks many tests in many files A UI change is fixed in one class Waiting and page checks are repeated The page object waits for its page once, in its constructor Lesson: Why page objects Table: rule from the documentation · where it shows in the example Rule from the documentation Where it shows in the example The public methods represent the services the page offers field, fields, submit, message Try not to expose the internals of the page Locators are private. Field.control() is private in Java Generally do not make assertions Every assert is in the test file The one allowed check: the right page is loaded The constructors check the title, or wait for it after a navigation Methods return other page objects, components or themselves submit() returns ReceivedPage, fill() returns the Field A page object need not represent an entire page Field represents one labelled control Lesson: Write page objects and components Table: choice · what the documentation or practice suggests Choice What the documentation or practice suggests Same action, different result Write two methods, for example loginAsValidUser and loginExpectingError, each returning the page that really follows A part that appears on many pages A component object such as a header, a menu or a product card, used inside page objects Where waits go Inside the page object, so tests do not repeat them Base class Shared code such as the driver field and a wait helper. Keep it small Lesson: Design choices and the limits of page objects Tips and traps Remember ReceivedPage waits for its title, while WebFormPage checks the title once. The web form is opened with get(), which returns after the page loads. The received page appears after a"],[21,"page-objects","Page objects and page components","click, and chapter 3.3 showed that the next command can run before the new page is there.Lesson: Write page objects and components 5.3"],[21,"practices","Good practices and things to avoid","Open chapter Test design, encouraged practices and the things the documentation says to avoid. Key ideas Need a browser? Ask first whether a lighter test can do the job. Browser tests are the most expensive kind. Short and focused Set up the data, perform a few actions, evaluate the result. Many small independent tests beat one grand journey. Clean start A new driver starts with a new profile. Use a fresh browser per test and never share test data. Prepare state outside the UI Log in and create data through APIs, then set cookies, instead of clicking through forms. Avoid CAPTCHAs, download progress, status codes, third party logins, test order, performance numbers, spidering and 2FA are jobs for other tools or test hooks. Code A new driver starts with a clean profile first.add_cookie({\"name\": \"cart\", \"value\": \"3 items\"})State a first test leaves behind: a cookie first.execute_script(\"localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark')\")and a value in local storage. second = webdriver.Chrome()A new driver gets a new, empty profile by default. Lesson: Encouraged practices Prepare the state without clicking through the user interface session = \"abc123\"In a real suite this value comes from a login API call, not from the login form. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")A small page on the domain, only to be allowed to set the cookie. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/cookies.html\")Now open the page under test. It reads its cookies on load. Lesson: Encouraged practices Tables Table: testing type · question it answers · selenium fits? Testing type Question it answers Selenium fits? Acceptance Are we building the right product? Yes, by simulating what the user expects Functional Are we building the product right? Yes Integration Do the modules work together, for example order plus payment? Yes, for flows through the UI System Does the whole product work end to end, in an environment like production? Yes, a few journeys Performance (load and stress) How fast is it, and does it survive a peak? Not as the measuring tool. The documentation points to tools such as JMeter Regression Did a change break what worked? Yes, by running the existing tests again Lesson: The shape of a good browser test Table: practice · what the documentation says Practice What the documentation says Avoid sharing state Do not share test data, clean up stale data, and create a new WebDriver per test Test independency Write each test as its own unit, not relying on another test to complete Mock external services Removing dependencies on external services improves speed and stability Improved reporting Selenium does not report. Use the reports of your test framework, such as xUnit XML or HTML Locators Unique ids first, then a well written CSS selector. XPath is flexible but harder to debug and often slower Page objects and a fluent API Chapter 5.2. Methods can return the page for chained calls Domain specific language Business level methods on top of page objects Lesson: Encouraged practices Table: avoid · why · do instead Avoid Why Do instead CAPTCHAs They are built to stop automation Disable them in the test environment, or add a hook that lets tests bypass them File downloads The API does not expose download progress Find the link with Selenium and fetch it with an HTTP library such as curl, using any cookies it needs HTTP status codes WebDriver acts like a user, and a status code is rarely the useful detail of a failure Check what the user sees, such as an error page. Chapter 6.2 shows BiDi network events if you really need codes Logging in to Gmail or Facebook Against their terms, slow and unreliable Use the APIs or test account tools they provide Test dependency Tests must run in any order Make each test set up its own state Performance testing Browser start, servers and third parties add noise to every number Use performance tools, and keep Selenium for function Link spidering A browser takes seconds per page Use curl or a library"],[21,"practices","Good practices and things to avoid","such as BeautifulSoup Two factor authentication Automating it is hard and less secure A test only token, or 2FA turned off for test users, test IPs or the test environment Lesson: Things to avoid Tips and traps Remember Every row has the same shape: Selenium is a tool for what a user does in a browser. When the job is not that, a lighter tool is faster and more reliable.Lesson: Things to avoid 5.4"],[21,"troubleshooting","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","Open chapter Common errors, stale elements and logging in Java, Python and JavaScript. Key ideas Error names Each W3C error has a class per language. Read the name, then the message, then your line in the trace. Stale element The node behind a reference was replaced, for example by a refresh. Find the element again. Context An element found in one frame or window is unknown in another. Switch back before using it. Client log Python: logger selenium at DEBUG. Driver log ChromeDriver writes COMMAND and RESPONSE lines when its service is given a log file and a verbose level. Code Make an element reference fail three ways, and fix each one box = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")The driver gives back a reference to this exact node. driver.refresh()The page is rebuilt. The old node is gone, a new one with the same name takes its place. box = driver.find_element(By.NAME, \"my-text\")The fix: find it again. email = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"email\")Found inside the frame. print(\"2. outside its frame:\", type(e).__name__)The reference is fine, but this context does not know it. driver.switch_to.frame(\"iframe1\")The fix: switch back to the context where it was found. driver.get(\"https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html\")Leave the page completely. print(\"3. after leaving the page:\", type(e).__name__)Go back to the page and find it again. Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Turn on the client log and see every command classRequests(logging.Handler):A tiny handler that prints one line per request. m = re.match(r\"(GET|POST|DELETE) http://[^/]+(\\S*)\", record.getMessage())Request lines start with the method and the URL. print(m.group(1), re.sub(\"/session/[0-9a-f]{32}\", \"/session/{id}\", m.group(2)))Hide the session id, which changes every run. log = logging.getLogger(\"selenium\")The parent of every Selenium logger in Python. log.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)Python logs at WARNING by default. Requests are written at DEBUG. Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Ask ChromeDriver for its own log service = Service(log_output=\"chromedriver.log\", service_args=[\"--log-level=DEBUG\"])Where ChromeDriver writes its log, and how much. print(line.split(\"] COMMAND \")[1].split(\" \")[0])Print the name of each command the driver received. Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Tables Table: w3c error · python · usual cause and fix W3C error Python Usual cause and fix no such element NoSuchElementException Wrong place, wrong time or a changed locator. Check the page, add an explicit wait, update the locator. stale element reference StaleElementReferenceException The node was replaced, for example by a refresh or a re render. Find it again. element click intercepted ElementClickInterceptedException Something covers the element: a sticky bar, a modal, an animation. Wait for it to go, or scroll. element not interactable ElementNotInteractableException Hidden, zero size or not ready. Wait until visible and enabled. invalid selector InvalidSelectorException The CSS or XPath is broken, or an XPath was passed to a CSS locator. timeout TimeoutException An explicit wait ran out. The message says which condition. script timeout TimeoutException A script took longer than the script timeout. unexpected alert open UnexpectedAlertPresentException An alert was left open. Handle it where it opens. no such window NoSuchWindowException The window was closed and the test did not switch back. invalid session id InvalidSessionIdException The session was quit or crashed. Look for a quit earlier in the test. session not created SessionNotCreatedException Driver and browser versions do not match, or the browser could not start. Lesson: Read an error the right way Table: what happened · error in this run · fix What happened Error in this run Fix Refresh, re render, list re sorted stale element reference Find the element again just before you use it Switched to another frame or window no such element Switch back to the context where it"],[21,"troubleshooting","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","was found Navigated to another page no such element Go back to the page and find it again Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Table: language · logger · default level · level that shows requests Language Logger Default level Level that shows requests Python logging, under the name selenium WARNING DEBUG Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Tips and traps Remember Search the error name together with the first line of the message. Ignore the long list of numbers under Stacktrace in Chrome messages. Those are addresses inside ChromeDriver, not your code.Lesson: Read an error the right way Remember Page objects that find elements inside their methods, as in chapter 5.2, rarely meet stale elements, because they look the element up fresh each time.Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Common mistake The documentation shows the JavaScript logger as getLogger(\"webdriver\"). In selenium-webdriver 4.51 that name printed nothing in our run, while driver.http and driver.http.Executor printed every request. In pytest, the documentation adds that logs of passing tests are hidden unless you run pytest -s -p no:logging.Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did"],[21,"step-6","Step 6Scale out with Grid","6.1"],[21,"grid","Selenium Grid 4","Open chapter Selenium Grid 4 components, roles, remote sessions and parallel runs. Key ideas Why a Grid Parallel runs on many browsers, versions and systems, and shorter suites: tests × average time ÷ nodes. Components Router (4444), New Session Queue, Distributor, Session Map, Node (5555) and Event Bus (4442, 4443). Roles Standalone for one machine, Hub and Node for most Grids, Distributed for very large ones. Remote driver Only the driver creation changes. se:name adds metadata, se:cdp gives DevTools through the Grid, and a local file detector sends upload files to the node. Queue and quit New sessions wait in the queue for a free slot. Always quit, or the slot stays busy until the session timeout. Code Ask the Grid if it is ready and what it offers with urllib.request.urlopen(\"http://localhost:4444/status\") as res:The same endpoint a driver has, now answered by the Grid. print(\"slot:\", slot[\"stereotype\"][\"browserName\"])Each slot can hold one session of this browser. Lesson: When and why to use a Grid Three tests, two slots: watch the queue driver = webdriver.Remote(\"http://localhost:4444\", options=webdriver.ChromeOptions())Returns when the Grid has given this test a slot. time.sleep(3)Pretend the test takes 3 seconds. driver.quit()Frees the slot for the next request in the queue. withThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) as pool:Three threads, so three tests start at the same moment. print(\"got a browser after:\", [f\"{t} s\"for t in times])Two at once, the third only after a slot was free. Lesson: Components, roles and ports Run a test on the Grid with a name and a file upload options = webdriver.ChromeOptions()Options say which browser you want. The Grid picks a node that has it. options.set_capability(\"se:name\", \"upload on the grid\")se: capabilities are Grid metadata. se:name shows in the Grid UI. driver = webdriver.Remote(\"http://localhost:4444\", options=options)The only change from a local test: a URL instead of a local driver. print(k + \":\", v if k != \"se:cdp\"else v.replace(driver.session_id, \"{id}\"))se:cdp is a DevTools address that goes through the Grid. driver.file_detector = LocalFileDetector()Sends the local file to the node, which may be another machine. print(\"attached:\", driver.execute_script(\"return arguments[0].files[0].name\", upload))The browser on the node has the file. Lesson: Remote sessions, metadata and files Numbers to remember Suite time on a GridExam favourite total time = number of tests × average test timenumber of nodes From the documentation: 15 tests of 45 s take 11 min 15 s on one node, 2 min 15 s on 5 nodes and 45 s on 15 nodes. Lesson: When and why to use a Grid Tables Table: component · job · default port Component Job Default port Router Entry point for every request. New sessions go to the queue. Commands for existing sessions go straight to the right node, found through the Session Map 4444 New Session Queue Holds new session requests in first in, first out order, with a timeout 5559 Distributor Knows every node and its slots. Takes requests from the queue, finds a matching free slot and creates the session 5553 Session Map Remembers which node runs which session id 5556 Node Runs the browsers. By default one slot per CPU for Chromium browsers and Firefox, one for Safari 5555 Event Bus Messages between the other components, cheaper than HTTP. Started first in a distributed Grid 4442, 4443 and 5557 Lesson: Components, roles and ports Table: role · command · use it for Role Command Use it for Standalone java -jar selenium-server-<version>.jar standalone All components in one process on one machine. Local debugging, quick suites, CI Hub and Node ... hub and ... node --hub http://<hub-ip>:4444 The most used role. Combine machines with different systems and browsers behind one address Distributed ... event-bus, sessionqueue, sessions, distributor, router, node Large Grids with more than 100 nodes, each component scaled on its own Lesson: Components, roles and ports Table: language · remote driver ·"],[21,"grid","Selenium Grid 4","file detector Language Remote driver File detector Python webdriver.Remote(gridUrl, options=options) driver.file_detector = LocalFileDetector() Lesson: Remote sessions, metadata and files Tips and traps Remember A node registers by sending a message on the Event Bus, and the Distributor then calls it over HTTP to confirm. When hub and node are on different machines, the Event Bus ports 4442 and 4443 and the node port must be reachable.Lesson: Components, roles and ports 6.2"],[21,"bidi-cdp","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","Open chapter WebDriver BiDi events, Chrome DevTools Protocol commands and Grid GraphQL. Key ideas BiDi A W3C protocol with a WebSocket, so the browser can push events. Turn it on with webSocketUrl, then subscribe to console and page errors. Events, not polling Handlers are called when events arrive. Wait for them, because they come after the action that caused them. CDP Chrome and Edge only, no stability promise, the three newest Chrome versions. Use it for Chromium features with no other API. GraphQL POST a query to /graphql on the Grid for nodes, slots, sessions and the queue size. Grid endpoints /status, DELETE /session, and drain or remove a node through the Distributor endpoints. Code Hear console messages and page errors as they happen options.enable_bidi = TrueAsk for a BiDi WebSocket next to the normal HTTP connection. driver.script.add_console_message_handler(lambda e: heard.append(f\"console {e.level}: {e.text}\"))Called for every console.log, console.error and so on. driver.script.add_javascript_error_handler(lambda e: heard.append(f\"page error: {e.text}\"))Called for every uncaught error in the page. WebDriverWait(driver, 5).until(lambda _: len(heard) == 3)Events are pushed, not returned, so wait until all three arrived. Lesson: WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back Send Chrome DevTools commands: read the version and change the time zone driver = webdriver.Chrome()execute_cdp_cmd exists on Chrome and Edge drivers only. version = driver.execute_cdp_cmd(\"Browser.getVersion\", {})Any CDP method by name, with a dict of parameters. driver.execute_cdp_cmd(\"Emulation.setTimezoneOverride\", {\"timezoneId\": \"Asia/Kolkata\"})Something WebDriver Classic has no command for. Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol Ask the Grid exactly what you need with GraphQL req = urllib.request.Request(\"http://localhost:4444/graphql\", data=json.dumps({\"query\": query}).encode(), method=\"POST\")GraphQL over HTTP: one POST with the query as a string. q = \"{ grid { nodeCount, totalSlots, maxSession, sessionCount, sessionQueueSize } }\"Ask only for these fields. print(\"busy:\", ask(q)[\"grid\"])One session is running now. print(\"names:\", [json.loads(s[\"capabilities\"]).get(\"se:name\") for s in sessions])The se:name metadata is in the session capabilities. Lesson: Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints Tables Table: how to switch it on · python How to switch it on Python Capability options.enable_bidi = True Console and page errors driver.script.add_console_message_handler Lesson: WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back Table: webdriver classic · cdp · webdriver bidi WebDriver Classic CDP WebDriver BiDi Standard W3C Recommendation Chrome only, no stability promise W3C, in progress Browsers All Chrome and Edge Chrome, Edge and Firefox, growing Style Request and response over HTTP Commands and events over WebSocket Commands and events over WebSocket Use it for Every normal test step Chromium features with no other API Events, logs, network, and the future of Selenium Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol Table: endpoint · what it does Endpoint What it does GET /status Current state of the Grid and every node, with slots and sessions POST /graphql Ask for exactly the fields you need: grid, nodesInfo, sessionsInfo, session(id) DELETE /session/<id> End a session and free its slot POST /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id>/drain Stop a node after its running sessions end, with no new sessions. Needs the registration secret header DELETE /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id> Remove a node from the Distributor. Its running sessions continue Lesson: Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints Tips and traps Common mistake BiDi is still growing. The documentation lists log, network, script and browsing context features, and some network APIs are marked as not yet stable in some bindings, for example the Python docs example uses an underscore method for intercepts. Check the documentation page for your binding before you build on a feature.Lesson: WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back"],[21,"bidi-cdp","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","Remember The documentation calls out two popular CDP examples with limited value: emulated geolocation, because most sites use the IP address, and overriding device metrics, because Chrome mobile emulation in the options is the better tool.Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol"],[21,"step-7","Step 7Revise and practise","7.1"],[21,"revision","Revision: predict, debug and design","Open chapter Predict the output, find the bug, design and sizing questions, a cheat sheet in three languages and a mock quiz. Key ideas Predict, then run The driver does what the protocol says, not what the code seems to say. Offsets add, Shift changes case, a refresh makes references stale. Cause before fix Name why a test is slow or flaky before changing it. Sleeps, mixed waits and missing quit calls are the usual causes. Layers Tests, page objects and components, a driver factory and support code. Each change should touch one layer. Size with the formula Suite time = tests × average time ÷ nodes, with about one CPU and 1 GB of RAM per browser. Know your binding Time is a Duration in Java, seconds in Python and milliseconds in JavaScript, and a missing cookie gives null, None or an error. Numbers to remember Explicit wait pollExam favourite 500 ms The default check interval in Java and Python. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Page load timeout 300,000 ms Five minutes by default. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Script timeout 30,000 ms Thirty seconds by default. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Tables Table: question shape · what it looks like · what earns the marks · lesson Question shape What it looks like What earns the marks Lesson Predict the output A short program and the question: what does it print, or which error comes back Knowing what the driver really does, not what the code seems to say 2 Find the bug A test that is slow, flaky or fails, and the question: why, and how do you fix it Naming the cause first, then a fix that removes it 3 Design and sizing Plan a framework, size a Grid, triage a flaky suite Clear layers, numbers from a formula, a reason for each choice 4 Write it in another language The same step in Java, Python or JavaScript The right method name and the right unit for time 5 Lesson: How Selenium skills are tested Table: step · python Step Python Start Chrome webdriver.Chrome() Open a page driver.get(url) Find one element driver.find_element(By.ID, \"q\") Type el.send_keys(\"text\") Wait for an element WebDriverWait(driver, 5).until(EC.visibility_of_element_located((By.ID, \"q\"))) Into a frame driver.switch_to.frame(el) Back to the page driver.switch_to.default_content() Run a script driver.execute_script(\"return 1\") Add a cookie driver.add_cookie({{\"name\": \"k\", \"value\": \"v\"}}) Use a Grid webdriver.Remote(command_executor=\"http://localhost:4444\", options=webdriver.ChromeOptions()) End the session driver.quit() Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Tips and traps Remember Use the Revise lessons in order the first time, then use the Quiz and Worked problems tabs as a timed mock test. Each answer says which chapter to reread if you got it wrong.Lesson: How Selenium skills are tested Common mistake A good bug answer has three parts: what the code really does, why that breaks, and a fix that removes the cause. A retry that hides the failure is not a fix.Lesson: Find the bug"],[22,"","JavaScript cheatsheet","Every key idea, JavaScript example, table and common mistake from the Selenium chapters, with only JavaScript code. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · JavaScript cheatsheet · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · revision · PDF · download · JavaScript · WebDriver · locators · waits · Grid"],[22,"step-1","Step 1Meet Selenium","1.1"],[22,"what-is-selenium","What Selenium is and where it came from","Open chapter What Selenium does, its three parts, the key words, its history to the W3C standard and a first real program. Key ideas What Selenium is An umbrella project of tools and libraries that automate real browsers the way a user would. At its core, a browser user agent library. Three parts WebDriver (library plus browser driver, for code), IDE (record and play back extension), Grid (many sessions on many machines). Not a test framework Selenium drives the browser. Assertions, test structure and reports come from JUnit, pytest, Mocha and similar tools. Key dates Selenium 2.0 on 8 July 2011, 3.0 on 13 October 2016, W3C Recommendation on 5 June 2018, Selenium 4.0 on 13 October 2021. Program shape Start a session, send the browser somewhere, ask or act, and always quit at the end. Code A first look: open a page, read its title, close the browser const { Builder, Browser } = require('selenium-webdriver');Load the two pieces we need from the selenium-webdriver package. (asyncfunction firstLook() {Every Selenium call in JavaScript returns a promise, so we work inside an async function. const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();Build a driver for Chrome. Selenium Manager finds chromedriver and a session starts. await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');Open the page and wait for it to load. console.log('Title: ' + await driver.getTitle());Ask for the title. await waits for the browser to answer. console.log('URL: ' + await driver.getCurrentUrl());Ask which address the browser shows. await driver.quit();End the session and close the browser. Lesson: A first look at real code Tables Table: what people use selenium for · an everyday example What people use Selenium for An everyday example Testing web applications Before a bank releases a new version of its login page, a test opens the page in Chrome, Firefox and Edge, logs in with a test account and checks the balance appears. Repetitive tasks A script logs into a supplier portal every morning at 6 and downloads the order report for the day. Collecting data from pages A researcher reads prices from a site that has no API. The docs ask you to check the terms of service of the site first, because some sites forbid it and some block Selenium. Lesson: What Selenium is Table: part · what it is · use it when Part What it is Use it when WebDriver A library for your programming language plus a driver for each browser. Your code sends commands, the driver makes the browser carry them out. You write automated tests or scripts in Java, Python, JavaScript, C# or Ruby. This is what most of this subject teaches. IDE A Chrome and Firefox extension that records what you do in the browser and plays it back as Selenium commands. You want a quick recording, or you are learning which command matches which action. Grid A server that spreads WebDriver sessions over many machines, browsers and operating systems. One machine is too slow, or you must test on browser and operating system pairs you do not have. Lesson: WebDriver, IDE and Grid Table: word · meaning in the selenium documentation Word Meaning in the Selenium documentation API The set of commands you use to control WebDriver, such as get, findElement and click. Library The code module that holds those commands for one language: a .jar file for Java, a pip package for Python, an npm package for JavaScript. Driver The program that controls one browser, such as chromedriver for Chrome and geckodriver for Firefox. Browser vendors write most of them. It usually runs on the same machine as the browser. Framework An extra library that supports a test suite: test runners such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha, or tools such as Cucumber. Lesson: WebDriver, IDE and Grid Table: version · released · what changed Version Released What changed Selenium 2.0 8 July 2011 The WebDriver APIs became the heart of Selenium. Selenium 1 code still ran. Selenium 3.0 13 October 2016 The original Selenium Core"],[22,"what-is-selenium","What Selenium is and where it came from","implementation was removed. Every major browser vendor now ships its own driver. W3C WebDriver 5 June 2018 WebDriver became a W3C Recommendation, a standard every browser can implement the same way. Selenium 4.0 13 October 2021 Support for the old JSON Wire Protocol was removed. Only the W3C protocol is used. Relative locators and a rebuilt Grid arrived. Lesson: How Selenium grew Tips and traps Common mistake Selenium only controls the browser. It does not decide whether a test passed, it does not write reports and it does not give you Given, When, Then syntax. Those come from a test framework such as JUnit, pytest or Mocha. Chapter 5.1 joins the two.Lesson: What Selenium is Remember Always end with quit. If a program stops without it, the driver process and the browser keep running in the background and hold memory and ports. On a busy test machine those leftovers pile up quickly.Lesson: A first look at real code 1.2"],[22,"architecture","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","Open chapter How a command travels from your code to the browser over the W3C WebDriver protocol, shown with real HTTP traffic. Key ideas Three programs Your code and the binding (local end), the driver (remote end), the browser. The driver is written by the browser vendor. HTTP and JSON Every command is one HTTP request with a fixed method and path. Answers are {\"value\": ...} on success or {\"value\": {\"error\", \"message\", \"stacktrace\"}} on failure. Session id POST /session returns a session id. Every later path contains it. DELETE /session/{id} ends it. Element reference Elements travel as {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"id\"}. Bindings turn id, name and class name into CSS selectors. BiDi Classic WebDriver is request and response. WebDriver BiDi adds a WebSocket so the browser can send events. CDP access is temporary until BiDi covers it. Code Drive Chrome with plain HTTP and no Selenium library at all const { spawn } = require('child_process');For starting the chromedriver program. No Selenium package is loaded. asyncfunction call(method, path, body) {Send one W3C command and return the part of the answer under \"value\". const res = await fetch('http://localhost:9515' + path, {fetch is built into Node.js 18 and later. body: body === undefined ? undefined : JSON.stringify(body),POST bodies are JSON objects. GET and DELETE have none. console.log(method, path, ' -> ', text.length > 70 ? text.slice(0, 70) + '...' : text);Print the request and the start of the answer. const driver = spawn('chromedriver', ['--port=9515'], { stdio: 'ignore' });Start the driver yourself and hide its own messages. awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000));Give it a moment to open its port. constKEY = 'element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf';The fixed key for element references. const s = '/session/' + (await call('POST', '/session', { capabilities: { alwaysMatch: { browserName: 'chrome' } } })).sessionId;New Session. await call('POST', s + '/timeouts', { implicit: 2000 });Set Timeouts: Find Element may keep looking for 2 seconds while the next page loads. await call('POST', s + '/url', { url: 'https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html' });Navigate To. await call('GET', s + '/title');Get Title. const box = (await call('POST', s + '/element', { using: 'css selector', value: \"[name='my-text']\" }))[KEY];Find Element. await call('POST', s + '/element/' + box + '/value', { text: 'Selenium' });Element Send Keys. await call('POST', s + '/element/' + button + '/click', {});Element Click. await call('GET', s + '/element/' + message + '/text');Get Element Text. await call('DELETE', s);Delete Session. driver.kill();Stop chromedriver. Lesson: See the wire for yourself Numbers to remember SuccessExam favourite HTTP 200 and {\"value\": result} The result is null for commands that only act, a string for text and titles, a list for Find Elements. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol ErrorExam favourite HTTP 4xx or 5xx and {\"value\": {\"error\", \"message\", \"stacktrace\"}} error is a fixed code such as no such element. The binding turns it into an exception. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Element reference {\"element-6066-11e4-a52e-4f735466cecf\": \"f.C6…e.6\"} A web element travels as an object with this one fixed key. The value is an opaque id made by the driver. Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Tables Table: spec word · in practice · example Spec word In practice Example Local end The client side, usually a language library on top of the protocol selenium for Python, selenium-java, selenium-webdriver for npm Remote end The server side that carries out the commands chromedriver, or Selenium Grid Intermediary node A remote end that passes commands on to another remote end Selenium Grid, which forwards to a node Endpoint node The last remote end in the chain, built into or next to the browser chromedriver driving Chrome Lesson: The pieces between your code and the browser Table: command · method · path Command Method Path New Session POST /session Delete Session"],[22,"architecture","Architecture and the W3C WebDriver protocol","DELETE /session/{session id} Navigate To POST /session/{id}/url Get Title GET /session/{id}/title Find Element POST /session/{id}/element Find Elements POST /session/{id}/elements Element Click POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/click Element Send Keys POST /session/{id}/element/{element id}/value Get Element Text GET /session/{id}/element/{element id}/text Set Timeouts POST /session/{id}/timeouts Execute Script POST /session/{id}/execute/sync Take Screenshot GET /session/{id}/screenshot Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Table: error code · http status Error code HTTP status no such element 404 stale element reference 404 invalid session id 404 element not interactable 400 element click intercepted 400 invalid selector 400 timeout 500 unexpected alert open 500 session not created 500 Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Table: layout · where things run · code difference Layout Where things run Code difference Direct Your code, the driver and the browser on one machine. new ChromeDriver(), webdriver.Chrome() Remote Your code on one machine, driver and browser on another, reached through Selenium Server or RemoteWebDriver. new RemoteWebDriver(url, options), webdriver.Remote(url, options=...) Grid Selenium Grid receives the commands and relays them to the driver on a node machine. Same as remote, the URL points at the Grid Lesson: Local, remote and Grid, plus BiDi Tips and traps Remember The binding changes some locators before they leave your machine. Look for it in the log in the next lesson. That is why the W3C spec only needs five strategies: css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath.Lesson: The W3C WebDriver protocol Remember You will never write raw HTTP in real tests. The point is to see that nothing magic happens: Selenium is a polite HTTP client with good manners about waiting, errors and types. When a test fails with a strange message, picture the request that was sent and the error code that came back.Lesson: See the wire for yourself 1.3"],[22,"setup-first-script","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","Open chapter Install Selenium, let Selenium Manager match the driver, and write and tidy up your first real script. Key ideas Install JavaScript: npm install selenium-webdriver (Node 22+). Selenium Manager Ships with Selenium since 4.6. A fallback that discovers the browser version, resolves, downloads and caches the driver in ~/.cache/selenium, and since 4.11 can download browsers. Driver mismatch An old driver on PATH or given by hand gives session not created. Delete it and let Selenium Manager match the driver. Eight components Start session, act on browser, ask the browser, wait strategy, find element, act on element, ask the element, end session. Code The eight basic components of a Selenium script const { Builder, Browser, By } = require('selenium-webdriver');Builder makes drivers, Browser names browsers, By builds locators. const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();1. Start the session. Chrome opens with a fresh profile. await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');2. Take action on the browser: go to the practice form. const title = await driver.getTitle();3. Request browser information: the page title. await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit: 500 });4. Establish a waiting strategy. JavaScript takes milliseconds. const textBox = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));5. Find an element by its name attribute. const submitButton = await driver.findElement(By.css('button'));5. Find the first button with a CSS selector. await textBox.sendKeys('Selenium');6. Take action on an element: type into the box. await submitButton.click();6. Click Submit. The browser loads the next page. const message = await driver.findElement(By.id('message'));Find the message on the new page. const value = await message.getText();7. Request element information: its visible text. await driver.quit();8. End the session. The browser and the driver both close. Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Quit even when something fails const { Builder, Browser, By, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');error holds the exception classes. try {Everything that might fail goes inside try. await driver.findElement(By.id('no-such-id'));This element does not exist, so the promise rejects. if (!(e instanceof error.NoSuchElementError)) throw e;Only swallow the error we expected. Anything else is a real bug. } finally {finally runs whether the try worked or failed. await driver.quit();So the browser always closes. Lesson: Always end the session Tables Table: language · minimum version for selenium 4.51 · how you add selenium Language Minimum version for Selenium 4.51 How you add Selenium JavaScript Node.js 22 npm install selenium-webdriver Lesson: Install Selenium for your language Table: setting · default · what it does Setting Default What it does cache-path ~/.cache/selenium Where drivers, browsers and metadata are stored ttl 3600 seconds How long resolved versions are trusted before asking the vendor again offline false No network requests or downloads at all avoid-stats false Selenium Manager sends anonymous usage numbers to Plausible once a day. Set SE_AVOID_STATS=true to stop it. proxy none An HTTP proxy for the downloads Lesson: Selenium Manager finds the driver Table: step · what it means · in this example Step What it means In this example 1. Start the session Create a driver. A browser opens. Chrome 2. Take action on the browser Navigate, go back, resize and so on. Open the web form 3. Request browser information Title, address, window handles, cookies. Read the title 4. Establish a waiting strategy Make sure an element is present before you look for it. Implicit wait of 500 ms 5. Find an element Most commands are about elements. The text box and the button 6. Take action on an element Type, click, clear. Type Selenium, click Submit 7. Request element information Text, attributes, size, state. Read the message 8. End the session Quit the driver and close the browser. quit Lesson: The"],[22,"setup-first-script","Setup, Selenium Manager and your first script","eight steps of a first script Table: quit · close quit close Closes Every window and tab of the session Only the current window or tab Driver process Stopped Keeps running while other windows are open After it The driver object cannot be used again Switch to another window to carry on Lesson: Always end the session Tips and traps Remember Use a virtual environment in Python and a package.json in JavaScript. Each project then pins its own Selenium version, so upgrading one project never breaks another.Lesson: Install Selenium for your language Common mistake If you pass a driver path yourself, Selenium trusts you and never calls Selenium Manager. The documentation says that with Selenium 4.6 or newer you should not need to set a driver location at all. Delete old drivers from PATH and let Selenium Manager do the job.Lesson: Selenium Manager finds the driver Common mistake The documentation itself warns that an implicit wait is rarely the best solution. It is used here because it is the easiest to show. Chapter 4.1 replaces it with explicit waits.Lesson: The eight steps of a first script Remember In a test framework the setup and teardown hooks do this job: start the driver before each test and quit it after each test, pass or fail. Chapter 5.1 shows the hooks in JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha.Lesson: Always end the session"],[22,"step-2","Step 2Control the browser","2.1"],[22,"sessions-options","Sessions, options and timeouts","Open chapter Options and capabilities, page load strategy, the three session timeouts and the driver service, shown with real runs. Key ideas Options Since Selenium 4, describe sessions with Options classes. They become W3C capabilities, and browser specific ones carry a prefix such as goog:chromeOptions. Page load strategy normal waits for complete (load), eager for interactive (DOMContentLoaded), none for the first download. It covers navigation by address, not clicks. Default timeouts Implicit 0, page load 300,000 ms, script 30,000 ms. Service Starts and stops a local driver: file, port, arguments and logs. Not used for remote sessions. Granted capabilities Read them after New Session to learn the real browser version, platform and defaults such as dismiss and notify. Code Ask for a session with options, then read what the browser agreed to const chrome = require('selenium-webdriver/chrome');The Chrome module holds chrome.Options. const options = new chrome.Options();An Options object describes the session you want. options.addArguments('--headless=new', '--window-size=1280,800');Chrome command line switches: no window, and a fixed size. options.setPageLoadStrategy('eager');A W3C capability: get() returns at DOMContentLoaded. const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(options).build();New Session is sent with these capabilities. const caps = await driver.getCapabilities();The capabilities the driver actually granted. console.log(key + ': ' + caps.get(key));caps.get reads one capability by name. Lesson: Options and capabilities Read the three session timeouts, then trip one on purpose console.log(await driver.manage().getTimeouts());One object with all three timeouts, in milliseconds. await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ script: 1000 });Allow scripts only one second from now on. await driver.executeAsyncScript('/* never calls its callback */');An async script must call its callback. This one never does. if (!(e instanceof error.ScriptTimeoutError)) throw e;The W3C error script timeout becomes ScriptTimeoutError. Lesson: The three session timeouts Start the driver on a port you choose and ask if it is ready const service = new chrome.ServiceBuilder().setPort(9600);A ServiceBuilder describes how to start chromedriver: here, on port 9600. const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeService(service).build();Selenium starts chromedriver on that port, then opens a session. const reply = await fetch('http://localhost:9600/status');GET /status is a W3C command that needs no session. fetch is built into Node. console.log('ready: ' + status.ready);The driver says whether it can start new sessions. await driver.quit();Ends the session and stops the service. Lesson: The driver service Numbers to remember Implicit wait 0 How long Find Element keeps looking before it gives up with no such element. Zero means it checks once. Lesson: The three session timeouts Page loadExam favourite 300,000 ms Five minutes. How long a navigation may take before a timeout error. Lesson: The three session timeouts Script 30,000 ms Thirty seconds. How long an injected script may run before the script timeout error. Lesson: The three session timeouts Tables Table: capability · what it controls · default Capability What it controls Default browserName Which browser. Set for you by each Options class. set by the class browserVersion Which version. Selenium Manager can download it if missing. whatever is installed platformName The operating system at the remote end. Matters most on Grid and in the cloud. the local OS pageLoadStrategy When navigation commands return: normal, eager or none. normal acceptInsecureCerts Trust expired or self signed TLS certificates. false timeouts Script, page load and implicit wait. 30 s, 300 s, 0 unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to an alert your code did not handle. dismiss and notify proxy Send browser traffic through a proxy server. none setWindowRect Whether the"],[22,"sessions-options","Sessions, options and timeouts","browser supports resizing and moving windows. reported by the driver strictFileInteractability Strict checks for hidden file inputs. off Lesson: Options and capabilities Table: chrome switch · what it does Chrome switch What it does --headless=new No visible window. Common on build servers. --start-maximized Open the window maximised. --window-size=1280,800 A fixed window size, so pages lay out the same on every machine. --user-data-dir=... Use a chosen profile folder instead of a fresh temporary one. Lesson: Options and capabilities Table: strategy · waits until readystate is · browser event · use it when Strategy Waits until readyState is Browser event Use it when normal (default) complete load: every image, font and style sheet is in You want the safest behaviour eager interactive DOMContentLoaded: the HTML is parsed, images may still load Large images or ads slow the page and the test does not need them none any only the first download of the page You control every wait yourself Lesson: Page load strategy Table: language · set the page load timeout to 30 s Language Set the page load timeout to 30 s JavaScript await driver.manage().setTimeouts({{ pageLoad: 30000 }}) Lesson: The three session timeouts Table: service setting · why you would change it Service setting Why you would change it Driver location Only with Selenium older than 4.6 or a special setup. Otherwise let Selenium Manager find it. Port A firewall allows only certain ports, or you want to talk to the driver yourself. Log output and level Driver logs are thrown away unless you send them somewhere. Levels are ALL, DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, SEVERE and OFF. Arguments Pass chromedriver switches, such as appending to a log file. Lesson: The driver service Tips and traps Remember Each Options class can also take the binary path of a Chromium based browser, extensions, and excludeSwitches to turn off a default switch of chromedriver. The documentation lists them on the Chrome page. Use them only when you need them, because every extra setting is one more difference from what real users run.Lesson: Options and capabilities Common mistake Switching to eager or none to make tests faster is a trade. Every element that arrives later now needs an explicit wait, or the test becomes flaky. Chapter 4.1 shows how to write those waits.Lesson: Page load strategy In the exam These timeouts belong to the browser session. Explicit waits such as WebDriverWait are different: they run in your code and poll the browser. Mixing an implicit wait with explicit waits can give surprising wait times, which chapter 4.1 explains.Lesson: The three session timeouts 2.2"],[22,"navigate-screenshots","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","Open chapter Navigation and history, title and URL, window size and position, screenshots and printing pages to PDF. Key ideas Navigation get or navigate().to, back, forward, refresh. Where am I Title and current URL. Check them when a test depends on a redirect or on history, because back on the first page raises no error. Window rect Set and get size and position. An exact size is more portable than maximize, which needs a window manager. Screenshots Take Screenshot (viewport) and Take Element Screenshot return base64 PNG. Save one when a test fails, before quit. Print Page Returns a base64 PDF. Use PrintOptions for orientation, ranges, size, margins and scale. Chromium needs headless. Code Go somewhere, go back, go forward, reload await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/web-form.html');Navigate To. Waits for the page load strategy. await driver.navigate().to('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/xhtmlTest.html');The long form of get. await driver.navigate().back();Press the Back button. await driver.navigate().forward();Press the Forward button. await driver.navigate().refresh();Reload the current page. Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Set the window size and position, then read them back const window = driver.manage().window();Window commands live under manage().window(). await window.setRect({ width: 1024, height: 768, x: 40, y: 30 });JavaScript sets size and position in one call to Set Window Rect. const { width, height, x, y } = await window.getRect();Get Window Rect returns all four numbers. Lesson: Window size and position Save a screenshot of the page and of one element const pngSize = file => { const b = fs.readFileSync(file); return [b.readUInt32BE(16), b.readUInt32BE(20)]; };A PNG keeps width and height at bytes 16 and 20. const page = await driver.takeScreenshot();Take Screenshot answers with the PNG as base64 text. fs.writeFileSync('page.png', page, 'base64');Decode the base64 and write the bytes to a file. fs.writeFileSync('button.png', await button.takeScreenshot(), 'base64');Take Element Screenshot: just the area of the button. Lesson: Screenshots Print the page to a PDF file const options = new chrome.Options().addArguments('--headless=new');Chromium prints to PDF only in headless mode. const base64 = await driver.printPage({ orientation: 'landscape' });The W3C Print Page command answers with base64 text. const pdf = Buffer.from(base64, 'base64');Decode it to the real bytes. console.log('page.pdf starts with ' + pdf.subarray(0, 5).toString());Every PDF file starts with %PDF-. Lesson: Print a page to PDF Tables Table: what you want · javascript What you want JavaScript Open an address driver.get(url) or navigate().to(url) Back navigate().back() Forward navigate().forward() Reload navigate().refresh() Page title getTitle() Current address getCurrentUrl() Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Table: command · what it does Command What it does Set window size Restores the window if it was maximised, then sets width and height in pixels Set window position Moves the top left corner to x and y on the screen Get window rect Width, height, x and y in one answer Maximize Makes the window fill the screen, without covering the system menus Minimize Hides the window, usually to the taskbar. The exact result depends on the window manager. Selenium 4 and later. Fullscreen Fills the whole screen, like pressing F11 Lesson: Window size and position Table: language · page screenshot to file · element screenshot to file Language Page screenshot to file Element screenshot to file JavaScript driver.takeScreenshot() gives base64 element.takeScreenshot() gives base64 Lesson: Screenshots Table: printoptions setting · example PrintOptions setting Example Orientation portrait or landscape Page ranges \"2-4\" Page size A4, Legal and others Margins top, bottom, left, right Scale 1.0 is 100 %, 0.25 is 25 % Background whether background colours and images print Shrink to fit whether wide content is shrunk to the"],[22,"navigate-screenshots","Navigation, window size, screenshots and PDF","page Lesson: Print a page to PDF Tips and traps Common mistake After refresh or any navigation, the elements you found before belong to the old page. Using them raises stale element reference. Find them again on the new page. Chapter 5.4 covers this error in depth.Lesson: Navigate and ask where you are Common mistake Maximize and minimize need a desktop window manager. On a bare Linux build server they can fail with an unknown error, which is what happened when this page was built. Setting an exact size with Set Window Rect or the --window-size switch works everywhere and gives the same layout on every machine.Lesson: Window size and position Remember Take a screenshot in the teardown of a failing test, before quit. Name the file after the test, so the report can link to it. Chapter 5.1 wires this into JUnit, pytest and Mocha.Lesson: Screenshots"],[22,"step-3","Step 3Find and use elements","3.1"],[22,"locators","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","Open chapter All eight locator strategies, CSS and XPath, relative locators and how to choose a locator that lasts. Key ideas Eight strategies id, name, class name, css selector, xpath, tag name, link text, partial link text. find element returns the first match. Five on the wire W3C defines css selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and xpath. Bindings turn id, name and class name into CSS. CSS and XPath CSS: #id, .class, tag[attr=\"v\"], parent > child. XPath adds text matching and moving up to a parent. Prefer relative XPath (//). Relative locators above, below, toLeftOf, toRightOf and near (50 px), built on getBoundingClientRect. They depend on layout, so check what they return. Preference Unique stable id, then a short CSS selector, then XPath. Avoid absolute paths, styling classes and generated ids. Consider data-test attributes. Code All eight locator strategies on the documentation example page asyncfunction show(how, e) {A helper that prints which element a locator found. await show('className', await driver.findElement(By.className('information')));The first element whose class list contains information. await show('css', await driver.findElement(By.css('#fname')));CSS: # means id, so the input with id fname. await show('id', await driver.findElement(By.id('lname')));The element whose id attribute is lname. await show('name', await driver.findElement(By.name('newsletter')));The element whose name attribute is newsletter. await show('linkText', await driver.findElement(By.linkText('Selenium Official Page')));A link whose whole visible text matches. await show('partialLinkText', await driver.findElement(By.partialLinkText('Official')));A link whose visible text contains Official. await show('tagName', await driver.findElement(By.tagName('a')));The first a element on the page. await show('xpath', await driver.findElement(By.xpath(\"//input[@value='f']\")));XPath: any input whose value attribute is f. Lesson: The eight locator strategies Relative locators: find an element by where it sits const { Builder, Browser, By, locateWith } = require('selenium-webdriver');locateWith() starts a relative locator. console.log(how + ' -> ' + await e.getAttribute('outerHTML'));outerHTML shows the whole tag that was found. await show('below last name label', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).below(By.css(\"label[for='lname']\"))));An input below the Last name label. The closest one wins. await show('above lname', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).above(By.id('lname'))));An input above the lname box. await show('right of Male', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).toRightOf(By.css(\"input[value='m']\"))));An input to the right of the Male radio. await show('below fname and above the checkbox', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).below(By.id('fname')).above(By.name('newsletter'))));Chained: both conditions must hold. await show('near Newsletter label', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).near(By.css(\"label[for='newsletter']\"))));near means at most 50 pixels away. Look at what it really returned. await show('left of password', await driver.findElement(locateWith(By.tagName('input')).toLeftOf(By.id('password-field'))));On the login page the three inputs sit in one row. Lesson: Relative locators Tables Table: strategy · finds · example Strategy Finds Example id The element whose id attribute matches lname name Elements whose name attribute matches newsletter class name Elements whose class list contains the value. Compound names such as \"a b\" are not allowed. information css selector Elements matching a CSS selector #fname xpath Elements matching an XPath expression //input[@value='f'] tag name Elements with that tag a link text Links whose visible text is exactly the value Selenium Official Page partial link text Links whose visible text contains the value Official Lesson: The eight locator strategies Table: to find · css selector ·"],[22,"locators","Locators: how Selenium finds an element","xpath To find CSS selector XPath By id #fname //*[@id=\"fname\"] By class .information //*[contains(@class,\"information\")] Tag with attribute input[name=\"fname\"] //input[@name=\"fname\"] Attribute starts with a[href^=\"https\"] //a[starts-with(@href,\"https\")] Attribute contains a[href*=\"selenium\"] //a[contains(@href,\"selenium\")] Direct child form > input //form/input Any descendant form input //form//input Second matching input input:nth-of-type(2) (//input)[2] By visible text not possible //label[text()=\"Last name:\"] The parent not possible //input[@id=\"lname\"]/.. Next sibling label + input //label[@for=\"lname\"]/following-sibling::input[1] Lesson: CSS selectors and XPath Table: method · finds an element that is · note Method Finds an element that is Note above higher on the page than the anchor below lower on the page than the anchor toLeftOf / to_left_of left of the anchor toRightOf / to_right_of right of the anchor near at most 50 pixels from the anchor Useful for an input next to its label Lesson: Relative locators Table: fragile locator · why it breaks · better Fragile locator Why it breaks Better /html/body/div[2]/form/input[3] Any new div or input shifts the path #lname .btn.btn-primary.mt-3 Styling classes change when the design changes [data-test=\"submit\"] //button[text()=\"Submit\"] Breaks when the text is translated or reworded An id or a data attribute #ember1234 Generated ids change on every page load A stable attribute near it Lesson: Choosing a good locator Tips and traps Remember find element (singular) returns the first match or raises no such element. find elements (plural) returns every match in page order, or an empty list. Chapter 3.2 covers the difference in detail.Lesson: The eight locator strategies Common mistake Each relative locator sends the page a script to run, so it is slower than a plain CSS selector. Prefer an id or a short CSS selector, and keep relative locators for elements with no good attributes.Lesson: Relative locators In the exam Keep locators in one place, apart from the steps that use them. The documentation recommends declaring them separately, which is exactly what a page object does. Chapter 5.2 shows the pattern.Lesson: Choosing a good locator 3.2"],[22,"finding-elements","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","Open chapter Singular and plural finds, scoped searches and the XPath trap, shadow DOM and the active element. Key ideas One or many find element returns the first match or raises no such element. find elements returns all matches or an empty list. Search context You can search from the driver, from an element or from a shadow root. CSS from an element stays inside it. XPath trap An XPath starting with // always starts at the document, even when called on an element. Use .// to search below the element. Shadow DOM Find the host, get its shadow root, then search with a CSS selector. Chrome refuses XPath there. Active element switchTo().activeElement() returns the focused element. Use it to test keyboard flow such as Tab order. Code One element, all elements, and what happens when nothing matches const inputs = await driver.findElements(By.tagName('input'));Plural: an array of every input, in page order. for (const e of inputs) {for...of with await inside, so the questions are asked one by one. const none = await driver.findElements(By.id('email'));No match: an empty array, never an error. await driver.findElement(By.id('email'));Singular with no match: the promise rejects. Lesson: Find one or find many Search inside one element, and the XPath trap const form = await driver.findElement(By.css('form'));First find the parent element. console.log('inputs in form: ' + (await form.findElements(By.css('input'))).length);Called on the form, CSS only looks inside it. console.log('links via //a: ' + (await form.findElements(By.xpath('//a'))).length);The trap: // starts at the top of the document, so the link outside the form is found. console.log('links via .//a: ' + (await form.findElements(By.xpath('.//a'))).length);The dot means start here. Now nothing outside the form counts. const last = await form.findElement(By.id('lname'));Two commands: find the form, then find inside it. const same = await driver.findElement(By.css('form #lname'));The optimised version: one CSS selector, one command. console.log('same element: ' + awaitWebElement.equals(last, same));Both references point at the same element. Lesson: Search inside an element Reach into a shadow root console.log('from the page: ' + (await driver.findElements(By.css(box))).length);The checkbox lives in a shadow tree, so a normal search finds nothing. const host = await driver.findElement(By.css('custom-checkbox-element'));The shadow host: the element the hidden tree is attached to. const shadowRoot = await host.getShadowRoot();Get Element Shadow Root. const checkbox = await shadowRoot.findElement(By.css(box));Search inside the shadow tree. await shadowRoot.findElement(By.xpath('.//input'));XPath inside a shadow root. console.log('xpath in shadow root: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]);Chrome refuses it. Lesson: The shadow DOM Which element has the focus? console.log('at start: ' + await (await driver.switchTo().activeElement()).getTagName());Nothing is focused yet, so the active element is the body. await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text')).click();Clicking a text box gives it the focus. await (await driver.switchTo().activeElement()).sendKeys(Key.TAB);Press Tab on whatever has the focus, as a user would. console.log('after Tab: ' + await (await driver.switchTo().activeElement()).getAttribute('name'));The focus moved to the next field. Lesson: The active element Tables Table: find element (singular) · find elements (plural) find element (singular) find elements (plural) Returns One element: the first match in page order A list of every match, in page order No match Raises no such element Returns an empty list W3C command Find Element Find Elements Use it for An element that must be there Counting, looping, or checking that something is absent Lesson: Find one or find many Tips and traps Common mistake find elements never waits for the first element to appear unless an implicit wait is set, and then it returns as soon as at least one matches. An empty list can mean the page is not"],[22,"finding-elements","Finding elements: one, many, scoped and in shadow DOM","ready yet. Chapter 4.1 shows how to wait properly.Lesson: Find one or find many Remember Searching inside a container is still very useful when the page repeats a pattern: the Delete button inside the third row of a table, or the price inside one product card. Find the row or card first, then the button inside it.Lesson: Search inside an element Remember A shadow root can contain another shadow host. Then you repeat the two steps: get that host, get its shadow root, search again. Each level is one more pair of commands.Lesson: The shadow DOM 3.3"],[22,"interacting","Interacting with elements and reading them","Open chapter Click, type, clear, the checks before every action and their errors, element information, select lists and uploads. Key ideas Five commands click, send keys, clear, submit and select. Prefer clicking the submit button over submit(). send keys appends, so clear first. Built in checks Before acting the driver scrolls the element into view and checks it is interactable. Errors: element not interactable, invalid element state, element click intercepted. Element information isDisplayed, isEnabled, isSelected, tag name, rect, CSS value and text. isDisplayed is a JavaScript approximation. Attribute vs property DOM attribute is the HTML, DOM property is the live value. The classic getAttribute mixes both. Select and upload Select works only on select elements: by visible text, value or index. Upload by sending the full path to input type=\"file\". Code Fill in a form: clear, type, tick and click await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit: 2000 });As in chapter 1.3: let finds retry for up to 2 seconds, so the page after Submit has time to load. await text.sendKeys('first try');Element Send Keys: types into the box, adding to what is already there. await text.clear();Element Clear: empties the box. await text.sendKeys('Selenium');Type the real value. console.log('check 2 before: ' + await check2.isSelected());isSelected reads whether a checkbox is ticked. await check2.click();Element Click: ticks it, as a mouse click would. await driver.findElement(By.id('my-radio-2')).click();Clicking the second radio button unselects the first one. await driver.findElement(By.css('button')).click();Click Submit. The documentation prefers this to the submit() method. Lesson: Click, type and clear The three errors interactions give, made on purpose asyncfunction attempt(what, step) {Run one step and report the error it raises, if any. if (!(e instanceof error.WebDriverError)) throw e;Every Selenium error extends WebDriverError. await attempt('type into disabled', async () => (await driver.findElement(By.name('my-disabled'))).sendKeys('x'));A disabled input cannot take keyboard input. await attempt('clear readonly', async () => (await driver.findElement(By.name('my-readonly'))).clear());A readonly input cannot be reset. await driver.executeScript(Add a cookie banner that covers the bottom of the page, as many real sites do. await attempt('click under banner', async () => (await driver.findElement(By.css('button'))).click());The banner sits over the centre of the button. Lesson: Click, type and clear Ask an element about itself await text.sendKeys('hello');Type, so the live value differs from the HTML. console.log('disabled box enabled: ' + await driver.findElement(By.name('my-disabled')).isEnabled());The disabled input reports false. const rect = await text.getRect();Get Element Rect: position and size in pixels. console.log('getAttribute(value): ' + await text.getAttribute('value'));The classic method: property first, then attribute. console.log('getDomAttribute(value): ' + await text.getDomAttribute('value'));Only what the HTML says. The HTML has no value attribute. console.log('getProperty(value): ' + await text.getProperty('value'));The live JavaScript property: what is typed now. console.log('getDomAttribute(myprop): ' + await text.getDomAttribute('myprop'));A made up attribute in the HTML. console.log('button colour: ' + await driver.findElement(By.css('button')).getCssValue('color'));The computed CSS value. console.log('heading text: ' + await driver.findElement(By.css('h1')).getText());The rendered, visible text. Lesson: Read information about an element Work with single and multiple select lists const single = newSelect(await driver.findElement(By.name('selectomatic')));Wrap a select element in a Select object. console.log('options: ' + (await single.getOptions()).length);getOptions lists every option element. await single.selectByVisibleText('Four');Pick by the text the user sees. await single.selectByValue('two');Pick by the value"],[22,"interacting","Interacting with elements and reading them","attribute. await single.selectByIndex(0);Pick by position, counting from 0. const multi = newSelect(await driver.findElement(By.id('multi')));A select with the multiple attribute. console.log('multi starts with: ' + (await multi.getAllSelectedOptions()).length + ' selected');Eggs and Sausages are selected in the HTML. await multi.deselectByValue('eggs');Only multiple lists can deselect. Lesson: Select lists Upload a file without the file dialog fs.writeFileSync('report.txt', 'quarterly numbers');Make a small file to upload. const input = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-file'));The input with type=\"file\". await input.sendKeys(path.resolve('report.txt'));Send the full path. No dialog opens, the file is simply attached. console.log('browser shows: ' + await input.getProperty('value'));Browsers hide the real folder and show a made up folder called fakepath. Lesson: File upload Tables Table: command · works on · watch out for Command Works on Watch out for click Any element It clicks the centre of the element. If something else is on top there, you get element click intercepted. send keys Text fields and other keyboard interactable elements It adds to the existing text. Clear first if you want to replace it. clear Editable, resettable elements: text inputs and content editable Readonly and disabled inputs give invalid element state. submit Form elements In Selenium 4 it runs a script, so the documentation recommends clicking the submit button instead. select select and option elements Use the Select class, covered below. Lesson: Click, type and clear Table: question · javascript Question JavaScript Is it displayed? isDisplayed() Is it enabled? isEnabled() Is it selected? isSelected() Tag name getTagName() Position and size getRect() Computed CSS getCssValue(name) Visible text getText() Lesson: Read information about an element Table: method · reads · use it for Method Reads Use it for get DOM attribute Only the attribute in the HTML Checking what the server sent, such as a data-test value get DOM property Only the live JavaScript property What is in a field now, whether a box is checked now get attribute (classic) The property if there is one, otherwise the attribute. Selenium does this with a JavaScript atom Old code. Prefer the two precise methods. Lesson: Read information about an element Table: rule from the documentation · what it means for you Rule from the documentation What it means for you Select only works on select and option elements Drop downs built from div or li with JavaScript need normal clicks instead Since Selenium 4.5 a Select cannot be created on a disabled select Check isEnabled first if the list may be disabled Disabled options cannot be selected Trying raises an error Only multiple lists can deselect deselect on a single list raises an error Lesson: Select lists Tips and traps In the exam isDisplayed is not defined by the W3C spec, because deciding what a person can see covers too many cases. The documentation explains that Selenium runs a large JavaScript function that makes approximations. Treat it as a good guide, not a perfect one.Lesson: Read information about an element Remember When the test runs on a Grid, the file must reach the remote machine. Chapter 6.1 shows it.Lesson: File upload"],[22,"interacting","Step 4Synchronise and control","4.1"],[22,"waits","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","Open chapter Why tests race the page, implicit and explicit waits, expected conditions, custom waits and why not to mix them. Key ideas The race Content added or shown by JavaScript after load is not covered by navigation waits. Finding it too early fails. Do not use fixed sleeps. Implicit wait A session setting, default 0. Every find keeps looking up to that long and returns as soon as the element appears. It only waits for presence. Explicit wait A loop with a condition, timeout and polling interval. JavaScript waits forever without a timeout. Expected conditions presence, visibility, clickable, text present, title contains, staleness. Wait for visibility before typing. Never mix Implicit plus explicit makes waits unpredictable: 10 s with 15 s can time out after about 20 s. Keep implicit at 0 and use explicit waits. Code The race: look for the box straight after the click await driver.findElement(By.id('adder')).click();The page adds a red box, but only 1 second after the click. await driver.findElement(By.id('box0'));Our code asks at once. No wait is set, so the driver checks only once. console.log('too early: no such element');Selenium was faster than the page. Lesson: The race between your code and the page Implicit wait: every find keeps looking await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ implicit: 2000 });For the whole session: a find may keep looking for up to 2 seconds. const box = await driver.findElement(By.id('box0'));Not there yet, so the driver keeps checking until the box appears. console.log('found ' + await box.getAttribute('class') + ' after about ' + Math.round(seconds) + ' s');Returns as soon as it is found, not after the full 2 seconds. Lesson: Implicit waits Explicit wait: wait for exactly the condition you need const revealed = await driver.findElement(By.id('revealed'));The input is in the page already, just hidden. await driver.findElement(By.id('reveal')).click();The page shows it 1 second later. await driver.wait(() => revealed.isDisplayed(), 2000);Keep calling the function until it is true, for at most 2000 ms. JavaScript checks every 200 ms. await revealed.sendKeys('Displayed');Safe now: the input is displayed, so it can take keys. Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions Ready made conditions, and what a timeout looks like const { Builder, Browser, By, until, error } = require('selenium-webdriver');until holds the ready made conditions in JavaScript. const box = await driver.wait(until.elementLocated(By.id('box0')), 2000);A ready made condition. It resolves to the element. await driver.wait(until.elementIsVisible(field), 2000);Present is not enough for typing. Wait until it is visible. await driver.wait(until.elementLocated(By.id('box1')), 1000, 'the second box never came');Nobody clicked again, so box1 never appears. The third argument is our message. Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions A customised wait: poll faster and retry on an error await driver.wait(async () => {JavaScript has no ignore list, so the condition catches the error itself. returntrue;Worked: the wait ends. if (e instanceof error.ElementNotInteractableError) returnfalse;Not yet: the wait tries again. throw e;Any other error is a real problem. }, 2000, 'the input never became usable', 300);Timeout 2000 ms, our message, and poll every 300 ms. Lesson: Customising waits, and never mixing them Tables Table: strength · weakness Strength Weakness One line covers every find in the session It only waits for presence. A present element can still be hidden, covered or disabled Finds return as soon as the element appears Every check that something is absent now takes the full wait Easy to show in a first script It cannot wait for text, a title, a count or anything that is not a find Lesson: Implicit waits Table: default · javascript driver.wait Default JavaScript driver.wait Polling interval 200 ms Errors ignored while looping none, catch them yourself Timeout 0, which means wait forever. Always pass one Error at the end"],[22,"waits","Waiting strategies: implicit, explicit and fluent","TimeoutError Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions Table: wait for · javascript Wait for JavaScript Element exists until.elementLocated Element visible until.elementIsVisible Element clickable until.elementIsEnabled plus visible Text present until.elementTextContains Title contains until.titleContains Element gone stale until.stalenessOf Lesson: Explicit waits and expected conditions 4.2"],[22,"windows-frames-alerts","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","Open chapter Switching windows and tabs, working inside iframes, and handling alerts, confirms and prompts. Key ideas Windows Each window or tab has a handle. Save the original, switch to the new one, and switch back after close(). newWindow(\"tab\") opens and switches in one step. Frames Switch into an iframe by element, name or id, or index before finding inside it. defaultContent goes to the top, parentFrame up one level. Title in frames Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. Alerts alert, confirm and prompt: getText, accept, dismiss, sendKeys. Wait with alert is present when it appears late. Forgotten pop ups unhandledPromptBehavior defaults to dismiss and notify: the pop up is closed and the next command raises unexpected alert open. Code Follow a link into a new window, come back, open a new tab const original = await driver.getWindowHandle();The id of the current window. It stays the same for the whole session. await driver.findElement(By.name('windowTwo')).click();This link has target=\"_blank\", so it opens a new window. await driver.wait(async () => (await driver.getAllWindowHandles()).length === 2, 2000);Wait until the second window exists. for (const handle ofawait driver.getAllWindowHandles()) {Every open window, in the order they were opened. if (handle !== original) await driver.switchTo().window(handle);Switch to the one that is not the original. await driver.close();Close only this window. await driver.switchTo().window(original);Switch back, or the next command fails with no such window. await driver.switchTo().newWindow('tab');Selenium 4: open a new tab and switch to it in one step. await driver.quit();Closes every window and tab at once. Lesson: Windows and tabs Switch into an iframe three ways and back out console.log('email boxes seen from the top: ' + (await driver.findElements(By.id('email'))).length);The form lives inside the iframe, so the top document sees none. await driver.switchTo().frame(frame);1. Switch with a WebElement: the most flexible way. await driver.switchTo().defaultContent();Leave the frame: back to the top document. await driver.switchTo().frame(await driver.findElement(By.name('iframe1-name')));2. JavaScript has no switch by name string, so find the frame by its name first. await driver.switchTo().frame(0);3. Switch with an index: the first frame of the page. console.log('title inside the frame: ' + await driver.getTitle());Get Title always reports the top level page, even from inside a frame. console.log('heading: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('iframe_page_heading')).getText());Outside elements are reachable again. Lesson: Frames and iframes Alert, confirm, prompt, a slow alert and one left open await driver.findElement(By.id('alert')).click();The page calls alert('cheese'). const alert = await driver.wait(until.alertIsPresent(), 2000);Wait for the alert, then get a handle on it. await alert.accept();Press OK. await driver.findElement(By.id('confirm')).click();A confirm box: OK or Cancel. const confirm = await driver.switchTo().alert();Switch to it without waiting, because it opens at once. await confirm.dismiss();Press Cancel. await driver.findElement(By.id('prompt')).click();A prompt asks for text. await prompt.sendKeys('Selenium');Type the answer into the prompt. console.log('page shows: ' + await driver.findElement(By.id('text')).getText());The page wrote our answer into a paragraph. await driver.findElement(By.id('slow-alert')).click();This one opens 200 ms later. const slow = await driver.wait(until.alertIsPresent(), 2000);switchTo().alert() now would fail, so wait. await driver.findElement(By.id('alert')).click();Open one more alert and forget about it. await driver.getTitle();Any command while an alert is open. console.log('left open: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]);The default behaviour dismisses it and reports an error. Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Tables Table: what you want · javascript What you want JavaScript Current handle"],[22,"windows-frames-alerts","Windows, tabs, frames and alerts","getWindowHandle() All handles getAllWindowHandles() Switch switchTo().window(h) New tab or window switchTo().newWindow(\"tab\") Close current close() Lesson: Windows and tabs Table: switch by · example · when to use Switch by Example When to use WebElement switchTo().frame(element) Most flexible. Find the frame with any locator. Name or id switchTo().frame(\"iframe1-name\") The frame has a stable name or id. If several match, the first one is used. Java and Python accept the string. JavaScript needs the element. Index switchTo().frame(0) Position in window.frames. Breaks if frames are added. Leave all frames switchTo().defaultContent() Back to the top document. Up one level switchTo().parentFrame() For frames inside frames. Lesson: Frames and iframes Table: pop up · buttons · what you can do Pop up Buttons What you can do alert OK Read the text, accept confirm OK and Cancel Read the text, accept or dismiss prompt OK and Cancel, plus a text box Read the text, send keys, accept or dismiss. Sending keys replaces the placeholder text Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Table: unhandledpromptbehavior · what happens to a forgotten pop up unhandledPromptBehavior What happens to a forgotten pop up dismiss and notify (default) Dismissed, and the command raises unexpected alert open accept and notify Accepted, and the command raises unexpected alert open dismiss Dismissed silently accept Accepted silently ignore Left open, and the command raises unexpected alert open Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts Tips and traps Common mistake After close() you are still pointing at the closed window. The documentation warns that the next command raises no such window. Always switch back to a handle you saved before.Lesson: Windows and tabs Remember Recent drivers dismiss beforeunload prompts, the \"Leave site?\" box, by default. Chapter 2.1 showed where unhandledPromptBehavior is set.Lesson: Alerts, confirms and prompts 4.3"],[22,"actions","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","Open chapter Mouse, keyboard and wheel actions, and how a chain becomes ticks. Key ideas Build then perform An action chain is sent in one request when perform() is called. Nothing happens before that. Ticks Each input source has one action per tick. A tick lasts as long as its slowest action. Mouse doubleClick, contextClick, moveToElement, clickAndHold, dragAndDrop, moveByOffset. The driver remembers the pointer position between chains. Keyboard keyDown and keyUp for modifiers, sendKeys for text. Use Command on a Mac and Control elsewhere. Release every key you press. Wheel scrollToElement, scrollByAmount, scrollFromOrigin. Wait for the scroll result, because it can land after perform() returns. Code Double click, right click, hover, drag and drop, and move to a point await driver.actions().doubleClick(box).perform();Build a chain, then perform() sends it to the browser in one request. await driver.actions().contextClick(box).perform();A right click. await driver.actions().move({ origin: await driver.findElement(By.id('hover')) }).perform();Move to the centre of the element. Nothing is clicked. await driver.actions().dragAndDrop(from, to).perform();Move to from, press, move to to, release. await driver.actions().move({ x: 8, y: 12 }).perform();With no origin, x and y are measured from the top left of the viewport. await driver.actions().move({ x: 13, y: 15, origin: Origin.POINTER }).perform();From wherever the pointer is now. The driver remembered it between chains. Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Hold Shift, select all with the right modifier, and type into an element .keyDown(Key.SHIFT)Press Shift and keep holding it. .sendKeys('abc')Goes to the element that has focus. The page focuses this box on load. .keyUp(Key.SHIFT)Let go of Shift. const cmdCtrl = process.platform === 'darwin' ? Key.COMMAND : Key.CONTROL;Select all is Command A on a Mac and Control A elsewhere. await driver.actions().click(input).sendKeys('Selenium!').perform();JavaScript has no send keys to element in actions, so click first, then type. Lesson: Keyboard actions Scroll to an element, by an amount, and inside a frame const frame = await driver.findElement(By.tagName('iframe'));The page starts with a 5000 px tall block, so the iframe is far below. await driver.actions().scroll(0, 0, 0, 0, frame).perform();scroll(x, y, deltaX, deltaY, origin). With an element as origin and no delta, it scrolls the element into view. await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/scrolling_tests/frame_with_nested_scrolling_frame_out_of_view.html');Load it again. A refresh would keep the scroll position. await driver.actions().scroll(0, 0, 0, 300).perform();Like turning the wheel: 300 px down from the top left of the viewport. await driver.wait(async () => (await driver.executeScript('return window.scrollY')) >= 300, 2000);The scroll can land after perform returns, so wait for it. await driver.actions().scroll(0, 0, 0, 200, inner).perform();Turn the wheel with the pointer over the frame, so the frame scrolls. Lesson: Scroll wheel actions Tables Table: mouse action · javascript Mouse action JavaScript Double click doubleClick(el) Right click contextClick(el) Hover move({{origin: el}}) Press and hold press() Drag and drop dragAndDrop(a, b) Point in the viewport move({{x, y}}) From the current point move({{x, y, origin: Origin.POINTER}}) Release everything actions().clear() Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Table: key method · what it does Key method What it does keyDown(key) Press a key and keep it down. Meant for modifier keys. keyUp(key) Release it. Forget this and the key stays down for the next chain. sendKeys(text) Type into whatever has focus, one key down and key up per character. sendKeys(element, text) Click the element first, then type. Java and Python only. Lesson: Keyboard actions Table: wheel action · javascript Wheel action JavaScript Into view scroll(0, 0, 0, 0, el) By an amount scroll(0, 0, 0, 300) From an element scroll(0, 0, 0, 200,"],[22,"actions","The Actions API: mouse, keyboard and wheel","el) Lesson: Scroll wheel actions Tips and traps Remember A pointer move has a duration. In the bindings used here, Python uses 250 ms by default and JavaScript 100 ms. Some drag libraries ignore a move that takes 0 ms, so if a drag does nothing, try a chain with a longer move.Lesson: How actions work, and the mouse Common mistake Actions type into the focused element. If nothing has focus, the keys go to the page body and the test seems to type into nothing. Element sendKeys focuses the element itself, which is why it is the better choice for plain typing.Lesson: Keyboard actions 4.4"],[22,"javascript-cookies","JavaScript execution and cookies","Open chapter Running JavaScript in the page, async scripts, and working with cookies. Key ideas executeScript The script runs as a function body. Use return to get a value and arguments[n] to read what you pass in. Elements go in and come back as WebElements. Async scripts executeAsyncScript adds a callback as the last argument. A returned promise is also awaited. The script timeout, 30 s by default, limits both. JavaScript click It skips the checks a real click makes, so use it only when you know why the real click cannot work. Cookies Visit the domain first, then add. httpOnly hides a cookie from page scripts. deleteAllCookies gives a clean start. Code Run JavaScript, pass an element in, and see what type comes back const type = v => v === null ? 'null' : Array.isArray(v) ? 'Array' : typeof v === 'object' ? v.constructor.name : typeof v;A readable name for whatever came back. console.log(script + ' -> ' + type(await driver.executeScript(script)));Run the script as the body of a function and convert the result. console.log('arguments: ' + await driver.executeScript(\"return arguments[0].innerText + ' / ' + arguments[1]\", dinner, 7));Extra arguments arrive in the script as arguments[0], arguments[1] and so on. await driver.executeScript('arguments[0].click();', await driver.findElement(By.id('updatediv')));A click done by JavaScript. It skips the checks a real click makes. Lesson: Run JavaScript in the page Asynchronous scripts, promises, the script timeout and a JavaScript error const waited = await driver.executeAsyncScript(\"const done = arguments[arguments.length - 1]; setTimeout(() => done('waited'), 300);\");Selenium adds a callback as the last argument. The command returns when the script calls it. console.log('promise: ' + await driver.executeScript(\"return new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('from promise'), 200));\"));A returned promise is awaited by the browser before it answers. await driver.manage().setTimeouts({ script: 200 });How long a script may run, in ms. The default is 30000. console.log('too slow: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]);The callback came after 500 ms, the limit was 200 ms. console.log('broken script: ' + e.message.split('\\n')[0]);A JavaScript error in the page becomes javascript error. Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout Add, read, protect and delete cookies await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'key', value: 'value' });The browser is still on an empty start page with no domain. await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');Visit the domain first. A small page is enough. const c = await driver.manage().getCookie('key');Read one cookie by name. It comes back as an object. await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'foo', value: 'bar', sameSite: 'Strict' });Any other field goes in the same object. await driver.manage().addCookie({ name: 'secret', value: 's', httpOnly: true });httpOnly hides the cookie from page JavaScript. console.log('missing cookie: ' + e.name);JavaScript raises NoSuchCookieError here, where Java and Python return null or None. await driver.manage().deleteAllCookies();A clean start, for example before logging in as another user. Lesson: Cookies Tables Table: error · javascript Error JavaScript The script took longer than the script timeout ScriptTimeoutError The script threw an error JavascriptError Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout Table: field · meaning Field Meaning name, value Required. The data. domain, path Where the cookie is sent. They must match the current page. secure Only sent over https. httpOnly Hidden from page JavaScript. sameSite Strict, Lax or None. Whether the cookie goes with requests started by other sites. expiry Seconds since 1970. With no expiry the cookie lasts for the session. Lesson: Cookies Tips and traps Common mistake A click done with JavaScript always works, even on a covered or disabled element, because it skips every check a user faces. A test that passes with a JavaScript click may be hiding a"],[22,"javascript-cookies","JavaScript execution and cookies","real bug. Use it only when you know why the real click cannot work.Lesson: Run JavaScript in the page Remember The script timeout is 30 seconds by default, as chapter 2.1 showed. It covers both sync and async scripts. A short timeout makes a broken async script fail fast.Lesson: Asynchronous scripts and the script timeout"],[22,"javascript-cookies","Step 5Build a test suite","5.1"],[22,"test-frameworks","Test frameworks: JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha","Open chapter Running Selenium inside JUnit 5, pytest and Mocha, with data-driven tests and screenshots on failure. Key ideas Framework jobs Selenium drives the browser. Fresh browser Start the driver in the before each step and quit it in the after each step, so tests stay independent and can run in any order. Teardown always runs @AfterEach, code after yield and afterEach run also when the test failed, so quit belongs there. Data-driven @ParameterizedTest with @CsvSource, @pytest.mark.parametrize, or a loop around it() in Mocha. One result per row. Screenshot on failure Take it after the test failed and before quit: Mocha this.currentTest.state. Code A first test class: a fresh browser for every test describe('Web form', function () {describe groups tests. Mocha gives every test a name. beforeEach(asyncfunction () {Runs before every test. it('has the right title', asyncfunction () {One test. assert.strictEqual(await driver.getTitle(), 'Web form');Node's own assert module. Actual first, then expected. afterEach(asyncfunction () {Runs after every test, also when the test failed. Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework One test, three rows of data const rows = [Each row is one run of the test. for (const [user, password, expected] of rows) {Mocha has no data annotation. A loop that calls it() once per row does the same job. const alert = await driver.wait(until.alertIsPresent(), 2000);The page answers with an alert. Lesson: Data-driven tests A failing test that saves a screenshot before the browser closes assert.strictEqual(await driver.getTitle(), 'Web form!');Wrong on purpose, so the test fails. if (this.currentTest.state === 'failed') {Mocha tells the hook how the test ended. fs.writeFileSync(name, await driver.takeScreenshot(), 'base64');Take the picture before quit. Lesson: A screenshot when a test fails Tables Table: job · mocha Job Mocha Before every test beforeEach After every test afterEach Once for the class or file before, after Check a value assert.strictEqual(actual, expected) Run it mocha or npx mocha Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework Table: framework · where the screenshot code goes · how it knows the test failed Framework Where the screenshot code goes How it knows the test failed Mocha The afterEach hook, before quit() this.currentTest.state is \"failed\" Lesson: A screenshot when a test fails Tips and traps Remember The Selenium documentation recommends a fresh browser for every test. Then no cookie, login or open dialog can leak from one test into the next, and the tests can run in any order or in parallel. It costs a browser start per test, a price that is almost always worth paying.Lesson: Selenium inside a test framework Remember Keep the data close to the test while it is small. When it grows, move it to a CSV or JSON file: JUnit has @CsvFileSource, and pytest and Mocha can read any file into the same list.Lesson: Data-driven tests 5.2"],[22,"page-objects","Page objects and page components","Open chapter Page objects, page component objects and the rules for writing them. Key ideas Page object A class that is the interface to one page. Tests call its methods, so a change to the page is fixed in one place. No assertions inside Assertions belong to the test. The only check in a page object is that the right page is loaded. Return the next page A method that moves to another page returns that page object. A method that stays may return itself. Components A page component object models a repeated part, such as a field or a product card, and searches only inside its root element. Waits inside Put waits in the page object, for example a constructor that waits for its title after a navigation. Code Two page objects, one component and three tests constFIELDS = By.css('label.form-label');Locators live here and nowhere else. staticasync at(driver) {A constructor cannot await, so an async factory does the page check. return (awaitthis.driver.findElements(FIELDS)).map(e => newField(e));Wrap every label element in a Field component. classField {A page component: one label and the control inside it. async name() { return (awaitthis.root.getText()).split('\\n')[0].trim(); }The first line of the label is its name. control() { returnthis.root.findElement(By.css('input, textarea, select')); }Search inside this component only. await driver.wait(until.titleIs('Web form - target page'), 3000);The page arrives after a navigation, so wait for it instead of checking once. const form = awaitWebFormPage.open(driver);The test talks to pages, not to locators. const received = await form.submit();submit() moves to another page, so it returns that page object. assert.strictEqual(await received.message(), 'Received!');The assertion stays in the test. assert.strictEqual(fields.length, 11);Eleven labelled controls on this page. await assert.rejects(WebFormPage.at(driver), /This is not the web form/);The check fails loudly. Lesson: Write page objects and components Tables Table: without page objects · with page objects Without page objects With page objects Locators are copied into every test that uses the page Each locator is written once, inside the page object A test reads as a list of clicks and CSS selectors A test reads as what the user does: open the form, fill a field, submit A UI change breaks many tests in many files A UI change is fixed in one class Waiting and page checks are repeated The page object waits for its page once, in its constructor Lesson: Why page objects Table: rule from the documentation · where it shows in the example Rule from the documentation Where it shows in the example The public methods represent the services the page offers field, fields, submit, message Try not to expose the internals of the page Locators are private. Field.control() is private in Java Generally do not make assertions Every assert is in the test file The one allowed check: the right page is loaded The constructors check the title, or wait for it after a navigation Methods return other page objects, components or themselves submit() returns ReceivedPage, fill() returns the Field A page object need not represent an entire page Field represents one labelled control Lesson: Write page objects and components Table: choice · what the documentation or practice suggests Choice What the documentation or practice suggests Same action, different result Write two methods, for example loginAsValidUser and loginExpectingError, each returning the page that really follows A part that appears on many pages A component object such as a header, a menu or a product card, used inside page objects Where waits go Inside the page object, so tests do not repeat them Base class Shared code such as the driver field and a wait helper. Keep it small Lesson: Design choices and the limits of page objects Tips and traps Remember ReceivedPage waits for its title, while WebFormPage checks the title once. The web form is opened with get(), which returns after the page loads. The"],[22,"page-objects","Page objects and page components","received page appears after a click, and chapter 3.3 showed that the next command can run before the new page is there.Lesson: Write page objects and components 5.3"],[22,"practices","Good practices and things to avoid","Open chapter Test design, encouraged practices and the things the documentation says to avoid. Key ideas Need a browser? Ask first whether a lighter test can do the job. Browser tests are the most expensive kind. Short and focused Set up the data, perform a few actions, evaluate the result. Many small independent tests beat one grand journey. Clean start A new driver starts with a new profile. Use a fresh browser per test and never share test data. Prepare state outside the UI Log in and create data through APIs, then set cookies, instead of clicking through forms. Avoid CAPTCHAs, download progress, status codes, third party logins, test order, performance numbers, spidering and 2FA are jobs for other tools or test hooks. Code A new driver starts with a clean profile await first.manage().addCookie({ name: 'cart', value: '3 items' });State a first test leaves behind: a cookie await first.executeScript(\"localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark')\");and a value in local storage. const second = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();A new driver gets a new, empty profile by default. Lesson: Encouraged practices Prepare the state without clicking through the user interface const session = 'abc123';In a real suite this value comes from a login API call, not from the login form. await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');A small page on the domain, only to be allowed to set the cookie. await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/cookies.html');Now open the page under test. It reads its cookies on load. Lesson: Encouraged practices Tables Table: testing type · question it answers · selenium fits? Testing type Question it answers Selenium fits? Acceptance Are we building the right product? Yes, by simulating what the user expects Functional Are we building the product right? Yes Integration Do the modules work together, for example order plus payment? Yes, for flows through the UI System Does the whole product work end to end, in an environment like production? Yes, a few journeys Performance (load and stress) How fast is it, and does it survive a peak? Not as the measuring tool. The documentation points to tools such as JMeter Regression Did a change break what worked? Yes, by running the existing tests again Lesson: The shape of a good browser test Table: practice · what the documentation says Practice What the documentation says Avoid sharing state Do not share test data, clean up stale data, and create a new WebDriver per test Test independency Write each test as its own unit, not relying on another test to complete Mock external services Removing dependencies on external services improves speed and stability Improved reporting Selenium does not report. Use the reports of your test framework, such as xUnit XML or HTML Locators Unique ids first, then a well written CSS selector. XPath is flexible but harder to debug and often slower Page objects and a fluent API Chapter 5.2. Methods can return the page for chained calls Domain specific language Business level methods on top of page objects Lesson: Encouraged practices Table: avoid · why · do instead Avoid Why Do instead CAPTCHAs They are built to stop automation Disable them in the test environment, or add a hook that lets tests bypass them File downloads The API does not expose download progress Find the link with Selenium and fetch it with an HTTP library such as curl, using any cookies it needs HTTP status codes WebDriver acts like a user, and a status code is rarely the useful detail of a failure Check what the user sees, such as an error page. Chapter 6.2 shows BiDi network events if you really need codes Logging in to Gmail or Facebook Against their terms, slow and unreliable Use the APIs or test account tools they provide Test dependency Tests must run in any order Make each test set up its own state Performance testing Browser start, servers and third parties add noise to every number Use performance tools, and keep Selenium for"],[22,"practices","Good practices and things to avoid","function Link spidering A browser takes seconds per page Use curl or a library such as BeautifulSoup Two factor authentication Automating it is hard and less secure A test only token, or 2FA turned off for test users, test IPs or the test environment Lesson: Things to avoid Tips and traps Remember Every row has the same shape: Selenium is a tool for what a user does in a browser. When the job is not that, a lighter tool is faster and more reliable.Lesson: Things to avoid 5.4"],[22,"troubleshooting","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","Open chapter Common errors, stale elements and logging in Java, Python and JavaScript. Key ideas Error names Each W3C error has a class per language. Read the name, then the message, then your line in the trace. Stale element The node behind a reference was replaced, for example by a refresh. Find the element again. Context An element found in one frame or window is unknown in another. Switch back before using it. Client log JavaScript 4.51: driver.http.Executor at FINER. Driver log ChromeDriver writes COMMAND and RESPONSE lines when its service is given a log file and a verbose level. Code Make an element reference fail three ways, and fix each one let box = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));The driver gives back a reference to this exact node. await driver.navigate().refresh();The page is rebuilt. The old node is gone, a new one with the same name takes its place. box = await driver.findElement(By.name('my-text'));The fix: find it again. const email = await driver.findElement(By.id('email'));Found inside the frame. console.log('2. outside its frame: ' + e.name);The reference is fine, but this context does not know it. await driver.switchTo().frame(frame);The fix: switch back to the context where it was found. await driver.get('https://www.selenium.dev/selenium/web/blank.html');Leave the page completely. console.log('3. after leaving the page: ' + e.name);Go back to the page and find it again. Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Turn on the client log and see every command const http = logging.getLogger('driver.http.Executor');In selenium-webdriver 4.51 the HTTP lines come from this logger. http.setLevel(logging.Level.FINER);Request lines are written at FINER. http.addHandler(entry => {A tiny handler that prints one line per request. const m = /^\\[[\\w.]+\\] >>> (\\w+) (\\S+)$/.exec(entry.message);Each message starts with the logger name in square brackets. if (m) console.log(m[1] + ' ' + m[2].replace(/\\/session\\/[0-9a-f]{32}/, '/session/{id}'));Hide the session id, which changes every run. Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Ask ChromeDriver for its own log const service = new chrome.ServiceBuilder().loggingTo('chromedriver.log').enableVerboseLogging();Where ChromeDriver writes its log, with full detail. if (line.includes('] COMMAND ')) console.log(line.split('] COMMAND ')[1].split(' ')[0]);Print the name of each command the driver received. Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Tables Table: w3c error · javascript · usual cause and fix W3C error JavaScript Usual cause and fix no such element NoSuchElementError Wrong place, wrong time or a changed locator. Check the page, add an explicit wait, update the locator. stale element reference StaleElementReferenceError The node was replaced, for example by a refresh or a re render. Find it again. element click intercepted ElementClickInterceptedError Something covers the element: a sticky bar, a modal, an animation. Wait for it to go, or scroll. element not interactable ElementNotInteractableError Hidden, zero size or not ready. Wait until visible and enabled. invalid selector InvalidSelectorError The CSS or XPath is broken, or an XPath was passed to a CSS locator. timeout TimeoutError An explicit wait ran out. The message says which condition. script timeout ScriptTimeoutError A script took longer than the script timeout. unexpected alert open UnexpectedAlertOpenError An alert was left open. Handle it where it opens. no such window NoSuchWindowError The window was closed and the test did not switch back. invalid session id NoSuchSessionError The session was quit or crashed. Look for a quit earlier in the test. session not created SessionNotCreatedError Driver and browser versions do not match, or the browser could not start. Lesson: Read an error the right way Table: what happened · error in this run · fix What happened Error in this run Fix Refresh, re render, list re sorted stale element reference Find the element"],[22,"troubleshooting","Troubleshooting: errors, stale elements and logs","again just before you use it Switched to another frame or window no such element Switch back to the context where it was found Navigated to another page no such element Go back to the page and find it again Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Table: language · logger · default level · level that shows requests Language Logger Default level Level that shows requests JavaScript selenium-webdriver logging, driver.http.Executor in 4.51 OFF until set FINER Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did Tips and traps Remember Search the error name together with the first line of the message. Ignore the long list of numbers under Stacktrace in Chrome messages. Those are addresses inside ChromeDriver, not your code.Lesson: Read an error the right way Remember Page objects that find elements inside their methods, as in chapter 5.2, rarely meet stale elements, because they look the element up fresh each time.Lesson: Stale elements and the element store Common mistake The documentation shows the JavaScript logger as getLogger(\"webdriver\"). In selenium-webdriver 4.51 that name printed nothing in our run, while driver.http and driver.http.Executor printed every request.Lesson: Logs: what the client sent and what the driver did"],[22,"step-6","Step 6Scale out with Grid","6.1"],[22,"grid","Selenium Grid 4","Open chapter Selenium Grid 4 components, roles, remote sessions and parallel runs. Key ideas Why a Grid Parallel runs on many browsers, versions and systems, and shorter suites: tests × average time ÷ nodes. Components Router (4444), New Session Queue, Distributor, Session Map, Node (5555) and Event Bus (4442, 4443). Roles Standalone for one machine, Hub and Node for most Grids, Distributed for very large ones. Remote driver Only the driver creation changes. se:name adds metadata, se:cdp gives DevTools through the Grid, and a local file detector sends upload files to the node. Queue and quit New sessions wait in the queue for a free slot. Always quit, or the slot stays busy until the session timeout. Code Ask the Grid if it is ready and what it offers const res = await fetch('http://localhost:4444/status');The same endpoint a driver has, now answered by the Grid. console.log('slot: ' + slot.stereotype.browserName);Each slot can hold one session of this browser. Lesson: When and why to use a Grid Three tests, two slots: watch the queue const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).usingServer('http://localhost:4444').build();Resolves when the Grid has given this test a slot. awaitnewPromise(r => setTimeout(r, 3000));Pretend the test takes 3 seconds. await driver.quit();Frees the slot for the next request in the queue. const times = (awaitPromise.all([oneTest(), oneTest(), oneTest()])).sort((a, b) => a - b);Three tests start at the same moment. console.log('got a browser after: ' + JSON.stringify(times.map(t => t + ' s')));Two at once, the third only after a slot was free. Lesson: Components, roles and ports Run a test on the Grid with a name and a file upload const options = new chrome.Options();Options say which browser you want. The Grid picks a node that has it. options.set('se:name', 'upload on the grid');se: capabilities are Grid metadata. se:name shows in the Grid UI. const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(options).usingServer('http://localhost:4444').build();The only change from a local test: a server URL. console.log(k + ': ' + (k === 'se:cdp' ? v.replace(id, '{id}') : v));se:cdp is a DevTools address that goes through the Grid. driver.setFileDetector(new remote.FileDetector());Sends the local file to the node, which may be another machine. console.log('attached: ' + await driver.executeScript('return arguments[0].files[0].name', upload));The browser on the node has the file. Lesson: Remote sessions, metadata and files Numbers to remember Suite time on a GridExam favourite total time = number of tests × average test timenumber of nodes From the documentation: 15 tests of 45 s take 11 min 15 s on one node, 2 min 15 s on 5 nodes and 45 s on 15 nodes. Lesson: When and why to use a Grid Tables Table: component · job · default port Component Job Default port Router Entry point for every request. New sessions go to the queue. Commands for existing sessions go straight to the right node, found through the Session Map 4444 New Session Queue Holds new session requests in first in, first out order, with a timeout 5559 Distributor Knows every node and its slots. Takes requests from the queue, finds a matching free slot and creates the session 5553 Session Map Remembers which node runs which session id 5556 Node Runs the browsers. By default one slot per CPU for Chromium browsers and Firefox, one for Safari 5555 Event Bus Messages between the other components, cheaper than HTTP. Started first in a distributed Grid 4442, 4443 and 5557 Lesson: Components, roles and ports Table: role · command · use it for Role Command Use it for Standalone java -jar selenium-server-<version>.jar standalone All components in one process on one machine. Local debugging, quick suites, CI Hub and Node ... hub and ... node --hub http://<hub-ip>:4444 The most used role. Combine machines with different systems and browsers behind one address Distributed ... event-bus, sessionqueue, sessions, distributor,"],[22,"grid","Selenium Grid 4","router, node Large Grids with more than 100 nodes, each component scaled on its own Lesson: Components, roles and ports Table: language · remote driver · file detector Language Remote driver File detector JavaScript new Builder().usingServer(gridUrl) setFileDetector(new remote.FileDetector()) Lesson: Remote sessions, metadata and files Tips and traps Remember A node registers by sending a message on the Event Bus, and the Distributor then calls it over HTTP to confirm. When hub and node are on different machines, the Event Bus ports 4442 and 4443 and the node port must be reachable.Lesson: Components, roles and ports 6.2"],[22,"bidi-cdp","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","Open chapter WebDriver BiDi events, Chrome DevTools Protocol commands and Grid GraphQL. Key ideas BiDi A W3C protocol with a WebSocket, so the browser can push events. Turn it on with webSocketUrl, then subscribe to console and page errors. Events, not polling Handlers are called when events arrive. Wait for them, because they come after the action that caused them. CDP Chrome and Edge only, no stability promise, the three newest Chrome versions. Use it for Chromium features with no other API. GraphQL POST a query to /graphql on the Grid for nodes, slots, sessions and the queue size. Grid endpoints /status, DELETE /session, and drain or remove a node through the Distributor endpoints. Code Hear console messages and page errors as they happen const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).setChromeOptions(new chrome.Options().enableBidi()).build();Ask for a BiDi WebSocket next to the normal HTTP connection. const inspector = awaitLogInspector(driver);Subscribes to log events. await inspector.onConsoleEntry(e => heard.push(`console ${e.level}: ${e.text}`));Called for every console.log, console.error and so on. await inspector.onJavascriptException(e => heard.push(`page error: ${e.text}`));Called for every uncaught error in the page. await driver.wait(() => heard.length === 3, 5000);Events are pushed, not returned, so wait until all three arrived. Lesson: WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back Send Chrome DevTools commands: read the version and change the time zone const driver = awaitnewBuilder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build();sendAndGetDevToolsCommand exists on Chrome and Edge drivers only. const version = await driver.sendAndGetDevToolsCommand('Browser.getVersion', {});Any CDP method by name, with an object of parameters. await driver.sendAndGetDevToolsCommand('Emulation.setTimezoneOverride', { timezoneId: 'Asia/Kolkata' });Something WebDriver Classic has no command for. Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol Ask the Grid exactly what you need with GraphQL const res = await fetch('http://localhost:4444/graphql', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ query }) });GraphQL over HTTP: one POST with the query as a string. const q = '{ grid { nodeCount, totalSlots, maxSession, sessionCount, sessionQueueSize } }';Ask only for these fields. console.log('busy: ' + JSON.stringify((await ask(q)).grid));One session is running now. console.log('names: ' + JSON.stringify(sessions.map(s => JSON.parse(s.capabilities)['se:name'])));The se:name metadata is in the session capabilities. Lesson: Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints Tables Table: how to switch it on · javascript How to switch it on JavaScript Capability new chrome.Options().enableBidi() Console and page errors await LogInspector(driver) Lesson: WebDriver BiDi: the browser talks back Table: webdriver classic · cdp · webdriver bidi WebDriver Classic CDP WebDriver BiDi Standard W3C Recommendation Chrome only, no stability promise W3C, in progress Browsers All Chrome and Edge Chrome, Edge and Firefox, growing Style Request and response over HTTP Commands and events over WebSocket Commands and events over WebSocket Use it for Every normal test step Chromium features with no other API Events, logs, network, and the future of Selenium Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol Table: endpoint · what it does Endpoint What it does GET /status Current state of the Grid and every node, with slots and sessions POST /graphql Ask for exactly the fields you need: grid, nodesInfo, sessionsInfo, session(id) DELETE /session/<id> End a session and free its slot POST /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id>/drain Stop a node after its running sessions end, with no new sessions. Needs the registration secret header DELETE /se/grid/distributor/node/<node-id> Remove a node from the Distributor. Its running sessions continue Lesson: Grid features: GraphQL and endpoints Tips and traps Remember The documentation calls out two popular CDP examples with limited value: emulated geolocation, because most"],[22,"bidi-cdp","WebDriver BiDi, CDP and Grid features","sites use the IP address, and overriding device metrics, because Chrome mobile emulation in the options is the better tool.Lesson: The Chrome DevTools Protocol"],[22,"step-7","Step 7Revise and practise","7.1"],[22,"revision","Revision: predict, debug and design","Open chapter Predict the output, find the bug, design and sizing questions, a cheat sheet in three languages and a mock quiz. Key ideas Predict, then run The driver does what the protocol says, not what the code seems to say. Offsets add, Shift changes case, a refresh makes references stale. Cause before fix Name why a test is slow or flaky before changing it. Sleeps, mixed waits and missing quit calls are the usual causes. Layers Tests, page objects and components, a driver factory and support code. Each change should touch one layer. Size with the formula Suite time = tests × average time ÷ nodes, with about one CPU and 1 GB of RAM per browser. Know your binding Time is a Duration in Java, seconds in Python and milliseconds in JavaScript, and a missing cookie gives null, None or an error. Numbers to remember Page load timeout 300,000 ms Five minutes by default. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Script timeout 30,000 ms Thirty seconds by default. Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Tables Table: question shape · what it looks like · what earns the marks · lesson Question shape What it looks like What earns the marks Lesson Predict the output A short program and the question: what does it print, or which error comes back Knowing what the driver really does, not what the code seems to say 2 Find the bug A test that is slow, flaky or fails, and the question: why, and how do you fix it Naming the cause first, then a fix that removes it 3 Design and sizing Plan a framework, size a Grid, triage a flaky suite Clear layers, numbers from a formula, a reason for each choice 4 Write it in another language The same step in Java, Python or JavaScript The right method name and the right unit for time 5 Lesson: How Selenium skills are tested Table: step · javascript Step JavaScript Start Chrome await new Builder().forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build() Open a page await driver.get(url) Find one element await driver.findElement(By.id(\"q\")) Type await el.sendKeys(\"text\") Wait for an element await driver.wait(until.elementLocated(By.id(\"q\")), 5000) Into a frame await driver.switchTo().frame(el) Back to the page await driver.switchTo().defaultContent() Run a script await driver.executeScript(\"return 1\") Add a cookie await driver.manage().addCookie({{name: \"k\", value: \"v\"}}) Use a Grid await new Builder().usingServer(\"http://localhost:4444\").forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build() End the session await driver.quit() Lesson: Cheat sheet in three languages Tips and traps Remember Use the Revise lessons in order the first time, then use the Quiz and Worked problems tabs as a timed mock test. Each answer says which chapter to reread if you got it wrong.Lesson: How Selenium skills are tested Common mistake A good bug answer has three parts: what the code really does, why that breaks, and a fix that removes the cause. A retry that hides the failure is not a fix.Lesson: Find the bug"],[23,"","Interview Questions","Selenium questions from automation and SDET interviews on how WebDriver works, sessions and options, locators, waits, windows, frames, alerts, Actions, JavaScript and cookies, frameworks and page objects, flaky tests and debugging, and Selenium Grid 4 with BiDi and CDP, with code in Java, Python and JavaScript. · interview · interview questions · viva · FAQ · job preparation · selenium architecture · W3C WebDriver · Selenium Manager · close vs quit · CSS vs XPath · relative locators · shadow root · implicit vs explicit wait · FluentWait · window handles · iframe · Actions API · stale element · click intercepted · page object model · Selenium Grid · BiDi · CDP Selenium · Interview Questions 🎤"],[23,"","Selenium Interview Questions","The questions asked in test automation and SDET interviews about Selenium: how WebDriver works, set up and sessions, locators, waits, windows, frames and alerts, the Actions API, JavaScript and cookies, test frameworks and page objects, flaky tests and debugging, and Selenium Grid 4 with BiDi and CDP. Code is shown in Java, Python and JavaScript, and the outputs quoted come from real runs in the chapters. ✍️ Read the question and write your own answer in the box, as you would say it in the interview. 💡 Stuck? Open the hint. It points you in the right direction without giving the answer away. ✅ Open the answer to compare. You get the model answer, key points and a detailed explanation. 🎯 Rate yourself. Your notes and ratings stay in this browser, so you can filter to the ones to review. 🎤"],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","Answer out loud first, the way you would in the interview. Open the hint only if you are stuck, then compare your answer with the model answer. 0/41known ⭐ Most important topics for interviews 1 How Selenium works The Selenium family, the W3C protocol, bindings, drivers and Selenium Manager. 2 Sessions and set up Options, capabilities, page load strategies, timeouts and quit. 3 Locators and elements The eight strategies, CSS against XPath, relative locators, shadow roots. 4 Waits Why sleeps fail, implicit and explicit waits, FluentWait, never mixing them. 5 Browser features Windows and tabs, frames, alerts, Actions, JavaScript, cookies, uploads. 6 Frameworks and design JUnit, pytest and Mocha, page objects, data driven tests, parallel runs. 7 Flaky tests and debugging Stale elements, intercepted clicks, logs, screenshots on failure. 8 Grid and advanced Grid roles and modes, RemoteWebDriver, sizing, BiDi, CDP and GraphQL. 0 of 41 answers opened, 0 known, 0 to review How Selenium works Q1 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptHow Selenium works What is Selenium, and what are its main parts? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first It is more than one tool. Your answer Model answer Selenium is an open source project for automating browsers. WebDriver is the library you call from code to drive a real browser. Selenium IDE is a browser extension that records and plays back steps. Selenium Grid runs WebDriver sessions on remote machines, many at once. Selenium Manager, part of the bindings, finds or downloads the right driver and browser. WebDriver drives the browser IDE records and plays back Grid runs sessions remotely Selenium Manager handles drivers Detailed explanation Most interview questions are about WebDriver. Grid comes up as soon as the question is about speed or many browsers. How did you do? Q2 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptHow Selenium works Explain the Selenium 4 architecture from a line of test code to a click in the browser. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Binding, protocol, driver, browser. Your answer Model answer Your test calls a language binding such as Java, Python or JavaScript. The binding turns each call into an HTTP request defined by the W3C WebDriver standard, with a JSON body. A driver such as chromedriver or geckodriver receives it, tells the browser to act through the browser's own automation interface, and sends back a JSON answer that the binding turns into a return value or an exception. Binding to HTTP and JSON W3C WebDriver protocol Driver controls the browser Answer or error comes back Detailed explanation For example, findElement becomes POST /session/{id}/element with a body like {\"using\": \"css selector\", \"value\": \"#q\"}. Each step is a separate request, so every command costs a round trip. How did you do? Q3 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptHow Selenium works What does Selenium Manager do, and why did it matter? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Before it, you downloaded chromedriver yourself. Your answer Model answer Selenium Manager is a tool shipped inside the bindings since Selenium 4.6. When no driver is on the PATH, it finds the browser version, downloads the matching driver, caches it and hands its path to the binding. Teams no longer need a separate driver download step or a helper library to keep drivers in step with browser updates. Ships with the bindings Matches driver to browser Caches downloads Detailed explanation It can also download a browser such as Chrome for Testing when none is installed. You can still point Selenium at your own driver, for example in locked down networks. How did you do? Q4 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptHow Selenium works What changed in Selenium 4 compared with Selenium 3 at the protocol level? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Which standard do bindings speak. Your answer Model answer Selenium 4 speaks the W3C WebDriver standard directly. The older JSON Wire Protocol and the translation layer between the two are gone, so the binding, the driver and the browser all use the same standard. Selenium 4 also brought"],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","relative locators, a new Grid, Chrome DevTools access and, later, WebDriver BiDi. W3C WebDriver only No JSON Wire Protocol Relative locators and new Grid Detailed explanation One visible result is that capabilities follow W3C names, and vendor specific settings live under prefixed keys such as goog:chromeOptions, which the browser options classes fill for you. How did you do? Q5 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptHow Selenium works How would you compare Selenium with Playwright in an interview? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Protocol, languages, waiting, ecosystem. Your answer Model answer Selenium follows the W3C WebDriver standard, works with every major browser through vendor drivers, and has bindings for Java, Python, JavaScript, C# and Ruby with a very large ecosystem and Grid for scaling. Playwright talks to browsers through its own protocol, ships patched browser builds, and has auto waiting and a test runner built in. Selenium needs explicit waits written by you, but fits teams with existing Java or Python frameworks and real browser coverage needs. Standard against own protocol Explicit waits against auto waiting Ecosystem and Grid Detailed explanation A fair answer names a reason for each. Neither is better for every team. Selenium BiDi is closing some gaps by adding browser events. How did you do? Sessions and set up Q6 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptSessions and set up What is the difference between close and quit? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One window or the whole session. Your answer Model answer close closes the current window or tab. The session stays alive if other windows are open. quit ends the session, closes every window and stops the driver process. Always call quit at the end of a test, in a finally block or in the tear down of the framework, so a failing test does not leave a browser running. close: one window quit: whole session and driver Quit in tear down Detailed explanation Leftover browsers from failed tests are a common reason why CI machines run out of memory. How did you do? Q7 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingSessions and set up How do you start Chrome headless with a fixed window size? Show it in Python. from selenium import webdriver options = webdriver.ChromeOptions() options.add_argument(\"--headless=new\") options.add_argument(\"--window-size=1280,800\") driver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Browser options. Your answer Model answer Create ChromeOptions, add the arguments and pass the options to the driver. options = webdriver.ChromeOptions(), options.add_argument(\"--headless=new\"), options.add_argument(\"--window-size=1280,800\"), then driver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options). ChromeOptions Arguments Pass options to the driver Detailed explanation Options are how you set capabilities in Selenium 4. They cover the browser binary, arguments, the page load strategy, timeouts and vendor settings. Set a window size in headless runs so layouts match what you test. How did you do? Q8 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptSessions and set up What are the page load strategies and when would you change them? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first normal, eager, none. Your answer Model answer normal waits for the load event, so images and other resources are done. eager returns when the DOM is ready, at DOMContentLoaded, without waiting for images. none returns right after the navigation starts. Use eager when pages are heavy and your test only needs the DOM, and pair it with explicit waits for what you use. normal: load event eager: DOM ready none: right away Detailed explanation The default is normal. In the chapter example a page needing 400 ms of HTML, 300 ms of parsing and 2,500 ms of images returns after 700 ms with eager. How did you do? Q9 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptSessions and set up Which timeouts does a WebDriver session have, and what are the defaults? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Three of them. Your answer Model answer Implicit wait, default 0, controls how long find element keeps looking. Page load,"],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","default 300,000 ms or five minutes, limits a navigation. Script, default 30,000 ms, limits injected scripts. Explicit waits are not session timeouts, they live in your code. Implicit 0 Page load 300 s Script 30 s Detailed explanation In Java the script timeout printed as PT30S, the ISO duration form of 30 seconds. How did you do? Locators and elements Q10 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptLocators and elements Which locator strategies does Selenium support? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Eight in the bindings. Your answer Model answer id, name, class name, tag name, link text, partial link text, CSS selector and XPath. The W3C standard itself defines CSS selector, link text, partial link text, tag name and XPath. The bindings convert id, name and class name into CSS selectors before sending them. Eight strategies W3C has five id, name, class become CSS Detailed explanation For example, By.NAME \"q\" travels as the CSS selector [name=\"q\"]. That is why id and CSS behave the same way in the driver. How did you do? Q11 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptLocators and elements What is the difference between findElement and findElements when nothing matches? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Error or empty. Your answer Model answer findElement raises NoSuchElementException. findElements returns an empty list. Use findElements to check that something is absent, and remember that with an implicit wait set, an empty result only comes back after the full wait. Exception against empty list Use plural for absence Implicit wait delays empty results Detailed explanation Searching from an element, such as form.find_elements(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"input\"), stays inside that element. An XPath starting with // searches the whole document even when called on an element. How did you do? Q12 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptLocators and elements When do you choose CSS selectors and when XPath? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Text and going up the tree. Your answer Model answer Prefer CSS for most locators: it is short, readable and fast, and covers ids, classes and attributes. Use XPath when you need something CSS cannot do, such as matching visible text or moving from a child up to a parent or ancestor. Avoid absolute XPath that copies the page structure, because any change to any level breaks it. CSS by default XPath for text and parents Never absolute XPath Detailed explanation Order of preference that most teams use: a stable id or test id attribute, then name, then a short CSS selector, then a relative XPath. How did you do? Q13 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptLocators and elements What are relative locators? Give an example. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first above, below, near. Your answer Model answer Relative locators find an element by its position next to another one: above, below, toLeftOf, toRightOf and near. In Java: driver.findElement(with(By.tagName(\"input\")).below(By.id(\"email\"))). They use the rendered layout, so they help when an element has no good attribute but sits in a fixed spot. Position based above, below, left, right, near Depend on layout Detailed explanation near matches elements within 50 pixels by default. Because they depend on layout, they can change with window size. How did you do? Q14 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptLocators and elements How do you find an element inside a shadow DOM? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Get the shadow root first. Your answer Model answer Find the host element, get its shadow root, then search inside the root. Java: host.getShadowRoot().findElement(By.cssSelector(\"button\")). Python: host.shadow_root.find_element(By.CSS_SELECTOR, \"button\"). Each nested level repeats the two steps. Find the host Get the shadow root Search with CSS Detailed explanation Inside a shadow root, use CSS selectors. Chrome does not accept XPath there. How did you do? Waits Q15 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptWaits Why is Thread.sleep or time.sleep a bad way to wait? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Too long or too short. Your answer Model answer A sleep always waits the full time. When the page"],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","is fast it wastes time on every run, and when the page is slower than the sleep the test still fails. An explicit wait checks a condition and returns as soon as it holds, so it is both faster and more reliable. Always waits the full time Still fails when slower Use a condition Detailed explanation 200 tests that each sleep 3 seconds when the page is ready after 1 second waste about 6.7 minutes per run. How did you do? Q16 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptWaits Explain implicit and explicit waits, and why they should not be mixed. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Global setting against a condition. Your answer Model answer An implicit wait is a session setting: every find element keeps looking for up to that time. An explicit wait, such as WebDriverWait, checks a condition you choose, every 500 ms by default in Java and Python, until it holds or the timeout passes. Mixing them makes each check of the explicit wait block for the implicit time, so waits become unpredictable. The documentation says not to mix them. Implicit: every find Explicit: one condition Mixing gives unpredictable times Detailed explanation With an implicit wait of 10 s and an explicit wait of 15 s for an element that never comes, the wait gives up after about 20.5 s, not 15. How did you do? Q17 🌿 Medium🌍 ScenarioWaits A button is in the DOM but covered by a loading overlay. Which wait do you use before the click? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Present is not enough. Your answer Model answer Wait for the overlay to become invisible, for example invisibilityOfElementLocated on the overlay, or wait for the button to be clickable. Waiting for presence only proves the button is in the DOM, and a click on a covered element fails with element click intercepted. Presence is not enough Wait for overlay to go Or wait for clickable Detailed explanation Choose the condition that matches what the user needs. Visible for reading text, clickable for buttons, invisible for spinners and overlays. How did you do? Q18 🔥 Hard⌨️ CodingWaits What is FluentWait in Java and when would you use it? Wait<WebDriver> wait = newFluentWait<>(driver) .withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10)) .pollingEvery(Duration.ofMillis(300)) .ignoring(StaleElementReferenceException.class); WebElement done = wait.until(d -> d.findElement(By.id(\"done\"))); ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Configurable explicit wait. Your answer Model answer FluentWait is the general explicit wait. You set the timeout, the polling interval and the exceptions to ignore while polling, then call until with a function. WebDriverWait is a FluentWait with WebDriver defaults. Use FluentWait when you need a custom interval or to ignore an extra exception such as StaleElementReferenceException. Timeout, polling, ignoring WebDriverWait is a FluentWait Custom conditions Detailed explanation Python and JavaScript get the same control through the arguments of WebDriverWait and driver.wait. How did you do? Browser features Q19 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptBrowser features How do you handle a JavaScript alert, confirm or prompt? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first switchTo().alert(). Your answer Model answer Wait for the alert with an explicit wait, switch to it, read its text, then accept or dismiss it. For a prompt, send keys before accepting. An open alert blocks other commands, so tests must deal with it before going on. Wait for it accept or dismiss send keys for prompts Detailed explanation Python: alert = WebDriverWait(driver, 5).until(EC.alert_is_present()), then alert.accept(). How did you do? Q20 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptBrowser features A link opens a new tab. How do you work in it and come back? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Window handles. Your answer Model answer Save the current window handle, click the link, wait until there are two handles, switch to the handle that is not the original, do the work, then close the tab and switch back to the saved handle. In Selenium 4 you can also open a tab yourself with switchTo().newWindow(WindowType.TAB)."],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","Save the original handle Wait for the new one Switch back after close Detailed explanation Never assume the order of handles in the set. Pick the new one by comparing with the saved handle. How did you do? Q21 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptBrowser features Why does find element fail for an element inside an iframe, and how do you fix it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Each frame is its own document. Your answer Model answer An iframe holds its own document, and the driver searches only the current one. Switch into the frame first with switchTo().frame by index, name or id, or element. Work inside it, then switch back with defaultContent or parentFrame. Forgetting to switch back makes the next find in the main page fail with no such element. Frame is a separate document Switch in, then out defaultContent or parentFrame Detailed explanation In the chapter runs, searching for a main page heading while still inside the frame raised NoSuchElementException. How did you do? Q22 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptBrowser features What is the Actions API and when do you need it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Low level input. Your answer Model answer The Actions API builds a sequence of low level input: pointer moves, button presses, key presses and wheel scrolls, sent together and run in ticks. Use it for drag and drop, hover menus, double and right click, holding keys such as Shift, and scrolling. Call perform to send the sequence. Pointer, keys, wheel Built then performed Drag, hover, key chords Detailed explanation In the chapter run, holding Shift while typing \"abc\" and then typing \"def\" produced ABCdef. Moving to (8, 12) and then by (13, 15) left the pointer at (21, 27). How did you do? Q23 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptBrowser features How can cookies make tests faster, and what is the common mistake? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Log in without the form. Your answer Model answer If the app accepts a session cookie, get one through an API, open any page on the domain, add the cookie and refresh. Tests skip the login form and save seconds each. The common mistake is adding the cookie before visiting the domain, which fails with invalid cookie domain. Visit the domain first Add cookie and refresh Keep one real login test Detailed explanation Keep one test that logs in through the real form, because the form is a feature too. httpOnly cookies are hidden from document.cookie but visible to Selenium. How did you do? Q24 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptBrowser features How do you upload a file with Selenium, locally and on a Grid? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Do not click the dialog. Your answer Model answer Send the file path to the input of type file with send keys. Do not click the button that opens the system dialog, because Selenium cannot drive it. On a Grid the file is on your machine, not the node, so use a local file detector: Java needs setFileDetector(new LocalFileDetector()) on the RemoteWebDriver, and JavaScript needs setFileDetector(new remote.FileDetector()). The Python remote driver uses a local file detector by default. send keys the path Never the system dialog File detector on a Grid Detailed explanation The detector uploads the file to the node, which then fills the input with the path there. How did you do? Q25 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptBrowser features Is clicking with JavaScript a good way to avoid \"element click intercepted\"? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first What does the user experience. Your answer Model answer Usually not. A JavaScript click skips the checks a real click goes through, so the test passes even when a user could not click the button because something covers it. Fix the cause with the right wait, scrolling or closing the overlay. Keep JavaScript clicks for rare cases you have reasoned about and comment why. Skips real checks Hides real bugs Fix the cause instead Detailed explanation execute_script is still useful for reading values the page knows, scrolling to exact positions or setting up state. How did you do? Q26 🔥 Hard📘"],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","ConceptBrowser features What does execute script return in each language for numbers, objects and elements? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first The binding maps JSON types. Your answer Model answer Java returns Long for whole numbers, Double for decimals, String, ArrayList, a Map for objects and a WebElement for elements. Python returns int, float, str, list, dict and WebElement. JavaScript returns number, string, Array, Object and WebElement. null or undefined comes back as null in Java and JavaScript and None in Python. Java Long, not Integer Python int and dict Elements come back as WebElement Detailed explanation These types were printed by the real runs in the JavaScript and cookies chapter. A common Java bug is casting the result to Integer, which fails because it is a Long. How did you do? Frameworks and design Q27 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptFrameworks and design What is the Page Object Model and why use it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Where the locators live. Your answer Model answer A page object is a class for one page or component that holds its locators and offers methods for what a user does there, such as login(user, password). Tests call these methods instead of using locators directly. When the page changes, you fix one class, and tests read like steps a user takes. Locators in one place Methods for user actions Tests stay readable Detailed explanation Page objects should not hold assertions about the test outcome. They return data or the next page object, and the test asserts. How did you do? Q28 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptFrameworks and design How do you run the same test with several rows of data? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Each runner has a way. Your answer Model answer JUnit 5 uses @ParameterizedTest with a source such as @CsvSource. pytest uses @pytest.mark.parametrize. Mocha uses a loop that creates one it block per row. Each row becomes its own test in the report, so one failing row does not hide the others. ParameterizedTest parametrize One test per row Detailed explanation Keep the data next to the test when it is small. Move it to a file when testers who do not write code maintain it. How did you do? Q29 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptFrameworks and design Should tests share one browser or start a fresh one each time? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Isolation against speed. Your answer Model answer Start a fresh browser per test by default. A new session has no cookies or storage left by another test, so tests cannot depend on each other and can run in parallel in any order. Make it fast instead of sharing: set up state through APIs and run tests in parallel. Isolation first No order dependence Parallel friendly Detailed explanation In the chapter run a new driver had 0 cookies and empty storage every time. How did you do? Q30 🔥 Hard🏗️ DesignFrameworks and design Describe the structure of a Selenium framework you would build. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Layers. Your answer Model answer Tests at the top, written with JUnit, TestNG, pytest or Mocha. Page objects and components below them. A driver factory that builds a local or remote driver from configuration. Support code for test data, API set up, waits, logging and a screenshot on failure. Configuration for browser, environment and Grid address. Reports from the test runner in CI. Tests, pages, driver factory, support Configuration outside code Each change touches one layer Detailed explanation Explain why each layer exists: each kind of change, a new feature, a page redesign or a move to a Grid, should touch only one layer. How did you do? Q31 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptFrameworks and design How do you take a screenshot only when a test fails? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Hook into the runner. Your answer Model answer Use the hook the runner gives after a test runs. JUnit 5: an extension with AfterTestExecutionCallback that checks context.getExecutionException(). pytest: a pytest_runtest_makereport hook wrapper in conftest.py that checks the report of the"],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","call phase. Mocha: an afterEach that checks this.currentTest.state. Then save the screenshot with the test name. Runner hook Check the outcome Name the file after the test Detailed explanation Taking screenshots only on failure keeps storage small: 6 failures out of 400 tests at 60 KB each is 360 KB. How did you do? Flaky tests and debugging Q32 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptFlaky tests and debugging What causes StaleElementReferenceException and how do you fix it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first The document changed. Your answer Model answer An element reference points to a node in one version of the DOM. If the page refreshes, navigates or rerenders that part, the node is gone and the reference is stale. Find the element again after the change, keep locators rather than elements in page objects, or wait for the change to finish before you find. DOM node replaced Find again after changes Store locators, not elements Detailed explanation In the chapter run a refresh followed by using the old reference raised StaleElementReferenceException. How did you do? Q33 🌿 Medium🌍 ScenarioFlaky tests and debugging A click fails with element click intercepted. What does it mean and what do you check? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Something else would get the click. Your answer Model answer The driver scrolled the element into view and found that another element, such as a cookie banner, sticky header or loading overlay, sits on top of the click point. Check the screenshot and the error message, which names the element that would receive the click. Close the banner, wait for the overlay to go, or scroll so the header does not cover the target. Another element on top Read which element Remove the cause Detailed explanation The W3C error code is element click intercepted with HTTP status 400. How did you do? Q34 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioFlaky tests and debugging Your suite fails a few different tests on each run. How do you approach it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Group, read, fix the cause. Your answer Model answer Group failures by error message. Read the screenshot, page source and logs of one failure in each group. Typical causes are missing or wrong waits, shared state between tests, test data clashes and environment problems. Fix the cause, such as replacing a sleep with a condition, then track the failure rate. Retries can keep a pipeline moving while you fix it, but they are not the fix. Group by error Evidence before changes Retries are not fixes Detailed explanation Shorter tests help too. A journey of 5 steps that each pass 98 % of the time passes only about 90 % of the time. How did you do? Q35 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptFlaky tests and debugging How can you see the HTTP commands Selenium sends? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Turn on the binding logs. Your answer Model answer Turn on the binding logger at debug level. In Python, set the selenium logger to DEBUG. In Java, set the level of the org.openqa.selenium logger to FINE. In JavaScript, enable the logging module. You see each request such as POST /session, POST /session/{id}/url and DELETE /session/{id}. For the driver side, start the driver service with a log file and verbose output. Binding logger at debug See each request Driver log for the other side Detailed explanation In the chapter runs, JavaScript also sent GET /status first and Java also called /json/version, which only shows up in these logs. How did you do? Grid and advanced Q36 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptGrid and advanced How does a test use a Grid instead of a local browser? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Point the driver at the Grid. Your answer Model answer Create a remote driver with the Grid address and the browser options. Java: new RemoteWebDriver(new URL(\"http://localhost:4444\"), new ChromeOptions()). Python: webdriver.Remote(command_executor=\"http://localhost:4444\", options=webdriver.ChromeOptions()). JavaScript: new Builder().usingServer(\"http://localhost:4444\").forBrowser(Browser.CHROME).build(). The rest of the"],[23,"interview","Selenium Interview Questions","test stays the same. Remote driver plus URL Same test code Switch in the driver factory Detailed explanation A driver factory that switches between local and remote from configuration keeps tests unaware of where they run. How did you do? Q37 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptGrid and advanced Name the Selenium Grid 4 components and what each does. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Six roles. Your answer Model answer The Router is the entry point on port 4444 and forwards requests. The New Session Queue holds new session requests in order. The Distributor takes requests from the queue and picks a node with a free slot. The Session Map remembers which node runs each session. Nodes run the browsers. The Event Bus carries messages between the parts. Router, Queue, Distributor Session Map, Node, Event Bus Commands skip the queue Detailed explanation Commands for a running session skip the queue and the Distributor: the Router asks the Session Map and forwards straight to the node. How did you do? Q38 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptGrid and advanced What are the ways to run Grid 4? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first From one process to many. Your answer Model answer Standalone runs every role in one process, good for a laptop or a small CI job. Hub and node runs the hub, which holds Router, Queue, Distributor, Session Map and Event Bus, with nodes registering to it. Distributed runs each role as its own process, for large Grids. Docker images are available for each. Standalone Hub and node Fully distributed Detailed explanation Check readiness with GET /status, which reports ready and the free slots of each node. How did you do? Q39 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioGrid and advanced 600 tests of 40 s must finish in 20 minutes. How many one slot nodes do you need? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first tests × time ÷ nodes. Your answer Model answer The Grid documentation gives total time = tests × average test time ÷ nodes. Rearranged, nodes = 600 × 40 ÷ 1,200 s = 20 nodes. Plan about one CPU and 1 GB of RAM per browser, so 20 CPUs and 20 GB across the nodes. Rearrange the formula 20 nodes One CPU and 1 GB per browser Detailed explanation The documentation prefers many small nodes over one large machine, for isolation and so one failure does not take everything down. How did you do? Q40 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptGrid and advanced What is the difference between WebDriver BiDi and the Chrome DevTools Protocol in Selenium? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Standard against Chrome only. Your answer Model answer WebDriver BiDi is a W3C protocol that adds a WebSocket so the browser can push events, such as console messages, page errors and network events, to the test as they happen. It is cross browser and is meant to be stable. CDP is Chrome's own debugging protocol. Selenium exposes it for Chromium browsers, but it has no stability promise and Selenium supports only the three newest Chrome versions for its CDP classes. BiDi: W3C, events, cross browser CDP: Chrome only, unstable Classic for normal steps Detailed explanation Use classic WebDriver for normal steps, BiDi for events, and CDP only for Chromium extras with no other API, such as overriding the time zone. How did you do? Q41 🧠 Expert🏗️ DesignGrid and advanced How would you monitor a running Grid from a CI job? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Endpoints the Grid offers. Your answer Model answer Call GET /status for overall readiness and node slots, or POST a GraphQL query to /graphql for exactly the fields you need, such as grid { totalSlots, sessionCount, sessionQueueSize }. A growing sessionQueueSize means tests wait for slots, which is the signal to add nodes. To retire a node gently, drain it so it finishes running sessions and takes no new ones. /status and /graphql Watch the queue size Drain before removing Detailed explanation Name sessions with the se:name capability so they are easy to find in the GraphQL sessions list and in the Grid UI. How did you do? No questions match these filters."]]};
